insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:reqs', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"reqs","block":"b1","title":"From Requirements to Constraints","tag":"Six numbers turn a vague AI ask into something you can actually design against","why":"The architecture was decided in the kickoff meeting, before anyone drew a box — you just weren''t shown the decision. A sentence like \"add AI search to the portal\" carries no constraints, so every person in the room silently fills the blanks with their own, and two quarters later you discover you all built different products. Six numbers, written down and sourced, eliminate most of the design space in an afternoon and give you something to defend a diagram with.","mental":"Requirements arrive as an <b>index card with six blanks</b>: quality bar, latency budget, cost ceiling, volume, blast radius, freshness. Until every blank carries a number <b>and a source</b>, you are not architecting — you are guessing with vocabulary. Every later module in this course spends this card. Fill it, or don''t draw anything.","concepts":[["Thresholds, not booleans","Requirements for probabilistic systems are thresholds on distributions. \"It should be accurate\" is unarchitectable. \"At least 92% on a 300-case golden set, abstaining on 8% or less\" eliminates three architectures before you draw a box, because it names a measurement, a set, a bar, and an escape valve."],["The six fields","Quality bar plus the measurement that defines it; p95 latency plus interaction mode; cost per task and per month; volume as steady plus peak multiplier; blast radius; freshness tolerance. Six is the minimum set that determines a shape — with five, the shape is still ambiguous, and you''ll find out which one was missing during load testing."],["Source, not just number","Every number carries who said it and what would falsify it. A latency budget invented by the architect is a preference; one derived from an abandonment curve is a constraint. Unsourced numbers get renegotiated the first time they become inconvenient, which is always the week before launch."],["The three-question elicitation","Who is harmed by a wrong answer; what does the user do in the next ten seconds; what does the status quo cost per task today. Three questions a PM can answer without an engineer translating, and they fill five of the six fields."],["Blast radius","The worst single wrong output, written as one concrete sentence about one specific user. This field, not the ask, picks the architecture: it decides whether you owe the system a verification seam, an abstention path, or a human in the loop — and each of those is a rung you have to climb and pay for."],["The impossible triangle","Quality, latency and cost: name the one you are formally refusing to optimise, and get it signed. The requirement you decline to meet is itself a requirements artifact. Declining in writing costs an awkward meeting; declining silently costs an incident review."],["Falsification pass","For each number, write what evidence would show it is wrong and who owns that evidence. Numbers that survive a falsification pass survive design review. Numbers that don''t were opinions wearing a decimal point, and they will move under pressure."]],"mistakes":["Accepting \"it should be accurate\" and quietly translating it into your own private threshold","Writing the latency budget as an average, then meeting it while the p95 is four times worse","Costing per model call when the unit that has to close is the task, which may be seven calls","Filling the card alone, so the first stakeholder review re-opens every number from scratch","Treating blast radius as a compliance checkbox instead of the field that selects the shape"],"flags":["\"Just make it accurate\" — no measurement, no set, no threshold, no abstention allowance","\"It has to feel real-time\" — no number, and nobody has checked what users actually tolerate","\"It should handle anything a customer asks\" — unbounded scope against a bounded budget","\"We''ll work out the cost after launch\" — the unit economics were never modelled at all","\"Legal signed off on the demo\" — blast radius assessed against a scripted happy path"],"ask":["What measurement, on what set, at what threshold, decides that this works?","What does the user do in the ten seconds after the system is confidently wrong?","What does the status quo cost per task today, in money or in minutes?","Which of quality, latency and cost are we formally refusing to optimise, and who signed that?","How stale can an answer be before it stops being annoying and starts being harmful?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"<b>The sentence:</b> \"Add AI search to our support portal.\" Same eleven words, two teams, two architectures that share almost nothing. The only field that differs is blast radius.<br><br><b>Run one — the answer is a self-serve FAQ result.</b> Worst case: a user reads a confidently wrong answer, sees it doesn''t match their situation, and opens a ticket anyway. Annoying, recoverable, no money moves. Quality bar lands at 0.92 on a 300-case golden set of real tickets, abstention allowed on 8%. Volume 4,000 tasks/day, peak multiplier 3.0. Interactive, so p95 3,500ms. Cost ceiling $0.08/task. Check the card against itself before believing it: 0.08 × 4,000 × 30 = $9,600/month against a $12,000 ceiling, so there''s 20% headroom and the card is internally consistent. Now price the shapes off a measured baseline call of $0.012 and 1,400ms p95. A retrieval read path costs about 1.9× the baseline call and adds an index hop: $0.0228/task, 1.5 × 1,400 + 220 = 2,320ms. Both clear. A tool loop at roughly 5.2× latency lands at 7,680ms and is dead on latency alone. <b>Answer: a retrieval read path, one shape, 0.8 FTE to operate.</b><br><br><b>Run two — the answer auto-closes the ticket.</b> Same sentence. Worst case: a wrong answer closes a billing dispute, the customer doesn''t get their refund, and they churn. Now do the arithmetic that changes everything. At 4,000 tasks/day and the 8% error rate the first card happily accepted, that''s 320 wrong auto-closes per day, 9,600 per month. Price one at $18 of re-open handling plus goodwill and you are burning $172,800/month of damage to save $9,600 of contact-centre cost. The feature is negative value at 92%. Push the bar to 0.985 and errors drop to 60/day, 1,800/month, $32,400 — still bruising, so you also buy an abstention path at 1%, routing 40 tickets/day to a human. At four minutes each that''s 160 minutes/day, roughly 0.3 FTE, and it converts the expensive failure into a cheap one.<br><br><b>What the second card now demands.</b> 92% → 98.5% is not a prompt-wrangling delta; it is an error rate cut by 5.3×, and you buy that with a verification seam, not enthusiasm. Cost per task rises to $0.30, which the card immediately refuses: 0.30 × 4,000 × 30 = $36,000/month against the $12,000 ceiling. That refusal is the artifact. You carry it to finance with the $172,800 damage figure attached, and either the ceiling moves to $36,000 or the auto-close scope dies — both are good outcomes, and both happened in week one instead of month five. Latency also relaxes, because auto-close is async: p95 moves from 3,500ms to 9,000ms, which quietly re-opens two rungs that were closed a paragraph ago. <b>Answer: a chain with a verification stage and a human queue behind an abstention signal.</b><br><br>Identical ask. Field five picked the architecture — not the ask, and not the enthusiasm in the room.","good":["Every field on the card carries a number and the name of the person who sourced it","Quality is a threshold on a named, versioned set, with an explicit abstention allowance","Cost is stated per task and per month, and the two are multiplied to prove they agree","Blast radius is one concrete sentence about one specific wrong output, not a risk tier","The impossible triangle has a named loser, signed by whoever owns that trade-off"],"build":"Fill the six-field spec card for a feature your team is actually shipping this quarter — not a hypothetical. Numbers only; if you catch yourself writing an adjective, stop and go find the number. Then run the falsification pass: for every field, write one line naming the evidence that would prove the number wrong and the person who owns that evidence. Multiply cost per task by volume by 30 and check it against the monthly ceiling; if they disagree, you have found your first real constraint. Then circulate it to two people — a PM, who will move the quality and freshness numbers, and the most skeptical engineer you know, who will move the latency and cost numbers. The card that survives both is the one you design against. Ninety minutes, and it will save you a quarter.","res":[["Google SRE Book: Service Level Objectives","https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/","Use when turning \"it should be fast and reliable\" into a threshold you can defend — the SLI/SLO/SLA distinction is the backbone of the whole card."],["Google SRE Workbook: Implementing SLOs","https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/","Use right now: the worked process for picking a measurement window and a target you can actually hold, applied directly to your quality and latency fields."],["Precision and recall","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Precision_and_recall","Use when the quality field needs a real metric — \"accurate\" almost always means one of these two, and stakeholders usually want the one they didn''t name."],["NIST AI Risk Management Framework","https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","Use when filling the blast-radius field for anything regulated — it gives you shared vocabulary with legal and risk instead of an argument."]]},"depth":{"mech":"<b>What this course assumes, stated once.</b> You already know how retrieval, agent loops, tool schemas and eval harnesses work. This course never re-explains them. If any of those is fuzzy, the AI Engineering Mastery Hub course covers the mechanics properly and you will get far more out of this one afterwards. Here, components are things you <b>size, place, budget, sequence and authorise</b> — never things you learn. When a component appears below it gets one clause and then we go straight to the decision.<br><br><b>The mechanism is a translation, and it runs in one direction.</b> Natural language in, thresholds out. The reason it feels hard is that stakeholders speak in outcomes (\"customers should get answers\") while architectures respond only to distributions (\"92% of a defined case population, measured this way, within this many milliseconds\"). Nobody hands you the second form. Producing it is the job.<br><br><b>Start with the three questions</b>, because they are answerable without technical vocabulary. <b>Who is harmed by a wrong answer?</b> This produces blast radius directly, and it produces the quality bar indirectly — the harm severity is what makes 92% acceptable or catastrophic. <b>What does the user do in the next ten seconds?</b> This produces interaction mode and therefore the latency budget: a person staring at a spinner tolerates one number, a person who fired off a request and went to lunch tolerates a different one by three orders of magnitude. <b>What does the status quo cost per task today?</b> This produces the cost ceiling, and it produces it in the only form finance respects — a comparison. If a human handles the task for $4.20, a ceiling of $0.30 is not a constraint you invented, it is a 93% saving you''re offering.<br><br><b>Then derive, don''t guess.</b> Quality: name the measurement first, the set second, the threshold third, in that order, because a threshold without a measurement is decoration. Add the abstention allowance in the same breath — a bar of 0.92 with no abstention path means the system must answer every time, and \"must answer\" is a far more expensive requirement than \"must be right when it answers.\" Latency: always p95, never mean, and say which mode it applies to. Cost: two numbers, per task and per month, and multiply one into the other in front of the room. Volume: steady plus a measured peak multiplier — measured, because a 3× guess against a real 12× is a launch-day outage with your name on it. Freshness: minutes, or the explicit token for a static corpus.<br><br><b>The unarchitectable catalogue.</b> Four requirements recur, and all four are refusals dressed as asks. <b>\"Accurate\"</b> without a measurement — you cannot eliminate an architecture with it, so it eliminates nothing and constrains nothing. <b>\"Real-time\"</b> without a number — every architecture claims it. <b>\"Handles anything\"</b> — an unbounded scope, which is the only requirement that makes every rung of the ladder simultaneously feasible and inadequate. <b>\"As cheap as possible\"</b> — an optimisation direction, not a ceiling, and a direction cannot fail a design review. When you meet one, do not translate it privately. Hand it back with a reformulation attached: that is what the validator in the Patterns tab does, and doing it on paper is the same move.<br><br><b>The impossible triangle is the closing move.</b> Quality, latency and cost cannot all be maximised, and the honest artifact is a sentence naming the loser: \"we are optimising quality and cost, and formally accepting a p95 of nine seconds on this surface.\" Get a name against it. This feels like an awkward meeting and it is — but the alternative is that the trade-off gets made anyway, silently, by whoever writes the code, and then gets discovered by a customer.<br><br><b>Finally, falsify.</b> Walk each of the six numbers and write what would prove it wrong. A quality bar falsified by a labelling review. A latency budget falsified by an abandonment curve. A cost ceiling falsified by a finance model. A peak multiplier falsified by last Black Friday''s traffic. Numbers with a falsification test attached behave like constraints under pressure. Numbers without one behave like opinions, and opinions move.","trade":[["Tight quality bar vs a generous abstention allowance","Coverage against correctness","A high bar with no abstention path forces a shape you can''t afford; full coverage at a low bar ships the errors to customers"],["Numbers sourced from users vs numbers invented by the team","Elicitation effort now against renegotiation later","Invented numbers get overturned by the first stakeholder who finds them inconvenient, usually mid-build"],["One p95 budget vs per-mode budgets","Simplicity against honesty","A single budget spanning interactive and async work either strangles one surface or flatters the other into a lie"],["Refusing a requirement in writing vs quietly under-delivering","Short-term friction against long-term trust","An unspoken refusal resurfaces as a quality incident, and the incident review finds your diagram"]],"scale":["Cards are per-surface, not per-company — one card per user-visible surface, or you average two products into one impossible spec","Measure the peak multiplier from your own traffic history; a guessed 3× against a real 12× is an outage, not a rounding error","Re-run the card at every order of magnitude of volume: the same six numbers select a different shape at 4K/day and at 80K/day","Version the card next to the design doc, so when a number changes the diff tells you exactly which decisions must be re-litigated"],"sec":["Blast radius has a twin: \"the worst single wrong output\" and \"the worst single leaked output\" are different sentences and you need both written down","A quality bar with no abstention allowance forces the system to answer when it should decline — refusal is a safety control you must budget for explicitly","Freshness tolerance is a compliance field: \"answers may be 24 hours stale\" is also a statement about revoked permissions and deleted records","Name who is authorised to change each number; a cost ceiling that anyone can raise in a thread is not a ceiling, it''s a suggestion"],"interview":"I don''t start with a diagram, I start with an index card that has six blanks on it: quality bar and the measurement that defines it, p95 latency and interaction mode, cost per task and per month, volume with a measured peak multiplier, blast radius, and freshness tolerance. Until every blank has a number and a source, there''s nothing to architect — you''re just guessing with vocabulary. I get five of those six from three questions anyone can answer: who''s harmed by a wrong answer, what does the user do in the next ten seconds, what does the status quo cost per task. The field that does the most work is blast radius, because it''s what actually picks the shape — the same sentence, \"add AI search to the portal,\" gives you a retrieval read path if a wrong answer is an annoyance, and a verified chain with a human queue if a wrong answer auto-closes a billing dispute. And I always name which of quality, latency and cost we''re refusing to optimise, and get someone to sign it. The requirement you decline in writing is cheaper than the one you decline silently in production."},"patterns":{"intro":"A spec card you can''t validate is a wish list. This is the card as a dataclass with a <code>validate()</code> that <b>refuses</b> — it names the offending field, says why it''s unarchitectable, and hands back a reformulation you can paste into the ticket. Then <code>feasible_shapes()</code> filters the architecture table by what''s left, printing what''s already eliminated and by which number. Run it on the real ticket you were handed this week; the refusals are your elicitation agenda.","code":"\"\"\"SPEC CARD - the six numbers that turn an AI ask into a design constraint.\n\nStdlib only. Deterministic. No network. Run: python spec_card.py\n\nPrices and per-call latency are PARAMETERS, not facts baked into this file.\nRefresh unit_call_cost_usd / unit_call_p95_ms from your own traces and every\nverdict below moves with them. The shape names match the shape ladder.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, List, Tuple\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- vocabulary\n\nVAGUE = (\n    \"accurate\", \"accuracy\", \"real time\", \"real-time\", \"realtime\", \"instant\",\n    \"fast\", \"snappy\", \"cheap\", \"cheapest\", \"anything\", \"everything\", \"asap\",\n    \"high quality\", \"world class\", \"best in class\", \"scalable\", \"always\",\n    \"fresh\", \"up to date\", \"reasonable\", \"good enough\", \"seamless\",\n)\n\nFIX = {\n    \"quality_bar\": \"a pass rate on a named set, e.g. 0.92\",\n    \"quality_metric\": \"the measurement AND the set: exact-match on 300 labelled tickets\",\n    \"golden_set_size\": \"an integer count of cases you have actually labelled (>= 50)\",\n    \"abstain_allowed\": \"the share of cases the system may refuse, e.g. 0.08\",\n    \"p95_latency_ms\": \"a p95 in milliseconds, e.g. 3500\",\n    \"interaction_mode\": \"one of: interactive, async, batch\",\n    \"cost_per_task_usd\": \"dollars per completed task, retries included, e.g. 0.08\",\n    \"monthly_ceiling_usd\": \"the monthly number finance actually signed, e.g. 12000\",\n    \"tasks_per_day\": \"steady-state completed tasks per day, e.g. 4000\",\n    \"peak_multiplier\": \"peak-over-steady ratio measured from traffic, e.g. 3.0\",\n    \"blast_radius\": \"one of: cosmetic, recoverable, costly, unrecoverable\",\n    \"worst_output\": \"one sentence naming the worst single wrong output\",\n    \"freshness_max_age_min\": \"max tolerable staleness in minutes, or -1 for a static corpus\",\n}\n\nMODES = (\"interactive\", \"async\", \"batch\")\nRADII = (\"cosmetic\", \"recoverable\", \"costly\", \"unrecoverable\")\n\nNUMERIC = {\n    \"quality_bar\": (0.0, 1.0),\n    \"abstain_allowed\": (0.0, 0.5),\n    \"golden_set_size\": (50, 10 ** 7),\n    \"p95_latency_ms\": (50, 60 * 60 * 1000),\n    \"cost_per_task_usd\": (0.0, 1000.0),\n    \"monthly_ceiling_usd\": (1.0, 10 ** 9),\n    \"tasks_per_day\": (1, 10 ** 9),\n    \"peak_multiplier\": (1.0, 100.0),\n    \"freshness_max_age_min\": (-1, 10 ** 7),\n}\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass SpecCard:\n    \"\"\"Six fields. Every blank needs a number AND a source.\"\"\"\n    name: str = \"unnamed feature\"\n    # 1 - quality bar and the measurement that defines it\n    quality_bar: Any = None\n    quality_metric: Any = None\n    golden_set_size: Any = None\n    abstain_allowed: Any = None\n    # 2 - latency budget and interaction mode\n    p95_latency_ms: Any = None\n    interaction_mode: Any = None\n    # 3 - cost ceiling, per task and per month\n    cost_per_task_usd: Any = None\n    monthly_ceiling_usd: Any = None\n    # 4 - volume, steady and peak\n    tasks_per_day: Any = None\n    peak_multiplier: Any = None\n    # 5 - blast radius\n    blast_radius: Any = None\n    worst_output: Any = None\n    # 6 - freshness tolerance\n    freshness_max_age_min: Any = None\n\n\nProblem = Tuple[str, str, str, str]  # field, found, why unarchitectable, fix\n\n\ndef _is_number(v: Any) -> bool:\n    return isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool)\n\n\ndef _vague_hit(v: Any) -> str:\n    if not isinstance(v, str):\n        return \"\"\n    low = v.lower()\n    for w in VAGUE:\n        if w in low:\n            return w\n    return \"\"\n\n\ndef validate(spec: SpecCard) -> List[Problem]:\n    \"\"\"Refuse any spec containing a field you cannot design against.\"\"\"\n    out: List[Problem] = []\n\n    for name, (lo, hi) in NUMERIC.items():\n        v = getattr(spec, name)\n        if v is None:\n            out.append((name, \"blank\",\n                        \"no number, so no architecture can be eliminated\", FIX[name]))\n        elif not _is_number(v):\n            hit = _vague_hit(v)\n            why = (\"the word %s is not a threshold on a distribution\" % hit) if hit \\\n                else \"a string where a number belongs\"\n            out.append((name, repr(v), why, FIX[name]))\n        elif not (lo <= v <= hi):\n            out.append((name, repr(v),\n                        \"outside the plausible range %s..%s\" % (lo, hi), FIX[name]))\n\n    for name, allowed in ((\"interaction_mode\", MODES), (\"blast_radius\", RADII)):\n        v = getattr(spec, name)\n        if v not in allowed:\n            out.append((name, repr(v),\n                        \"must be one of %s\" % (\", \".join(allowed)), FIX[name]))\n\n    for name in (\"quality_metric\", \"worst_output\"):\n        v = getattr(spec, name)\n        if not isinstance(v, str) or len(v.strip()) < 12:\n            out.append((name, repr(v), \"unstated, so nobody can falsify it\", FIX[name]))\n        else:\n            hit = _vague_hit(v)\n            if hit:\n                out.append((name, repr(v),\n                            \"%s names a feeling, not a measurement\" % hit, FIX[name]))\n\n    # Cross-field arithmetic: the card must not contradict itself.\n    if _is_number(spec.cost_per_task_usd) and _is_number(spec.tasks_per_day) \\\n            and _is_number(spec.monthly_ceiling_usd):\n        implied = spec.cost_per_task_usd * spec.tasks_per_day * 30.0\n        if implied > spec.monthly_ceiling_usd:\n            out.append((\n                \"monthly_ceiling_usd\",\n                \"$%.0f/mo vs implied $%.0f/mo\" % (spec.monthly_ceiling_usd, implied),\n                \"per-task cost x volume x 30 already exceeds the monthly ceiling\",\n                \"cut cost per task to <= $%.4f, or raise the ceiling to $%.0f\"\n                % (spec.monthly_ceiling_usd / (spec.tasks_per_day * 30.0), implied),\n            ))\n\n    if _is_number(spec.quality_bar) and _is_number(spec.abstain_allowed):\n        if spec.quality_bar + spec.abstain_allowed > 1.0:\n            out.append((\n                \"abstain_allowed\",\n                \"%.2f + %.2f > 1.00\" % (spec.quality_bar, spec.abstain_allowed),\n                \"you cannot answer correctly and abstain on the same case\",\n                \"quality_bar + abstain_allowed must be <= 1.0\",\n            ))\n\n    return out\n\n\ndef report(spec: SpecCard) -> bool:\n    probs = validate(spec)\n    print(\"SPEC CARD: %s\" % spec.name)\n    if not probs:\n        print(\"  ACCEPTED - all six fields carry a number you can design against.\")\n        return True\n    print(\"  REFUSED - %d unarchitectable field(s):\" % len(probs))\n    for f, found, why, fix in probs:\n        print(\"    %-22s found %-30s %s\" % (f, found[:30], why))\n        print(\"    %-22s   ->  %s\" % (\"\", fix))\n    return False\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------- archetype feasibility\n# cost_mult / lat_mult are multiples of ONE baseline model call, which you pass\n# in. Nothing here rots when prices change.\n\nARCHETYPES = (\n    # id,              label,                    cost_mult, lat_mult, fixed_ms, verifiable\n    (\"single_call\",    \"single call\",                  1.0,      1.0,       30, False),\n    (\"prompt_chain\",   \"prompt chain/workflow\",        2.6,      2.4,       60, True),\n    (\"retrieval_read\", \"retrieval read path\",          1.9,      1.5,      220, True),\n    (\"agent_loop\",     \"single-agent tool loop\",       6.5,      5.2,      400, True),\n    (\"orchestrator\",   \"orchestrator-worker\",         14.0,      3.1,      650, True),\n    (\"batch_pipeline\", \"offline batch pipeline\",       0.5,    900.0,    60000, True),\n)\n\n\ndef feasible_shapes(spec: SpecCard, unit_call_cost_usd: float,\n                    unit_call_p95_ms: float) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Filter the ladder by the card. Print what is already eliminated, and why.\"\"\"\n    print(\"\\nFEASIBLE SHAPES for: %s\" % spec.name)\n    print(\"  parameters: unit call = $%.4f, p95 %dms  (refresh from your traces)\"\n          % (unit_call_cost_usd, unit_call_p95_ms))\n    print(\"  %-24s %10s %9s   %s\" % (\"shape\", \"cost/task\", \"p95\", \"verdict\"))\n    survivors = []\n    for sid, label, cmult, lmult, fixed, verifiable in ARCHETYPES:\n        cost = cmult * unit_call_cost_usd\n        p95 = lmult * unit_call_p95_ms + fixed\n        reasons = []\n        if cost > spec.cost_per_task_usd:\n            reasons.append(\"cost $%.4f > ceiling $%.4f\" % (cost, spec.cost_per_task_usd))\n        if p95 > spec.p95_latency_ms:\n            reasons.append(\"p95 %.0fms > budget %.0fms\" % (p95, spec.p95_latency_ms))\n        if spec.interaction_mode == \"interactive\" and sid == \"batch_pipeline\":\n            reasons.append(\"batch cannot serve an interactive mode\")\n        if spec.blast_radius in (\"costly\", \"unrecoverable\") and not verifiable:\n            reasons.append(\"blast radius %s needs a verification seam\" % spec.blast_radius)\n        verdict = \"FEASIBLE\" if not reasons else \"rejected: \" + \"; \".join(reasons)\n        print(\"  %-24s %10s %9s   %s\"\n              % (label, \"$%.4f\" % cost, \"%.0fms\" % p95, verdict))\n        if not reasons:\n            survivors.append((sid, label, cost, p95))\n    if survivors:\n        sid, label, cost, p95 = survivors[0]\n        print(\"  --> lowest feasible rung: %s ($%.4f, %.0fms). Climbing higher needs a\"\n              % (label, cost, p95))\n        print(\"      written forcing constraint naming which card field forced the climb.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"  --> nothing on the ladder clears this card. The card is the bug.\")\n    return survivors\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------- demo\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    ticket = SpecCard(\n        name=\"Add AI search to our support portal (as written)\",\n        quality_bar=\"should be accurate\",\n        quality_metric=\"users like the answers\",\n        golden_set_size=None,\n        abstain_allowed=None,\n        p95_latency_ms=\"real-time\",\n        interaction_mode=\"fast\",\n        cost_per_task_usd=\"as cheap as possible\",\n        monthly_ceiling_usd=None,\n        tasks_per_day=None,\n        peak_multiplier=None,\n        blast_radius=None,\n        worst_output=\"a bad answer\",\n        freshness_max_age_min=\"always fresh\",\n    )\n    report(ticket)\n\n    print()\n    faq = SpecCard(\n        name=\"Support search - self-serve FAQ\",\n        quality_bar=0.92,\n        quality_metric=\"human-graded correct on a 300-case golden set of real tickets\",\n        golden_set_size=300,\n        abstain_allowed=0.08,\n        p95_latency_ms=3500,\n        interaction_mode=\"interactive\",\n        cost_per_task_usd=0.08,\n        monthly_ceiling_usd=12000,\n        tasks_per_day=4000,\n        peak_multiplier=3.0,\n        blast_radius=\"recoverable\",\n        worst_output=\"a confidently wrong answer the user sees and escalates anyway\",\n        freshness_max_age_min=1440,\n    )\n    if report(faq):\n        feasible_shapes(faq, unit_call_cost_usd=0.012, unit_call_p95_ms=1400)\n\n    print()\n    auto = SpecCard(**dict(faq.__dict__,\n                           name=\"Support search - auto-closes the ticket\",\n                           quality_bar=0.985,\n                           abstain_allowed=0.01,\n                           blast_radius=\"costly\",\n                           worst_output=\"a wrong answer auto-closes a billing dispute and the customer churns\",\n                           cost_per_task_usd=0.30,\n                           p95_latency_ms=9000,\n                           interaction_mode=\"async\"))\n    if not report(auto):\n        # The card caught the contradiction before anyone drew a box. Take the\n        # refusal back to finance, get the ceiling raised, then re-run.\n        print(\"  (ceiling renegotiated to $36000 on the strength of that arithmetic)\")\n        auto = SpecCard(**dict(auto.__dict__, monthly_ceiling_usd=36000))\n    if report(auto):\n        feasible_shapes(auto, unit_call_cost_usd=0.012, unit_call_p95_ms=1400)\n\n    print()\n    print(\"Same sentence, two blast radii, two ladders. Field 5 picked the\")\n    print(\"architecture - not the ask, and not the enthusiasm in the room.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","notes":["Prices and per-call latency are parameters, never constants. Refresh <code>unit_call_cost_usd</code> and <code>unit_call_p95_ms</code> from your own traces and every verdict moves with them — that is the only way a tool like this survives a year.","The cross-field check earns its keep: cost per task × volume × 30 versus the monthly ceiling catches a contradiction that survives most design reviews, because the two numbers usually come from two different people who never met.","Note what the demo does with the refusal — it doesn''t shrug and continue, it takes the arithmetic to finance and re-runs with a renegotiated ceiling. A refusal is an input to a conversation, not a dead end.","The archetype table deliberately uses the same six shape names as the shape ladder. Keeping one vocabulary across your calculators is what makes their outputs composable in a design doc.","Verifiability is a hard gate, not a cost: a blast radius of \"costly\" or worse rejects any shape with no seam to check the output before it lands, regardless of how cheap or fast that shape is."],"debug":[["validate() passes but the design still gets rebuilt in month three","Fields were filled with numbers nobody sourced","Add an owner and a falsification test per field; unsourced numbers pass validators and fail reviews"],["Every shape comes back rejected","The card is over-constrained — usually latency and blast radius fighting","Print the binding constraint per shape and take the two tightest fields back to their owners"],["Every shape comes back feasible","The card is under-constrained; the ceilings are generous fiction","Tighten cost and latency to the numbers the business would actually defend, then re-run"],["Feasible set changes wildly between runs","Unit call cost or p95 is being estimated rather than measured","Pull both from a week of production traces, p95 not mean, and pin them in the design doc"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:reqs', 'paid', '[{"q":"A PM writes the requirement as \"the assistant should be accurate.\" Your first move as the architect:","o":["Ask which measurement, on which set, at which threshold decides this works","Propose the strongest available model and benchmark it against the baseline","Ship a prototype and let real user feedback settle the accuracy question","Write an acceptance suite covering the failure cases the team already knows"],"a":0,"exp":"\"Accurate\" eliminates no architecture, so it constrains nothing. A threshold on a named set does the work: 92% on 300 labelled cases immediately kills the shapes that can''t reach it. Benchmarking and prototyping are premature until you know what number you''re aiming at."},{"q":"Two teams get the identical sentence \"add AI search to our support portal\" and build different architectures. The field that diverged:","o":["Expected query volume, which drove one team toward a batch precompute","Freshness tolerance, which decided how often the index gets rebuilt","Blast radius: what the worst single wrong output actually costs someone","Cost ceiling, which forced one team onto a cheaper generation model"],"a":2,"exp":"Blast radius decides whether you owe the system a verification seam, an abstention path, or a human in the loop — each of which is a rung you must climb and fund. A wrong FAQ answer is an annoyance; a wrong answer that auto-closes a billing dispute is churn, and it selects a different shape entirely."},{"q":"The requirement reads \"p95 under 3 seconds.\" Which addition makes it architecturally useful?","o":["The interaction mode it applies to, since async and interactive differ hugely","A stretch target of 1 second, so the team has something ambitious to aim at","The mean latency alongside it, so both ends of the distribution are covered","A note that the budget excludes network time outside your own service boundary"],"a":0,"exp":"A latency number with no mode attached gets applied to everything — strangling async surfaces and flattering interactive ones — and mode is what decides which rungs of the ladder are even available. A stretch target adds no constraint at all, and the mean is precisely the number that hides your worst requests."},{"q":"Cost ceiling is $0.08 per task, volume is 4,000/day, monthly ceiling is $12,000. What does the card tell you?","o":["The numbers agree with roughly 20% headroom against the monthly ceiling","The per-task ceiling is too tight and should be renegotiated upward first","The monthly ceiling is redundant once a per-task ceiling has been agreed","The volume figure needs a peak multiplier before any of this can be checked"],"a":0,"exp":"0.08 × 4,000 × 30 = $9,600 against $12,000, so the card is internally consistent with headroom to spare. Doing this multiplication out loud is the point: the two numbers usually come from two people who never spoke, and they frequently contradict each other."},{"q":"Quality bar 0.92, no abstention allowance stated. The architectural consequence:","o":["The bar is unreachable for any shape below an orchestrator-worker pattern","Nothing changes — abstention is an implementation detail decided later on","The system must answer every case, which is far pricier than being right","Evaluation cost rises because the golden set must now cover every input"],"a":2,"exp":"\"Must be right when it answers\" and \"must answer every time\" are different requirements with different price tags. Forcing 100% coverage removes the cheapest correctness tool you have — declining to answer — and pushes you up the ladder to buy back the same quality."},{"q":"Which of these is a legitimate requirements artifact rather than a failure to gather requirements?","o":["\"We will support any question a customer could conceivably think to ask\"","\"Latency should be as low as we can reasonably get it within the sprint\"","\"We formally refuse to optimise latency; p95 of 9s is accepted, signed by Dana\"","\"Quality will be validated by the team''s judgement during the beta period\""],"a":2,"exp":"The requirement you refuse to meet, named and signed, is an artifact — it tells every later reviewer which trade was made and by whom. Unbounded scope, directional wishes, and judgement-based validation all leave the trade-off to be made silently by whoever writes the code."},{"q":"You inherit a card where the peak multiplier is 3.0, sourced from \"the team''s estimate.\" The risk:","o":["Provisioning against a guess, when last year''s real peak may be far higher","Multipliers below 5.0 are generally too low for any consumer-facing surface","Peak multipliers belong in capacity planning, not in a requirements artifact","The steady-state number becomes meaningless once a multiplier is applied"],"a":0,"exp":"A peak multiplier is measurable — the traffic history exists. A guessed 3× against a real 12× is a launch-day outage, not a rounding error. Every number on the card should name its source, and \"someone''s estimate\" is the source most likely to be wrong by an order of magnitude."},{"q":"The falsification pass asks you to write, for each number:","o":["A confidence interval reflecting how certain the team feels about it","The evidence that would prove it wrong, and who owns that evidence","A fallback value to use if the primary number turns out unworkable","The date it was agreed and the meeting in which it was first raised"],"a":1,"exp":"A number with a falsification test behaves like a constraint under pressure; a number without one behaves like an opinion, and opinions move the week before launch. Naming the owner matters as much as the test — it tells you who to go argue with when the evidence arrives."},{"q":"\"Answers may be up to 24 hours stale\" is a freshness field. What else is it?","o":["A capacity statement, since it caps how often the pipeline must run","A cost statement, since staleness is the cheapest lever on spend","A compliance statement about revoked permissions and deleted records","A quality statement, since stale answers score lower on any golden set"],"a":2,"exp":"If a document is deleted or a permission revoked at 9am, a 24-hour staleness tolerance says you may still be serving it at 8am tomorrow. That''s a decision legal needs to make, not one you should make alone — freshness is where the requirements card touches data governance."},{"q":"The status-quo question (\"what does this task cost today?\") primarily produces which field?","o":["Blast radius, by revealing what happens when the human process fails","The cost ceiling, in the comparative form that finance actually respects","Volume, since today''s task count is the best predictor of tomorrow''s","The quality bar, since the human baseline is what you must beat"],"a":1,"exp":"A ceiling of $0.30 in isolation is a number you invented. Against a human baseline of $4.20 per task it becomes a 93% saving you''re offering, which is a fundamentally different conversation. The status-quo cost is what converts your constraint into someone else''s business case."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:budgets', 'public', '{"mod":{"id":"budgets","block":"b1","title":"Cost, Latency & Reliability Budgets","tag":"Every box in your diagram debits three accounts, and you only get to overdraw once","why":"Nobody argues with a diagram. Everybody argues with a number. Budgets are how an architecture stops being a matter of taste: once each stage has a millisecond, cent and reliability allocation, \"let''s add a verification step\" becomes a subtraction anyone in the room can check, and it either fits or it doesn''t. Teams that skip this step don''t avoid the arithmetic — they just do it after launch, in an incident channel, with a customer watching.","mental":"Every request carries <b>three envelopes</b>: milliseconds, cents, and nines. Every stage you add debits all three, and none of them refill. Architecture is deciding who gets to spend — and the discipline is that you allocate <b>down from the SLO</b>, never sum up from whatever you happened to build.","concepts":[["Top-down allocation","Start at the number you promised, subtract the fixed floors, and what remains is the entire allowance for cleverness. Summing bottom-up from what you already built produces a number, but it is a description of your system, not a budget — and a description can never fail."],["Fixed floors","Network round trips, index lookups, and output-token generation time are not negotiable by design cleverness. Subtract them first. On an interactive surface they frequently eat half the budget, and knowing that before you design stops you allocating money you never had."],["Reliability composes multiplicatively","Five stages at 99.5% is 97.5% end to end, not 99.5%. Every box you add is a tax on the product of the chain, which is why box count is a reliability decision and not just an elegance one. The arithmetic is unforgiving and it never gets mentioned in a design review."],["You may not sum p95s","Stages do not have their bad day simultaneously. Summing p95s systematically over-states the real p95 — often by 15-25% — so it isn''t just wrong, it silently deletes architecture options you could have afforded. Compose with a root-sum-square of the spreads, or simulate."],["Availability and correctness are two budgets","Did it answer, and was it right. Classic systems only need the first. Conflating them is precisely why quality regressions have no error budget, no alert, and no owner — the dashboard is green while the product is wrong."],["Cost per task, not per call","A task may be seven calls, plus retries, plus the ones that failed and were paid for anyway. Per-call cost is an accounting number. Per-task cost is the number the unit economics close or fail against, and it is the only one worth putting on a slide."],["Budget the p95 cost, not the mean","Retries and variable output length give cost a fat tail. A mean of $0.057 with a p95 of $0.097 means one task in twenty costs 70% more than you modelled — at scale, that tail is the difference between a margin and a surprise."]],"mistakes":["Summing p95 latencies to get an end-to-end p95, then designing against a number that''s 20% pessimistic","Quoting per-stage availability in a review when only the composed product is real","Modelling cost on the happy path, excluding retries, failures, and the abandoned partial tasks you still paid for","Having an availability error budget and no correctness error budget, so quality regressions page nobody","Building bottom-up, then reverse-engineering an SLO that your existing latency happens to satisfy"],"flags":["\"Our p95 is the sum of the stage p95s\" — the composition is wrong in a knowable direction","\"Each service is three nines\" — nobody has multiplied them together in front of the room","\"It costs about two cents a call\" — the task is six calls and nobody has said so","\"Quality dipped last month\" — said calmly, because no error budget was ever burned","\"We''ll add a verification pass\" — proposed with no subtraction from any envelope"],"ask":["What are the fixed floors on this surface, and what''s actually left to allocate?","What''s the composed availability — the product, not the best stage in the chain?","What does a task cost at p95, including retries and the failures we pay for anyway?","Which stage do we cut first if the budget halves, and has anyone written that down?","What burns the correctness error budget, and who gets paged when it''s gone?"],"estMin":50},"deep":{"worked":"<b>The promise:</b> support deflection at $0.08/task, p95 3,500ms, 92% quality, 4,000 tasks/day. Allocate downward.<br><br><b>Step one: subtract the floors.</b> Network in and out on an interactive surface, measured: 120ms. That is not yours to spend. Remaining: 3,380ms.<br><br><b>Step two: allocate the stages.</b> Retrieval 260ms / $0.001. Rerank 300ms / $0.004. Generation 2,100ms / $0.050. Output rails 200ms / $0.002. Running totals as you go: 260 → 560 → 2,660 → 2,860ms of stage p95, and $0.001 → $0.005 → $0.055 → $0.057 per task. Generation is 73% of the milliseconds and 88% of the cents, which is the normal shape and tells you where every future argument will be.<br><br><b>Step three: compose properly, because 2,860 is the wrong number.</b> Summing p95s assumes retrieval, rerank, generation and rails all have their worst day on the same request. They don''t. Composing the spreads correctly (root-sum-square over each stage''s p50-to-p95 gap) gives 2,467ms; a seeded simulation over 40,000 requests gives 2,520ms. So the honest end-to-end figure is 2,520 + 120 = <b>2,640ms against a 3,500ms budget — 860ms of headroom, 25%.</b> Note the direction of the error: the naive sum said 2,980ms and left you believing you had 520ms. Summing p95s is conservative, and conservatism here is not free — it deletes design options you could have paid for.<br><br><b>Step four: the other two envelopes.</b> Reliability: four stages at 99.9%, 99.8%, 99.5% and 99.9% composes to 99.1% raw. Add one retry to the three retryable stages and effective availability rises to 99.9%. Cost with the retry tail: mean $0.057, p95 $0.057, and 4.01 calls per task — that last number is the one to put on the slide, because \"two cents a call\" and \"5.7 cents a task\" are the same system described honestly and dishonestly. Monthly: 0.057 × 4,000 × 30 = $6,840.<br><br><b>Step five: someone proposes \"just one more verification call.\"</b> It''s a good idea. It is also a subtraction, so do it. Measured on a similar stage: p50 760ms, p95 1,600ms, $0.040, 99.7% success with one retry. Re-compose: simulated end-to-end p95 becomes 3,584ms, plus the 120ms floor = <b>3,704ms. Over budget by 204ms.</b> Cost becomes $0.097/task at p95 — <b>over the $0.08 ceiling by $0.017</b>, which at 4,000/day is $2,040/month of overrun, and lifts total spend from $6,840 to $11,640, a 70% increase. Calls per task go from 4.01 to 5.01.<br><br><b>That arithmetic took four minutes and it killed the proposal before the PR existed.</b> Not because verification is a bad idea — because it costs 204ms and $4,800/month more than the promise allows, and now the conversation is the right one: which envelope do we renegotiate, or which stage do we cut to fund it? Drop rerank (300ms, $0.004) and verification nearly fits. That is a real design conversation. \"Let''s add a verification pass\" was not.<br><br><b>The counter-example worth knowing.</b> Take a pipeline of two thin stages (26ms and 20ms p95) plus generation at 2,100ms. Naive sum 2,146ms, simulated 2,133ms — 0.6% apart. When one stage owns essentially all the variance, summing p95s is fine. The rule of thumb: if the widest stage''s spread exceeds the root-sum-square of every other spread, sum away and spend the saved effort elsewhere.","good":["Budgets allocated top-down from the SLO, with fixed floors subtracted before anything else","End-to-end p95 composed or simulated, never summed — and the composition method written down","Availability quoted as the product across stages, and re-quoted every time a box is added","Cost tracked per task at p95 with retries and paid failures included, plus calls-per-task alongside","A correctness error budget with the same standing as availability: a threshold, an owner, and an alert"],"build":"Build the budget table for one live feature, from real traces rather than estimates. For each stage: measured p50 and p95, actual cost per task including retries and the failures you paid for, and measured success rate. Compose them — multiplicatively for reliability, root-sum-square or simulated for latency, with the retry tail for cost. Then put the composed numbers next to the SLO you have been claiming in reviews. Most teams discover one of two things: that they have far more latency headroom than the naive sum suggested, or that their composed availability has a digit fewer nines than anyone has been saying out loud. Finish with one sentence, written down and circulated: \"if this budget halved tomorrow, the first stage we would cut is ___, because ___.\" Deciding that in calm conditions is worth more than the table.","res":[["Google SRE Book: Embracing Risk","https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/","Use right now: the error-budget model this module borrows and then splits in two — read it, then ask what your correctness budget would be."],["Google SRE Workbook: Alerting on SLOs","https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/","Use when a budget exists but nothing pages — burn-rate alerting is what turns a number in a doc into an operational control."],["Log-normal distribution","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Log-normal_distribution","Use when you need to justify the composition math to a skeptic — it''s why a p50/p95 pair is enough to model a stage and why summing p95s over-states the tail."],["Little''s Law","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little%27s_law","Use when the latency budget meets the volume field — it converts arrival rate and service time into the concurrency you must provision for at peak."]]},"depth":{"mech":"<b>A budget is an allocation, and allocation runs downward.</b> You begin with the SLO, because that is the only number that came from outside the engineering team and therefore the only one you cannot quietly edit. Everything else is derived from it by subtraction.<br><br><b>First subtract the floors.</b> Three costs are not available for clever design: the network round trip, the index or datastore lookup, and the time the model spends actually emitting output tokens. That last one is the one people forget, and it dominates. Generation time scales with output length, so \"make the answer more thorough\" is a latency decision wearing a quality costume. On a typical interactive surface these floors consume 40-60% of the budget before you have designed anything. What remains after the subtraction is your entire allowance for cleverness — for reranking, verification, routing, rails, and every other box you would like to draw. Architects who skip this step spend money they never had and discover it in staging.<br><br><b>Then compose, and compose correctly, because the three envelopes compose by three different rules.</b><br><br><b>Reliability multiplies.</b> Five stages at 99.5% each is 0.995^5 = 97.5% end to end. Eight stages at 99.9% is 99.2%. This is the arithmetic that makes box count a first-class design constraint rather than an aesthetic preference, and it is almost never done out loud in a review, where people quote the per-stage number because it sounds better. Retries buy availability back — one retry on a 99.5% stage takes it to 99.9975% for that stage — but retries cost latency and money, and they buy back only availability. Nothing buys back correctness.<br><br><b>Cost adds, with a tail.</b> Sum the per-stage costs, multiply each by its expected attempt count, and include the stages you pay for on requests that ultimately fail. Then look at the p95, not the mean, because retries and variable output length give cost a genuinely fat right tail. A mean of $0.057 sitting under a p95 of $0.097 means one task in twenty costs 70% over model. At 120,000 tasks a month that gap is real money, and it is the gap that shows up as \"the bill was higher than the forecast.\"<br><br><b>Latency composes serially or in parallel, and you may not sum p95s.</b> This is the one worth slowing down for. Serial stages add their <b>distributions</b>, not their percentiles. Summing p95s assumes every stage has its worst day on the same request, which is exactly as likely as it sounds. Model each stage from its measured p50/p95 pair and combine the spreads by root-sum-square: end-to-end p95 ≈ sum of p50s + sqrt(sum of squared p50-to-p95 gaps). In the worked example that''s 2,467ms where naive summing said 2,860ms — a 14% over-statement. Parallel stages contribute the maximum of the group, not the sum, which is why fan-out costs money rather than time: two 300ms stages run concurrently cost two stages'' worth of cents and roughly one stage''s worth of milliseconds. And know when the shortcut is fine: when one stage owns nearly all the variance — generation usually does — naive summing lands within a percent or two, and you should take the shortcut and spend the effort somewhere it matters.<br><br><b>Now the part classic systems don''t need: two reliability budgets.</b> <b>Availability</b> asks did the system answer. <b>Correctness</b> asks was the answer right. A conventional service only has the first, so the whole SRE apparatus — error budgets, burn-rate alerts, freeze policies — attaches to it by default and nothing attaches to the second. That is the single clearest reason quality regressions in AI systems drift for weeks: the availability dashboard is green, the pager is silent, and the product is wrong. Give correctness the same machinery. A 92% quality bar is an 8% correctness error budget — at 4,000 tasks/day, 320 wrong answers a day is the budget you agreed to spend. Set a threshold, name an owner, alert on burn rate, and treat exhausting it exactly as you would treat exhausting an availability budget. Note also that the two multiply into what the user experiences: 99.9% available and 92% correct is 91.9% of requests that actually worked.<br><br><b>Price and quota anchors — as of August 2026.</b> This is the one dated table in the course. Refresh it against your provider''s current rate card and nothing else in these twelve modules changes, because every other module and every calculator takes prices as parameters. These are planning ratios, not quotes: <b>output tokens cost roughly 3-5× input tokens</b>, so verbosity is a cost lever before it is a quality one. <b>A frontier tier costs roughly 10-20× a small fast tier per token</b>, which is what makes routing worth building. <b>Caching a stable prompt prefix returns up to ~90% of that prefix''s input cost</b>, which is why long fixed context is cheaper than it looks and long variable context is not. <b>Asynchronous batch processing runs around 50% of synchronous pricing</b>, the single easiest discount available and the reason interaction mode belongs on the spec card. <b>Reranking a candidate set costs one to two orders of magnitude less than generating over it</b>, and <b>embedding a document costs about two orders of magnitude less than generating over it</b> — which is why retrieval read paths are cheap and agent loops are not. Generation throughput in the tens of output tokens per second means time-to-last-token is roughly linear in answer length. Every one of those is a ratio, and ratios rot far more slowly than prices.<br><br><b>The output is a table, and the table is the artifact.</b> Stage, p50, p95, cost, success rate, retries. Composed row at the bottom. SLO row beneath that. Headroom row beneath that, with PASS or FAIL per envelope. Anyone can read it, anyone can check it, and any proposal to add a box is now a subtraction that has to survive it.","trade":[["Retries to buy availability vs failing fast","Nines against latency and cost tail","Retries on a slow stage blow the p95 budget to protect an availability number nobody was missing"],["Fan-out in parallel vs running stages serially","Milliseconds against cents","Parallel work costs full price for every branch — you buy latency with money, and at high volume that trade inverts"],["Budgeting p95 cost vs mean cost","Forecast accuracy against modelling effort","Mean-cost budgets under-forecast by the size of the retry tail, which is exactly the gap finance notices"],["One combined reliability budget vs separate availability and correctness budgets","Operational simplicity against detecting quality drift","A single budget means the green dashboard keeps paging nobody while the answers get worse"]],"scale":["Peak concurrency comes from Little''s Law, not intuition: arrival rate × service time. At 4,000/day bursting 3× with a 2.6s task, that''s the number you provision and the number you negotiate quotas against","Rate limits are a fourth envelope in disguise — a per-minute token or request quota is a latency budget with a queue attached, and it binds first at peak, not at steady state","Re-derive the budget table at every 10× of volume; stages that were rounding errors at 4K/day become the binding constraint at 400K/day","Publish the composed table, not the stage numbers — teams optimise whatever number is on the dashboard, and stage p95 is the number that lets everyone pass while the product fails"],"sec":["Retries on non-idempotent stages don''t just cost money, they can duplicate side effects — budget retries only where the stage is safe to repeat","A correctness error budget is a security control on any surface with a costly blast radius: burning it should be able to disable the automated path, not just file a ticket","Timeouts are a containment mechanism, not a latency tweak — an unbounded stage converts a slow dependency into an availability incident across every caller","Cost budgets need per-tenant attribution, or one abusive tenant spends the whole month''s envelope and the alert fires against the aggregate long after"],"interview":"I treat every request as carrying three envelopes — milliseconds, cents and nines — and every stage debits all three. Budgets get allocated top-down from the SLO, never summed bottom-up from what we happened to build, because a bottom-up number is a description of the system and a description can''t fail. First I subtract the fixed floors: network, index lookup, and output-token generation time, which on an interactive surface is usually about half the budget. What''s left is the allowance for cleverness. Then I compose by the right rules: reliability multiplies, so five stages at 99.5% is 97.5% end-to-end and box count is a reliability decision; cost adds with a retry tail, so I budget p95 cost, not mean; and latency you compose or simulate, never sum, because stages don''t have their bad day simultaneously and summing p95s over-states the tail by fifteen to twenty-five percent — which quietly deletes designs you could have afforded. The AI-specific piece is that we need two reliability budgets, not one: availability, did it answer, and correctness, was it right. Conflating them is why quality regressions have no error budget and no pager. And the practical payoff is that \"let''s add a verification call\" becomes a subtraction anyone can check — in our case 204 milliseconds and $4,800 a month, which ended the debate before the PR existed."},"patterns":{"intro":"This is the most-shared artifact in the course, so it''s built to stand alone: paste it into a repo, feed it your measured stages, get a verdict. <code>Stage</code> holds the six numbers you can actually measure. <code>compose()</code> does serial and parallel latency composition, multiplicative reliability, and cost with a retry tail — and reports the naive p95 sum, the root-sum-square estimate, and a seeded simulation side by side so you can see the size of the error you''d have made. <code>headroom()</code> prints PASS/FAIL per envelope and names the single largest debit in each. The last block shows the case where summing p95s is perfectly fine, so you know when to skip the ceremony.","code":"\"\"\"THREE LEDGERS - compose latency, cost and reliability across a pipeline.\n\nStdlib only. Deterministic (seeded). No network. Run: python three_ledgers.py\n\nEvery stage debits three envelopes: milliseconds, cents, nines. This composes\nthem properly - serial vs parallel latency, multiplicative reliability, cost\nwith a retry tail - and tells you which envelope is about to blow.\n\nPRICES ARE INPUTS. Stage costs come from your own rate card and your own\ntraces; nothing in this file assumes what a call costs.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\nimport random\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\nZ95 = 1.6448536269514722  # standard normal 95th percentile\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Stage:\n    \"\"\"One box in the diagram, measured - not estimated, not hoped.\"\"\"\n    name: str\n    p50_ms: float\n    p95_ms: float\n    cost_usd: float\n    success_rate: float = 1.0      # per attempt\n    retries: int = 0               # extra attempts allowed\n    parallel_group: Optional[str] = None  # same label = runs concurrently\n\n    @property\n    def attempts_max(self) -> int:\n        return self.retries + 1\n\n    @property\n    def effective_success(self) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Retries buy availability, not correctness.\"\"\"\n        return 1.0 - (1.0 - self.success_rate) ** self.attempts_max\n\n    @property\n    def expected_attempts(self) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Sum_{k=1..n} P(still trying at attempt k).\"\"\"\n        f = 1.0 - self.success_rate\n        return sum(f ** k for k in range(self.attempts_max))\n\n    @property\n    def sigma(self) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Lognormal spread implied by the measured p50/p95 pair.\"\"\"\n        if self.p50_ms <= 0 or self.p95_ms <= self.p50_ms:\n            return 0.0\n        return math.log(self.p95_ms / self.p50_ms) / Z95\n\n\ndef _plan(stages: List[Stage]) -> List[List[Stage]]:\n    \"\"\"Order-preserving execution plan: each step is one stage, or a fan-out.\"\"\"\n    steps: List[List[Stage]] = []\n    seen: Dict[str, int] = {}\n    for s in stages:\n        if s.parallel_group is None:\n            steps.append([s])\n        elif s.parallel_group in seen:\n            steps[seen[s.parallel_group]].append(s)\n        else:\n            seen[s.parallel_group] = len(steps)\n            steps.append([s])\n    return steps\n\n\ndef _pct(xs: List[float], q: float) -> float:\n    xs = sorted(xs)\n    if not xs:\n        return 0.0\n    i = min(len(xs) - 1, max(0, int(round(q * (len(xs) - 1)))))\n    return xs[i]\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Composed:\n    p50_ms: float\n    p95_naive_ms: float     # sum of stage p95s - the wrong number everyone quotes\n    p95_rss_ms: float       # root-sum-square of spreads - the right closed form\n    p95_sim_ms: float       # simulated ground truth\n    availability: float\n    cost_mean_usd: float\n    cost_p95_usd: float\n    calls_per_task: float\n    steps: List[List[Stage]]\n\n\ndef compose(stages: List[Stage], trials: int = 40000, seed: int = 7) -> Composed:\n    steps = _plan(stages)\n\n    # --- closed forms -------------------------------------------------\n    p50 = sum(max(s.p50_ms for s in step) for step in steps)\n    p95_naive = sum(max(s.p95_ms for s in step) for step in steps)\n    spread_sq = sum(max(s.p95_ms - s.p50_ms for s in step) ** 2 for step in steps)\n    p95_rss = p50 + math.sqrt(spread_sq)\n\n    availability = 1.0\n    for s in stages:\n        availability *= s.effective_success\n\n    cost_mean = sum(s.cost_usd * s.expected_attempts for s in stages)\n    calls = sum(s.expected_attempts for s in stages)\n\n    # --- simulation ---------------------------------------------------\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    lat_ok: List[float] = []\n    costs: List[float] = []\n    for _ in range(trials):\n        total = 0.0\n        spend = 0.0\n        ok = True\n        for step in steps:\n            branch = 0.0\n            for s in step:\n                elapsed = 0.0\n                done = False\n                for _attempt in range(s.attempts_max):\n                    spend += s.cost_usd\n                    elapsed += s.p50_ms * math.exp(s.sigma * rng.gauss(0.0, 1.0))\n                    if rng.random() < s.success_rate:\n                        done = True\n                        break\n                if not done:\n                    ok = False\n                branch = max(branch, elapsed)\n            total += branch\n        costs.append(spend)\n        if ok:\n            lat_ok.append(total)\n\n    return Composed(\n        p50_ms=p50,\n        p95_naive_ms=p95_naive,\n        p95_rss_ms=p95_rss,\n        p95_sim_ms=_pct(lat_ok, 0.95),\n        availability=availability,\n        cost_mean_usd=cost_mean,\n        cost_p95_usd=_pct(costs, 0.95),\n        calls_per_task=calls,\n        steps=steps,\n    )\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Budget:\n    p95_ms: float\n    cost_usd: float          # per TASK, not per call\n    availability: float\n\n\ndef headroom(budget: Budget, c: Composed, stages: List[Stage]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"PASS/FAIL per envelope, plus the single largest debit in each.\"\"\"\n    print(\"  %-14s %12s %12s %10s   %s\" % (\"envelope\", \"budget\", \"composed\", \"headroom\", \"verdict\"))\n    rows = (\n        (\"latency p95\", budget.p95_ms, c.p95_sim_ms, \"%.0fms\", budget.p95_ms - c.p95_sim_ms),\n        (\"cost/task p95\", budget.cost_usd, c.cost_p95_usd, \"$%.4f\", budget.cost_usd - c.cost_p95_usd),\n        (\"availability\", budget.availability, c.availability, \"%.4f\", c.availability - budget.availability),\n    )\n    ok = True\n    for label, b, actual, fmt, slack in rows:\n        passed = slack >= 0\n        ok = ok and passed\n        print(\"  %-14s %12s %12s %10s   %s\"\n              % (label, fmt % b, fmt % actual, fmt % slack, \"PASS\" if passed else \"FAIL\"))\n\n    lat_hog = max(stages, key=lambda s: s.p95_ms)\n    cost_hog = max(stages, key=lambda s: s.cost_usd * s.expected_attempts)\n    rel_hog = min(stages, key=lambda s: s.effective_success)\n    print(\"  largest debits -> ms: %s (%.0fms p95) | $: %s ($%.4f) | nines: %s (%.4f)\"\n          % (lat_hog.name, lat_hog.p95_ms, cost_hog.name,\n             cost_hog.cost_usd * cost_hog.expected_attempts,\n             rel_hog.name, rel_hog.effective_success))\n    print(\"  calls per task: %.2f  (unit economics close on the TASK, never the call)\"\n          % c.calls_per_task)\n    return ok\n\n\ndef show(label: str, stages: List[Stage], budget: Budget) -> Composed:\n    print(\"\\n%s\" % label)\n    c = compose(stages)\n    print(\"  p95 estimates: naive sum %.0fms | root-sum-square %.0fms | simulated %.0fms\"\n          % (c.p95_naive_ms, c.p95_rss_ms, c.p95_sim_ms))\n    print(\"  naive sum over-states the real p95 by %.0fms (%.0f%%) - stages do not\"\n          % (c.p95_naive_ms - c.p95_sim_ms,\n             100.0 * (c.p95_naive_ms - c.p95_sim_ms) / max(c.p95_sim_ms, 1.0)))\n    print(\"  have their bad day simultaneously. You may not sum p95s.\")\n    headroom(budget, c, stages)\n    return c\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    # Rate card: YOUR numbers, from YOUR traces. Parameters, not facts.\n    SLO = Budget(p95_ms=3500, cost_usd=0.08, availability=0.99)\n\n    base = [\n        Stage(\"retrieve\",  p50_ms=110, p95_ms=260,  cost_usd=0.0010, success_rate=0.999, retries=1),\n        Stage(\"rerank\",    p50_ms=140, p95_ms=300,  cost_usd=0.0040, success_rate=0.998, retries=1),\n        Stage(\"generate\",  p50_ms=980, p95_ms=2100, cost_usd=0.0500, success_rate=0.995, retries=1),\n        Stage(\"rails\",     p50_ms=90,  p95_ms=200,  cost_usd=0.0020, success_rate=0.999, retries=0),\n    ]\n    print(\"THREE LEDGERS - support deflection\")\n    print(\"SLO: p95 %.0fms, $%.2f/task, %.1f%% available\"\n          % (SLO.p95_ms, SLO.cost_usd, SLO.availability * 100))\n    show(\"A. As designed (serial)\", base, SLO)\n\n    # \"Just one more verification call.\" Run the arithmetic before the PR exists.\n    plus = base + [Stage(\"verify\", p50_ms=760, p95_ms=1600, cost_usd=0.0400,\n                         success_rate=0.997, retries=1)]\n    show(\"B. Plus ''just one more verification call''\", plus, SLO)\n\n    # Same work, fanned out: rerank and rails-precheck run concurrently.\n    par = [\n        Stage(\"retrieve\", p50_ms=110, p95_ms=260, cost_usd=0.0010, success_rate=0.999, retries=1),\n        Stage(\"rerank\",   p50_ms=140, p95_ms=300, cost_usd=0.0040, success_rate=0.998,\n              retries=1, parallel_group=\"prep\"),\n        Stage(\"precheck\", p50_ms=120, p95_ms=280, cost_usd=0.0015, success_rate=0.999,\n              retries=1, parallel_group=\"prep\"),\n        Stage(\"generate\", p50_ms=980, p95_ms=2100, cost_usd=0.0500, success_rate=0.995, retries=1),\n        Stage(\"rails\",    p50_ms=90,  p95_ms=200,  cost_usd=0.0020, success_rate=0.999, retries=0),\n    ]\n    show(\"C. Fan-out: rerank || precheck (parallel costs money, not time)\", par, SLO)\n\n    # When IS summing p95s fine? When one stage owns essentially all the variance.\n    dominated = [\n        Stage(\"thin_a\",   p50_ms=20,  p95_ms=26,   cost_usd=0.0002),\n        Stage(\"thin_b\",   p50_ms=15,  p95_ms=20,   cost_usd=0.0002),\n        Stage(\"generate\", p50_ms=980, p95_ms=2100, cost_usd=0.0500),\n    ]\n    d = compose(dominated)\n    print(\"\\nD. When summing p95s IS fine\")\n    print(\"  naive %.0fms vs simulated %.0fms - only %.1f%% apart, because one stage\"\n          % (d.p95_naive_ms, d.p95_sim_ms,\n             100.0 * abs(d.p95_naive_ms - d.p95_sim_ms) / d.p95_sim_ms))\n    print(\"  owns nearly all the variance. Rule of thumb: if the widest stage''s\")\n    print(\"  spread exceeds the root-sum-square of every other spread, sum away.\")\n\n    # Reliability is the ledger that punishes box-count directly.\n    print(\"\\nE. Reliability composes multiplicatively (the tax on adding boxes)\")\n    for n in (3, 5, 8):\n        for per in (0.995, 0.999):\n            e2e = per ** n\n            print(\"  %d stages at %.3f -> %.4f end-to-end  (%.2f%% of requests fail)\"\n                  % (n, per, e2e, 100.0 * (1 - e2e)))\n    print(\"  Retries buy AVAILABILITY back. Nothing buys CORRECTNESS back - that is\")\n    print(\"  a second, separate budget, and it is the one with no error budget yet.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","notes":["The simulation is seeded, so two engineers running it on the same table get the same verdict. A budget tool that returns different answers to different people doesn''t end arguments, it starts them.","Each stage is modelled log-normally from its measured p50/p95 pair — that pair is enough to pin the distribution, which is why those are the two numbers worth pulling from traces rather than a full histogram.","Latency percentiles are computed over successful requests only, matching how your monitoring almost certainly reports them. Failed requests are still charged in the cost ledger, because you paid for them.","Case C is the one to show a team that wants to parallelise: fan-out added a whole stage''s cost and about 35ms of latency. Parallelism buys milliseconds with money, and at high volume that trade inverts.","<code>calls_per_task</code> is deliberately printed next to cost. It''s the number that turns \"about two cents a call\" into \"5.7 cents a task\" — the same system described honestly."],"debug":[["Composed p95 far below what production reports","Stages are correlated — shared queue, shared pool, shared throttle","Independence is the model''s assumption; measure end-to-end directly and use composition only for allocation"],["Cost p95 barely differs from the mean","Retries aren''t modelled, or success rates were entered as ideals","Pull real success rates from traces, and charge every attempt, including those on tasks that ultimately failed"],["Availability looks great, users complain constantly","Only the availability budget exists; correctness has no budget","Add a correctness threshold with an owner and burn-rate alerting, and track it beside availability"],["Budget passes in staging, fails in production at peak","Rate limits and queueing aren''t in the model","Add the queue wait as its own stage with its own p95, derived from arrival rate via Little''s Law"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:budgets', 'public', '[{"q":"A pipeline has five stages, each measured at 99.5% availability. What do you quote in the design review?","o":["99.5%, since every stage independently meets the reliability target set","97.5%, the product — reliability composes multiplicatively across stages","99.9%, since retries at each stage recover almost all transient failures","98.8%, the mean availability weighted by each stage''s latency contribution"],"a":1,"exp":"0.995^5 = 0.9752. Per-stage numbers sound better and are meaningless to a user, who experiences the product of the chain. This arithmetic is what makes box count a reliability decision rather than an aesthetic one."},{"q":"Stage p95s are 260, 300, 2100 and 200ms. Your end-to-end p95 is:","o":["2,860ms — the sum, which is the conservative and therefore safe estimate","2,100ms — set by the slowest stage, since the others overlap with it","Around 2,500ms — composed, because stages don''t peak on the same request","Unknowable without a full latency histogram captured for every single stage"],"a":2,"exp":"Summing p95s assumes every stage has its worst day simultaneously. Composing the spreads by root-sum-square gives 2,467ms and simulation gives 2,520ms. The naive sum over-states by 14% — conservative, but it deletes design options you could have afforded."},{"q":"Why does \"budgets are allocated top-down from the SLO\" matter more than it sounds?","o":["Bottom-up totals describe the system you built, and a description cannot fail","Top-down allocation is faster to compute than summing every stage''s measurement","Bottom-up budgets tend to overstate cost because retries get counted repeatedly","Top-down allocation is required before you can negotiate provider rate limits"],"a":0,"exp":"A number summed from what you already built will always be satisfied by what you already built. Only a number that came from outside engineering — the promise — can fail, and only a budget that can fail constrains a design."},{"q":"On an interactive surface, which cost is a fixed floor rather than an allocation you control?","o":["The reranking pass, which can be tuned or dropped from the read path","The output rails, which can be moved off the critical path asynchronously","The time the model spends emitting output tokens, set by answer length","The retry policy, which trades latency against availability at every stage"],"a":2,"exp":"Generation time scales with output length, so \"make answers more thorough\" is a latency decision wearing a quality costume. Floors get subtracted first; what remains is the entire allowance for cleverness. Rerank, rails and retries are all things you chose."},{"q":"Mean cost per task is $0.057; p95 is $0.097. What should the budget be built on?","o":["The mean, since averages are what monthly invoices ultimately reflect anyway","The p95, since retries and output-length variance give cost a fat right tail","The median, which is robust to the outliers that distort both other figures","The maximum observed, since that bounds the worst case you could ever face"],"a":1,"exp":"A p95 70% above the mean means one task in twenty costs far more than you modelled. At scale that tail is the gap between a forecast and a surprise invoice, and it''s driven by exactly the things you can''t schedule: retries and long answers."},{"q":"Two 300ms stages are moved from serial to parallel execution. What actually changes?","o":["Latency and cost both drop, since the stages now share a single request path","Latency drops by roughly one stage; cost stays at two stages'' worth of spend","Cost drops because the second stage reuses the context the first already built","Nothing measurable changes until the stages are also given independent retries"],"a":1,"exp":"A parallel group contributes the maximum of its members to latency, but every branch is fully paid for. Fan-out buys milliseconds with money. At high volume that trade often inverts and you should serialise again."},{"q":"Availability is 99.9% and quality is 92%. What share of requests actually worked for the user?","o":["99.9%, since quality issues are separate from the availability commitment","92%, since the quality bar is strictly the tighter of the two constraints","91.9%, the product — the request must both complete and be right","95.9%, the mean of the two, since each failure mode affects a distinct set"],"a":2,"exp":"0.999 × 0.92 = 0.919. Answering and being right are two independent ways to fail, and users experience both. This is why AI systems need two reliability budgets where classic systems need one."},{"q":"A team''s availability dashboard is green while answer quality has been drifting worse for six weeks. The structural cause:","o":["Sampling rates are too low for the eval pipeline to detect the drift in time","The golden set has aged and no longer represents current production traffic","There is no correctness error budget, so nothing burns and nothing pages","Alert thresholds were set against staging traffic rather than production data"],"a":2,"exp":"All four are real problems, but only one is structural. The whole SRE apparatus — budgets, burn-rate alerts, freeze policies — attaches to availability by default and to nothing else. Give correctness a threshold, an owner, and a pager, and the other three become findable."},{"q":"Someone proposes adding a verification call: +1,600ms p95, +$0.040. Budget is 3,500ms and $0.08/task. The right response:","o":["Prototype it and measure the actual quality lift before deciding anything","Approve it, since verification improves correctness and correctness leads","Re-compose: it lands at 3,704ms and $0.097, so name what gets cut to fund it","Reject it outright, since the latency budget is a hard external commitment"],"a":2,"exp":"The arithmetic takes four minutes and reframes the debate. It''s not \"is verification good\" — it''s 204ms and $4,800/month over the promise, so either an envelope gets renegotiated or a stage gets cut. Dropping rerank nearly funds it. That''s a real conversation."},{"q":"When is simply summing stage p95s a perfectly acceptable approximation?","o":["When every stage in the pipeline has roughly the same measured p95 value","When the pipeline has three or fewer stages, keeping compounding error small","Never — summing percentiles is invalid regardless of the distributions involved","When one stage''s spread exceeds the root-sum-square of all the other spreads"],"a":3,"exp":"If generation owns nearly all the variance, the naive sum lands within a percent or two of the truth — 2,146ms versus 2,133ms in the worked case. Knowing when the shortcut is safe is as valuable as knowing why it''s usually wrong; spend the saved effort elsewhere."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:shapes', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"shapes","block":"b1","title":"System Shapes: Picking the Architecture","tag":"Six reference shapes, and the spec-card fact that picks between them","why":"There are not a hundred AI architectures. There are about six, and the interesting question was never which one is best — it''s which one your spec card lets you get away with. Most production disappointment traces back to a team that climbed two rungs higher than they needed for a reason nobody wrote down, and now can''t tell whether the reason still holds.","mental":"Six rungs on a <b>shape ladder</b>, each roughly an order of magnitude more expensive to <b>operate</b> than the last: single call → prompt chain → retrieval read path → single-agent tool loop → orchestrator-worker → offline batch pipeline. You climb only when a specific number on the spec card forces you to, <b>and you write down which number it was</b>. That sentence is the whole module.","concepts":[["The lowest feasible rung","Your job is not the most capable architecture, it''s the least capable one that clears the card. Capability you don''t need still costs you: operate burden, failure surface, observability spend, and the review time of everyone who inherits it."],["Forcing constraint","The specific spec-card number that made the rung below infeasible, recorded next to the decision. Without it, the choice can never be re-litigated — and constraints change constantly, so an architecture with no recorded forcing constraint is one nobody is allowed to simplify."],["Operate cost, not build cost","The ladder is ordered by what a shape costs to own, not what it costs to write. A batch pipeline is the cheapest rung per task and the most expensive to run — backfills, reprocessing, freshness monitoring, and a schedule that pages someone."],["Failure modes introduced","Each rung adds new ways to be wrong, not just more of the old ones. Climbing is a trade of one failure profile for another, and the profile you''re taking on should appear in the decision record beside the capability you''re buying."],["Degradation behaviour","What the shape does when it cannot do its job. This is a selection criterion, not an afterthought: a shape whose degraded mode is \"nothing\" is a different product on its worst day than one that falls back to keyword results or yesterday''s run."],["Composite with contracts","Most real systems are three shapes, not one — commonly a batch pipeline feeding a read path with an escape hatch behind it. Draw them as separate shapes with an explicit contract on each seam, because the seams are where you''ll be debugging."],["The escape hatch is a budget line","The expensive rung you keep for the hard 3% is priced by how often it fires. If that share drifts from 3% to 25%, blended cost per task nearly doubles — so the hatch needs a rate cap and a dashboard, not just a condition."]],"mistakes":["Climbing a rung for capability nobody on the card asked for, then owning it for three years","Choosing a shape from a conference talk rather than from the binding number on your own card","Recording the decision but not the forcing constraint, so nobody can ever safely simplify it","Drawing a composite system as one blob, hiding the seams that will produce most incidents","Ignoring operate cost because build cost was comparable — the headcount arrives later, quietly"],"flags":["\"We went with agents because it''s more flexible\" — flexibility is not a spec-card field","\"Nobody remembers why it''s built this way\" — the forcing constraint was never written down","\"It''s one system\" — said about something with three distinct shapes and no named contracts","\"We''ll simplify it later\" — no rung below has ever been re-tested against the current card","\"The fallback path handles that\" — nobody has measured what share of traffic the fallback takes"],"ask":["Which number on the spec card made the rung below this one infeasible?","Is that number still true, and when did anyone last check?","What does this shape do on its worst day — what exactly does it degrade to?","How many people does it take to operate this, steady state, including the on-call?","What share of traffic takes the expensive path, and what''s the cap when that drifts?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"<b>The card:</b> internal policy answers. Quality bar 0.93, p95 3,500ms, $0.08/task, 4,000 tasks/day at 3× peak, blast radius recoverable, corpus unbounded, step count fixed. Measured baseline: one model call costs $0.012 and lands at 1,400ms p95. Now walk the ladder from the bottom and stop at the first rung that clears.<br><br><b>Rung 1, single call — $0.0120, 1,430ms.</b> Cheapest and fastest by a distance. <b>Rejected:</b> the corpus is unbounded, and a single call cannot ground on a corpus it can''t fit. Note that it wasn''t cost or latency that killed it — those were comfortable. It was a capability fact.<br><br><b>Rung 2, prompt chain — $0.0312, 3,420ms.</b> More steps, same problem. <b>Rejected</b> on the same forcing constraint: chaining calls doesn''t make an unbounded corpus fit.<br><br><b>Rung 3, retrieval read path — $0.0228, 2,320ms.</b> Handles the unbounded corpus. Cost is 29% of the ceiling, latency 66% of the budget. Monthly: 0.0228 × 4,000 × 30 = $2,736, and at 3× peak $8,208 — provision for the second number. <b>Feasible. Stop here.</b> Cheaper than rung 2, incidentally, which is a reminder that the ladder is ordered by operate cost, not unit cost.<br><br>For completeness, what''s above: <b>rung 4, tool loop — $0.0780, 7,680ms</b>, rejected because the p95 budget of 3,500ms is under its 7,680ms floor. <b>Rung 5, orchestrator-worker — $0.1680, 4,990ms</b>, rejected on both cost and latency. <b>Rung 6, batch — $0.0060</b>, cheapest per task on the whole ladder, rejected because the mode is interactive.<br><br><b>The artifact is not \"we chose a retrieval read path.\"</b> It''s: <b>rungs 1 and 2 are unavailable because the corpus is unbounded.</b> That sentence is a re-litigation trigger. The day someone scopes the corpus down to 40 documents, rung 1 becomes available and the system should get simpler. Without the sentence, that day never comes.<br><br><b>Now change one number: volume ×20, to 80,000/day.</b> Nothing about the problem changed — same corpus, same users, same quality bar. Re-run: retrieval read path now costs 0.0228 × 80,000 × 30 = <b>$54,720/month against a $12,000 ceiling.</b> Rejected. Tool loop: $187,200/month, rejected. Orchestrator: rejected on per-task cost. <b>Every rung fails.</b> That''s not a bug in the ladder, it''s the card telling you a field has to move.<br><br><b>Which field?</b> Two candidates. Raise the ceiling — finance signs $30,000 — and the read path still fails at $54,720. Or change interaction mode: these are policy answers, and the same questions recur, so precompute them nightly. Mode becomes async, the p95 budget relaxes from 3,500ms to four hours, and <b>rung 6 opens: batch at $0.0060/task = $14,400/month, inside the $30,000 ceiling.</b> Peak ×3 is absorbed by the schedule window rather than by spend, which is precisely what buys the rung.<br><br><b>Read that movement carefully.</b> One number changed by 20×, and the answer moved from rung 3 to rung 6 — down the per-task cost ladder and up the operate ladder. You now own a pipeline: backfills, reprocessing, freshness monitoring, roughly 3 FTE of steady-state ownership against 0.8 for the read path. The batch pipeline is the cheapest shape per task and the most expensive shape to own, and both of those facts are load-bearing.<br><br><b>The real system is a composite.</b> In practice you''d run all three: batch precompute serving 55% of traffic at $0.0060, a read path serving 42% at $0.0228, and a tool loop as an escape hatch for the hard 3% at $0.0780. Blended: <b>$0.0152/task</b>. And price the hatch drifting, because it will — at 10% instead of 3% the blend is $0.0191 (1.3×); at 25% it''s $0.0274 (1.8×). That''s why the escape hatch gets a rate cap and a dashboard, not just a trigger condition. Three shapes, two contracts: batch→read is a versioned index snapshot; read→hatch is an explicit low-confidence signal with a cap on it.","good":["The chosen rung is the lowest one that clears the current card, and someone can say so out loud","Every climb has a written forcing constraint naming the exact card field that forced it","Composite systems are drawn as separate shapes with a named contract on every seam","Each shape''s degraded mode is specified — what it serves on its worst day, not just its best","Operate cost is estimated in people, not just dollars, before the shape is committed to"],"build":"Draw your current system as shapes-with-contracts on one page. Hard limit: seven boxes. If it doesn''t fit, you''ve drawn implementation rather than architecture — zoom out until each box is one of the six shapes or a datastore. Then, for every box, write the forcing constraint that justifies it: the spec-card number that made the cheaper alternative infeasible. Do this honestly; \"we already had it\" is not a forcing constraint, it''s a fact about history. Any box that ends up without one is a deletion candidate. Take that list of candidates to your team and defend it. You will get one of two outcomes, and both are wins: someone supplies the missing constraint and it goes into the doc where it should have been, or nobody can, and you''ve found real complexity to remove. Do the same exercise for the seams — for each contract, write what the consumer is entitled to assume and what happens when that assumption breaks.","res":[["Google SRE Book: Simplicity","https://sre.google/sre-book/simplicity/","Use when defending the lowest feasible rung against a team that wants the more impressive one — it''s the canonical argument for boring, and it''s written by people who ran the hard version."],["Architecture Decision Records","https://adr.github.io/","Use right now: the forcing constraint needs a permanent home next to the decision, and this is the lightest format that survives team turnover."],["Strangler Fig Application","https://martinfowler.com/bliki/StranglerFigApplication.html","Use when a composite means migrating off an existing blob rather than greenfielding — the pattern for adding shapes at the seams without a rewrite."],["Amdahl''s Law","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amdahl%27s_law","Use when someone proposes an orchestrator-worker shape for latency reasons — it tells you the ceiling on what parallelising the parallelisable part can actually buy."]]},"depth":{"mech":"<b>The ladder has six rungs and they are ordered by operate cost.</b> Not by capability, not by fashion, and specifically not by per-task price — a batch pipeline is the cheapest rung to run a task through and the most expensive one to own, and if you order by unit cost you will make exactly the wrong decision. What follows is a profile per shape. How each of these works internally is the Mastery course''s job; here you get one clause and then the facts you allocate against.<br><br><b>1. Single call.</b> One request, one response. <b>Latency shape:</b> one call, essentially all of it generation, so the p95 is your model''s p95. <b>Cost:</b> 1× baseline. <b>Failure modes introduced:</b> none you didn''t already have. <b>Observability:</b> 1× — one span, one cost line. <b>Operate:</b> ~0.1 FTE. <b>Degrades to:</b> a canned response. Realistic quality ceiling around 0.88 on non-trivial tasks, because there''s no seam to check anything.<br><br><b>2. Prompt chain / workflow.</b> A fixed sequence of calls with deterministic control flow. <b>Latency:</b> serial, roughly 2.4× a single call. <b>Cost:</b> ~2.6×. <b>Introduces:</b> partial-completion states and inter-step format drift. <b>Observability:</b> 2× — you now need per-step traces or debugging is guesswork. <b>Operate:</b> ~0.3 FTE. <b>Degrades to:</b> the last good step''s output, which is genuinely useful. Quality ceiling ~0.93, because a fixed chain gives you somewhere to put a check.<br><br><b>3. Retrieval read path.</b> A lookup feeding a call. <b>Latency:</b> index hop plus generation, ~1.5× plus a fixed 200-250ms floor. <b>Cost:</b> ~1.9× — cheaper than a chain, note. <b>Introduces:</b> staleness, permission drift between index and source, and a whole second system that can be down. <b>Observability:</b> 2.5× — you''re now debugging two subsystems and the join between them. <b>Operate:</b> ~0.8 FTE, most of it index freshness. <b>Degrades to:</b> keyword results with no synthesis, which is a real product rather than an error page. Quality ceiling ~0.94.<br><br><b>4. Single-agent tool loop.</b> A call that decides its own next step until it stops. <b>Latency:</b> the killer — an unbounded step count means a p95 driven by the tail of the step distribution, ~5× and highly variable. <b>Cost:</b> ~6.5×, also variable. <b>Introduces:</b> non-determinism in the execution path itself, which means two identical requests can cost and take wildly different amounts. <b>Observability:</b> 6× — every step needs a span or you cannot answer \"why did this one take nine seconds.\" <b>Operate:</b> ~1.5 FTE. <b>Degrades to:</b> hand off to a human with the trace attached. Quality ceiling ~0.95.<br><br><b>5. Orchestrator-worker.</b> Independent subtasks fanned out and rejoined. <b>Latency:</b> better than a loop for the same work — parallel, so ~3× — but with a fixed coordination floor around 650ms. <b>Cost:</b> ~14×, the steepest jump on the ladder, because every branch is paid in full. <b>Introduces:</b> partial-failure semantics, result-merge conflicts, and a coordination layer that is itself a system. <b>Observability:</b> 9×. <b>Operate:</b> ~2.5 FTE. <b>Degrades to:</b> partial results from the workers that finished. Quality ceiling ~0.96.<br><br><b>6. Offline batch pipeline.</b> Work done ahead of time, served from storage. <b>Latency:</b> not a request-path shape at all — hours. <b>Cost:</b> ~0.5×, the cheapest on the ladder, because you can use async pricing and spend more compute per item. <b>Introduces:</b> staleness as a permanent condition, backfill complexity, and reprocessing whenever anything upstream changes. <b>Observability:</b> 10× — pipeline health, freshness lag, per-run diffs. <b>Operate:</b> ~3 FTE and a schedule that pages someone. <b>Degrades to:</b> yesterday''s run, which is often perfectly fine. Quality ceiling ~0.97, because offline you can afford the compute nobody would pay for in a request path.<br><br><b>The forcing-function catalogue</b> is how you traverse it. Each of these is a capability fact that no amount of budget fixes, and each forces exactly one climb. <b>Unbounded corpus → retrieval.</b> If the knowledge doesn''t fit and won''t fit, no chain of calls solves it. <b>Unknown step count → tool loop.</b> If you genuinely cannot enumerate the steps in advance, a fixed chain will be wrong on the cases that matter. Be strict with yourself here: \"we haven''t enumerated the steps\" and \"the steps cannot be enumerated\" are different claims, and the first one is usually what''s true. <b>Independent subtasks under context pressure → orchestrator-worker.</b> Both halves are required; independent subtasks that fit comfortably do not justify a coordination layer. <b>Non-interactive plus volume → batch.</b> When the mode field says async and the volume field is large, batch is not a compromise, it''s the correct answer and the cheapest one.<br><br><b>Composites are the normal case, and the seams are the architecture.</b> A production system is typically a batch pipeline feeding a read path with an escape hatch behind it. Draw that as three shapes and two contracts, never as one blob, because the blob hides exactly the things that will page you. Each contract needs a written answer to three questions: what the consumer is entitled to assume, how it''s versioned, and what happens when the assumption breaks. Batch→read is usually a versioned index snapshot with an explicit freshness guarantee. Read→hatch is usually a low-confidence signal with a rate cap — and the cap matters, because the escape hatch is a budget line whose cost is set by how often it fires. At 3% of traffic a 6.5× shape is a rounding error; at 25% it has doubled your blended cost per task while everyone was looking at the dashboard for the cheap path.","trade":[["Lowest feasible rung vs one rung of headroom","Operate cost against future flexibility","Headroom you never use is permanent operate cost; no headroom is a rewrite when one card number moves"],["Composite of three shapes vs one general-purpose shape","Seam complexity against blended cost","One shape for everything means the hard 3% sets the cost and latency for the easy 97%"],["Batch precompute vs serving live","Per-task cost against staleness and operate burden","Batch is cheapest per task and most expensive to own — teams buy the unit price and inherit the pipeline"],["Tool loop vs orchestrator-worker for the same workload","Latency variance against coordination cost","A loop''s unbounded step count makes p95 unpredictable; an orchestrator makes it predictable and roughly doubles the bill"]],"scale":["Re-run the ladder at every 10× of volume — the same card selected rung 3 at 4K/day and rung 6 at 80K/day, and nothing about the problem changed","Per-task cost and operate cost move in opposite directions at the top of the ladder; at low volume operate cost dominates, at high volume unit cost does, and the crossover is worth computing explicitly","Escape-hatch share is the number to alert on — blended cost is roughly linear in it, and it drifts upward silently as inputs get harder","Each shape has a different scaling bottleneck: read paths hit index capacity, loops hit rate limits, orchestrators hit coordination, batch hits the schedule window"],"sec":["Each rung climbed widens the authorisation surface: a shape that can take actions needs an explicit permission boundary per action, sized before it''s built","Retrieval introduces permission drift between the index and the source of truth — the index must carry access decisions, not just content","Composite seams are trust boundaries: a contract between shapes needs validation on the consuming side, because the producing side will eventually be wrong","Degraded modes need their own threat model — falling back to a cheaper shape must not fall back to a weaker authorisation check"],"interview":"I think about AI architecture as a ladder of six shapes — single call, prompt chain, retrieval read path, tool loop, orchestrator-worker, offline batch — ordered by what they cost to operate, roughly an order of magnitude per rung. My job is the lowest rung that clears the spec card, plus a written forcing constraint naming which card number made the rung below infeasible. That second part is what most teams skip, and it''s why systems never get simpler: nobody can prove the original reason still holds, so nobody''s allowed to touch it. A concrete example: unbounded corpus killed rungs one and two, so we built a read path at two point three cents a task. Then volume went up twentyfold and every rung failed on the monthly ceiling — so we changed the interaction mode to async, which opened the batch rung at six tenths of a cent. Cheapest shape per task, most expensive to own, about three FTE versus zero point eight. Both facts matter. And in practice it ended up a composite: batch serving fifty-five percent, read path forty-two, an expensive escape hatch for the hard three percent, blended at a cent and a half. That hatch gets a rate cap, because at twenty-five percent instead of three it nearly doubles the blend."},"patterns":{"intro":"A shape-selection engine, deterministic, that consumes the same six-field spec card the requirements module defines. <code>SHAPES</code> is the profile table — cost multipliers, latency floors, capability flags, operate cost. <code>select()</code> returns the ranked feasible set and, for every rejection, names <b>the single binding constraint</b>, in the form you want in a design doc: <code>rejected: p95 budget 3500ms &lt; agent-loop floor 7680ms</code>. The demo runs one card down the ladder, changes volume by 20× and shows the answer move to a different rung, then prices a three-shape composite. The output you paste into the doc isn''t the winner — it''s the losers, each with the number that killed it.","code":"\"\"\"SHAPE LADDER - pick the lowest rung the spec card allows, and record why.\n\nStdlib only. Deterministic. No network. Run: python shape_ladder.py\n\nConsumes the same six-field SpecCard the requirements module defines (restated\nhere so this file stands alone). Costs are MULTIPLES of one baseline call,\nwhich you pass in - no price is baked into this file.\n\nThe output you paste into a design doc is not the winner. It is the losers,\neach with the constraint that killed it.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass SpecCard:\n    name: str\n    quality_bar: float\n    p95_latency_ms: float\n    interaction_mode: str          # interactive | async | batch\n    cost_per_task_usd: float\n    monthly_ceiling_usd: float\n    tasks_per_day: int\n    peak_multiplier: float\n    blast_radius: str              # cosmetic | recoverable | costly | unrecoverable\n    freshness_max_age_min: int     # -1 = static corpus\n    # the three facts that force a climb\n    corpus: str = \"bounded\"        # bounded | unbounded\n    steps: str = \"fixed\"           # fixed | unknown\n    subtasks: str = \"single\"       # single | independent\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Shape:\n    rung: int\n    sid: str\n    label: str\n    cost_mult: float        # x one baseline call\n    lat_mult: float         # x one baseline call p95\n    fixed_ms: float         # orchestration/IO floor\n    quality_ceiling: float  # best pass rate this shape realistically reaches\n    ops_fte: float          # people-cost to operate, steady state\n    obs_mult: float         # observability spend vs a single call\n    handles_unbounded: bool\n    handles_unknown_steps: bool\n    handles_independent: bool\n    interactive_ok: bool\n    verifiable: bool\n    degrades_to: str\n\n\n# One clause per shape, then the profile. How each one works is Mastery''s job.\nSHAPES: Tuple[Shape, ...] = (\n    Shape(1, \"single_call\", \"single call\",\n          1.0, 1.0, 30, 0.88, 0.1, 1.0, False, False, False, True, False,\n          \"a canned response\"),\n    Shape(2, \"prompt_chain\", \"prompt chain/workflow\",\n          2.6, 2.4, 60, 0.93, 0.3, 2.0, False, False, False, True, True,\n          \"the last good step''s output\"),\n    Shape(3, \"retrieval_read\", \"retrieval read path\",\n          1.9, 1.5, 220, 0.94, 0.8, 2.5, True, False, False, True, True,\n          \"keyword results with no synthesis\"),\n    Shape(4, \"agent_loop\", \"single-agent tool loop\",\n          6.5, 5.2, 400, 0.95, 1.5, 6.0, True, True, False, True, True,\n          \"hand off to a human with the trace\"),\n    Shape(5, \"orchestrator\", \"orchestrator-worker\",\n          14.0, 3.1, 650, 0.96, 2.5, 9.0, True, True, True, True, True,\n          \"partial results from the workers that finished\"),\n    # Cheapest per task, most expensive to own: scheduling, backfills, reprocessing,\n    # freshness monitoring. The ladder is ordered by OPERATE cost, not unit cost.\n    Shape(6, \"batch_pipeline\", \"offline batch pipeline\",\n          0.5, 900.0, 60000, 0.97, 3.0, 10.0, True, True, True, False, True,\n          \"yesterday''s run\"),\n)\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Verdict:\n    shape: Shape\n    feasible: bool\n    binding: Optional[str]   # the ONE constraint that killed it\n    cost: float\n    p95: float\n    monthly: float\n\n\ndef _binding_constraint(sh: Shape, spec: SpecCard, cost: float,\n                        p95: float, monthly: float) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Return the single most binding violation, or None. Capability beats\n    latency beats cost beats volume - fix them in that order.\"\"\"\n    if spec.corpus == \"unbounded\" and not sh.handles_unbounded:\n        return \"unbounded corpus: %s cannot ground on a corpus it can''t fit\" % sh.label\n    if spec.steps == \"unknown\" and not sh.handles_unknown_steps:\n        return \"unknown step count: %s runs a fixed number of steps\" % sh.label\n    if spec.subtasks == \"independent\" and not sh.handles_independent:\n        return \"independent subtasks under context pressure: %s runs one line of work\" % sh.label\n    if spec.interaction_mode == \"interactive\" and not sh.interactive_ok:\n        return \"interaction mode is interactive: %s is not a request-path shape\" % sh.label\n    if spec.blast_radius in (\"costly\", \"unrecoverable\") and not sh.verifiable:\n        return \"blast radius %s: %s has no seam to verify before the action lands\" % (\n            spec.blast_radius, sh.label)\n    if spec.quality_bar > sh.quality_ceiling:\n        return \"quality bar %.3f > %s ceiling %.3f\" % (\n            spec.quality_bar, sh.label, sh.quality_ceiling)\n    if p95 > spec.p95_latency_ms:\n        return \"p95 budget %.0fms < %s floor %.0fms\" % (\n            spec.p95_latency_ms, sh.label, p95)\n    if cost > spec.cost_per_task_usd:\n        return \"cost ceiling $%.4f/task < %s at $%.4f/task\" % (\n            spec.cost_per_task_usd, sh.label, cost)\n    if monthly > spec.monthly_ceiling_usd:\n        return \"monthly ceiling $%.0f < $%.0f at %d tasks/day\" % (\n            spec.monthly_ceiling_usd, monthly, spec.tasks_per_day)\n    return None\n\n\ndef select(spec: SpecCard, unit_call_cost_usd: float,\n           unit_call_p95_ms: float) -> List[Verdict]:\n    \"\"\"Rank the ladder. Feasible rungs first, lowest rung wins.\"\"\"\n    out: List[Verdict] = []\n    for sh in SHAPES:\n        cost = sh.cost_mult * unit_call_cost_usd\n        p95 = sh.lat_mult * unit_call_p95_ms + sh.fixed_ms\n        monthly = cost * spec.tasks_per_day * 30.0\n        binding = _binding_constraint(sh, spec, cost, p95, monthly)\n        out.append(Verdict(sh, binding is None, binding, cost, p95, monthly))\n    out.sort(key=lambda v: (not v.feasible, v.shape.rung))\n    return out\n\n\ndef explain(spec: SpecCard, unit_call_cost_usd: float, unit_call_p95_ms: float) -> Optional[Verdict]:\n    print(\"\\nSPEC: %s\" % spec.name)\n    print(\"  bar %.3f | p95 %.0fms | $%.4f/task | %d/day x%.1f peak | %s | %s corpus, %s steps\"\n          % (spec.quality_bar, spec.p95_latency_ms, spec.cost_per_task_usd,\n             spec.tasks_per_day, spec.peak_multiplier, spec.blast_radius,\n             spec.corpus, spec.steps))\n    verdicts = select(spec, unit_call_cost_usd, unit_call_p95_ms)\n    for v in sorted(verdicts, key=lambda x: x.shape.rung):\n        sh = v.shape\n        head = \"  %d. %-24s $%-9s %-9s %.1f FTE  \" % (\n            sh.rung, sh.label, \"%.4f\" % v.cost, \"%.0fms\" % v.p95, sh.ops_fte)\n        if v.feasible:\n            print(head + \"FEASIBLE   (degrades to: %s)\" % sh.degrades_to)\n        else:\n            print(head + \"rejected: %s\" % v.binding)\n\n    winners = [v for v in verdicts if v.feasible]\n    if not winners:\n        print(\"  --> no rung clears this card. Renegotiate a field or cut scope.\")\n        return None\n    win = winners[0]\n    climbed = [v for v in verdicts if v.shape.rung < win.shape.rung and not v.feasible]\n    print(\"  --> BUILD: %s (rung %d)\" % (win.shape.label, win.shape.rung))\n    if climbed:\n        print(\"      FORCING CONSTRAINT (write this down, it is the re-litigation trigger):\")\n        for v in climbed:\n            print(\"        rung %d unavailable because %s\" % (v.shape.rung, v.binding))\n    else:\n        print(\"      No climb needed - rung 1 clears the card. Do not decorate it.\")\n    if win.shape.sid == \"batch_pipeline\":\n        print(\"      Monthly at steady state: $%.0f. Peak x%.1f is absorbed by the\"\n              % (win.monthly, spec.peak_multiplier))\n        print(\"      schedule window, not by spend - that is what buys the rung.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"      Monthly at steady state: $%.0f | at %.1fx peak: $%.0f (provision for this)\"\n              % (win.monthly, spec.peak_multiplier, win.monthly * spec.peak_multiplier))\n    return win\n\n\ndef composite(unit_call_cost_usd: float, mix: List[Tuple[str, float]]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Most real systems are three shapes with contracts between them.\"\"\"\n    print(\"\\nCOMPOSITE: batch pipeline -> read path -> agent escape hatch\")\n    by_id = {s.sid: s for s in SHAPES}\n    blended = 0.0\n    for sid, share in mix:\n        sh = by_id[sid]\n        cost = sh.cost_mult * unit_call_cost_usd\n        blended += cost * share\n        print(\"  %5.1f%% of traffic -> %-24s $%.4f/task\" % (share * 100, sh.label, cost))\n    print(\"  blended cost per task: $%.4f\" % blended)\n    # The escape hatch is the contract that quietly eats the budget. Price it.\n    hatch, hatch_share = by_id[mix[-1][0]], mix[-1][1]\n    donor = by_id[mix[-2][0]]\n    for worse in (0.10, 0.25):\n        shifted = worse - hatch_share\n        alt = blended + shifted * (hatch.cost_mult - donor.cost_mult) * unit_call_cost_usd\n        print(\"  escape hatch at %.0f%% instead of %.0f%% -> blended $%.4f (%.1fx)\"\n              % (worse * 100, hatch_share * 100, alt, alt / blended))\n    print(\"  Contracts, not vibes: batch->read is a versioned index snapshot;\")\n    print(\"  read->agent is an explicit low-confidence signal with a rate cap.\")\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    UNIT_COST = 0.012     # $ per baseline call - YOUR rate card\n    UNIT_P95 = 1400.0     # ms per baseline call - YOUR traces\n\n    base = SpecCard(\n        name=\"Internal policy answers (steady state)\",\n        quality_bar=0.93,\n        p95_latency_ms=3500,\n        interaction_mode=\"interactive\",\n        cost_per_task_usd=0.08,\n        monthly_ceiling_usd=12000,\n        tasks_per_day=4000,\n        peak_multiplier=3.0,\n        blast_radius=\"recoverable\",\n        freshness_max_age_min=1440,\n        corpus=\"unbounded\",\n        steps=\"fixed\",\n        subtasks=\"single\",\n    )\n    explain(base, UNIT_COST, UNIT_P95)\n\n    # Change ONE number: peak volume x20. Watch the answer move down the ladder.\n    surge = SpecCard(**dict(base.__dict__,\n                            name=\"Same feature at 20x volume\",\n                            tasks_per_day=80000))\n    explain(surge, UNIT_COST, UNIT_P95)\n\n    # And the version where the work itself is non-interactive.\n    # Relax the two fields the surge broke - answers may be a day old, and\n    # finance signed a bigger number once the 20x volume was real.\n    nightly = SpecCard(**dict(surge.__dict__,\n                              name=\"Same feature at 20x, precomputed nightly\",\n                              interaction_mode=\"async\",\n                              p95_latency_ms=4 * 60 * 60 * 1000,\n                              monthly_ceiling_usd=30000,\n                              freshness_max_age_min=1440))\n    explain(nightly, UNIT_COST, UNIT_P95)\n\n    composite(UNIT_COST, [(\"batch_pipeline\", 0.55), (\"retrieval_read\", 0.42),\n                          (\"agent_loop\", 0.03)])\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","notes":["Constraints are checked in a deliberate order — capability, then latency, then cost, then volume — so the binding constraint reported is the one you should go fix first. A shape rejected on capability will never be rescued by budget.","Costs are multiples of one baseline call that you supply, so nothing here rots when prices change. Pull <code>UNIT_COST</code> and <code>UNIT_P95</code> from a week of your own traces, p95 not mean.","The batch rung has the lowest cost multiplier and the highest operate cost on the table. That inversion is intentional and it''s the most commonly mispriced decision on the ladder.","<code>explain()</code> prints the forcing constraint for every rung below the winner, not just the winner''s own justification. That block is the re-litigation trigger — paste it verbatim into the decision record.","The composite function exists to price escape-hatch drift. Run it with your real traffic split, then again with the hatch at 3× its current share, and set the alert threshold between the two."],"debug":[["Every rung is rejected on capability","A capability flag is set that the card doesn''t actually require","Re-read the card: \"we haven''t enumerated the steps\" is not the same claim as \"the steps cannot be enumerated\""],["Selected rung is far above what the team actually built","The real system climbed for a reason that was never a card field","Ask what the forcing constraint was; if nobody can name one, the extra rungs are deletion candidates"],["Blended composite cost creeps up month over month","Escape-hatch share is drifting as inputs get harder","Alert on hatch share, not just total cost — the blend is roughly linear in it, and total cost moves too slowly to notice"],["Selection flips between runs on the same card","The card sits right on a boundary, usually the monthly ceiling","Print the margin, not just the verdict; a shape that clears by 2% is not a decision, it''s a coin flip"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:shapes', 'paid', '[{"q":"The shape ladder orders six architectures by which property?","o":["Per-task cost, from the cheapest single call up to the priciest orchestrator","Answer quality, since each rung reaches a strictly higher accuracy ceiling","Operate cost — what the shape costs to own, roughly 10× per rung climbed","Implementation effort, measured as engineer-weeks to a first working version"],"a":2,"exp":"Ordering by unit cost gets it exactly wrong: a batch pipeline is the cheapest rung per task and the most expensive to own — backfills, reprocessing, freshness monitoring, roughly 3 FTE. Operate cost is what you actually inherit."},{"q":"A card specifies an unbounded corpus. Which rungs does that single fact eliminate?","o":["Rungs 1 and 2 — no chain of calls makes a corpus fit that doesn''t fit","Rung 6 only, since batch precompute cannot cover an open-ended corpus","Rungs 4 and 5, since neither one includes a grounding step by default","None directly — corpus size is a cost constraint rather than a capability one"],"a":0,"exp":"This is a capability fact, not a budget fact, which is why no amount of money rescues rungs 1 and 2. Note what didn''t kill them: cost and latency were both comfortable. Capability constraints get checked first because budget can never fix them."},{"q":"You choose a retrieval read path. What is the actual artifact of that decision?","o":["The diagram showing the index, the retrieval hop, and the generation stage","The sentence \"rungs 1 and 2 are unavailable because the corpus is unbounded\"","The benchmark comparing this shape''s quality against the two rungs above it","The cost model projecting monthly spend at both steady state and peak load"],"a":1,"exp":"The forcing constraint is the re-litigation trigger. The day someone scopes the corpus to 40 documents, rung 1 becomes available and the system should get simpler — but only if that sentence exists. Without it, nobody can prove simplification is safe."},{"q":"Volume goes from 4,000 to 80,000 tasks/day. The read path was $0.0228/task against a $12,000 monthly ceiling. What happens?","o":["It still fits, since per-task cost is unchanged by the increase in volume","It fails at $54,720/month, and a card field has to move before anything else","It fits after negotiating volume discounts, which scale with committed spend","It fails on latency first, because peak concurrency drives the p95 upward"],"a":1,"exp":"0.0228 × 80,000 × 30 = $54,720. Every rung fails at this volume, which isn''t a flaw in the ladder — it''s the card telling you a field must move. Changing interaction mode to async opened the batch rung at $14,400/month."},{"q":"Which pair of facts together forces an orchestrator-worker shape?","o":["High request volume combined with a strict per-task cost ceiling","Independent subtasks combined with context pressure on a single path","An unbounded corpus combined with a tight end-to-end latency budget","Unknown step count combined with a costly or unrecoverable blast radius"],"a":1,"exp":"Both halves are required. Independent subtasks that fit comfortably don''t justify a coordination layer, and context pressure alone doesn''t either. Orchestration is the steepest cost jump on the ladder — roughly 14× — so it needs both."},{"q":"Which claim justifies climbing to a tool loop?","o":["\"The steps cannot be enumerated in advance for the cases that matter\"","\"We haven''t yet enumerated the steps this workflow needs to perform\"","\"A loop would be more flexible if requirements change later in the year\"","\"The team has more experience operating loops than fixed prompt chains\""],"a":0,"exp":"Be strict with yourself here: \"we haven''t enumerated the steps\" and \"the steps cannot be enumerated\" are different claims, and the first is usually what''s true. Flexibility and familiarity aren''t spec-card fields, so neither can be a forcing constraint."},{"q":"A composite serves 55% batch ($0.0060), 42% read path ($0.0228), 3% tool loop ($0.0780). Blended cost per task:","o":["About $0.0356, the straight average across the three shapes in the mix","About $0.0152, each shape''s cost weighted by its share of the traffic","About $0.0780, since the most expensive path sets the ceiling for planning","About $0.0228, since the read path handles the majority of live requests"],"a":1,"exp":"0.55×0.0060 + 0.42×0.0228 + 0.03×0.0780 = $0.0152. The weighted blend is the number the unit economics close against, and it''s why serving the easy 97% with a cheap shape matters so much."},{"q":"The escape hatch in that composite drifts from 3% of traffic to 25%. Why does this need an alert?","o":["Blended cost nearly doubles, and total spend moves too slowly to notice","The hatch''s latency profile degrades once it exceeds its designed capacity","Quality drops, since the hatch was tuned against the hard 3% of cases only","Rate limits on the expensive path will begin rejecting traffic at that share"],"a":0,"exp":"$0.0152 becomes $0.0274, a 1.8× blend. Blended cost is roughly linear in hatch share, so the share is the leading indicator while total spend is a lagging one. Alert on the share and cap it; don''t wait for the invoice."},{"q":"Why is a shape''s degradation behaviour a selection criterion rather than an afterthought?","o":["Degraded modes are what regulators examine first during a compliance audit","It determines the retry policy, which drives the composed availability figure","It''s what your product actually is on its worst day, and shapes differ hugely","Cheaper rungs degrade more gracefully, so degradation tracks cost inversely"],"a":2,"exp":"A read path degrades to keyword results — a real product. A single call degrades to a canned response. A batch pipeline degrades to yesterday''s run, which is often fine. Those are different products on a bad day, and bad days are not rare."},{"q":"You inherit a seven-box system where three boxes have no recorded forcing constraint. Your first move:","o":["Rewrite the system at the lowest rung that clears the card as it stands today","Instrument all three to measure what share of traffic each one actually serves","List them as deletion candidates and take that list to the team to defend","Document the current design thoroughly so the next architect isn''t stuck too"],"a":2,"exp":"Both outcomes of that conversation are wins: someone supplies the missing constraint and it finally goes in the doc, or nobody can and you''ve found real complexity to remove. Rewriting first skips the cheap step, and documenting without challenging preserves the problem."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:failure', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"failure","block":"b2","title":"Failure-Mode Architecture","tag":"AI systems fail with a 200 OK, so you enumerate the failures the status code hides","why":"Classic systems have the decency to fail loudly. A timeout throws, a null dereferences, a bad migration takes the pod down and pages you. AI systems'' worst failures return successfully: a 200, a well-formed body, a confident paragraph, and a wrong answer that no runtime on earth will raise for you. Which means every failure class either has a detector you deliberately designed, or a structure that makes it impossible, or it is simply happening to your customers right now and you do not know. This module is the pre-build pass that decides which is which. You come out of it with a scored table, a design-change backlog, and the ability to answer the review question nobody in your org currently answers: <b>how would we find out?</b>","mental":"Hold one picture for this whole module: <b>the silent 200</b>.<br><br>Every response your system emits leaves through the same door, wearing the same status code. The correct answer and the catastrophically wrong one are dressed identically. Your load balancer sees a 200. Your error rate is 0.02%. Your dashboards are green. And somewhere in the last hour, the system posted the wrong amount to a ledger, cited a contract clause that was superseded in March, and told a customer something that will cost you the account.<br><br>None of that raised. None of it ever will. The transport succeeded, and transport success is the only thing the runtime knows how to measure.<br><br>So the architecture has to supply what the runtime cannot: for every way this thing can be wrong, either a detector you designed on purpose, or a structure that makes the failure unreachable. There is no third option, and \"we will monitor it\" is not a detector — it is a promise to build one, and it only counts once you can name what the check compares against. <b>The silent 200 is the reason failure-mode work moves from operations to architecture.</b>","concepts":[["Detectability is the first column you fill, not the last","Most teams score severity and likelihood, argue about them, then bolt detection on at the end as an afterthought. Invert it. Detectability decides which of the three dispositions is even legal for a row, so it has to be established before anyone proposes a fix. A row you cannot observe cannot be mitigated — only redesigned or accepted — and knowing that up front saves the meeting."],["The RPN you can actually defend","Severity x likelihood x detection-difficulty, each 1-10, all three scored higher-is-worse. Detection 1 means a deterministic check catches it before the response ships; detection 10 means only an angry customer can tell you. The absolute number is meaningless; the <b>ranking</b> is the product. Its real job is forcing three separate judgements into the open instead of letting one loud voice collapse them into a vibe."],["Three dispositions, and only three","A rail is an inline gate that runs before the response ships and can block it. A sampler is an async scorer that tells you a rate, days later, on a fraction of traffic. An amputation is a design change that removes the surface the failure needs to exist. Detectability picks: detection 1-3 earns a rail, 4-7 earns a sampler, above 7 earns neither."],["The undetectable-and-severe quadrant","High severity plus detection above 7 is the only quadrant with a forced move. You do not get to mitigate it, because a mitigation you cannot verify is a story you tell in review. The legal moves are a constrained output set, a restricted action, a schema the answer must satisfy, a deterministic lookup replacing a generated answer, or a human gate. All five shrink the surface; none of them are monitoring."],["Linear modes you budget, compounding modes you cap","A per-row classification error is linear: eight rows, eight independent chances, and the arithmetic behaves. Drift, partial writes and context pollution are compounding: step three inherits step two''s contamination, so its error rate is larger than step two''s was. At a 4% base with 35% growth per step, eight steps is 72% chance of a bad step against a linear 28%. Compounding modes get a step ceiling, not a better prompt."],["Severity is spec-card arithmetic, not a feeling","Severity comes straight off the blast-radius line of your spec card, converted to a number: how many customers, how much money, is it reversible, does it cross a tenant boundary, does it end up in a regulator''s inbox. Two engineers who both start from the spec card converge on severity within a point. Two engineers who start from intuition argue for forty minutes and land wherever the senior one was."],["The table is a dated artifact with an owner","An FMEA that lives in someone''s head is a conversation. One with a date, an owner, a row count, and a linked design-change backlog is an architectural commitment you can be held to, and re-run against on the next provider bump or feature expansion. Rescore it when the action surface widens, when volume crosses an order of magnitude, or when a new failure mode shows up in the wild."]],"mistakes":["Scoring severity and likelihood carefully and leaving detection at a hand-wave, which is exactly the column that decides what you are allowed to do about the row","Answering an undetectable high-severity row with \"we will add an alert\", which commits you to a detector nobody has designed and quietly closes the row","Ranking purely by severity, so a 10-severity once-a-year row eats the quarter while a 6-severity daily invisible one ships to production untouched","Treating the FMEA as a launch checklist rather than a living register, so the action surface widens three times and the table is never rescored","Assuming failure rates compose linearly across a multi-step flow, which understates every mode where a step inherits the previous step''s output"],"flags":["\"Error rate is 0.02%\" offered as evidence of quality, when the only thing measured is whether the transport succeeded","A risk register where every row''s mitigation is a dashboard, an alert, or a metric, and none is a change to what the system can produce","Nobody can answer \"how would we find out?\" for the top-severity failure mode without saying the word \"customer\"","The word \"monitoring\" appearing in a design doc before any detector has been specified in terms of what it compares against","A multi-step flow whose per-step accuracy is quoted and whose end-to-end accuracy has never been computed"],"ask":["For our worst failure mode, what is the detector, what does it compare the output against, and how many minutes until it fires?","Which rows on this table can only be discovered by a customer telling us, and what design change is queued for each?","Which of our failure modes compound per step, and what caps the step count?","When was this table last rescored, and what changed in the system since then?","If this exact failure happened at 09:00 today, what is the first artifact that would show it, and who is looking at that artifact?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"Thirty minutes, one whiteboard, one feature: an assistant that reads supplier invoices, extracts line items, answers questions about supplier terms, and can post the result to the ledger. Six people in the room. The rule for the session is that no one proposes a fix before the detectability score is on the board.<br><br>Eleven rows come out. Two of them get resolved in opposite directions, and the contrast is the whole lesson.<br><br><b>Row A: a transposed digit in an extracted amount.</b> Severity 8 — it is money, and it posts. Likelihood 4 — it happens, not often. Detectability 2 — and here is why: the invoice carries a stated total, the extracted line items have a sum, and those two numbers either match or they do not. That is a deterministic arithmetic comparison available before anything ships. RPN = 8 x 4 x 2 = <b>64</b>.<br><br>Disposition: a rail. A totals reconciliation that runs on every extraction and blocks the post on mismatch. We estimate it catches 97% of transposition errors, so the escaping likelihood drops from 4 to effectively 0.12, which we round up to 1 because pretending a control is perfect is how you get a bad table. Residual RPN = 8 x 1 x 2 = <b>16</b>. A 75% reduction, from roughly forty lines of code and no model involvement at all.<br><br><b>Row B: an answer about supplier terms drawn from a superseded contract version.</b> Severity 7 — someone acts on the wrong payment terms and we are in a commercial dispute. Likelihood 5 — the index holds every version of every contract, and half of them read almost identically. Detectability 9 — and this is the number that ends the discussion. The answer is fluent. It cites a real clause. The clause exists, verbatim, in a real document we really do hold. Nothing about the output is malformed. No reviewer scanning outputs would flag it. It surfaces when a supplier disputes an invoice, roughly six weeks later. RPN = 7 x 5 x 9 = <b>315</b>, nearly five times Row A.<br><br>The room''s first instinct is an alert: \"score a sample of terms answers for staleness.\" Follow it one step. The scorer would have to know which contract version is current for that supplier on that date. That means resolving supplier_id plus effective_date against the contract table — a deterministic lookup. Which is the punchline: <b>if you can build the detector, build the answer instead.</b> The moment you can compute the right answer reliably enough to grade the model, you no longer need the model for that question.<br><br>So Row B gets an amputation. Terms questions stop being answered in free text. The assistant resolves supplier_id + effective_date to exactly one contract record and returns the clause text plus a link, generated nothing. The generative surface for that question class is deleted. Post-amputation likelihood is not reduced, it is zero, because the mode has nowhere to occur. RPN 315 -> <b>0</b>. You did not mitigate it. You removed the thing that could produce it.<br><br>Two rows, two directions, one rule doing the deciding. Row A had a cheap oracle sitting right there in the document, so it earned a rail. Row B had no oracle short of building the answer, so mitigation was never on the table and the only honest move was to shrink what the system is allowed to say.<br><br>The register''s totals: 1,503 RPN before controls, 928 after, of which 715 is the three structural rows. That last number is the one you take to the planning meeting. It is not a monitoring backlog. It is a design backlog, and it has to be spent before launch, because none of it gets better with a dashboard.","good":["Every row on the register names a detector and what that detector compares against, or it is explicitly marked as having none","The highest-RPN rows are structural, and the structural rows are on the build plan rather than the observability plan","Severity scores trace back to the spec card''s blast-radius line, so two engineers scoring the same row independently land within a point","Compounding failure modes are identified as such and answered with a step ceiling, not with a better prompt","The table has a date, an owner, and a trigger list for when it gets rescored, and the last rescore is more recent than the last capability added"],"build":"Take one feature you personally own and build the register: minimum ten rows, each scored severity, likelihood and detection-difficulty on 1-10 with all three higher-is-worse. Rank by the product. Then do the part that makes it real — circle every row with detection above 7. That circled set is your design-change backlog, and it is a different backlog from your monitoring one. For each circled row, write the specific structural move: which output gets constrained, which capability gets removed, which generated answer becomes a deterministic lookup, which step gets a human in front of it. If you cannot write one, write \"accepted, revisit at X\" with a real trigger — that is a legitimate answer and a dishonest silence is not. Finally, walk the table to a senior peer and let them attack your detection scores specifically. Detection is where optimism hides, and it is the column that changed what you are allowed to build.","res":[["Failure mode and effects analysis (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Failure_mode_and_effects_analysis","Use when you want the original discipline this module adapts, including the detection-rating convention and why RPN rankings beat RPN values."],["Google SRE Book - Embracing Risk","https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/","Use when someone argues for zero failures. It is the cleanest published argument for choosing a failure budget deliberately instead of pretending to have none."],["NIST AI Risk Management Framework","https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","Use right now: if you need a recognised vocabulary for the register when it goes to legal, risk or a customer''s security review, this is the one they will already know."],["Fault tree analysis (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fault_tree_analysis","Use when a failure mode has several independent contributing paths and one FMEA row is flattening a structure you need to see branch by branch."]]},"depth":{"mech":"The mechanism behind <b>the silent 200</b> is worth being precise about, because the imprecise version leads people to the wrong fix.<br><br>A traditional service has a type system, a schema, a transaction boundary and an exception mechanism, and those four things collectively encode a huge number of correctness assumptions. When one is violated, something raises. The raise is not a courtesy — it is a structural property of the system, and it is why observability for classic services can be mostly generic: count the raises, time the requests, alert on both.<br><br>A generative call has almost none of that. The output is a string. Every string is a valid string. If you constrain the output to a schema you recover shape validity, which is real and worth having, but shape validity is orthogonal to being right. A perfectly-typed <code>{\"amount\": 4310.00}</code> is indistinguishable at the transport layer from a perfectly-typed <code>{\"amount\": 3410.00}</code>. The runtime has no opinion. The status code is 200 in both cases because in both cases the transport did its job.<br><br>So the entire correctness signal has to be manufactured. And manufacturing it requires an <b>oracle</b>: something outside the model that can say whether this particular output is acceptable. This is the load-bearing idea of the module. Every detector is an oracle plus a comparison, and the cost and feasibility of a detector is entirely determined by which oracle is available.<br><br>Some oracles are free and sitting in the payload. An invoice states its own total; line items sum. That is an oracle. A schema is a weak oracle. A step counter is an oracle for stuck loops. A token meter is an oracle for cost runaway. These give you detection scores of 1 to 3 and they earn inline rails, because the check is cheap, deterministic and can run before the response leaves.<br><br>Some oracles exist but only cheaply in aggregate. Whether abstention rate has crept up, whether faithfulness scores drifted after a version bump, whether latency''s tail fattened — these need a sample, a scorer and a few days of data. Detection 4 to 7. These earn samplers: you learn the rate, not the instance, and you learn it late.<br><br>And some failure modes have no oracle short of computing the correct answer. Which contract version was current. Whether the summary the agent passed to step three quietly dropped the constraint that mattered. Whether this cache entry belongs to this tenant. Detection 8 to 10. Here the arithmetic turns on you: <b>the moment an oracle becomes cheap enough to run as a detector, it is cheap enough to run as the answer</b> — so either you build it and use it directly, or you accept that no detector exists and the only remaining move is to shrink the surface.<br><br>That is the whole ordering rule, and it is why detectability goes in the first column. It is not a property of how much you care about the failure. It is a property of what oracles your architecture happens to make available, and you can change that — by adding a structural check, by constraining an output set, by keeping a deterministic record alongside a generated one. Architecture is the act of manufacturing oracles for the failures you are not willing to be blind to.","trade":[["Rail (inline gate) vs sampler (async scorer)","Per-request latency and cost vs how late you learn","Rail everything and you pay a check on every request and add a blocking dependency to the hot path; sample everything and every individual bad answer still ships, you only learn the rate"],["Structural amputation vs keeping the capability and gating it","Product surface vs residual risk","Amputate too eagerly and you ship a system so narrow nobody wants it; gate instead and you have inherited a control whose effectiveness you have to keep proving forever"],["Rescore the whole register vs patch the row that just bit you","Consistency vs speed after an incident","Patch-only and the scores drift into folklore within two quarters; full rescore every time and it becomes a ceremony people schedule around and then skip"],["Score detection optimistically vs pessimistically when unsure","Build cost now vs discovery cost later","Optimism moves rows out of the structural quadrant and into a monitoring plan that will not work; pessimism costs you real design effort on failures that a cheap check would have caught"]],"scale":["Detection scores are volume-dependent and this surprises people: a mode you catch by eyeballing outputs at 200 requests a day is detection 4, and the identical mode at 200,000 a day is detection 9, because the human oracle silently vanished when you were not looking","Rail cost scales with every request while sampler cost scales with the sample, so the rail/sampler line moves as volume grows and a rail that was obviously correct at launch can become the single largest line item in the feature''s budget","Compounding modes cap your shape choices: if the register says context pollution is your top structural row, the step ceiling that follows is a hard constraint on how deep an agent loop you are allowed to design, and it belongs in the shape decision rather than being discovered afterwards","Rescore triggers that actually work in practice: any new capability added to the action surface, any order-of-magnitude change in volume, any provider version change, and any failure mode observed in the wild that is not already a row"],"sec":["Cross-tenant leak is the row people score wrong most often: severity is always near 10 because it is a reportable event, and detectability is near 9 because a correct-looking answer built from the wrong tenant''s data looks exactly like a correct answer — that combination is a forced structural fix, and the usual one is putting the tenant identifier inside the cache key rather than trusting the query text","Traces, logs and eval samples are copies of the same sensitive payload the request carried, so a detector you add for quality reasons has quietly created a new place the data lives, with its own retention and access story","Capability regression after a provider version bump is a security row as much as a quality one, because the behaviour that changed may be a refusal you were relying on as a control — pin the version and rescore rather than assuming refusals are stable","Any failure mode whose only detector is a human reviewer is also a social-engineering surface: reviewers approve faster under load, so a control that is 95% effective in a calm week is worth considerably less on the Monday your volume spikes"],"interview":"Here is how I would say it out loud in a design review. Normal systems fail loudly, and that is a gift we stopped noticing — a timeout throws, a constraint violation raises, and the failure announces itself. AI systems fail with a 200. The wrong answer and the right answer come back with the same status code, the same shape, and the same confidence, and nothing in the stack has an opinion about which one it just returned. So a low error rate tells you the transport worked. It tells you nothing about whether the thing was right.<br><br>What that means for design is that we cannot find our failure modes by watching errors — we have to enumerate them on purpose, before we build, and score each one three ways: how bad, how often, and how hard to notice. The third score is the one that changes what we are allowed to do. If a failure is visible inline, we gate it. If it is only visible in aggregate, we sample it and we accept that we will learn the rate rather than the instance. And if nobody can see it at all, then monitoring is not an option and we either shrink what the system is allowed to say, or we write down that we are accepting it. <b>The one thing we do not get to do is plan a mitigation for a failure we cannot observe</b> — because we will never know whether the mitigation worked, and we will feel safe anyway. That is the rule I would want on the wall."},"patterns":{"intro":"A register, not a runtime. You type in the failure modes you can think of with three scores each; it ranks them, applies the ordering rule mechanically, and refuses to let an unobservable high-severity row be answered with monitoring. The markdown table it emits pastes straight into the design doc, and the compounding table at the bottom is the argument you will need when someone proposes a twelve-step chain.","code":"\"\"\"FMEA for probabilistic systems: a design-time calculator, not a monitor.\n\nScores each failure mode on severity x likelihood x detection-difficulty, sorts\nby RPN, and hard-flags every row you cannot observe as a STRUCTURAL problem\nrather than a monitoring one. Emits a markdown table you paste into the design\ndoc, plus the two backlogs the table implies.\n\nAll three scales run \"higher is worse\" (classic FMEA convention):\n  severity   1 = a shrug            10 = money moves, data leaks, someone is hurt\n  likelihood 1 = once a year        10 = several times a day\n  detection  1 = a deterministic check catches it before the response ships\n             10 = only an angry customer can tell you it happened\n\nRun: python fmea.py     (stdlib only, no network, deterministic)\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nDETECTION_CEILING = 7      # above this, mitigation is a fiction\nSEVERITY_FLOOR = 6         # below this, an unobservable mode may be accepted\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass FailureMode:\n    name: str\n    severity: int              # 1-10\n    likelihood: int            # 1-10\n    detection: int             # 1-10, higher = harder to see\n    control_point: str         # WHERE in the architecture you would act\n    catch_rate: float = 0.0    # fraction the proposed control actually catches\n    compounding: bool = False  # does it get worse per step, or stay linear?\n\n    def rpn(self) -> int:\n        return self.severity * self.likelihood * self.detection\n\n    def disposition(self) -> str:\n        if self.detection > DETECTION_CEILING and self.severity >= SEVERITY_FLOOR:\n            return \"STRUCTURAL FIX REQUIRED - mitigation will not work\"\n        if self.detection > DETECTION_CEILING:\n            return \"ACCEPT + REVISIT (unobservable, low severity)\"\n        if self.detection <= 3:\n            return \"RAIL (inline gate)\"\n        return \"SAMPLER (async detection)\"\n\n    def residual_rpn(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"RPN after the proposed control. A control that catches nothing\n        changes nothing, which is the point of making you type catch_rate.\"\"\"\n        if self.disposition().startswith(\"STRUCTURAL\"):\n            return self.rpn()  # unchanged until the design changes\n        escaped = max(1, round(self.likelihood * (1.0 - self.catch_rate)))\n        return self.severity * escaped * self.detection\n\n\ndef chain_escape(per_step: float, steps: int, growth: float = 0.0) -> float:\n    \"\"\"P(at least one bad step) across a chain.\n\n    growth=0.0 is LINEAR: every step is independently p and errors do not\n    infect each other. growth>0 is COMPOUNDING: each step inherits the\n    previous step''s contamination, so its own error rate is scaled up.\n    Drift, partial writes and context pollution live in the compounding\n    column; a stateless per-row classification lives in the linear one.\"\"\"\n    survive = 1.0\n    for k in range(steps):\n        p = min(0.95, per_step * (1.0 + growth) ** k)\n        survive *= (1.0 - p)\n    return 1.0 - survive\n\n\ndef render(rows):\n    hdr = \"| Failure mode | S | L | D | RPN | Control point | Residual | Disposition |\"\n    sep = \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"\n    out = [hdr, sep]\n    for f in sorted(rows, key=lambda r: r.rpn(), reverse=True):\n        out.append(\n            f\"| {f.name} | {f.severity} | {f.likelihood} | {f.detection} | \"\n            f\"{f.rpn()} | {f.control_point} | {f.residual_rpn()} | {f.disposition()} |\"\n        )\n    return \"\\n\".join(out)\n\n\nREGISTER = [\n    FailureMode(\"Wrong-but-confident amount extracted from an invoice\",\n                8, 4, 2, \"totals reconciliation before post\", 0.97),\n    FailureMode(\"Answer drawn from a superseded contract version\",\n                7, 5, 9, \"no inline check exists\", 0.0),\n    FailureMode(\"Over-abstention: refuses a question it can answer\",\n                3, 6, 4, \"abstention-rate sampler\", 0.6),\n    FailureMode(\"Agent loop repeats the same tool call until budget death\",\n                5, 4, 2, \"step counter + no-progress detector\", 0.99),\n    FailureMode(\"Cost runaway on a single tenant''s malformed batch\",\n                6, 3, 3, \"per-tenant spend cap at the gateway\", 0.95),\n    FailureMode(\"Latency tail: p99 blows the interactive budget\",\n                4, 7, 1, \"deadline + degrade at the call site\", 0.9),\n    FailureMode(\"Partial write: ledger posted, notification never sent\",\n                7, 4, 5, \"idempotency key + compensating action\", 0.8,\n                compounding=True),\n    FailureMode(\"Cross-tenant leak via a shared semantic cache key\",\n                10, 2, 8, \"tenant id in the cache key (structural)\", 0.0),\n    FailureMode(\"Capability regression after a provider version bump\",\n                6, 5, 6, \"pinned version + pre-promotion eval\", 0.85),\n    FailureMode(\"Context pollution: step 3 inherits step 2''s bad summary\",\n                6, 5, 8, \"no inline check exists\", 0.0, compounding=True),\n    FailureMode(\"Stale index: answer correct for last week''s catalogue\",\n                5, 6, 7, \"freshness stamp compared to spec-card tolerance\", 0.7),\n]\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(render(REGISTER))\n\n    structural = [f for f in REGISTER if f.disposition().startswith(\"STRUCTURAL\")]\n    rails = [f for f in REGISTER if f.disposition().startswith(\"RAIL\")]\n    samplers = [f for f in REGISTER if f.disposition().startswith(\"SAMPLER\")]\n\n    print(\"\\nDESIGN-CHANGE BACKLOG (you cannot monitor your way out of these)\")\n    for f in structural:\n        print(f\"  ! RPN {f.rpn():>4}  {f.name}\")\n    print(\"\\nMONITORING BACKLOG\")\n    for f in rails:\n        print(f\"  rail    RPN {f.rpn():>4} -> {f.residual_rpn():>4}  {f.name}\")\n    for f in samplers:\n        print(f\"  sampler RPN {f.rpn():>4} -> {f.residual_rpn():>4}  {f.name}\")\n\n    before = sum(f.rpn() for f in REGISTER)\n    after = sum(f.residual_rpn() for f in REGISTER)\n    print(f\"\\nTotal RPN {before} -> {after} \"\n          f\"({100 * (before - after) / before:.0f}% removed by controls); \"\n          f\"{sum(f.rpn() for f in structural)} of the remainder is structural.\")\n\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"COMPOUNDING CHECK - P(at least one bad step), 4% per-step base\")\n    print(\"steps |    linear |  compounding (35%/step) |  ratio\")\n    for steps in (1, 3, 5, 8, 12):\n        lin = chain_escape(0.04, steps, growth=0.0)\n        comp = chain_escape(0.04, steps, growth=0.35)\n        print(\"{:>5} | {:>9.1%} | {:>23.1%} | {:>5.2f}x\".format(\n            steps, lin, comp, comp / lin))\n    print(\"Linear modes you can budget for. Compounding modes need a cap on\")\n    print(\"the step count - the arithmetic outruns any per-step control.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","notes":["<b>All three scales run higher-is-worse</b>, including detection. A 9 in the detection column is not a compliment — it means only a customer can tell you. If your team scores detectability the other way round (\"how well can we see it\"), invert before you type it in or every disposition comes out backwards","<b>catch_rate</b> is a required argument for a reason. A control with no measured catch rate does not reduce residual RPN, and being made to type a number is what stops \"we added a guardrail\" from counting as a fix","The <code>DETECTION_CEILING</code> and <code>SEVERITY_FLOOR</code> constants encode a policy, not a law. Raising the ceiling to 8 is a legitimate decision for a low-stakes internal tool and an indefensible one for anything touching money — either way, change it deliberately and in one place","Structural rows deliberately keep their full RPN after \"controls\" are applied, so the totals line is honest about how much risk you have actually removed versus described","The compounding table is parameterised on a per-step growth rate. Measure yours if you can: run the same chain at 3, 6 and 12 steps against a fixed input set and read the curve. If you cannot measure it, 30-40% growth is a defensible planning assumption for anything passing summaries forward"],"debug":[["Every row lands in SAMPLER and the design backlog is empty","Detection scores are clustered in the 4-7 middle because nobody wanted to commit to a hard call","Force each row to justify its detection score by naming the oracle. Rows where you cannot name one are 8+, and the backlog fills immediately"],["Total RPN barely moves after controls are applied","Most of the remaining risk is in structural rows, which by design do not respond to controls","This is the tool working. Read the structural subtotal on the totals line and take that number to planning as a build estimate, not an ops estimate"],["Two engineers score the same row four points apart on severity","Severity is being sourced from intuition rather than the blast-radius line of the spec card","Rescore both from the spec card explicitly: customers affected, money at risk, reversibility, tenant boundary crossed. Disagreement usually collapses to under a point"],["The compounding column reads 99.9% and looks like a bug","It is not - a 4% base error growing 35% per step reaches near-certainty around twelve steps","That is the number to show whoever proposed the twelve-step chain. The fix is a step ceiling or a checkpoint that resets contamination, not a better prompt"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:failure', 'paid', '[{"q":"A row scores severity 9, likelihood 3, detectability 9. The team proposes adding a dashboard alert. What is wrong with that answer?","o":["The RPN of 243 is too low to justify spending engineering time on this row","You cannot alert on something no detector can see, so this needs a design change","Alerts belong on likelihood-driven rows, and likelihood 3 is far too rare to alert","Severity 9 rows always need a human reviewer rather than any automated control"],"a":1,"exp":"Detectability 9 means no oracle exists to compare the output against, so there is nothing for an alert to fire on. High severity plus detection above 7 is the one quadrant with a forced move: constrain the output, remove the capability, or gate it with a human. Monitoring is not available here."},{"q":"Why does the ordering rule insist that detectability be scored before anyone proposes a mitigation?","o":["Detectability is the hardest column to score, so it needs the freshest attention in the room","Regulators require detection ratings to be documented before any control is written down","Detectability determines which of the three dispositions is legal for that row at all","Severity and likelihood are usually obvious, so scoring them first wastes the meeting"],"a":2,"exp":"Detection score picks the disposition: 1-3 earns an inline rail, 4-7 earns an async sampler, above 7 earns neither and forces a structural change. Proposing a fix before you know the detection score means half the proposals on the table are not available to you."},{"q":"Three rows: A is 8/4/2, B is 7/5/9, C is 10/2/8. Ranked by RPN, which order comes out and what does it tell you?","o":["C then B then A, because the highest-severity row always deserves the first design pass","B then C then A, and B outranks the severity-10 row on frequency and invisibility","A then B then C, because a row with a working inline check is the cheapest to fix first","B then A then C, since likelihood dominates the product once severity is above seven"],"a":1,"exp":"B is 315, C is 160, A is 64. A severity-10 row that is rare outranks nothing here: B is worse because it happens often and nobody can see it. That inversion is precisely why you multiply three scores instead of sorting by the one that feels scariest."},{"q":"In this register''s convention, what does a detection score of 9 actually mean?","o":["The system catches roughly nine out of ten occurrences before the response ships","The failure is visible only after it has already reached and affected a customer","Detection coverage is strong, so this row is a monitoring item rather than a design one","Nine separate detectors exist, but none of them runs on the request''s critical path"],"a":1,"exp":"All three scales run higher-is-worse, so detection 9 means very hard to detect, not well detected. Teams that score detectability as \"how well can we see it\" get every disposition backwards, which is why the scale direction is stated on the register itself."},{"q":"Which failure mode should be answered with a hard ceiling on step count rather than a better per-step control?","o":["A transposed digit in an extracted amount, checked against the document''s stated total","A stuck loop that repeats one tool call until the request''s token budget is exhausted","Context pollution, where each step inherits and amplifies the previous step''s summary","An over-abstention rate that rises after a provider version bump changes refusal behaviour"],"a":2,"exp":"Context pollution compounds: each step''s error rate is larger than the last because it inherits contamination. At a 4% base with 35% growth, eight steps is a 72% chance of a bad step versus 28% if the steps were independent. Per-step controls cannot outrun that curve, so you cap the depth."},{"q":"A row has detection 5: you can score it on sampled traffic, but not inline. What does that buy you?","o":["You learn the rate a few days late, and every individual bad response still ships","You block the bad responses at the gate as long as the sample rate stays above 3%","You get instance-level detection, but only for the fraction of traffic you sampled","You get inline coverage at a lower cost, since sampling avoids checking every request"],"a":0,"exp":"A sampler is an async scorer over a fraction of traffic. It tells you a rate, not an instance, and it tells you late. That is genuinely valuable for drift and regression, and it is worth nothing to the specific customer who got the wrong answer this morning."},{"q":"A totals-reconciliation rail catches 97% of a mode scored 8 severity, 4 likelihood, 2 detection. How should the residual be recorded?","o":["Residual 0, since a control catching 97% of occurrences removes the row from the register","Residual 64, because inline rails reduce severity of impact rather than the RPN score","Residual 16, with escaping likelihood rounded up to 1 rather than recorded as zero","Residual 32, halving the original RPN as the standard allowance for any inline control"],"a":2,"exp":"8 x 4 x 2 = 64 before. A 97% catch rate drops escaping likelihood to about 0.12, which rounds up to 1 rather than to zero, giving 8 x 1 x 2 = 16. Rounding up is deliberate: pretending a control is perfect is how registers stop being useful."},{"q":"Someone proposes an async scorer that checks whether a cited contract clause came from the currently effective version. What should you notice?","o":["The scorer needs a golden set, so it should be deferred until enough traffic accumulates","The scorer would run too slowly to be useful, so it belongs in a nightly batch instead","The scorer needs an oracle that, once built, answers the question without the model","The scorer would double the cost per request, which the feature''s budget cannot absorb"],"a":2,"exp":"To grade the answer, the scorer must resolve supplier and effective date against the contract table. That deterministic lookup is the correct answer. If you can build the detector, build the answer instead and delete the generative surface for that question class."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:blast', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"blast","block":"b2","title":"Blast Radius & Autonomy Tiers","tag":"Every action the system can take is an action an attacker can trigger","why":"There is a question that decides more about your system''s safety than any control you will ever add, and most design docs never ask it: <b>if one model turn were fully compromised — every instruction in it written by someone hostile — what is the worst thing that turn could do?</b> Not \"how do we stop that from happening.\" Assume it happened. Read the answer out loud. If it makes the room go quiet, you have a design problem, and no amount of prompt hardening is going to fix it, because you cannot patch your way out of a capability you granted. This module inventories the action surface, assigns each path an autonomy tier, and then does the arithmetic that turns tiers from a governance nicety into an engineering lever — one that is frequently worth more than a model upgrade you cannot buy.","mental":"The image for this module is <b>the bulkheads</b>.<br><br>A ship does not survive a hull breach because the hull is strong. It survives because the hull is divided. Water comes in, floods one compartment, and stops — not because anyone talked it out of spreading, but because a steel wall is there and water does not negotiate with steel. The compartment boundary is a structural fact, decided in a shipyard years before the iceberg.<br><br>Your system survives a bad generation the same way. Not because the generation was good, and not because a rail talked it down, but because the set of actions reachable from that generation was bounded by <b>structure</b> — a token that does not carry the scope, an allowlist resolved from data the attacker never touched, an operation that requires a second human. Structure, not persuasion.<br><br>The design question follows directly. You do not ask how strong the hull is. You ask where the walls are, and you keep moving them inward until the answer to \"what floods\" is boring. <b>A prompt is a request. A bulkhead is a fact.</b>","concepts":[["Capability is privilege, and privilege is a design decision","The moment a tool is in the model''s reach, it is in the reach of anything that can influence the model''s context — which includes every document, email, ticket and web page that flows in. So the security question is not defensive, it is inventory: what is the complete set of actions a turn can take, who authorised each one, and what is the union of their effects. That set is what you design against, and it only shrinks by removing things from it."],["The four autonomy tiers","Suggest, draft-with-approval, act-with-undo, act-irreversibly. These are not a maturity ladder to climb — they are a placement decision per action path, made from what a wrong answer costs. Most systems have several tiers running at once, and the mistake is granting one tier to a whole feature rather than to each individual write path. The tier is a property of the action, not the product."],["Required correctness is a function of reversibility","This is the module''s central arithmetic. Fix your tolerance for bad actions per month and your volume, and each tier implies a per-generation accuracy requirement. At 20,000 actions a month and a tolerance of four, acting irreversibly demands 99.98%, act-with-undo demands 99.90%, approval-gated demands 99.60%, and suggest-only demands 93.3%. <b>A tier demotion is a model improvement you cannot purchase</b>, and it ships this week."],["Containment primitives you actually place","Four that earn their keep: capability tokens scoped narrower than the invoking user, so a compromised turn cannot do what the human could; idempotency keys plus compensating actions, so a retry is safe and a mistake is undoable; dual control on irreversible operations, so one bad turn is not sufficient; and allowlisted egress, so bytes can only leave to destinations chosen before the untrusted content arrived. Each is a wall, not an argument."],["Isolation has to survive the cache, the trace and the log","Tenant boundaries are usually enforced at the query and forgotten everywhere else. A semantic cache keyed on question text will happily serve tenant A''s answer to tenant B, and that answer will look completely correct — which is exactly why it is a reportable event and not a bug report. Carry the tenant identifier into every cache key, trace attribute and log line, then test the boundary from both sides."],["The trifecta test, in one line","Private data reachable, untrusted content reachable, egress capability present — all three inside the same turn is an escalation path, regardless of how good the rails are. Say it as a single question in review: can this turn read something valuable, read something an attacker wrote, and send bytes somewhere? If yes, break one leg or write down that you are accepting it."],["The worst-case sentence","For every action path, one sentence written in plain language: what happens if this fires wrongly, at the worst moment, on the largest account. \"There is no unsend\" is a sentence that changes budgets. \"Recoverable in a click\" is a sentence that unblocks a launch. The inventory is only useful when every row has one, and the exercise of writing them is where half the tier demotions get discovered."]],"mistakes":["Designing against prompt injection as the threat, rather than designing so a fully compromised turn is boring, which is the only version of the goal you can actually achieve","Granting an autonomy tier to a feature instead of to each write path, so one low-stakes justification carries a high-stakes action along with it","Handing the agent a service token with the invoking user''s full scope, when the turn needs three operations and the user can perform three hundred","Enforcing tenant isolation in the query layer only, leaving the cache, trace store and logs as a boundary that was never actually drawn","Retrying a non-idempotent action after a timeout, converting an ambiguous failure into a confirmed duplicate side effect"],"flags":["Nobody in the room can list every action the system can take without opening the code, which means the action surface has never been designed, only accumulated","\"The model would never do that\" offered as a control, with no experiment behind it and no consequence if it turns out to be false","An agent whose tools include both a reader of customer-supplied text and anything that sends, posts or pays, with no boundary named between them","Undo described as \"we can restore from backup\", which is a recovery procedure and not a reversibility class","Autonomy described as a roadmap milestone (\"fully autonomous by Q3\") rather than as a per-action decision derived from what wrong costs"],"ask":["If a single turn were entirely attacker-authored, what is the worst thing it could do, in one sentence?","Which token does this action execute with, and is its scope narrower than the human who triggered it?","Which of our actions are genuinely irreversible, and what would it take to demote each one a tier?","Where does untrusted text enter the context, and what is the shortest path from there to something that sends bytes outward?","How is the tenant identifier carried through the cache key, the trace and the log, and who has tested that boundary from the other side?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"A support agent with six tools. Five are fine. One of them turns the whole system into an exfiltration channel, and the interesting part is what it costs to fix.<br><br>The tools: <code>search_kb</code> (internal articles), <code>read_ticket</code> (the customer''s own words), <code>read_crm</code> (contract value, renewal date, contacts), <code>create_ticket</code> and <code>update_ticket_status</code> (both trivially reversible), and <code>send_email</code>.<br><br>Run the trifecta check. <code>read_crm</code> puts private data in the turn. <code>read_ticket</code> puts attacker-writable text in the turn — a stranger typed that, which is the entire point of a support inbox. <code>send_email</code> moves bytes out of the boundary to an address chosen inside the turn. Private, untrusted, egress: <b>one turn, all three legs.</b> No rail changes that. A rail lowers the probability; the path exists at probability greater than zero, and at 12,000 tickets a month, greater than zero is a schedule.<br><br>Three options, priced.<br><br><b>Option 1: remove <code>send_email</code>.</b> The agent drafts, a human sends. The trifecta breaks cleanly — egress is gone. Now price it. The auto-reply path currently resolves 3,100 of 12,000 tickets a month with no human touch. Average human handling is 4.5 minutes, so removal costs 3,100 x 4.5 = 13,950 minutes, or <b>232.5 agent-hours a month</b>, and it deletes the feature''s headline number. Safe, and expensive enough that someone will overrule you in a quarter.<br><br><b>Option 2: narrow egress to an internal allowlist.</b> <code>send_email</code> may only reach addresses on your own domain. The trifecta breaks — an attacker cannot name the destination. It also breaks the product: the entire use case is replying to customers, who are by definition external. Value retained: approximately zero of the 232.5 hours. This is the option that looks responsible in a slide and ships nothing.<br><br><b>Option 3: resolve the recipient from data that predates the untrusted content.</b> <code>send_email</code> can only address participants already on the ticket when the turn began — a list built from the ticket record, not from anything the model produced. Anything else routes to a human. Measured over three months of history, 6.1% of replies went to an address not already on the ticket: 190 a month, needing 190 x 4.5 = 855 minutes = <b>14.3 agent-hours a month</b> of human time.<br><br>Compare: 232.5 hours retained for 14.3 hours spent. <b>94% of the value, 6% of the cost</b>, and the trifecta is genuinely broken rather than argued down — because the destination set is resolved from a source the attacker never wrote to. That last clause is the whole design, and it generalises: an allowlist is only a bulkhead when its contents are decided outside the compromised turn.<br><br>Now the tier arithmetic, which is where this stops being a story. Volume is 20,000 model-authored actions a month. In review, the business will own four bad actions a month — not zero, four, written down. The model measures 94.0% on your golden set.<br><br>Multiply out what escapes at each tier. Bad generations are 20,000 x 6% = 1,200 a month. Acting irreversibly, essentially all of them become bad actions: 1,200 against a tolerance of 4, a <b>300x</b> overshoot. Act-with-undo, where perhaps 20% are noticed and reversed in time: 240, still 60x over. Approval-gated, where a reviewer catches roughly 95%: 60, still 15x over. Suggest-only, where the human authors the artifact and the model merely proposes: 3.6, which <b>passes</b>.<br><br>Invert it and read the requirement instead. Irreversible demands 99.98% per generation. Act-with-undo demands 99.90%. Approval demands 99.60%. Suggest demands 93.3%. Your model is at 94.0%.<br><br>Nobody is going to hand you a 99.90% model this quarter, at any price. But the gap between \"unshippable\" and \"shipped\" here was never a modelling problem — it was one line in an action inventory. <b>A tier demotion is a five-point accuracy improvement you can deploy on Thursday.</b>","good":["Every write path in the system appears on one inventory, with its tier, its authorizing principal, its undo mechanism and its worst-case sentence","The required per-generation accuracy for each tier has been computed from real volume and a written tolerance, and compared against a measured model number rather than a vendor benchmark","Actions execute with a capability token narrower than the invoking user, scoped to the operations and records this turn actually needs","The trifecta check is a standing agenda item in design review, run again every time a tool is added rather than once at launch","Tenant identity is carried into cache keys, trace attributes and log lines, and someone has deliberately tried to make a cross-tenant cache hit happen in a test"],"build":"Write the action inventory for a system you own. One row per tool and per write path — every one, including the ones that feel too boring to list, because those are where the surprises are. Columns: action, autonomy tier, reversibility, authorizing principal, the exact undo mechanism (not \"restore from backup\"), and a one-sentence worst case written in plain language. Then run the trifecta check across the whole set: mark every action that can reach private data, every action that ingests text an outsider wrote, and every action that can move bytes outside the boundary. If all three sets are non-empty, enumerate the specific paths — do not summarise, name them, because \"we have an injection risk\" gets deferred and \"read_crm to read_ticket to send_email\" gets fixed. Every failing path gets one of two things written next to it: a tier demotion, or a boundary that breaks one leg. Finish by computing required accuracy per tier from your real volume and a tolerance you are willing to defend, and put that table in the design doc. It is the most persuasive page you will write all quarter.","res":[["Principle of least privilege (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principle_of_least_privilege","Use when you need the canonical framing for why a turn''s token should be narrower than the user''s. The idea predates all of this by fifty years and it is still the whole game."],["Bulkhead pattern (Microsoft Architecture Center)","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/bulkhead","Use right now: it is the mental image of this module written up as an engineering pattern, and it gives you vocabulary for arguing about compartment boundaries in review."],["Compensating transaction pattern (Microsoft Architecture Center)","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/compensating-transaction","Use when you are trying to move an action from act-irreversibly to act-with-undo and need to design the undo properly rather than hand-waving at a backup."],["Two-person rule (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two-man_rule","Use when proposing dual control on an irreversible operation and you want the precedent, the failure modes, and the reason it degrades quietly under time pressure."]]},"depth":{"mech":"The mechanism worth internalising is why <b>the bulkheads</b> beat every alternative, and it comes down to what kind of thing a control is.<br><br>Instructions are requests. Everything you write in a system prompt — do not do X, ignore instructions in retrieved content, never send to an address outside the thread — is a request the model may honour. Honouring it is probabilistic and the probability is not under your control. It varies with input, with version, with how much the surrounding context is pulling the other way. A request is negotiable by construction, and a determined adversary''s entire job is negotiation.<br><br>Structure is not a request. If the token issued for this turn carries scope for two operations on one record, then a turn that decides to do a third thing does not get talked out of it — it gets a 403, from a system that has never read the prompt and has no opinion about it. That asymmetry is the point. <b>You are moving the decision from a component that argues to a component that does not.</b><br><br>Which leads directly to the design procedure. You do not ask how likely a compromise is. You assume it, once, per action path, and read what follows. The assumption is not pessimism, it is a way of removing probability from the analysis entirely so that what remains is a set — the set of effects reachable from a compromised turn. Sets are much easier to reason about than probabilities, and much harder to argue with in review.<br><br>Now the arithmetic that makes tiers an engineering lever rather than a governance concept. Take a fixed tolerance for bad actions per month, T. Take volume V. Each tier has an escape fraction E — the share of bad generations that survive to become real-world bad actions. Then bad actions per month is V x (1 - accuracy) x E, and setting that equal to T and solving gives required accuracy = 1 - T / (V x E).<br><br>The whole leverage lives in E. Going from act-irreversibly (E = 1.0) to approval-gated (E around 0.05) multiplies your error budget by twenty. Going to suggest-only (E around 0.003) multiplies it by more than three hundred. Against a fixed tolerance, those multipliers land directly on the accuracy requirement — 99.98%, 99.60%, 93.3% — and the last of those is a number your current model already meets.<br><br>Two honest caveats, because this arithmetic is easy to abuse. First, E is not a constant you inherit; measure yours. Reviewer catch rates degrade under volume and time pressure, which means an approval gate is worth more in a calm quarter than on the Monday your traffic spikes, and a system that leans hard on human review has coupled its safety to its load. Second, undo only counts if the failure is <b>noticed</b>. An undo mechanism nobody triggers because nobody knew is a reversibility class you claimed and do not have — which is exactly the seam where this module hands back to the failure register, because \"noticed\" is a detectability score.<br><br>Then there is the trifecta, which is a reachability question and nothing more. Private data reachable, attacker-writable content reachable, egress present. Three legs, one turn. Break any leg and the path is gone: don''t put the private read in the same turn as the untrusted read, resolve egress destinations from data that predates the untrusted content, or remove the egress. Notice that none of those three moves is a filter, a classifier or a better prompt. They are all walls.","trade":[["Demote a tier vs invest in accuracy","Product capability now vs a model improvement you may never get","Chase accuracy and you may spend two quarters closing a gap the tier change closes on Thursday; demote reflexively and you ship a product whose whole value was the automation you just removed"],["Human approval gate vs structural containment","Flexibility of judgement vs a control that holds under load","Approval handles cases no rule anticipates, and its catch rate quietly collapses exactly when volume spikes; structural containment holds under any load and refuses the legitimate exception too"],["Narrow capability tokens vs one service identity","Containment vs operational simplicity","Per-turn scoped tokens mean a compromised turn does almost nothing, and they add issuance, rotation and debugging surface; a single broad identity is simple right up until one turn inherits everything the service can do"],["Allowlist resolved from pre-turn data vs allowlist checked at call time","Real containment vs the appearance of it","An allowlist the turn can influence is not a boundary, it is a suggestion with extra steps; resolving it from data that predates the untrusted content is what makes it structural"]],"scale":["Blast radius grows with tenancy, not with traffic: a per-user mistake at a thousand users is a support ticket, and the same mistake behind a shared cache or a shared index is one event affecting everyone, which is a completely different severity row","Approval-gate economics invert as volume rises - at 200 actions a day a reviewer is thorough and E stays low, at 20,000 a day the reviewer is a rubber stamp and E drifts toward the ungated number while your design doc still claims the old one","Every new tool multiplies the reachability graph rather than adding to it, so the trifecta check has to be re-run on tool addition, not on release - the sixth tool is what turned five safe ones into an exfiltration channel","Compensating actions need designing at the same time as the action, because retrofitting undo onto a write path that already ran a million times means reconciling a million rows you no longer have the inputs for"],"sec":["The turn''s token should be narrower than the invoking user in both scope and lifetime: the operations this task needs, on the records already in scope, expiring in minutes - a token that merely impersonates the user has contained nothing","Egress is broader than it looks. Email, webhooks, outbound HTTP, a URL rendered into a response the browser will fetch, an image source, a link a user is likely to click - each of those moves bytes outward, and each belongs on the inventory with the same scrutiny as an API call","Cross-tenant cache hits are leaks and they are invisible by construction, because the wrong tenant''s answer is a perfectly plausible answer - put the tenant identifier in the cache key and write the test that proves a miss, from both directions","Dual control degrades under time pressure in an entirely predictable way, so it belongs on operations that are rare and consequential, never on the hot path - a second approver who approves everything within four seconds has cost you latency and bought you a log line"],"interview":"The framing I would use in a review is this. We are not going to win the argument about whether bad instructions can reach the model. They can — anything the system reads is something someone can write, and that includes tickets, documents, emails and web pages. So instead of asking how we stop it, I want to ask what happens if we lose. Assume one turn is entirely attacker-authored. What is the worst thing that turn can do? If the answer makes people uncomfortable, we have a design problem, and no prompt is going to fix it, because you cannot patch away a capability you granted.<br><br>The way out is bulkheads. A ship survives a breach because the compartments are sealed, not because the hull is strong, and our version is the same: bound the set of actions reachable from a turn by structure rather than by instruction. A token that carries less authority than the person who triggered it. A recipient list resolved from the ticket record before any untrusted text arrived. A second human on anything irreversible.<br><br>And the part that usually changes the meeting is the arithmetic. If we act irreversibly at our volume, we need 99.98% per generation to stay inside our tolerance. If we put a human on it, we need 99.6%. If we demote to suggest, we need 93.3%, and we are already at 94. <b>A tier demotion is a model upgrade we cannot buy</b>, and it ships this week."},"patterns":{"intro":"Three artifacts in one file, none of which calls anything. The <code>Action</code> registry is the inventory you were going to write in a spreadsheet, except it computes. <code>trifecta_check</code> walks reachability and prints the specific offending path rather than a warning nobody acts on. And <code>required_accuracy</code> is the table that ends the \"can we just make the model better\" conversation, because it prices each tier in per-generation accuracy against your real volume.","code":"\"\"\"Action registry + blast-radius calculator.\n\nThree things, none of which touch a network:\n  1. An Action registry: every tool and write path with its reversibility,\n     reach, authorizing principal and egress flag. This IS the artifact.\n  2. trifecta_check(): walks what a single model turn can reach and prints the\n     specific offending path when private data + untrusted content + egress all\n     land inside one turn.\n  3. required_accuracy(): derives the per-generation correctness rate each\n     autonomy tier demands from your tolerance for bad actions. This is where a\n     tier demotion turns out to be worth more than a model upgrade you cannot buy.\n\nRun: python blast.py     (stdlib only, no network, deterministic)\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List\n\n# How much of a bad generation survives to become a real-world bad action.\n# These are YOUR numbers - measure them, do not inherit mine. The approval\n# figure is \"what fraction of bad drafts a reviewer waves through\", which is\n# never zero and gets worse as volume rises.\nTIER_ESCAPE = {\n    \"suggest\": 0.003,             # a human authors the artifact; model only proposes\n    \"draft-with-approval\": 0.05,  # human reviews every action before it fires\n    \"act-with-undo\": 0.20,        # acts, but a cheap reliable undo exists AND is noticed\n    \"act-irreversibly\": 1.00,     # money moves, mail leaves, rows vanish\n}\nTIER_ORDER = list(TIER_ESCAPE)\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Action:\n    name: str\n    tier: str                 # one of TIER_ORDER\n    reach: str                # self | tenant | cross_tenant | public\n    authorized_by: str        # end_user | service_account | elevated\n    egress: bool = False      # can it move bytes outside the trust boundary?\n    reads_private: bool = False\n    reads_untrusted: bool = False\n    undo: str = \"none\"\n    worst_case: str = \"\"\n\n    def reversibility(self) -> str:\n        return {\"suggest\": \"no side effect\",\n                \"draft-with-approval\": \"gated\",\n                \"act-with-undo\": \"reversible\",\n                \"act-irreversibly\": \"IRREVERSIBLE\"}[self.tier]\n\n\ndef trifecta_check(actions: List[Action]) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Private data reachable + untrusted content reachable + egress capability\n    in the same turn = an escalation path, however good the rails are.\"\"\"\n    private = [a for a in actions if a.reads_private]\n    untrusted = [a for a in actions if a.reads_untrusted]\n    egress = [a for a in actions if a.egress]\n    findings = []\n    if private and untrusted and egress:\n        for p in private:\n            for u in untrusted:\n                for e in egress:\n                    findings.append(\n                        \"  FAIL  {} (private) -> {} (attacker-writable) -> {} (egress, {})\"\n                        .format(p.name, u.name, e.name, e.reversibility()))\n    return findings\n\n\ndef required_accuracy(tier: str, tolerated_bad_per_month: float, volume: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Per-generation correctness this tier demands, given how many bad actions\n    per month you are willing to own.\"\"\"\n    escape = TIER_ESCAPE[tier]\n    allowed_bad_generations = tolerated_bad_per_month / escape\n    return max(0.0, 1.0 - allowed_bad_generations / volume)\n\n\ndef bad_actions_per_month(tier: str, accuracy: float, volume: float) -> float:\n    return volume * (1.0 - accuracy) * TIER_ESCAPE[tier]\n\n\nREGISTRY = [\n    Action(\"search_kb\", \"suggest\", \"tenant\", \"service_account\",\n           reads_private=False, undo=\"n/a\",\n           worst_case=\"Returns an unhelpful passage. Nobody notices.\"),\n    Action(\"read_ticket\", \"suggest\", \"tenant\", \"end_user\",\n           reads_untrusted=True, undo=\"n/a\",\n           worst_case=\"Ingests text a stranger wrote. This is the untrusted edge.\"),\n    Action(\"read_crm\", \"suggest\", \"tenant\", \"end_user\",\n           reads_private=True, undo=\"n/a\",\n           worst_case=\"Pulls contract value and contacts into the turn.\"),\n    Action(\"create_ticket\", \"act-with-undo\", \"tenant\", \"service_account\",\n           undo=\"close + audit note\",\n           worst_case=\"Noise in the queue. Recoverable in a click.\"),\n    Action(\"update_ticket_status\", \"act-with-undo\", \"tenant\", \"service_account\",\n           undo=\"revert status\",\n           worst_case=\"An SLA clock starts wrong. Recoverable.\"),\n    Action(\"send_email\", \"act-irreversibly\", \"public\", \"service_account\",\n           egress=True, undo=\"none\",\n           worst_case=\"Customer data leaves the boundary to an address the \"\n                      \"attacker chose. There is no unsend.\"),\n]\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"ACTION REGISTRY\")\n    print(\"{:<22} {:<20} {:<13} {:<7} {}\".format(\n        \"action\", \"reversibility\", \"reach\", \"egress\", \"authorized by\"))\n    for a in REGISTRY:\n        print(\"{:<22} {:<20} {:<13} {:<7} {}\".format(\n            a.name, a.reversibility(), a.reach, \"YES\" if a.egress else \"-\", a.authorized_by))\n\n    print(\"\\nLETHAL-TRIFECTA CHECK\")\n    findings = trifecta_check(REGISTRY)\n    if findings:\n        for f in findings:\n            print(f)\n        print(\"  {} escalation path(s). Break ONE leg or accept the exposure.\"\n              .format(len(findings)))\n    else:\n        print(\"  PASS - no single turn reaches private data, untrusted content and egress.\")\n\n    volume = 20_000        # model-authored actions per month\n    tolerated = 4          # bad actions per month you will own in a review\n    model_accuracy = 0.94  # measured on your golden set, not the vendor''s\n\n    print(\"\\nREQUIRED ACCURACY BY TIER \"\n          \"(volume {:,}/mo, tolerance {} bad actions/mo)\".format(volume, tolerated))\n    print(\"{:<22} {:>8} {:>12} {:>14} {}\".format(\n        \"tier\", \"escape\", \"required\", \"bad @ 94.0%\", \"verdict\"))\n    for tier in reversed(TIER_ORDER):\n        req = required_accuracy(tier, tolerated, volume)\n        bad = bad_actions_per_month(tier, model_accuracy, volume)\n        verdict = \"PASS\" if model_accuracy >= req else \"FAIL by {:.0f}x\".format(bad / tolerated)\n        print(\"{:<22} {:>8.1%} {:>12.2%} {:>14.1f} {}\".format(\n            tier, TIER_ESCAPE[tier], req, bad, verdict))\n\n    print(\"\\nA tier demotion is a model upgrade you cannot buy:\")\n    for hi, lo in ((\"act-irreversibly\", \"act-with-undo\"),\n                   (\"act-with-undo\", \"draft-with-approval\"),\n                   (\"draft-with-approval\", \"suggest\")):\n        r_hi = required_accuracy(hi, tolerated, volume)\n        r_lo = required_accuracy(lo, tolerated, volume)\n        print(\"  {:<20} -> {:<20} requirement {:.2%} -> {:.2%} \"\n              \"({:.0f}x less error budget spent)\".format(\n                  hi, lo, r_hi, r_lo, (1 - r_lo) / (1 - r_hi)))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","notes":["<b>TIER_ESCAPE is the only interesting number in the file and it is the one you must replace.</b> These are placeholders. Measure your reviewer catch rate on real traffic, and measure it again during a busy week - the approval figure is not a constant, it is a function of load","The <code>act-with-undo</code> escape fraction of 0.20 encodes an assumption worth saying out loud: undo only helps when the mistake is <b>noticed</b>. If your detection score for that failure mode is 8 or higher, set this number close to 1.0, because you have an undo nobody will ever trigger","<code>trifecta_check</code> prints one line per offending path rather than a single summary verdict, on purpose. \"You have an injection risk\" gets deferred to next quarter; \"read_crm -> read_ticket -> send_email\" gets fixed on Thursday","Egress is defined broadly in the registry and should be defined broadly in yours: email, webhooks, outbound fetches, and any URL rendered into a response that a browser will resolve. If bytes can leave, the flag is true","The final block prints the requirement change for each single-tier demotion. That ratio - how much error budget the demotion buys - is usually the most persuasive number in the whole design doc, so put it in the doc rather than leaving it in the terminal"],"debug":[["trifecta_check passes but the system still feels wrong","Reachability is being read per tool rather than per turn, and two tools that are individually safe are composed inside one turn","Model the turn, not the tool. If a single turn can call both, the legs are joined - split them into separate turns with separate tokens, or accept the path in writing"],["Required accuracy comes out above 99.99% for every tier","Tolerance is set to zero or near-zero, which makes the formula demand perfection and the table useless","Set a tolerance you would actually defend in a review. Zero is not a tolerance, it is a refusal to choose, and it turns the calculator into a machine for generating impossible requirements"],["The suggest tier passes easily, so everything gets demoted to suggest","The escape fraction for suggest assumes a human genuinely authors the artifact, which stops being true when the UI is a one-click accept","If accepting is one click and the default, you are running an approval gate at best. Re-measure E against what users actually do rather than what the tier is called"],["A tool''s worst_case sentence is blank or reads ''minor issue''","The row has not actually been thought about, and blank worst cases cluster on exactly the tools people assume are boring","Make the sentence mandatory and make it name a real account on a real bad day. Half of all tier demotions get discovered while writing these sentences"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:blast', 'paid', '[{"q":"An agent can read CRM records, read customer-written tickets, and send email. Rails are in place. What is the correct architectural read?","o":["Acceptable, since the injection classifier reduces the risk to a manageable residual level","An escalation path exists in every turn, and one of the three legs has to be broken","Acceptable, provided outbound email is logged and reviewed by a human within the hour","A monitoring problem, since the risk only materialises when an actual payload arrives"],"a":1,"exp":"Private data, attacker-writable content and egress in one turn is the lethal trifecta, and rails only lower the probability of a path that structurally exists. At meaningful volume, a non-zero probability is a schedule. Break a leg or accept it in writing."},{"q":"Volume is 20,000 actions a month, tolerance is 4 bad actions, and the action is irreversible. What per-generation accuracy does that demand?","o":["99.98%, because essentially every bad generation becomes a bad action at this tier","99.20%, since the tolerance of four spreads across the full month''s action volume","96.00%, which is a realistic target for a well-tuned model on a narrow task domain","99.60%, matching the standard reliability bar for any customer-facing write path"],"a":0,"exp":"Required accuracy is 1 - T / (V x E). At the irreversible tier E is 1.0, so it is 1 - 4/20,000 = 99.98%. Compare that to the same tolerance behind an approval gate, where E drops to about 0.05 and the requirement falls to 99.60%."},{"q":"Why should the token an agent action executes with be scoped narrower than the user who triggered the turn?","o":["Narrow tokens are cheaper to rotate, which lowers the operational cost of key management","Provider rate limits are applied per token, so narrower scopes spread load more evenly","A compromised turn should not be able to do everything its human is permitted to do","Audit logs are easier to read when each action carries its own dedicated credential"],"a":2,"exp":"Impersonating the user contains nothing: the turn inherits everything the human could do. A token scoped to the operations and records this task needs turns containment into a structural fact enforced by a system that has never read the prompt."},{"q":"A semantic cache keyed on question text serves tenant A''s stored answer to tenant B. Why is this scored as a severe and near-undetectable row?","o":["Cache hits skip the guardrails, so the answer is never checked before it is returned","The answer is plausible and correct-looking, so nothing downstream flags it as wrong","Cache entries have no trace attached, making post-incident reconstruction impossible","Cross-tenant reads are logged as errors, but the logs are sampled at too low a rate"],"a":1,"exp":"The leaked answer looks exactly like a correct answer, so no rail, reviewer or user reports it. That combination of high severity and high detectability score forces a structural fix: the tenant identifier belongs inside the cache key, with a test proving the miss."},{"q":"Removing send_email costs 232 agent-hours a month. Narrowing recipients to ticket participants costs 14 hours. What makes the second option a real bulkhead?","o":["The recipient list is resolved from data that existed before the untrusted content arrived","The allowlist is checked at call time, immediately before the outbound request is issued","The remaining volume is small enough that a human can review each outbound message","The model is instructed to only address participants already present on the ticket record"],"a":0,"exp":"An allowlist is only a boundary when the compromised turn cannot influence its contents. Resolving recipients from the ticket record as it stood before the turn began means the attacker never had a write to the destination set. Checking at call time against a list the turn produced is theatre."},{"q":"An agent times out mid-way through a non-idempotent write and the harness retries. What has that retry actually accomplished?","o":["It has recovered cleanly, since the original write never committed before the timeout","It has converted an ambiguous outcome into a confirmed duplicate side effect","It has doubled the latency budget for this request but left the data model consistent","It has triggered the compensating action, which will reconcile both writes on the next pass"],"a":1,"exp":"A timeout tells you nothing about whether the write landed. Retrying without an idempotency key turns \"maybe once\" into \"probably twice\". Idempotency keys plus a designed compensating action are what let a retry be safe and a mistake be undone."},{"q":"Where does dual control belong on a system with a mix of high-volume and consequential operations?","o":["On the highest-volume path, where the greatest number of bad outcomes can be prevented","On every write path, since a uniform policy is far simpler to audit and to explain","On rare irreversible operations, because it degrades badly when applied under load","On the read paths that touch private data, where a leak cannot be undone afterwards"],"a":2,"exp":"A second approver who clears everything in four seconds has cost you latency and bought a log line. Dual control works where the operation is rare enough that the reviewer stays engaged, and irreversible enough that the delay is obviously worth it."},{"q":"Your model measures 94.0%. Required accuracy is 99.90% at act-with-undo and 93.3% at suggest. What is the honest recommendation?","o":["Ship at act-with-undo and close the gap with prompt work over the following two sprints","Ship at suggest now, since a tier demotion is an accuracy gain you cannot otherwise buy","Hold the launch until an evaluation harness can verify the model reaches 99.90% reliably","Ship at act-with-undo behind a feature flag while a larger model is procured and tested"],"a":1,"exp":"Nobody is delivering a 99.90% model this quarter at any price, and the gap was never a modelling problem. Demoting the tier multiplies the error budget by more than three hundred and lands the requirement below what you already measure."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:capacity', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"capacity","block":"b2","title":"Capacity, Saturation & the Degradation Ladder","tag":"What your system does at 10x is a design decision, not an emergent property","why":"Every system has a behaviour at ten times its normal load. Yours has one right now. The only question is whether anybody chose it, or whether it is going to be revealed to you at 09:14 on a Monday by a queue nobody sized and a provider quota nobody negotiated. Capacity for AI systems is not a CPU problem — it is a quota-and-concurrency problem, and the arithmetic is unusually tractable: tokens per task, tasks per second, a peak multiplier, a service time, and Little''s Law. Do it in an afternoon and you get a number to negotiate with, a worker pool that is right rather than lucky, and the artifact this module actually exists to produce: a written ladder of degraded states, each one signed off by the person who owns the customer relationship, <b>before</b> anyone needs it.","mental":"The image is <b>the brownout ladder</b>.<br><br>Power grids do not go from on to off. When supply cannot meet demand, the operator walks down a pre-agreed sequence: voltage reduction first, then interruptible industrial customers, then rotating blocks — each rung defined years in advance, each with a named authority who signed it, each with a known and accepted consequence. Nobody invents a rung at 3am. The decisions were made in daylight, by people who understood what each one costs, and the night shift''s job is to execute the ladder, not to design it.<br><br>Your system needs exactly that, and almost certainly does not have it. What it has instead is a queue somebody added to absorb bursts, a timeout that fires at an arbitrary number, and an implicit final rung — fall over — that nobody wrote down and nobody authorised.<br><br>So write the ladder. Five rungs, each with a trigger, what turns off, what the customer sees, what the quality delta is, and who pre-authorised it. <b>Every rung you have not written is a rung someone will improvise at 3am, and improvised rungs are how an outage becomes an incident.</b>","concepts":[["Capacity is the smaller of your fleet and your quota","Your worker pool sets one ceiling and the provider''s tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute grants set another, and the binding constraint is whichever is lower. Teams size the fleet carefully and discover the quota during the incident. Compute both numbers, in the same units, on the same page — tasks per minute — and put the min() in the design doc, because that is your real capacity and nothing else is."],["Required throughput comes straight off the spec card","Volume times tokens per task gives you tokens per minute, and the only subtlety is that output tokens usually count for more against a throughput meter than input tokens do. Carry an output weight as a parameter, apply the peak multiplier from the spec card, add headroom, and you have the number you take to a quota negotiation — which has a lead time, and therefore belongs on the schedule, not in the launch week."],["Little''s Law sizes the pool before you guess at it","L = lambda x W. Arrival rate times time in system gives the number of tasks in flight, and that is the floor on concurrency — not a target, a floor. At 3.7 tasks a second and 9 seconds each, 33 things are in flight at all times. Any pool smaller than 34 cannot keep up at all, and a pool of exactly 34 is running at 98% utilisation, which is a different kind of disaster."],["The queueing cliff","Wait time is not linear in utilisation, it is asymptotic. Same load, same service time, only the worker count changing: 42 workers gives 79% utilisation and a 0.11 second wait; 36 workers gives 93% and 1.86 seconds; 34 gives 98% and 11.7 seconds. Six workers moved and the wait went up a hundredfold. Above roughly 80%, utilisation stops being an efficiency metric and becomes a latency risk."],["A queue without admission control makes p95 worse for everyone","This is the counter-intuitive one. A queue added to absorb bursts does not create capacity; it converts rejection into waiting, and it distributes that waiting across every user including the ones who would otherwise have been served instantly. If arrivals exceed service rate, backlog grows without bound and every request inherits the whole accumulated delay. A queue is only a lever when it has a depth cap and an admission rule that refuses honestly at the boundary."],["Four levers, four different customer costs","Shed refuses some requests entirely so the rest stay fast. Queue makes everyone wait so nobody is refused. Degrade serves everyone at lower quality. Borrow moves traffic to a second provider or a smaller model, paying in cost or in quality. They are not interchangeable and they are not ranked — they trade different currencies, and choosing between them at 3am with a director on the call is how an outage becomes an incident."],["The rung is only real when it has all five fields","Trigger, what gets disabled, what the customer sees, the quality delta, and the named pre-authorizer. Four fields is a suggestion. The fifth is the one that makes it executable at 3am, because the on-call engineer is not authorised to decide that support answers get worse tonight — but they are authorised to execute a rung that the VP of Support signed in March."]],"mistakes":["Sizing the worker fleet from latency requirements and discovering the provider quota is the binding constraint during the first real peak","Adding a queue to absorb bursts with no depth cap and no admission rule, which converts a fast failure for a few into a slow failure for everyone","Targeting high utilisation as an efficiency win, when above roughly 80% the queueing curve turns the last few points of utilisation into most of your p95","Writing a degradation plan with a trigger and an action but no named pre-authorizer, so the on-call engineer at 3am has a runbook they are not empowered to execute","Treating failover as a pure capacity lever when the failover target has never been evaluated on your task, which turns a capacity incident into an undetected quality incident"],"flags":["Nobody can state the system''s tokens-per-minute ceiling from memory, which means it has never been compared against the peak the spec card already predicted","\"We added a queue\" offered as the answer to a load problem, with no depth cap, no admission rule, and no statement of what happens when the queue is full","A degradation plan that goes straight from fully working to an error page, with no rungs in between and no quality delta stated for any of them","Failover configured to a provider or model that has never been run against the golden set, so the save silently degrades quality instead of preserving it","Peak multipliers described qualitatively (\"Mondays are busy\") when the spec card already carries a number that could have been multiplied through weeks ago"],"ask":["What is our tokens-per-minute ceiling today, and what multiple of our observed peak is it?","At what utilisation does our worker pool run at peak, and what is the wait time two points above that?","If the queue fills, what happens - and is that behaviour written down or is it whatever the runtime does by default?","Which rung of the degradation ladder are we authorised to pull without waking anyone, and who signed it?","Has the failover target been run against the golden set, and what was the quality delta?"],"estMin":50},"deep":{"worked":"The spec card says 40,000 tasks a day, Monday peak 8x, 3,200 tokens per task. Forty minutes of arithmetic turns that into a quota request, a worker count, and a ladder.<br><br><b>Step one: effective tokens.</b> 3,200 splits as roughly 2,800 in and 400 out. Output tokens weigh more against a throughput meter than input tokens do — carry the weight as a parameter rather than a constant, since it is provider policy and it changes. At a weight of 4.0: 2,800 + (400 x 4.0) = <b>4,400 effective tokens per task</b>. Skipping this step is how teams under-request by 35% and find out on a Monday.<br><br><b>Step two: throughput.</b> 40,000 / 86,400 seconds = 0.463 tasks a second. Times 4,400 effective tokens times 60 = <b>122,222 TPM steady</b>, at 27.8 RPM. Apply the 8x peak: <b>977,778 TPM, 222 RPM</b>. Add 25% headroom for the peak you have not seen yet: <b>1,222,222 TPM</b>. Against a tier table of 400k / 800k / 2M, the smallest tier that fits is the 2M one. That is the ask, and it has a procurement lead time measured in weeks, which is why this arithmetic belongs at design time rather than launch week.<br><br><b>Step three: the pool.</b> Little''s Law, L = lambda x W. At peak, lambda is 3.70 tasks a second and a task takes 9.0 seconds end to end, so L = <b>33.3 tasks in flight at all times</b>. That is a floor, not a target — a pool of 34 runs at 98% utilisation.<br><br>Which is where the cliff shows up. Same load, same service time, only the worker count changing:<br>34 workers, 98.0% utilisation, <b>11.73s</b> mean wait, 43 tasks deep.<br>36 workers, 92.6%, <b>1.86s</b>, 7 deep.<br>38 workers, 87.7%, <b>0.64s</b>.<br>40 workers, 83.3%, <b>0.26s</b>.<br>42 workers, 79.4%, <b>0.11s</b>.<br><br>Six workers separate a 0.11 second wait from an 11.73 second one — a factor of a hundred and six, from an 18% change in fleet size. Nothing else moved. This is why \"we run hot for efficiency\" is a latency decision disguised as a cost decision. Take 42.<br><br><b>Step four: the number that ruins it.</b> 42 workers at 9 seconds is 280 tasks a minute. But procurement came back with the 800k tier, not the 2M one. 800,000 / 4,400 = <b>182 tasks a minute</b>. Real capacity is min(280, 182) = <b>182</b>. The fleet is not the constraint and never was.<br><br><b>Step five: run it at 12x and read the customer''s experience.</b> 12x is 5.56 tasks a second, 333 a minute, against 182. Demand exceeds service by 151 a minute. If you simply queue with no cap, backlog grows by 151 every minute — 9,060 an hour — and at 182 a minute of drain, the task arriving at 09:20 is waiting <b>fifty minutes</b> behind work that arrived while everyone was still drinking coffee. That is the counter-intuitive result stated as arithmetic: <b>the queue did not absorb the burst, it converted a fast refusal for 45% of users into a slow one for all of them.</b><br><br>Now the ladder, minute by minute (the simulator in the Patterns tab prints exactly this). Rung 1, cache-only: novel queries only, effective demand drops to 227, still over. Rung 2, smaller model: capacity rises to 291, demand 227, utilisation 78% — <b>stable</b>. A 12x Monday is absorbed at rung 2 and the customer''s experience is a slightly blunter answer nobody writes in about.<br><br>Push it to 26x, which is what the incident actually looked like. The ladder climbs: rung 3 deflects the top intents to templates, rung 4 turns on admission control and starts a bounded queue with an email, rung 5 begins refusing honestly with a retry-after. Backlog peaks at 229 tasks and drains by minute 39. Total dropped: <b>9 tasks</b>. Pinned at rung 0 with no ladder at all, the same spike drops <b>7,433</b>.<br><br>Nine versus seven thousand four hundred. The difference is not capacity, it is that somebody wrote five rungs down in daylight and got them signed.","good":["The design doc states capacity as min(worker pool, provider quota) in tasks per minute, with both numbers shown and the binding one named","Peak utilisation for the worker pool sits below the cliff by design, and the wait time two points above the target is written down next to it","Every queue in the system has a depth cap and a stated behaviour when full, and that behaviour is a deliberate refusal rather than whatever the runtime does","The degradation ladder exists as a document with five rungs and all five fields, and the pre-authorizer column contains names rather than roles","The failover target has been run against the golden set, and the quality delta is a number in the runbook rather than an assumption in someone''s head"],"build":"Write the degradation ladder for one real feature. Five rungs between fully working and honestly refusing, and every rung gets all five fields: the trigger that engages it (a number, not a feeling), exactly what turns off, what the customer sees and is told, the quality delta you expect, and the name of the person who pre-authorised it. Take it to that person and get the sign-off in writing — that conversation is the actual deliverable, because a rung the on-call engineer is not empowered to pull is decoration.<br><br>Then run a game day. Pick rung 3, engage it deliberately in staging with real-shaped traffic, and watch what actually happens. Expect to be wrong about at least one thing: the cache hit rate under degraded conditions is usually lower than you assumed, the smaller model''s output shape often breaks a downstream parser nobody tested against it, and the customer-facing copy for the rung frequently does not exist at all. Every one of those is cheaper to discover on a Tuesday afternoon than during the event the ladder was written for.","res":[["Little''s Law (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little%27s_law","Use right now: L = lambda x W is the single highest-leverage formula in this module, and this is the shortest correct statement of what it does and does not assume."],["Google SRE Book - Handling Overload","https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/","Use when you are choosing between shedding, queueing and degrading. It is the clearest published treatment of why graceful degradation has to be designed before the load arrives."],["Google SRE Book - Addressing Cascading Failures","https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/","Use when your retries, queues and timeouts are interacting badly. Most capacity incidents are actually cascade incidents, and this chapter names the specific mechanisms."],["M/M/c queue (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M/M/c_queue","Use when someone challenges the cliff numbers and you need the model behind them, including the assumptions it makes that your real traffic will violate."]]},"depth":{"mech":"The mechanism behind <b>the brownout ladder</b> is worth taking apart, because two separate pieces of arithmetic drive it and people usually only know one.<br><br>The first is the capacity ceiling, and its shape is unfamiliar. In a classic service you scale out until CPU stops being the constraint. Here your fleet is rarely the constraint at all — the provider''s tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute grants are, and those do not scale by adding machines. They scale by a conversation with a lead time. So the capacity number you carry in your head has to be min(fleet, quota), expressed in the same unit both ways, and the second term is the one that moves on someone else''s schedule. This is why capacity work is a schedule dependency and not an operational chore: a quota you need in March is a request you make in January.<br><br>The second is the queueing curve, and this is the piece that produces genuinely counter-intuitive results. Mean wait in an M/M/c system is governed by the gap between servers and offered load, and as that gap closes, wait rises asymptotically rather than linearly. Concretely, at 33.3 erlangs of offered load: 42 servers is 79% utilisation and 0.11 seconds of wait; drop to 36 and you are at 93% and 1.86 seconds; drop to 34 and you are at 98% and 11.73 seconds. Six servers, a hundredfold change. Nothing about the load moved.<br><br>Two consequences follow that are worth stating plainly. <b>Utilisation above roughly 80% is a latency decision, not an efficiency win</b> — the last few points buy you very little cost and most of your tail. And a small fleet reduction that looks harmless on an average-latency dashboard can be catastrophic at p95, because the average is dominated by the fast path and the tail is where the queue lives.<br><br>Now the part that most systems get backwards. A queue does not create capacity. It converts refusal into waiting. If arrivals exceed service rate, the backlog grows without bound — at 333 arrivals a minute against 182 of service, it grows by 151 a minute, 9,060 an hour — and every request that joins inherits the entire accumulated delay. The user who would have been served instantly at 09:03 is now waiting behind everything that arrived since. So an unbounded queue does not protect p95, it destroys it, and it does so for everyone rather than for the subset who would have been refused. <b>A queue is a lever only when it has a depth cap and an admission rule</b>: accept while there is room, refuse honestly and immediately when there is not. Admission control is what turns a queue from a way of hiding overload into a way of managing it.<br><br>Which brings you to the four levers, and the reason they are four rather than one. Shed refuses some so the rest stay fast — you have chosen who loses. Queue makes everyone wait so nobody is refused — you have chosen that everyone loses a little, which is not obviously better. Degrade serves everyone worse — you have spent quality instead of latency. Borrow moves traffic to another provider or a smaller model — you have spent money, or quality you have not measured. Four different currencies, and no ordering between them that holds across products. That is precisely why the choice cannot be made during the incident: it is a product decision wearing an infrastructure costume, and the person qualified to make it is asleep.<br><br>Hence the ladder, and hence the fifth field. The trigger, the disabled feature, the customer-visible effect and the quality delta are all engineering. The pre-authorizer is not, and it is the one that makes the other four executable at 3am. An on-call engineer is not empowered to decide that every support answer gets worse tonight. They are entirely empowered to execute rung 3, which the VP of Support signed in March. The ladder''s real function is <b>converting a judgement call under pressure into an execution step</b>.<br><br>One last piece: failover is a capacity lever, and it is the one that lies to you. Moving traffic to a second provider preserves availability and says nothing about quality, so an un-evaluated failover target turns a capacity incident into a quality incident that nobody detects — a silent 200 at fleet scale. Run the failover target against your golden set before you need it, write the delta in the runbook, and now it is a rung with a known quality cost rather than a save with an unknown one.","trade":[["Shed vs queue at saturation","Who absorbs the loss","Shedding refuses a subset and keeps everyone else fast, and you have to defend choosing who loses; queueing spreads the delay across everyone and quietly destroys p95 for users who would have been served instantly"],["Degrade to a smaller model vs reject honestly","A worse answer vs no answer","Degrading keeps the product working at a quality delta you should have measured, and risks shipping answers below your quality bar without saying so; rejecting is honest, costs you the interaction, and is far easier to explain afterwards"],["Headroom vs utilisation target","Cost per task vs tail latency","Running at 90% looks efficient on a cost dashboard and puts you a few points from the cliff; running at 70% is visibly wasteful and is what buys you a p95 that survives a peak nobody predicted"],["Multi-provider failover vs a single provider with a deeper ladder","Availability vs quality certainty and operational surface","Failover preserves uptime and imports a second quality profile, a second quota to negotiate and a second thing to evaluate; a deeper ladder keeps one known quality profile and accepts that a provider outage is visible to customers"]],"scale":["Quota is a schedule dependency with a lead time measured in weeks, so the peak-TPM number belongs in the design doc rather than the launch checklist - a quota you need in March is a conversation you start in January","Peak multipliers are usually larger than teams remember, because memory averages over the hour while the queue experiences the minute - derive the multiplier from per-minute data and expect it to be roughly double the number people quote from feel","The cliff moves as service time moves, so anything that lengthens a task (a longer chain, a bigger context, a slower model tier) raises offered load in erlangs and silently pushes a comfortable pool toward the edge without a single extra request arriving","Batch and interactive traffic must not share a quota pool without priority, or the nightly job will eat the interactive envelope at exactly the wrong moment - separate keys, separate limits, or an explicit priority scheme"],"sec":["Rate limits are a security control as much as a capacity one: per-tenant admission caps stop one compromised or malformed account from consuming the envelope everyone else depends on, which is a denial of service you inflicted on yourself","Degraded rungs must not degrade authorisation - a smaller model, a template path or a cache-only mode still has to carry tenant scoping, and the rungs are exactly where that gets forgotten because they were written under time pressure","An unbounded queue is a resource-exhaustion surface, since anything that can enqueue cheaply can push the backlog past the point where legitimate traffic is unrecoverable; the depth cap is the control","Failover targets need their own security review, not an inherited one - a second provider means a second data-handling agreement, a second retention policy and a second place your customers'' text now lives"],"interview":"The way I would put it in a planning meeting is that our behaviour at ten times load already exists — we just have not read it yet. And the first thing to get straight is that our ceiling probably is not our fleet. It is the tokens-per-minute our provider has agreed to, which does not scale by adding machines, it scales by a conversation with a lead time. So real capacity is the smaller of the two, and I want that number in the doc.<br><br>Then there is one result that always surprises people, so I would put it up front. Adding a queue to absorb bursts does not add capacity. It converts refusing a few people quickly into making everybody wait. If work arrives faster than we finish it, the backlog just grows and every request inherits the whole accumulated delay, so our median user gets slower in order that nobody gets told no. A queue only helps when it has a depth cap and an admission rule that refuses honestly at the boundary.<br><br>And then the actual deliverable, which is the ladder. Grids never go from on to off — they shed load in pre-agreed rungs, decided in daylight by people with the authority to decide them. We need the same: five rungs between working and honestly refusing, and each one has to say what turns off, what the customer sees, what the quality cost is, and <b>who signed it</b>. That last field is the whole point. At 3am the on-call engineer is not authorised to decide that support answers get worse tonight, but they are absolutely authorised to pull rung 3 that the VP signed in March."},"patterns":{"intro":"Three calculators and no network. The planner turns spec-card numbers into a quota you can request and a worker count you can defend. The M/M/c approximation prints the cliff as a table so you can see the hundredfold jump rather than be told about it. And the ladder simulator takes an arrival spike and prints which rung engages minute by minute, with a counterfactual run pinned at rung 0 so you can price the ladder itself. No prices anywhere: capacity is tokens, seconds and workers.","code":"\"\"\"Capacity planner, queueing-cliff calculator, and degradation-ladder simulator.\n\nPrices are NOT in this file on purpose - capacity is a quota and concurrency\nproblem, and the one dated price table lives elsewhere. Everything here is\ntokens, seconds and workers.\n\n  1. plan()        required TPM/RPM from volume + token profile, output-weighted,\n                   with a peak multiplier and headroom, checked against a quota\n                   table you pass in.\n  2. erlang_wait() M/M/c approximation: wait time at a given utilisation,\n                   printed as a table so you can see the cliff rather than be\n                   told about it.\n  3. ladder()      arrival-spike simulator that prints which rung engages\n                   minute by minute, with hysteresis so it does not flap.\n\nRun: python capacity.py     (stdlib only, no network, seeded)\n\"\"\"\n\nimport random\nfrom math import factorial\n\nSEED = 7\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. planning\n\ndef plan(tasks_per_day, in_tokens, out_tokens, output_weight,\n         peak_multiplier, headroom, service_seconds):\n    \"\"\"output_weight is how many input-token-equivalents one output token costs\n    against your provider''s throughput meter. Pass 1.0 if yours meters flat.\"\"\"\n    effective = in_tokens + out_tokens * output_weight\n    tasks_per_sec = tasks_per_day / 86400.0\n    steady_tpm = tasks_per_sec * effective * 60.0\n    steady_rpm = tasks_per_sec * 60.0\n    peak_tpm = steady_tpm * peak_multiplier\n    peak_rpm = steady_rpm * peak_multiplier\n    offered_load = tasks_per_sec * peak_multiplier * service_seconds  # erlangs\n    return {\n        \"effective_tokens_per_task\": effective,\n        \"tasks_per_sec\": tasks_per_sec,\n        \"steady_tpm\": steady_tpm, \"steady_rpm\": steady_rpm,\n        \"peak_tpm\": peak_tpm, \"peak_rpm\": peak_rpm,\n        \"ask_tpm\": peak_tpm * headroom, \"ask_rpm\": peak_rpm * headroom,\n        \"offered_load_erlangs\": offered_load,\n    }\n\n\ndef check_quota(ask_tpm, ask_rpm, quota_tiers):\n    \"\"\"quota_tiers: [(name, tpm, rpm), ...] - passed in, never hardcoded.\"\"\"\n    for name, tpm, rpm in quota_tiers:\n        if tpm >= ask_tpm and rpm >= ask_rpm:\n            return name, tpm, rpm\n    return None, None, None\n\n\n# -------------------------------------------------------------- 2. the cliff\n\ndef erlang_c(servers, load):\n    if load >= servers:\n        return 1.0\n    head = sum(load ** k / factorial(k) for k in range(servers))\n    tail = load ** servers / factorial(servers) * (servers / (servers - load))\n    return tail / (head + tail)\n\n\ndef erlang_wait(servers, load, service_seconds):\n    \"\"\"Mean seconds spent waiting BEFORE service starts (M/M/c).\"\"\"\n    if load >= servers:\n        return float(\"inf\")\n    return erlang_c(servers, load) * service_seconds / (servers - load)\n\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------- 3. the ladder\n\nRUNGS = [\n    (\"0 full\", 1.00, 1.00),\n    (\"1 cache-only\", 0.68, 1.00),\n    (\"2 smaller model\", 0.68, 1.60),\n    (\"3 template/deflect\", 0.45, 1.60),\n    (\"4 queue+notify\", 0.45, 1.60),\n    (\"5 honest reject\", 0.45, 1.60),\n]\nESCALATE_AT = 0.85\nDEESCALATE_AT = 0.45\nCOOLDOWN = 4\nQUEUE_CAP = 400  # tasks; past this, admission control refuses rather than lies\n\n\ndef ladder(arrivals_per_min, base_capacity_per_min, frozen=False):\n    \"\"\"frozen=True pins rung 0: the counterfactual where nobody pre-agreed\n    anything and the system just drops what it cannot serve.\"\"\"\n    rung, calm, backlog = 0, 0, 0.0\n    rows = []\n    for minute, arriving in enumerate(arrivals_per_min, start=1):\n        _, demand_mult, cap_mult = RUNGS[rung]\n        demand = arriving * demand_mult + backlog\n        capacity = base_capacity_per_min * cap_mult\n        util = demand / capacity\n\n        shed = 0.0\n        if rung >= 5 and demand > capacity + QUEUE_CAP:\n            shed = demand - (capacity + QUEUE_CAP)\n            demand -= shed\n        served = min(demand, capacity)\n        queued = demand - served\n        backlog = queued if rung >= 4 else 0.0\n        if rung < 4:\n            shed += queued  # no admission control below rung 4: overflow drops\n\n        rows.append((minute, arriving, demand, capacity, util,\n                     RUNGS[rung][0], backlog, shed))\n\n        if frozen:\n            continue\n        if util > ESCALATE_AT and rung < len(RUNGS) - 1:\n            rung += 1\n            calm = 0\n        elif util < DEESCALATE_AT and rung > 0:\n            calm += 1\n            if calm >= COOLDOWN:\n                rung -= 1\n                calm = 0\n        else:\n            calm = 0\n    return rows\n\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------- report\n\ndef main():\n    rng = random.Random(SEED)\n    quota_tiers = [(\"tier A\", 400_000, 400), (\"tier B\", 800_000, 1_000),\n                   (\"tier C\", 2_000_000, 4_000)]\n\n    p = plan(tasks_per_day=40_000, in_tokens=2_800, out_tokens=400,\n             output_weight=4.0, peak_multiplier=8.0, headroom=1.25,\n             service_seconds=9.0)\n\n    print(\"CAPACITY PLAN\")\n    print(\"  effective tokens/task   {:,.0f}  (2,800 in + 400 out x 4.0 weight)\"\n          .format(p[\"effective_tokens_per_task\"]))\n    print(\"  steady                  {:>10,.0f} TPM   {:>7,.1f} RPM\"\n          .format(p[\"steady_tpm\"], p[\"steady_rpm\"]))\n    print(\"  Monday peak (8x)        {:>10,.0f} TPM   {:>7,.1f} RPM\"\n          .format(p[\"peak_tpm\"], p[\"peak_rpm\"]))\n    print(\"  ask with 25% headroom   {:>10,.0f} TPM   {:>7,.1f} RPM\"\n          .format(p[\"ask_tpm\"], p[\"ask_rpm\"]))\n    tier, tpm, rpm = check_quota(p[\"ask_tpm\"], p[\"ask_rpm\"], quota_tiers)\n    print(\"  smallest tier that fits: {} ({:,} TPM / {:,} RPM)\"\n          .format(tier, tpm, rpm))\n\n    load = p[\"offered_load_erlangs\"]\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  offered load at peak    {:.1f} erlangs  (L = lambda x W)\"\n          .format(load))\n    print(\"  {:.0f} tasks are in flight at all times - the floor on worker count.\"\n          .format(load))\n\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"QUEUEING CLIFF - mean wait before service starts, 9.0s service time\")\n    print(\"  workers | utilisation |     wait | queue depth\")\n    for c in (34, 36, 38, 40, 42, 46, 52):\n        util = load / c\n        w = erlang_wait(c, load, 9.0)\n        lam = load / 9.0\n        depth = \"unbounded\" if w == float(\"inf\") else \"{:.1f}\".format(lam * w)\n        wait = \"unstable\" if w == float(\"inf\") else \"{:.2f}s\".format(w)\n        print(\"  {:>7} | {:>10.1%}  | {:>8} | {}\".format(c, util, wait, depth))\n    print(\"  Utilisation is the only variable that moved. Wait is not linear in it.\")\n\n    workers = 42\n    pool_cap = workers / 9.0 * 60.0\n    granted_tpm = 800_000  # tier B: what procurement got, not the tier C you asked for\n    quota_cap = granted_tpm / p[\"effective_tokens_per_task\"]\n    base_cap = min(pool_cap, quota_cap)\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"REAL CAPACITY = min(worker pool, provider quota)\")\n    print(\"  {} workers at 9.0s          -> {:.0f} tasks/min\".format(workers, pool_cap))\n    print(\"  granted {:,} TPM / 4,400 tok -> {:.0f} tasks/min\"\n          .format(granted_tpm, quota_cap))\n    print(\"  binding constraint            -> {:.0f} tasks/min \"\n          \"(the quota, not the fleet)\".format(base_cap))\n\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"DEGRADATION LADDER - 12x Monday, then a 26x incident, same window\")\n    ramp = []\n    for m in range(1, 46):\n        if m <= 5:\n            mult = 1.0\n        elif m <= 18:\n            mult = 1.0 + 11.0 * min(1.0, (m - 5) / 6.0)\n        elif m <= 24:\n            mult = 3.0\n        elif m <= 34:\n            mult = 3.0 + 23.0 * min(1.0, (m - 24) / 4.0)\n        else:\n            mult = max(1.0, 26.0 - 2.6 * (m - 34))\n        ramp.append(p[\"tasks_per_sec\"] * 60.0 * mult * rng.uniform(0.95, 1.05))\n\n    print(\"  min | arriving | demand | capacity |  util | rung               \"\n          \"| backlog | shed\")\n    total_shed = 0.0\n    peak_backlog = 0.0\n    for (m, arr, dem, cap, util, name, back, shed) in ladder(ramp, base_cap):\n        total_shed += shed\n        peak_backlog = max(peak_backlog, back)\n        if m % 3 == 0 or shed > 0 or back > 0:\n            print(\"  {:>3} | {:>8.0f} | {:>6.0f} | {:>8.0f} | {:>5.0%} | {:<18} \"\n                  \"| {:>7.0f} | {:>4.0f}\"\n                  .format(m, arr, dem, cap, util, name, back, shed))\n    frozen_shed = sum(r[7] for r in ladder(ramp, base_cap, frozen=True))\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  with the ladder      : {:>6.0f} tasks dropped, backlog peaked at \"\n          \"{:.0f} and drained\".format(total_shed, peak_backlog))\n    print(\"  pinned at rung 0     : {:>6.0f} tasks dropped, no backlog, no notice\"\n          .format(frozen_shed))\n    print(\"  the ladder was worth {:.0f}x here - and every rung was a decision\"\n          .format(frozen_shed / max(1.0, total_shed)))\n    print(\"  someone signed BEFORE this minute, not during it.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","notes":["<b>output_weight</b> is a parameter and not a constant on purpose. How heavily output tokens count against a throughput meter is provider policy, it differs between vendors, and it changes - pass 1.0 if yours meters flat, but check rather than assume, because a wrong weight understates your peak by a third","<code>check_quota</code> takes the tier table as an argument rather than embedding one. Fill it from your own contract. The point of the function is the comparison and the lead-time conversation it triggers, not the numbers","The Erlang C implementation is exact for M/M/c but M/M/c is a model: it assumes Poisson arrivals and exponential service times, and your real traffic is burstier than Poisson and your service times are more variable than exponential. Which means <b>the real cliff is worse than the table, not better</b> - use it to find the shape, not to shave headroom","<code>ladder()</code> has hysteresis for a reason: escalate above 85% utilisation, de-escalate only after four consecutive minutes below 45%. Without the gap, a system hovering near a threshold flaps between rungs and the customer sees quality oscillate, which is worse than either rung","The <code>frozen=True</code> counterfactual run is the number to put in the design doc. On the seeded spike it is 9 tasks dropped with the ladder against 7,433 without - that ratio is what buys you the sign-off meeting you need for the fifth field"],"debug":[["Required TPM comes out far below what production actually consumes","Retries, system prompt, few-shot examples and tool-result payloads are not in the token profile - only the visible user text was counted","Measure effective tokens per task from real traffic including retries, then re-run. The gap is routinely 2-3x and it lands entirely inside your peak"],["The pool is sized correctly and the system still saturates","The binding constraint is the quota, not the fleet, so adding workers changes nothing except how many of them are blocked","Print both ceilings in tasks per minute and take the min(). If quota is binding, the fix is a negotiation with a lead time, not an autoscaling rule"],["The ladder flaps between two rungs during a spike","The escalate and de-escalate thresholds are too close together, or the cooldown is too short for the smoothing window","Widen the gap and lengthen the cooldown. Oscillating quality reads worse to customers than sitting one rung lower than strictly necessary"],["The simulator shows almost no shedding and the result looks too good","Backlog is being absorbed by the queue at rungs 4 and 5, so the cost showed up as delay rather than as drops","Read the backlog column alongside the shed column, and check the drain time. A 229-task backlog that clears in eleven minutes is a success; one that never clears is an unbounded queue with a nicer name"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:capacity', 'paid', '[{"q":"40,000 tasks a day at 4,400 effective tokens each, with an 8x Monday peak. What throughput do you request, before headroom?","o":["About 978,000 TPM, from 122,000 steady multiplied by the peak factor of eight","About 122,000 TPM, since the daily average is what a provider quota is sized against","About 293,000 TPM, using a blended factor that averages peak hours against quiet ones","About 176,000,000 TPM, which is the daily effective token total across all tasks"],"a":0,"exp":"40,000/86,400 = 0.463 tasks a second; times 4,400 tokens times 60 gives 122,222 TPM steady; times 8 gives 977,778. Quotas are enforced per minute, so you size against the peak minute, not the daily average, and then add headroom on top."},{"q":"Arrivals are 3.7 tasks a second and each task takes 9.0 seconds end to end. What does Little''s Law tell you?","o":["Peak throughput is 33 tasks a second once the pool has fully warmed up under load","Mean queue wait will be 33 seconds at the utilisation the current pool is running at","33 tasks are in flight at all times, which is the floor on concurrency, not a target","33 workers is the right pool size, giving a comfortable margin above the arrival rate"],"a":2,"exp":"L = lambda x W = 3.7 x 9.0 = 33.3 tasks in flight. That is a floor: a pool of 34 runs at 98% utilisation, which is deep past the cliff. You size above it — 42 workers puts you at 79% and a 0.11 second wait."},{"q":"At fixed load, 42 workers gives 79% utilisation and 0.11s of wait; 34 gives 98% and 11.73s. What is the design lesson?","o":["Wait rises asymptotically near saturation, so utilisation past ~80% is a latency risk","Wait scales roughly linearly with utilisation, so trimming the pool has a predictable cost","The pool should be sized to the arrival rate, since service time is what drives the wait","Utilisation is an efficiency metric, and the extra workers are unused capacity to reclaim"],"a":0,"exp":"Six workers, a hundredfold change in wait, with load and service time unchanged. Above roughly 80% the queueing curve turns asymptotic, so the last few points of utilisation buy little cost saving and account for most of your tail latency."},{"q":"Demand is 333 tasks a minute against 182 of capacity, and someone adds an uncapped queue to absorb the burst. What happens?","o":["Throughput improves, because work is smoothed across minutes instead of arriving in bursts","Backlog grows 151 a minute and every arriving request inherits the accumulated delay","p95 improves for most users, while the slowest 45% of requests absorb the added waiting","The system stabilises once the queue reaches its natural depth at the current arrival rate"],"a":1,"exp":"A queue converts refusal into waiting; it creates no capacity. With arrivals above service rate the backlog grows without bound — 151 a minute, 9,060 an hour — and the user who would have been instant at 09:03 waits behind all of it. Depth cap plus admission control is what makes a queue a lever."},{"q":"Four saturation levers: shed, queue, degrade, borrow. Why must the choice be made before the incident rather than during it?","o":["The levers must be applied in a fixed order, and getting that sequence wrong cascades","Only one lever can be active at a time, so picking wrongly means a costly rollback","They spend different currencies, so choosing is a product decision, not an ops one","Three of the four require a config deploy, which is unsafe to attempt during a spike"],"a":2,"exp":"Shed picks who loses, queue makes everyone lose a little, degrade spends quality, borrow spends money or unmeasured quality. There is no ordering that holds across products, so the person qualified to choose is a product owner — who at 3am is asleep."},{"q":"A rung has a trigger, a disabled feature, a customer-visible effect and a quality delta. What is missing, and why does it matter?","o":["A rollback procedure, without which the rung cannot be safely disengaged afterwards","A named pre-authorizer, without which the on-call engineer is not empowered to pull it","A cost estimate, without which finance cannot approve the degraded operating mode","A dashboard panel, without which nobody can confirm the rung actually took effect"],"a":1,"exp":"The first four fields are engineering; the fifth is authority. An on-call engineer is not empowered to decide that every support answer gets worse tonight, but is entirely empowered to execute a rung the VP of Support signed in March. That field is what makes the ladder executable."},{"q":"Failover to a second provider is configured, but that provider has never been run against your golden set. What have you actually built?","o":["A capacity save whose quality cost is unknown, so it converts one incident into another","A reasonable v1, since availability failures hurt customers more than quality drops do","A latency risk, because the failover target''s response times have not been characterised","A cost exposure, since traffic will shift to a provider whose rates were never negotiated"],"a":0,"exp":"Failover preserves availability and says nothing about quality. An un-evaluated target turns a visible capacity incident into an undetected quality incident — a silent 200 at fleet scale. Run it against the golden set first and record the delta in the runbook."},{"q":"Your pool of 42 workers serves 280 tasks a minute; the granted quota supports 182. What is the system''s capacity?","o":["280 a minute, with the quota acting as a soft limit that throttles rather than blocks","231 a minute, since sustained throughput settles between the two competing ceilings","182 a minute, and adding workers changes nothing except how many of them are blocked","462 a minute, because the fleet and the quota constrain different stages of the pipeline"],"a":2,"exp":"Capacity is min(fleet, quota), and here the quota binds. That matters operationally because autoscaling cannot fix it: the remedy is a quota negotiation with a lead time measured in weeks, which is why the number belongs in the design doc, not the launch checklist."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:seams', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"seams","block":"b3","title":"Seams, Substitution & the Real Cost of Buying","tag":"You aren''t choosing a vendor, you''re choosing how expensive it will be to stop","why":"Every vendor decision cuts a seam into your system, and the seam decides what leaving costs, not the contract and not the price sheet. Teams argue unit price and feature grids for six weeks, sign, and then discover the number that mattered was engineer-weeks to unwind. This module makes that number computable before you commit, so \"we''re locked in\" becomes a figure you chose rather than one you found out.","mental":"Every vendor decision cuts <b>a seam</b> into your system, the surface where their thing meets yours. Where you put that seam, and how much of your behaviour crosses it, <b>is</b> your exit cost. There is no other number. Not the contract, not the discount, not the feature grid, the seam. So stop asking which vendor is better and start asking where the cut goes and how many engineer-weeks it takes to reopen it.","concepts":[["Exit cost, in engineer-weeks","The unit that makes vendor arguments finishable. Four lines: distinct behaviours you rely on times hours to port each, data egress, re-derivation compute (re-embedding, index builds), and eval revalidation. Quote weeks first and money second, because cash you can find on short notice and calendar you cannot. Under two weeks is not lock-in. Over ten is a strategic commitment that deserves a signature, not a procurement ticket."],["Coupling points, not call sites","Exit cost scales with the number of <b>distinct behaviours</b> you depend on, not the number of lines that call them. Thirty-one call sites through three methods is a three-point seam. Fourteen call sites each using a different filter dialect, scoring knob, pagination rule and error convention is a fourteen-point seam. Count behaviours during the audit, or you will size a migration with find-and-replace arithmetic and be wrong by more than 4x."],["The seam ladder","Four places to cut, cheapest exit first. <b>Config seam</b>: the vendor is a value in a file and the swap is a deploy. <b>Protocol seam</b>: you speak a shape more than one vendor implements (OpenAI-compatible endpoints, MCP servers) and the vendor is whoever answers. <b>Adapter seam</b>: your interface, their implementation, you own the translation and its upkeep. <b>Data seam</b>: their storage holds state you cannot re-derive, the most expensive rung and the one teams reach by accident rather than choice."],["The portability test","One question, asked before signing rather than at renewal: <b>what must I keep if I leave?</b> An honest answer names artifacts, not intentions, golden sets, labelled traces, judge rubrics, prompt and schema history, tenant mappings, ACL snapshots. Anything on that list that lives only inside a vendor''s tenancy is an exit-cost line item nobody has priced yet. The test takes ten minutes and routinely changes the answer."],["The asymmetric rule, and why evals are the hinge","<b>Buy things whose output is portable, build things whose data is yours.</b> A managed index emits documents and vectors you already had, so buy it. Golden sets, labelled failures and domain judge rubrics encode judgments nobody else can regenerate, so build and hold them. Evals are the hinge: you cannot swap what you cannot compare, so a suite that runs only inside a vendor''s platform makes substitution <b>unmeasurable</b>, and nobody authorises an unmeasurable swap. Eval ownership is a portability decision before it is ever a quality decision."],["Carrying cost of a clean seam","Abstractions are not free and their bill arrives annually: tracking vendor features you deliberately excluded, running a conformance suite against a second implementation, explaining the indirection to every new hire. Price it in engineer-weeks per year, divide the exit saving by it, and you get the honest figure, <b>the number of months inside which leaving repays the wrapper</b>. Past that month, the raw call sites were the correct architecture and saying so out loud is the senior move."],["The union abstraction","The characteristic failure: an interface that is the <b>union</b> of two vendors'' feature sets. It exposes everything both of them do, so it leaks both, and it forbids nothing, so it buys no portability, you have paid carrying cost for an option that was never created. A real seam is an intersection plus an explicit decision about what you give up. If you cannot name what your abstraction forbids, it is not a seam, it is a second SDK with your logo on it."]],"mistakes":["Arguing unit price for six weeks and never once computing engineer-weeks to leave","Sizing the port by call sites, so a fourteen-behaviour seam gets estimated as a three-day find-and-replace","Letting the vendor hold the golden set and the labelled traces, the two artifacts that make any future swap measurable","Counting re-derivation compute and forgetting revalidation, which is usually the larger half of the number","Shipping an abstraction that is the union of two vendors'' features, paying carry for an option it never actually created"],"flags":["\"It''s just an API call, we can swap it in a day\", nobody has priced re-embedding the corpus","\"We''ll abstract it later\", later is after the fortieth call site and the filter dialect","\"Their benchmark shows parity\", on whose traffic, scored by whose judge, against which of your suites?","\"We''re not locked in, there''s an export button\", an export format is not a re-derivation plan","\"The wrapper is basically free\", then it has no owner, and in eight months it has no tests"],"ask":["What must we keep if we leave this vendor next year? Name the artifacts, not the intentions.","How many distinct behaviours of theirs do we depend on, and who actually counted them?","Who holds the golden set and the labelled traces, us or them?","What is this wrapper''s carrying cost in engineer-weeks per year, and how many months until leaving repays it?","Have we ever run one of our own suites against a second implementation? What did that cost, in hours?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"Same decision, two seam placements: a managed vector service holding 12M chunks for a support product. Rate assumptions, stated once and passed everywhere: 37.5 productive hours per engineer-week, 12 hours to port one distinct behaviour, 16 hours to re-baseline one eval suite.<br><br><b>Seam A, the thin repository.</b> Three methods, upsert, search, delete, and every call site goes through them. Exit at 18 months: 3 coupling points x 12h = <b>36h</b>, plus 9h of index-build compute (the vectors are already in your object storage, so no re-embed), plus 4 eval suites x 16h = <b>64h</b>. Total 109h = <b>2.91 engineer-weeks</b>.<br><br><b>Seam B, the raw SDK.</b> Thirty-one call sites, but the number that matters is 14 distinct behaviours: their filter dialect in nine places, hybrid-scoring knobs in four, one pagination convention. Exit: 14 x 12h = <b>168h</b>, plus the same 9h build, plus the same 64h revalidation. Total 241h = <b>6.43 engineer-weeks</b>.<br><br>So Seam A saves <b>3.52 engineer-weeks</b> at exit. Now price keeping it: feature-lag tracking and a quarterly conformance run against a second implementation cost about 18 engineer-days a year, <b>3.6 engineer-weeks annually</b>. Over 18 months that is <b>5.4 weeks paid to save 3.52</b>. Break-even is 11.7 months, and honest probability of leaving inside the horizon is 35%, so the expected saving is 0.35 x 3.52 = <b>1.23 weeks against 5.4 certain</b>. Roughly 4.4 to 1 against the abstraction. <b>The messy seam wins.</b><br><br>Now look at what both columns share: 64 hours of revalidation, identical, because the evals are yours either way. Make them the vendor''s and that line stops being 64 hours and becomes \"build an eval capability first\", about six engineer-weeks, on the critical path of <b>both</b> options. The highest-leverage seam change here was never the wrapper. It was owning the suite, and it cuts six weeks off every vendor decision you will make afterwards.","good":["Every vendor in the architecture carries a one-line exit cost in engineer-weeks, recomputed at renewal and visible in the design doc","Golden sets, labelled traces and judge rubrics live in your storage in an open format, whatever platform happens to run them","Seams are chosen per vendor rather than by habit: config where the swap is genuinely cheap, adapter where the behaviour is rich, data seams named as such and argued for","Every abstraction has a named owner and a stated carrying cost in engineer-weeks per year, reviewed against its exit saving at least annually","Once a year one real suite runs against a second implementation, so \"we could switch\" is a measurement with a date on it rather than a belief"],"build":"Pick your most-embedded vendor, the one people wince about, and write the exit memo. One page.<br><br>(1) <b>What breaks</b>: enumerate distinct behaviours you depend on, not call sites. Filter dialects, scoring knobs, error semantics, pagination, consistency guarantees, auth model. Grep for their import outside your adapter; every hit outside is a coupling point. (2) <b>What must be re-derived</b>: embeddings, indexes, caches, anything you cannot copy and must recompute, with hours. (3) <b>What must be revalidated</b>: which suites, how many hours each to re-run and re-baseline, and be honest, most teams undercount this by 3x. (4) <b>The tail</b>: egress volume and remaining contract months. (5) <b>Total in engineer-weeks</b>, money second.<br><br>Then propose exactly one seam change that halves the number, and state its ongoing cost in engineer-weeks per year. Divide the saving by the carry to get the month inside which leaving repays it. Compare that to your honest probability of leaving.<br><br>If the current exit cost is already acceptable, the correct deliverable is <b>do nothing</b>, and writing that down, with the arithmetic that supports it, is the exercise. An exit memo that always recommends a refactor is a memo nobody will believe the third time.","res":[["StranglerFigApplication (Martin Fowler)","https://martinfowler.com/bliki/StranglerFigApplication.html","Use when the exit has to happen incrementally on a live system, this is the shape a real migration off a data seam takes."],["The Law of Leaky Abstractions","https://www.joelonsoftware.com/2002/11/11/the-law-of-leaky-abstractions/","Use right now: read it before you propose a wrapper, it is the clearest statement of why union abstractions cost more than they save."],["Anti-corruption layer pattern","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/anti-corruption-layer","Use when you have decided an adapter seam is correct and need the standard shape and its known failure modes."],["Model Context Protocol specification","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/","Use when evaluating whether a protocol seam is available for a category, a published shape more than one vendor implements is the cheapest seam you can get."]]},"depth":{"mech":"Exit cost is an arithmetic object with four terms, and every argument about lock-in is really a disagreement about one of them.<br><br><b>Term one, coupling.</b> Distinct behaviours you depend on, times hours to port each. The word doing the work is <b>distinct</b>. A codebase with 31 call sites all going through one interface has three behaviours to port; a codebase with 14 call sites, each using a different corner of the vendor''s filter language, has fourteen. This is why estimates made by counting call sites are wrong in both directions and why the audit that produces this number should be a grep for the vendor''s import outside the adapter, not a story someone tells in standup.<br><br><b>Term two, egress.</b> The one everyone remembers and the one that almost never dominates. Volume times a published per-gigabyte rate, plus the wall-clock to move it. Include it, then stop thinking about it.<br><br><b>Term three, re-derivation.</b> The state you cannot copy and must recompute. Re-embedding a corpus, rebuilding an index, recomputing summaries. This is compute hours and it is usually cheap in money and awkward in calendar, because the rebuild has to happen while the old system is still serving.<br><br><b>Term four, revalidation.</b> Number of eval suites times hours to re-run and re-baseline each. This is the term teams forget and it is frequently the largest. It is also the term with the interesting property: <b>it is identical across every seam placement</b>, because it depends on what you own rather than how you integrated. That structural fact is why eval ownership outranks abstraction design. Moving your suite in-house cuts the same number of hours off the wrapper option and the raw option, and it does it once for all future vendors.<br><br>Against exit cost stands <b>carrying cost</b>, and the comparison is deliberately asymmetric. Exit cost is probabilistic, you pay it only if you leave. Carrying cost is certain, you pay it every year regardless. So the correct comparison is expected saving against certain carry, and the derived quantity worth quoting is the break-even month: exit saving divided by annual carry, times twelve. If that lands at eleven months and your contract runs three years, the wrapper loses on arithmetic and you should say so plainly rather than building it because clean code feels responsible.<br><br>The last mechanism is the <b>portability test</b>, which converts all of this into a purchasing question. What must I keep if I leave? Whatever the answer names, golden sets, labelled traces, judge rubrics, tenant mappings, must be in your storage in a format you can read without them. Everything else is a rental, and rentals are fine. The mistake is not renting. The mistake is renting the one thing that makes substitution measurable.","trade":[["Config seam vs adapter seam for a model provider","Swap speed vs behavioural fidelity","A config-only swap ships a different personality to production and calls it a deploy"],["Owning the eval suite vs living on the vendor''s platform","Portability vs a year of platform work you get to skip","The suite you don''t own turns every substitution into an unmeasurable one, so nobody signs it off"],["Thin wrapper vs raw call sites","Certain annual carry vs probabilistic exit saving","Carrying a wrapper for three years to save four weeks once, at 35% probability"],["Protocol seam vs the vendor''s proprietary edge","A live second option vs the 8% they genuinely do better","Standardising on the intersection, then quietly reaching around it in nine places"],["Multi-year term for a discount vs month-to-month","Unit price vs a contract tail inside the exit number","Finding out the exit cost includes eleven months you cannot cancel"]],"scale":["Exit cost grows with corpus size only through re-derivation; it grows with behaviours, and is nearly flat in call sites, which is exactly why teams mis-size it","Below roughly ten call sites the raw seam usually wins the arithmetic; past forty the wrapper wins on comprehension long before it wins on exit","Multi-region and multi-tenant deployments multiply the data seam, not the code seam, one egress bill per region and one deletion path per tenant boundary","Recompute the number at every renewal, corpus growth quietly doubles re-derivation while everyone in the room is still quoting last year''s figure"],"sec":["Data residency and subprocessor terms are part of the seam, a vendor who cannot honour a region constraint has already set your exit date for you","Termination clauses: how long do they hold data after notice, in what format, and who pays the egress, all three belong in the exit number","Key custody at the seam, if their SDK holds your provider credentials then the swap is also a rotation, with its own blast radius","Audit rights and log export, an exit you cannot evidence is an exit your compliance function will not approve regardless of the engineering estimate"],"interview":"The way I think about buying is that every vendor decision cuts a seam into the system, the surface where their thing meets ours, and where I put that seam and how much of our behaviour crosses it is our exit cost. There isn''t another number. So before I look at pricing I count coupling points, distinct behaviours we''d depend on rather than call sites, because thirty-one calls through three methods is a three-point seam and fourteen calls each using a different filter dialect is a fourteen-point seam. Then I add egress, re-derivation, re-embedding and index builds, and eval revalidation, and I express the total in engineer-weeks, because money we can find on short notice and calendar we can''t. My default is asymmetric: buy things whose output is portable, build things whose data is ours. In practice that means we hold the golden sets, the labelled traces and the judge rubrics no matter whose platform runs them, because you cannot swap what you cannot compare. And I price abstractions honestly, a wrapper has an annual carrying cost, so I divide the exit saving by it and get the number of months inside which leaving actually repays the indirection. Sometimes that number tells me to keep the mess, and I''d rather say that than build something tidy that loses on arithmetic."},"patterns":{"intro":"The artifact is an exit memo, and an exit memo is one number: engineer-weeks to leave. This model computes it from design facts, then ranks which single seam change moves it most, so the room argues about the biggest lever instead of the loudest one. Every rate is a parameter, edit them for your org and never read a price out of this file.","code":"\"\"\"Exit-cost model: what it costs, in engineer-weeks, to stop using a vendor.\n\nDesign facts in, a number you paste into an exit memo out.\nStdlib only, deterministic, no network. Every rate is a PARAMETER --\nedit RATES for your org; never read a price out of this file.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, replace\n\nHORIZON_MONTHS = 18\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Rates:\n    hours_per_week: float           # productive engineer-hours per week\n    usd_per_engineer_week: float    # fully loaded\n    hours_per_coupling_point: float # to port ONE distinct vendor behaviour\n    hours_per_eval_suite: float     # to re-baseline ONE suite on a new vendor\n    usd_per_gb_egress: float\n    usd_per_compute_hour: float\n    usd_per_contract_month: float   # unavoidable tail on the signed term\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Seam:\n    \"\"\"One placement of one vendor boundary.\"\"\"\n    name: str\n    coupling_points: int          # DISTINCT behaviours relied on, not call sites\n    data_volume_gb: float\n    rederivation_hours: float     # re-embed / re-index compute\n    revalidation_evals: int       # suites that must be re-run and re-baselined\n    contract_months_left: int\n    carrying_weeks_per_year: float  # cost of KEEPING this seam clean\n\n\ndef exit_cost(seam: Seam, r: Rates) -> dict:\n    coupling_h = seam.coupling_points * r.hours_per_coupling_point\n    eval_h = seam.revalidation_evals * r.hours_per_eval_suite\n    engineer_h = coupling_h + seam.rederivation_hours + eval_h\n    weeks = engineer_h / r.hours_per_week\n    cash = (weeks * r.usd_per_engineer_week\n            + seam.data_volume_gb * r.usd_per_gb_egress\n            + seam.rederivation_hours * r.usd_per_compute_hour\n            + seam.contract_months_left * r.usd_per_contract_month)\n    return {\n        \"weeks\": weeks,\n        \"usd\": cash,\n        \"coupling_h\": coupling_h,\n        \"rederive_h\": seam.rederivation_hours,\n        \"eval_h\": eval_h,\n    }\n\n\nLEVERS = {\n    \"halve coupling points\": lambda s: replace(s, coupling_points=max(1, s.coupling_points // 2)),\n    \"halve re-derivation\":   lambda s: replace(s, rederivation_hours=s.rederivation_hours / 2),\n    \"halve eval suites\":     lambda s: replace(s, revalidation_evals=max(1, s.revalidation_evals // 2)),\n    \"halve data to move\":    lambda s: replace(s, data_volume_gb=s.data_volume_gb / 2),\n}\n\n\ndef sensitivity(seam: Seam, r: Rates):\n    \"\"\"Which SINGLE seam change moves the number most? Rank them.\"\"\"\n    base = exit_cost(seam, r)[\"weeks\"]\n    rows = []\n    for label, f in LEVERS.items():\n        after = exit_cost(f(seam), r)[\"weeks\"]\n        rows.append((label, after, base - after))\n    rows.sort(key=lambda x: -x[2])\n    return base, rows\n\n\ndef wrapper_verdict(clean: Seam, messy: Seam, r: Rates, p_leave: float):\n    \"\"\"Does the cleaner seam earn its annual carrying cost inside the horizon?\"\"\"\n    saving = exit_cost(messy, r)[\"weeks\"] - exit_cost(clean, r)[\"weeks\"]\n    carry = clean.carrying_weeks_per_year * (HORIZON_MONTHS / 12.0)\n    breakeven_months = 12.0 * saving / clean.carrying_weeks_per_year if clean.carrying_weeks_per_year else float(\"inf\")\n    return {\n        \"saving_weeks\": saving,\n        \"expected_saving_weeks\": saving * p_leave,\n        \"carry_weeks\": carry,\n        \"breakeven_months\": breakeven_months,\n        \"verdict\": \"BUILD the clean seam\" if saving * p_leave > carry else \"SKIP it -- carry exceeds expected saving\",\n    }\n\n\ndef breakeven_year(build_weeks_upfront, build_weeks_per_year, buy_usd_per_year, r, horizon=6):\n    \"\"\"First year in which cumulative build cost drops below cumulative buy cost.\"\"\"\n    for year in range(1, horizon + 1):\n        build = (build_weeks_upfront + build_weeks_per_year * year) * r.usd_per_engineer_week\n        buy = buy_usd_per_year * year\n        if build <= buy:\n            return year, build, buy\n    return None, None, None\n\n\ndef bar(weeks, unit=0.5, width=40):\n    return \"#\" * min(width, int(round(weeks / unit)))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # ---- EDIT THESE. They are your org''s rates, not the model''s opinion. ----\n    RATES = Rates(\n        hours_per_week=37.5,\n        usd_per_engineer_week=6000.0,\n        hours_per_coupling_point=12.0,\n        hours_per_eval_suite=16.0,\n        usd_per_gb_egress=0.09,\n        usd_per_compute_hour=3.20,\n        usd_per_contract_month=4500.0,\n    )\n    P_LEAVE_IN_HORIZON = 0.35   # your honest probability, not your hope\n\n    clean = Seam(\"A: thin repository (3 methods)\", 3, 240, 9, 4, 6, carrying_weeks_per_year=3.6)\n    messy = Seam(\"B: 31 raw call sites\",          14, 240, 9, 4, 6, carrying_weeks_per_year=0.0)\n\n    print(\"=\" * 66)\n    print(\"EXIT COST  (horizon: %d months)\" % HORIZON_MONTHS)\n    print(\"=\" * 66)\n    for s in (clean, messy):\n        c = exit_cost(s, RATES)\n        print(\"\\n%s\" % s.name)\n        print(\"  coupling %5.0fh | re-derive %5.0fh | revalidate %5.0fh\"\n              % (c[\"coupling_h\"], c[\"rederive_h\"], c[\"eval_h\"]))\n        print(\"  EXIT = %5.2f engineer-weeks  /  $%s   %s\"\n              % (c[\"weeks\"], format(round(c[\"usd\"]), \",\"), bar(c[\"weeks\"])))\n\n    v = wrapper_verdict(clean, messy, RATES, P_LEAVE_IN_HORIZON)\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"-\" * 66)\n    print(\"IS THE CLEAN SEAM WORTH ITS CARRY?\")\n    print(\"-\" * 66)\n    print(\"  exit saving              %5.2f weeks\" % v[\"saving_weeks\"])\n    print(\"  p(leave) = %.0f%%  ->  expected %5.2f weeks\" % (P_LEAVE_IN_HORIZON * 100, v[\"expected_saving_weeks\"]))\n    print(\"  carrying cost over %dmo   %5.2f weeks  (certain, not probabilistic)\"\n          % (HORIZON_MONTHS, v[\"carry_weeks\"]))\n    print(\"  pays for itself only if you leave within %.1f months\" % v[\"breakeven_months\"])\n    print(\"  VERDICT: %s\" % v[\"verdict\"])\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"-\" * 66)\n    print(\"SENSITIVITY -- which ONE seam change moves the number most?\")\n    print(\"-\" * 66)\n    base, rows = sensitivity(messy, RATES)\n    print(\"  baseline (messy seam): %.2f weeks\" % base)\n    for label, after, delta in rows:\n        print(\"  %-24s -> %5.2f weeks   (-%.2f)  %s\" % (label, after, delta, bar(delta)))\n    print(\"  Note: the top lever is usually NOT the wrapper.\")\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"-\" * 66)\n    print(\"BUILD VS BUY BREAK-EVEN\")\n    print(\"-\" * 66)\n    yr, build, buy = breakeven_year(build_weeks_upfront=26, build_weeks_per_year=8,\n                                    buy_usd_per_year=140000.0, r=RATES)\n    if yr:\n        print(\"  build overtakes buy in year %d  ($%s vs $%s cumulative)\"\n              % (yr, format(round(build), \",\"), format(round(buy), \",\")))\n    else:\n        print(\"  build never overtakes buy inside the horizon -- buy, and own the artifacts.\")\n    print(\"\\nPaste the EXIT line and the VERDICT line into the exit memo. Nothing else.\")\n","notes":["<code>coupling_points</code> counts distinct behaviours, not call sites. It is the field teams fill in wrong and the field with the largest coefficient in the whole model.","The clean seam and the messy seam share an identical revalidation line, because evals are yours either way. That is precisely why owning the suite is the seam change with the best return.","<code>carrying_weeks_per_year</code> is the field people leave at zero. An abstraction with no carrying cost is an abstraction with no owner, and in eight months it has no tests either.","The verdict probability-weights the exit saving but not the carry, because you pay carry whether or not you leave. That asymmetry is the entire calculation.","Break-even uses cumulative cost. A build that turns cheaper in year four has still spent three years of optionality you cannot buy back afterwards."],"debug":[["Migration came in 4x over the exit estimate","Sized by call sites instead of distinct behaviours","Re-run the audit counting behaviours: filter dialects, scoring knobs, error conventions, pagination, consistency guarantees"],["The wrapper exists but exit is still expensive","It is a union abstraction, vendor specifics reach around it","Grep for the vendor''s import outside the adapter, every hit is a coupling point the model never counted"],["Sensitivity says re-derivation, the actual migration says otherwise","Compute hours are cheap, the eval re-baseline is the real cost","Raise hours_per_eval_suite to the measured value from your last swap, most teams undercount it by 3x"],["The verdict flips week to week","p_leave is being set by whoever is annoyed with the vendor","Fix it once per renewal from the contract term and the roadmap, then leave it alone until the next renewal"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:seams', 'paid', '[{"q":"A service has 31 call sites into a vendor SDK, all going through three shared helper methods. For the exit memo, coupling points =","o":["31, because every call site must be visited, tested and re-reviewed during the port","3, because exit cost scales with distinct behaviours you depend on, not with call sites","34, the call sites plus the helpers, since both layers need rewriting on a vendor swap","0, since the helpers already form an adapter and the vendor is fully hidden behind them"],"a":1,"exp":"Porting 31 mechanical call sites behind three methods is find-and-replace. The expensive work is reproducing distinct behaviours, filter dialects, scoring knobs, error semantics. Count behaviours. Note the inverse case is the dangerous one: 14 call sites each using a different corner of the vendor''s API is a 14-point seam."},{"q":"A wrapper would save 3.5 engineer-weeks at exit and costs 3.6 engineer-weeks a year to carry. Your contract runs 3 years. Build it?","o":["Yes, because a clean seam also improves testability and onboarding for every new hire","Yes, but only after the vendor has been in production for a full year without incident","No, break-even is under 12 months and you are committed for 36, so carry beats saving","No, wrappers are always the wrong call when a vendor offers a stable versioned interface"],"a":2,"exp":"Exit saving divided by annual carry, times twelve, gives the break-even month: 3.5/3.6 x 12 is about 11.7. You would pay roughly 10.8 weeks of carry over the term to save 3.5 once. The other reasons may be real, but they are not this argument, and D overreaches into a rule."},{"q":"Your eval suite runs only inside the vendor''s platform. In an exit memo this shows up as:","o":["A data export task, sized in gigabytes and priced against the published egress rate","A minor line item, since eval definitions are usually short and easy to re-key by hand","A blocker, because substitution becomes unmeasurable until you can compare on your data","A contract negotiation, since the vendor is obliged to hand back derived artifacts on exit"],"a":2,"exp":"You cannot swap what you cannot compare. The cost is not moving files, it is that no one can authorise a substitution nobody can measure. Building the capability lands on the critical path of every vendor decision you make afterwards, which is why owning evals outranks abstraction design."},{"q":"Two seam placements for the same vendor produce exit costs of 2.9 and 6.4 engineer-weeks. Both include 64 hours of eval revalidation. That shared line tells you:","o":["Revalidation is fixed overhead and should be excluded when comparing the two placements","The eval suite is over-sized and ought to be trimmed before either migration is attempted","Evals are yours regardless of seam, so moving them in-house pays down both columns at once","The two estimates were produced from the same template and one of them was not re-derived"],"a":2,"exp":"A term identical across placements is invariant to integration and therefore depends on ownership. That makes eval ownership the higher-leverage change: it cuts hours off every option and every future vendor, while the wrapper only ever helps against this one."},{"q":"Which is the clearest sign you have built a union abstraction rather than a seam?","o":["The interface has grown to more than a dozen methods across several vendor capabilities","You cannot name a single vendor capability the abstraction deliberately refuses to expose","New vendor features take about a sprint to surface through the abstraction to callers","Two different teams disagree about which layer a given retry policy properly belongs in"],"a":1,"exp":"A seam is an intersection plus an explicit decision about what you give up. If it forbids nothing it constrains nothing, so it buys no portability while still charging carry. Size, feature lag and layering arguments are symptoms at most; the naming test is diagnostic."},{"q":"Your corpus tripled since the vendor was chosen. Which term of the exit-cost model moves most?","o":["Coupling, since a larger corpus tends to pull in more of the vendor''s query surface","Revalidation, because a larger corpus makes each golden-set run take proportionally longer","Egress, which scales linearly with stored bytes and dominates at any meaningful scale","Re-derivation, the term that scales with corpus, re-embedding and index build hours"],"a":3,"exp":"Re-derivation is the only term corpus size drives directly. Coupling tracks behaviours, revalidation tracks suite count, and egress scales but rarely dominates. The practical consequence: recompute the exit number every renewal, because growth changes it while everyone quotes last year''s figure."},{"q":"You want a live second option for a model provider with the least ongoing carry. The seam to reach for is:","o":["An adapter seam with your own interface and a maintained translation layer per vendor","A data seam, keeping prompts and outputs in your storage rather than in their workspace","A protocol seam, standardising on a request shape several vendors already implement","A config seam, with the model name promoted to an environment variable per deployment"],"a":2,"exp":"A protocol seam costs least to carry because someone else maintains the shape and multiple vendors already conform. A config seam is cheaper still to build but swaps behaviour without validating it. An adapter is yours to maintain forever. A data seam is a different concern entirely."},{"q":"Honest probability of leaving a vendor within 18 months is 35%. The wrapper saves 3.5 weeks at exit and carries 5.4 weeks over that window. The correct framing is:","o":["Expected saving 1.2 weeks against 5.4 certain weeks, so roughly 4 to 1 against building","Saving 3.5 weeks against 5.4 weeks of carry, close enough that either decision is defensible","Probability applies to both sides equally, so the 35% cancels and 3.5 versus 5.4 stands","Carry should also be discounted by 35%, giving 1.9 weeks against 1.2 weeks of saving"],"a":0,"exp":"Exit saving is contingent, you collect it only if you leave. Carry is certain, you pay it every year either way. Weighting only the contingent side is the whole point of the comparison; discounting both, or neither, hides the asymmetry that makes the decision."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:statearch', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"statearch","block":"b3","title":"State, Truth & Freshness Architecture","tag":"Where does truth live, and how long may a copy of it be wrong?","why":"Every index, embedding, cache, summary and memory file in your system is a copy of something that lives somewhere else, and every copy is wrong for some interval. Teams architect the copies carefully and the intervals not at all, which is why a large share of \"the AI said something wrong\" incidents are stale-mirror incidents diagnosed as model incidents, for weeks. This module makes the interval a designed, written, auditable number.","mental":"Every index, embedding, cache, summary and memory file is <b>a mirror</b> of some system of record, and every mirror is wrong for some interval. Architecture is choosing that interval on purpose and making it visible. You do not get to choose whether your mirrors lag. You choose the lag, you write it down, and you make the system say so when it is exceeded.","concepts":[["AI systems are derived-data systems","The architectural unit is not the store, it is the <b>contract between the system of record and each derived copy</b>. Seven fields, every time: source, maximum staleness, invalidation trigger, rebuild time, rebuild cost, tenant partition key, deletion path. A store without those seven declared is not designed, it is accumulated, and it will be discovered during an incident or an audit rather than during a review."],["The freshness contract","Maximum staleness is a promise made against the spec card''s freshness tolerance, and it must be <b>measured, not aspirational</b>. The measurement is trivial and almost nobody takes it: compare the derived store''s last successful write timestamp to the source''s, continuously, and emit the gap as a metric. A contract you cannot observe being violated is a comment, and comments do not page anyone at 03:00."],["Invalidation trigger vs damage bound","These get conflated and the difference is the whole discipline. A change-data-capture stream or a scheduled sweep <b>detects</b> that a mirror is wrong. A time-to-live only <b>bounds how long you stay wrong</b> without ever noticing. Both are legitimate, and a TTL is often the right call, but a register that records a TTL in the invalidation column is telling you a store has no invalidation path at all."],["Stale-mirror incidents","The dominant misdiagnosis in production AI. Output is wrong, the model is the interesting component, so three weeks go into prompts and retrieval tuning while an index sits nine hours behind its source. Fix the diagnostic order instead: before anyone opens a prompt, compare last-successful-rebuild to last-source-write for every store in the answer path. It is a thirty-second check that resolves a surprising share of quality regressions."],["Deletion fan-out","A deletion request must arrive at the index, every cache tier, the agent memory file, the trace store and the eval fixtures. Trace one real record through the whole graph and <b>count the survivors</b>. That count is simultaneously a compliance finding and an architecture diagram, which is why it is the single most useful hour you can spend on this. The stores with no expiry are the ones to fix first, not the index everyone is watching."],["Re-index economics set your iteration rate","Units times tokens per unit times your embedder''s rate, plus index-build hours, plus the engineer-hours to verify and re-baseline, gives cost per full re-derivation. Divide your annual budget by it and you get how many times a year you are <b>allowed</b> to change your mind. The surprise is always the same: people expense dominates, embedding is rounding error, and the answer is two or three, one of which is spent on a mandatory model upgrade."],["Dual-index cutover and resumable rebuilds","Rebuild time greater than your outage budget means you cannot rebuild in place, so you build beside and swap an alias, which creates a bounded window in which <b>two indexes both claim to be true</b>. Bound it, name it in the register, and make the rebuild idempotent and resumable by content hash so a crash at 80% costs minutes rather than the whole run. This is the price of being allowed to change your mind at all."]],"mistakes":["Designing the derived stores carefully and the staleness intervals not at all, so nobody can say how wrong a copy is allowed to be","Recording a TTL in the invalidation column, which means the store has no invalidation path and the register is quietly lying","Diagnosing a stale-mirror incident as a model incident, and spending three weeks on prompts while an index sits nine hours behind","Building the deletion path to the index and stopping there, while memory files and eval fixtures keep the record forever","Discovering re-index cost during the sprint that proposed a new chunking strategy, rather than during the roadmap that promised four of them"],"flags":["\"The index updates in near real time\", near what, measured how, and what does the graph look like at 09:00 Monday?","\"We honour deletions, it''s a delete-by-filter on the index\", name the other six stores","\"The cache has a 24-hour TTL so we''re fine\", that is a damage bound, not an invalidation path","\"We''ll re-chunk next quarter and see if it helps\", with which of your two annual re-derivations?","\"Nobody has ever complained about stale data\", nobody can distinguish stale data from a wrong answer, that''s the problem"],"ask":["For every store in the answer path: what is the measured gap between its last write and the source''s, right now?","Which derived stores have no invalidation trigger, and which of those never expire?","Walk me through a deletion request. How many stores does it reach, and how many hold the data afterwards?","What does one full re-derivation cost, and how many can we afford this year after the mandatory model upgrade?","How long is the two-truths window during a cutover, and what reads from the old index during it?"],"estMin":50},"deep":{"worked":"One customer exercises their right to erasure. You have 30 days. Follow the record.<br><br><b>1. Orders database (system of record).</b> Row deleted, immediate. <b>2. Change-data-capture topic.</b> Tombstone emitted, but the pre-delete image sits in the topic for the 7-day retention window. Both of these have a deletion path.<br><br>Now the seven derived stores. <b>3. Chunk staging bucket</b>, 90-day lifecycle rule, no deletion path. <b>4. Primary index</b>, delete-by-filter, tombstone reclaimed at the next segment merge, measured lag <b>6 hours</b>. <b>5. Replica index</b> in the second region, replicates the delete, <b>+40 minutes</b>. <b>6. Semantic cache</b>, 24-hour TTL, no invalidation on tombstone, so the deleted record can still be served for <b>1,440 minutes</b> against a spec-card freshness tolerance of 15 minutes, <b>96x over contract</b>. <b>7. Agent memory files</b>, written as free text by an extraction step with no customer identifier, so no key exists to delete by, and no expiry. <b>8. Trace store</b>, 90-day retention, prompts contain the record verbatim. <b>9. Eval fixtures</b>: 14 golden cases were built from this customer''s tickets, and golden sets do not expire.<br><br>Count: <b>4 of 9 locations</b> can receive a deletion request, and only <b>2 of the 7 derived stores</b>. Two of the survivors, memory files and eval fixtures, <b>never expire</b>.<br><br>Price the fix. Customer-id front matter on memory files plus a delete-by-owner sweep: 3 engineer-days build, half a day per quarter to run. Provenance tags on eval fixtures plus a paraphrase-and-re-baseline procedure for the 14 affected cases: 3 days. Tombstone-driven cache invalidation replacing the TTL: 1 day, and it drops the serving-path exposure from 1,440 minutes to under 60 seconds. Staging bucket lifecycle 90 days to 7: one hour, and it lowers the storage bill. CDC retention stays at 7 days because your rebuild replays 5, so you document the exposure instead of shrinking it. Total <b>7.1 engineer-days, about 1.5 engineer-weeks</b>, plus half a day a quarter.<br><br>For 1.5 engineer-weeks the exposure goes from indefinite in two stores to at most 7 days anywhere and under a minute in the answer path. The expensive part was never the work. It was that nobody had ever drawn the graph.","good":["A derived-state register exists as a file in the repo, one row per store, seven columns, reviewed whenever a store is added","Staleness is measured continuously as the gap between each store''s last successful write and its source''s, and it is a graph somebody looks at","Every serving-path store has a real invalidation trigger; TTLs appear only as an explicitly named damage bound alongside one","Deletion is tested, not asserted: a synthetic record is erased quarterly and the sweep proves how many stores still hold it","Cost per full re-derivation is a known number, and the roadmap''s chunking and embedding experiments are budgeted against it before they are promised"],"build":"Build the derived-state register for a system you actually run. One row per store, seven columns, no exceptions and no \"n/a\": <b>store, source, maximum staleness, invalidation trigger, rebuild time, rebuild cost, tenant partition key, deletion path</b>.<br><br>Rules that make it useful rather than decorative. Staleness must be <b>measured</b>, so go and measure it, do not write what the design intended. A TTL goes in a column called damage bound, never in the invalidation column. Include the stores nobody thinks of as stores: prompt caches, summary tables, agent memory files, the trace store, and the eval fixtures. If a store has no tenant partition key, say so, because it means a scoped deletion cannot even be expressed.<br><br>Then run the audit in the Patterns tab against your spec card''s freshness tolerance and outage budget, and read the findings out loud in a review.<br><br><b>There is always one store with no invalidation path.</b> Always. It is usually something written by a background job that somebody added in a hurry and that nothing else references by key. Find it. Then decide, explicitly, whether to give it a path, give it an expiry, or delete the store, and write the decision next to the row. The register is only worth keeping if a row can be closed.","res":[["GDPR Article 17, right to erasure","https://gdpr-info.eu/art-17-gdpr/","Use when scoping the deletion fan-out, the obligation attaches to copies as well as originals, which is exactly what the register enumerates."],["Google SRE Book, Data Integrity","https://sre.google/sre-book/data-integrity/","Use when designing rebuild procedures, it is the clearest treatment of restore time as a first-class requirement rather than a backup detail."],["Change data capture (overview)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change_data_capture","Use when a store needs a real invalidation trigger and you are weighing a stream against a scheduled sweep."],["Turning the database inside out (Kleppmann)","https://martin.kleppmann.com/2015/11/05/database-inside-out-at-strange-loop.html","Use right now: the clearest framing of derived data as materialised views over a log, which is what your index actually is."]]},"depth":{"mech":"Treat the system as a log and a set of materialised views over it, because that is what it is. The system of record accepts writes. Everything else, the index, the embeddings, the summary tables, the caches, the agent''s memory file, the trace store, the eval fixtures, is a view that was correct at some past moment and has been drifting since. The design work is not choosing the stores. It is writing the contract each one owes the source.<br><br><b>The seven fields.</b> Source. Maximum staleness. Invalidation trigger. Rebuild time. Rebuild cost. Tenant partition key. Deletion path. Every field is a decision someone is making whether or not they write it down, and the register exists so that the decision happens in a review rather than in an incident.<br><br><b>Staleness must be measured.</b> The metric is the gap between a store''s last successful write and its source''s last write, sampled continuously. That single time series turns \"the index is roughly current\" into a number you can put next to the spec card''s freshness tolerance, and it converts a class of quality regressions from mysteries into one-line diagnoses. The alternative is the stale-mirror incident: output goes wrong, the model is the suspicious component because it is the probabilistic one, and three weeks disappear into prompt archaeology while an index sits nine hours behind. The countermeasure is a diagnostic order, not a tool. Before anyone opens a prompt, compare last-rebuild to last-source-write for every store in the answer path.<br><br><b>Invalidation is not the same as a damage bound.</b> A capture stream or a sweep detects that a view is wrong. A TTL merely limits how long you remain wrong without ever noticing. Both are legitimate engineering, and TTLs are often correct, but a register that records a TTL under invalidation is describing a store nothing will ever tell you about. The distinction matters most on deletion, because a TTL will eventually expire a deleted record while an invalidation trigger removes it now, and only one of those satisfies an erasure obligation with a deadline.<br><br><b>Re-index economics are a people bill, not a compute bill.</b> Take a real corpus: 1.2M documents at roughly 1,400 tokens each is 1,680 million tokens. At any current embedding rate that is tens of dollars. Nine hours of index build is tens more. Then 60 engineer-hours to run the migration, verify the output and re-baseline the suites, and that term is 99% of the total. Divide your annual re-derivation budget by the result and you get the number of times a year you may change your mind about chunking or embedding. It is usually two or three, one of which is consumed by a mandatory model upgrade. That number, not enthusiasm, is what governs how fast the pipeline can improve, and it should be stated before the roadmap promises four experiments.<br><br><b>Rebuild time versus outage budget</b> decides the cutover shape. If a full rebuild exceeds what you may be down, you cannot rebuild in place, so you build beside and swap an alias, and you accept a bounded window in which two indexes both claim to be true. Bound it, write it in the register, and decide what reads from the old one meanwhile. Make the rebuild idempotent and resumable, keyed by content hash, so a crash at 80% resumes rather than restarts. An unresumable nine-hour rebuild is a nine-hour rebuild you will avoid doing, and a rebuild you avoid doing is a mind you have stopped being able to change.","trade":[["Capture stream vs scheduled sweep for invalidation","Freshness and cost vs operational simplicity","A sweep tuned to the average update rate leaves the p99 document stale for a full cycle"],["TTL as damage bound vs explicit invalidation","Cheap and forgettable vs correct on deletion deadlines","A deleted record served for the rest of the TTL, from a cache with no path to reach it"],["In-place rebuild vs dual-index cutover","One store to operate vs a bounded two-truths window","A rebuild that outruns the outage budget, so it never gets scheduled and the strategy never changes"],["Tenant partition key on every store vs filters at query time","Deletion and isolation you can express vs slightly less plumbing","A store where a scoped erasure literally cannot be written, discovered during the audit"],["Fewer derived stores vs a store per access pattern","Fan-out you can hold in your head vs local performance wins","Nine mirrors, four deletion paths, and nobody who can draw the graph from memory"]],"scale":["Rebuild time grows with corpus while the outage budget stays fixed, so every system eventually crosses into needing a dual-index cutover, plan the crossing before it arrives","Deletion fan-out grows with the number of stores, not with data volume, which is why adding a cache is a compliance change as well as a latency change","Per-tenant rebuild is the scaling escape hatch, a full corpus rebuild is unaffordable monthly while a per-tenant one is routine, if every store has the partition key","Capture-stream lag is bimodal, fine at the median and dreadful during bulk imports, so measure staleness at p99 and alert there rather than on the average"],"sec":["Erasure obligations attach to derived copies, so the register is the evidence artifact, an auditor asks how many stores hold the record and the honest answer should be written down already","Access-control changes propagate exactly like deletions and are usually slower, a revoked permission that reaches the index in six hours is a six-hour leak with a metric attached","Trace stores are the quietest exposure in the system, they hold verbatim prompts including whatever was retrieved, under a retention policy nobody chose deliberately","Agent memory files are usually written without a tenant key or provenance, which makes them both undeletable and unattributable, fix the key before you fix the content"],"interview":"The way I frame state in an AI system is that everything except the system of record is a mirror, the index, the embeddings, the caches, the summaries, the agent''s memory file, the eval fixtures, and every mirror is wrong for some interval. Architecture is choosing that interval deliberately and making it visible. So I keep a derived-state register: one row per store, seven columns, source, maximum staleness, invalidation trigger, rebuild time, rebuild cost, tenant partition key, deletion path. Staleness has to be measured rather than intended, it''s the gap between the store''s last write and the source''s, as a live metric, because otherwise a stale-mirror incident gets diagnosed as a model incident and you lose three weeks to prompt archaeology while an index sits nine hours behind. I''m strict about one distinction: a capture stream detects that a mirror is wrong, a TTL only bounds how long you stay wrong, and a TTL in the invalidation column means the store has no invalidation at all. Then I price re-derivation, corpus times tokens times the embedder rate, plus build hours, plus the engineer-hours to verify and re-baseline, and that last term is almost all of it. Dividing the annual budget by that gives the number of times a year we''re allowed to change our mind about chunking. It''s usually two, and one of them is spent on a model upgrade we didn''t choose."},"patterns":{"intro":"The register is the artifact and <code>audit()</code> is the thing that makes people read it. Stores go in as data, findings come out ranked, and the deletion fan-out prints as a fraction you can put on a slide. The second half prices a full re-derivation and tells you how many strategy changes a year you can afford. Prices are parameters, read them off your invoice and your payroll.","code":"\"\"\"Derived-state register + audit + re-index economics.\n\nEvery mirror of a system of record declares a freshness contract. audit()\nreads the register against the spec card and prints findings you paste into\na design doc. Stdlib only, deterministic, no network.\nPrices are PARAMETERS -- nothing here knows what an embedder costs.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Optional\n\nINF = float(\"inf\")\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass SpecCard:\n    \"\"\"The two spec-card fields this module is allowed to touch.\"\"\"\n    freshness_tolerance_min: float   # how long a serving copy may be wrong\n    outage_budget_min: float         # how long you may be down rebuilding\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass DerivedStore:\n    name: str\n    source: str                    # what it mirrors\n    serving: bool                  # in the answer path? freshness only binds here\n    staleness_bound_min: float     # MEASURED, not aspirational. INF = no contract\n    invalidation: Optional[str]    # cdc | cron | ttl | manual | None\n    rebuild_min: float             # wall-clock to rebuild from source\n    tenant_key: Optional[str]      # None = a scoped delete cannot be expressed\n    deletion_path: Optional[str]   # None = a deletion request never arrives\n    retention_days: float          # INF = forever\n\n\nDETECTS = {\"cdc\", \"cron\"}   # ttl bounds the damage; it does not detect the change\n\n\ndef fmt(x):\n    return \"inf\" if x == INF else (\"%.1f\" % x).rstrip(\"0\").rstrip(\".\")\n\n\ndef audit(stores, spec):\n    out = []\n    for s in stores:\n        if s.deletion_path is None:\n            out.append((\"BLOCKER\", s.name,\n                        \"no deletion path; data survives %s days\" % fmt(s.retention_days)))\n        if s.tenant_key is None:\n            out.append((\"BLOCKER\", s.name,\n                        \"no tenant key; a scoped delete cannot be expressed\"))\n        if s.serving:\n            if s.staleness_bound_min == INF:\n                out.append((\"HIGH\", s.name,\n                            \"unbounded staleness in the answer path -- no contract at all\"))\n            elif s.staleness_bound_min > spec.freshness_tolerance_min:\n                out.append((\"HIGH\", s.name,\n                            \"staleness %s min vs spec freshness %.0f min (%.0fx over)\"\n                            % (fmt(s.staleness_bound_min), spec.freshness_tolerance_min,\n                               s.staleness_bound_min / spec.freshness_tolerance_min)))\n            if s.invalidation not in DETECTS:\n                out.append((\"HIGH\", s.name,\n                            \"invalidation=%s -- bounds the error, never detects it\"\n                            % (s.invalidation or \"none\")))\n        if 0 < s.rebuild_min < INF and s.rebuild_min > spec.outage_budget_min:\n            out.append((\"MEDIUM\", s.name,\n                        \"rebuild %.0f min > outage budget %.0f min -- needs a dual-index cutover\"\n                        % (s.rebuild_min, spec.outage_budget_min)))\n        if s.rebuild_min == INF:\n            out.append((\"MEDIUM\", s.name,\n                        \"no rebuild procedure -- not reconstructible from source\"))\n    rank = {\"BLOCKER\": 0, \"HIGH\": 1, \"MEDIUM\": 2}\n    out.sort(key=lambda f: rank[f[0]])\n    return out\n\n\ndef reindex_budget(units, tokens_per_unit, usd_per_mtok,\n                   index_build_hours, usd_per_compute_hour,\n                   engineer_hours_per_run, usd_per_engineer_hour,\n                   annual_rederivation_usd, mandatory_runs_per_year=1):\n    \"\"\"How many times a year are you ALLOWED to change your mind?\"\"\"\n    mtok = units * tokens_per_unit / 1_000_000.0\n    embed = mtok * usd_per_mtok\n    compute = index_build_hours * usd_per_compute_hour\n    people = engineer_hours_per_run * usd_per_engineer_hour\n    per_run = embed + compute + people\n    runs = int(annual_rederivation_usd // per_run) if per_run else 0\n    return {\"mtok\": mtok, \"embed\": embed, \"compute\": compute, \"people\": people,\n            \"per_run\": per_run, \"runs\": runs,\n            \"discretionary\": max(0, runs - mandatory_runs_per_year),\n            \"people_share\": people / per_run if per_run else 0.0}\n\n\ndef two_truths_window(rebuild_min, verify_min, cutover_min):\n    \"\"\"How long two indexes both claim to be true. Bound it or explain it.\"\"\"\n    return rebuild_min + verify_min + cutover_min\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    SPEC = SpecCard(freshness_tolerance_min=15, outage_budget_min=30)\n\n    REGISTER = [\n        DerivedStore(\"primary index\", \"orders_db\", True, 360, \"cdc\", 540,\n                     \"tenant_id\", \"delete-by-filter\", 365),\n        DerivedStore(\"replica index\", \"primary index\", True, 400, \"cdc\", 540,\n                     \"tenant_id\", \"replicated\", 365),\n        DerivedStore(\"semantic cache\", \"primary index\", True, 1440, \"ttl\", 0,\n                     \"tenant_id\", None, 1),\n        DerivedStore(\"chunk staging\", \"orders_db\", False, INF, \"cron\", 40,\n                     \"tenant_id\", None, 90),\n        DerivedStore(\"agent memory\", \"sessions\", True, INF, \"manual\", INF,\n                     None, None, INF),\n        DerivedStore(\"trace store\", \"runtime\", False, INF, None, INF,\n                     \"tenant_id\", None, 90),\n        DerivedStore(\"eval fixtures\", \"orders_db\", False, INF, \"manual\", INF,\n                     None, None, INF),\n    ]\n\n    print(\"=\" * 74)\n    print(\"DERIVED-STATE REGISTER  (%d stores)\" % len(REGISTER))\n    print(\"=\" * 74)\n    print(\"%-15s %-14s %-5s %8s %-7s %9s %-16s\"\n          % (\"store\", \"source\", \"serve\", \"stale/m\", \"inval\", \"rebuild/m\", \"delete\"))\n    for s in REGISTER:\n        print(\"%-15s %-14s %-5s %8s %-7s %9s %-16s\"\n              % (s.name, s.source, \"yes\" if s.serving else \"no\",\n                 fmt(s.staleness_bound_min), s.invalidation or \"-\",\n                 fmt(s.rebuild_min), s.deletion_path or \"NONE\"))\n\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"=\" * 74)\n    print(\"AUDIT vs SPEC CARD (freshness %.0f min | outage budget %.0f min)\"\n          % (SPEC.freshness_tolerance_min, SPEC.outage_budget_min))\n    print(\"=\" * 74)\n    for sev, name, msg in audit(REGISTER, SPEC):\n        print(\"  [%-7s] %-15s %s\" % (sev, name, msg))\n\n    gaps = [s.name for s in REGISTER if s.deletion_path is None]\n    forever = [s.name for s in REGISTER\n               if s.deletion_path is None and s.retention_days == INF]\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  DELETION FAN-OUT: %d of %d stores can receive a deletion request.\"\n          % (len(REGISTER) - len(gaps), len(REGISTER)))\n    print(\"  survivors: %s\" % \", \".join(gaps))\n    print(\"  of those, %s never expire -- start there, not with the index.\"\n          % \" and \".join(forever))\n\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"=\" * 74)\n    print(\"RE-INDEX ECONOMICS -- how often may you change your mind?\")\n    print(\"=\" * 74)\n    # ---- EDIT THESE. Read them off your invoice and your payroll, not this file.\n    b = reindex_budget(units=1_200_000, tokens_per_unit=1_400,\n                       usd_per_mtok=0.02,\n                       index_build_hours=9, usd_per_compute_hour=3.20,\n                       engineer_hours_per_run=60, usd_per_engineer_hour=160.0,\n                       annual_rederivation_usd=30_000, mandatory_runs_per_year=1)\n    print(\"  corpus               %.0f million tokens\" % b[\"mtok\"])\n    print(\"  embed       per run  $%8s\" % format(round(b[\"embed\"]), \",\"))\n    print(\"  index build per run  $%8s\" % format(round(b[\"compute\"]), \",\"))\n    print(\"  PEOPLE      per run  $%8s   <- %.0f%% of the run\"\n          % (format(round(b[\"people\"]), \",\"), 100 * b[\"people_share\"]))\n    print(\"  TOTAL       per run  $%8s\" % format(round(b[\"per_run\"]), \",\"))\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  Budget buys %d full re-derivations/year. %d are DISCRETIONARY once the\"\n          % (b[\"runs\"], b[\"discretionary\"]))\n    print(\"  mandatory model upgrade is paid. That is your entire experiment budget.\")\n    print(\"  The embedding bill is %.1f%% of a run -- optimising the embedder buys nothing.\"\n          % (100 * b[\"embed\"] / b[\"per_run\"]))\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  Two-truths window on a dual-index cutover: %.0f min. Bound it or explain it.\"\n          % two_truths_window(rebuild_min=540, verify_min=45, cutover_min=5))\n","notes":["<code>serving</code> separates stores in the answer path from ones that are not, so freshness findings land on the cache and the index rather than drowning in the trace store.","<code>DETECTS</code> deliberately excludes <code>ttl</code>. A TTL bounds the damage and never detects the change, and collapsing the two is how registers end up describing systems that do not exist.","Staleness and rebuild time are meant to be <b>measured</b> values. A register full of design intentions passes its own audit and tells you nothing.","The re-index calculator prints the people share on purpose. It is typically 99%, which is why optimising the embedder never buys you another experiment.","Findings sort BLOCKER, HIGH, MEDIUM so the top of the output is always the stores that cannot receive a deletion at all, which is the correct place to start."],"debug":[["Answers went wrong on Tuesday, prompts unchanged","A mirror is lagging, not a model regressing","Compare last-successful-rebuild to last-source-write for every serving store before opening a single prompt"],["Deleted record still served hours later","Cache has a TTL where an invalidation trigger belongs","Wire tombstone-driven invalidation; keep the TTL as a damage bound and record it in that column"],["Audit reports a store as unbounded that the team believes is fresh","Staleness field holds the design intent, not a measurement","Emit the source-to-store gap as a metric for a week, then put the p99 in the register"],["Re-index budget says two, roadmap promises five","Cost was estimated from embedding price alone","Add engineer-hours for migration, verification and eval re-baseline, then take the smaller number to planning"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:statearch', 'paid', '[{"q":"A register lists a semantic cache with invalidation \"24h TTL\". The spec card''s freshness tolerance is 15 minutes. The finding is:","o":["Acceptable, since a 24-hour TTL bounds staleness and the cache is only a performance layer","Two findings: no invalidation path at all, and a staleness bound running 96x over contract","One finding: the TTL should be lowered to 15 minutes so it matches the spec card exactly","Not a finding yet, because cache staleness only matters once a deletion request arrives"],"a":1,"exp":"A TTL bounds how long you stay wrong; it never detects that you are wrong, so the invalidation column is empty. And 1,440 minutes against a 15-minute tolerance is 96x over. Lowering the TTL to 15 minutes would destroy the hit rate and still leave no invalidation path."},{"q":"Answer quality dropped on Tuesday. No prompt, model or retrieval change shipped. What do you check first?","o":["Sample fifty recent traces and score them against a judge to characterise the regression","Check whether the provider silently moved an alias, since that is the usual cause of drift","Compare each serving store''s last successful write against its source''s last write","Diff last week''s retrieval results for the golden set to see which documents stopped ranking"],"a":2,"exp":"The stale-mirror check takes thirty seconds and rules out the most commonly misdiagnosed cause. The others are all legitimate investigations, and all of them cost hours or days. Order matters more than technique: cheap eliminations first, and the model is the expensive place to look."},{"q":"You trace a deletion through nine locations. Four can receive it. Two of the five survivors never expire. Where do you start?","o":["The primary index, because it is the store users read from on every single request","The two stores that never expire, since bounded exposure is a schedule and unbounded is not","The trace store, since it holds verbatim prompts and therefore the richest personal data","The change-data-capture topic, because it is upstream and fixes propagate from there down"],"a":1,"exp":"The index already has a path. A 90-day retention is an exposure with an end date; a store with no expiry and no key is an obligation you cannot ever discharge. Fix unbounded before bounded, then shorten the bounded windows that exceed the deadline."},{"q":"Full re-derivation: embedding costs $34, index build $29, and 60 engineer-hours of migration, verification and re-baselining. The design implication is:","o":["Batch the embedding calls, since the embed line is the only variable cost in the whole run","Iteration rate is set by engineer-hours, so the embedder price cannot buy another experiment","The corpus is too small for the run to be worth optimising along any of the three dimensions","Move the index build to cheaper hardware, as compute is the largest controllable line here"],"a":1,"exp":"People are roughly 99% of the run. That is why re-index economics govern how often you may change chunking or embedding models, and why teams who optimise the embedding bill are surprised to still get two runs a year. Cut verification effort, or accept the rate."},{"q":"A full index rebuild takes 9 hours. Your outage budget is 30 minutes. The consequence for architecture is:","o":["Rebuilds must be scheduled overnight, when a longer outage window is generally available","The index must be sharded so that each shard rebuilds inside the thirty-minute allowance","In-place rebuild is impossible, so you build beside and accept a bounded two-truths window","The outage budget should be renegotiated upward, since nine hours is normal at this corpus size"],"a":2,"exp":"Rebuild time exceeding the outage budget is what forces dual-index cutover, and the cost of that pattern is a window where two indexes both claim to be true. Bound it, name it in the register, and decide what reads the old index meanwhile. Overnight windows are still outages."},{"q":"Which store in a typical AI system is most often found to have no invalidation path at all?","o":["The primary vector index, since delete-by-filter leaves tombstones until the next merge","The replica index in the second region, which lags the primary by a replication interval","The agent memory files, written by an extraction step with no key anything else references","The change-data-capture topic, which retains the pre-delete image for its retention window"],"a":2,"exp":"Memory files are written as free text by a background step, usually with no tenant key and no provenance, so nothing can address them for invalidation or deletion, and they do not expire. The other three all have a path, imperfect but real, and a measurable lag."},{"q":"A rebuild crashes at 80% after seven hours. Rerunning starts from zero. The architectural defect is:","o":["Insufficient compute provisioning, which made the total run long enough to be fragile","Missing checkpointing, so the job is neither idempotent nor resumable by content hash","The absence of a dual-index cutover, which would have kept the old index serving throughout","Poor observability, since the failure should have alerted well before the seven-hour mark"],"a":1,"exp":"Resumability is what makes rebuilds routine. An unresumable nine-hour job is one you quietly stop scheduling, and a rebuild you avoid is a chunking or embedding decision you can no longer revisit. Key work by content hash and skip what is already done."},{"q":"Your register has a store with no tenant partition key. Beyond deletion, the sharpest consequence is:","o":["Query performance suffers, because scans cannot be pruned to a single tenant''s partition","Rebuild cost rises, since the whole store must be rebuilt rather than one tenant at a time","Per-tenant rebuild is impossible, and scoped erasure cannot even be expressed as a request","Access-control propagation slows, as permission changes must fan out across every partition"],"a":2,"exp":"The partition key is what makes both a scoped delete and a per-tenant rebuild expressible at all. Losing per-tenant rebuild is what turns re-derivation from routine into an annual event, which is the same constraint that caps your iteration rate."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:migrate', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"migrate","block":"b3","title":"Migration & Rollout Architecture","tag":"The model will change under you, design so that''s a Tuesday, not a project","why":"In a field with quarterly turnover, the model you launched on will be deprecated, superseded, or repriced while you are still reading its release notes. Teams treat each swap as a project, budget it as a project, and are surprised every quarter. The architectural question is not how to migrate. It is what property the system needs so that migrating is configuration, and what evidence a given change is required to produce before it is allowed to be believed.","mental":"You never <b>switch</b>. You <b>run both</b> and let evidence decide. The architecture that can run two versions side by side on real traffic, and measure the difference at a stated resolution, is the one that gets to change its mind cheaply. Everything in this module is downstream of that one image: the dual-run, and the sample size that makes it mean something.","concepts":[["Migration capability is an architectural property","Not an ops task, not a ticket. The test is blunt: can you route 5% of traffic to a second version, and revert, <b>without a deploy</b>? If either half needs code, migration is a project every time and you will do it four times a year forever. Shadow, canary and instant revert belong in configuration, backfills belong in idempotent resumable jobs, and the whole capability gets exercised on a schedule whether or not you need it."],["The migration ladder and its blind spots","Each rung buys evidence and each rung is blind to something specific. <b>Offline eval</b> only knows the cases you wrote, so it misses distribution shift entirely. <b>Shadow</b> runs on real inputs but nobody acts on the output, so it misses user-behaviour change while cleanly catching schema, latency and cost surprises. <b>Canary</b> catches both and is slow on rare failures. Choosing a rung is choosing what you have agreed not to know, so say the sentence out loud."],["Sample size is the rollout plan","Given a baseline and the smallest regression you need to catch, the required sample per arm is fixed by arithmetic and the schedule falls out of it. It scales as <b>1 over the square of the effect</b>, which is the fact that surprises people: halving the effect you want to detect quadruples the traffic and the days. So the minimum detectable effect is a negotiation, not an input, and when the calendar is short it is the honest lever, provided the sentence gets written down: <b>we agreed not to detect a two-point drop, and this person signed for it.</b> Undocumented, the same trade is just an optimistic schedule."],["Rare failures need exposures, not percentages","A 1-in-2,000 failure is invisible to a canary sized for average quality. To be 95% confident of seeing at least one instance you need roughly <b>three times the inverse of the rate</b> in exposures, so about 6,000. A 5% canary on 40,000 tasks a day reaches that in three days and not in one. Percentages feel like coverage; only exposure counts are coverage, and the two diverge exactly where the dangerous failures live."],["Rollback triggers are numbers with owners","Defined before rollout, not during: the metric, the threshold, the observation window, and the human who pulls it without convening anyone. \"We''ll roll back if it looks bad\" is not a trigger, it is a hope with a passive voice. And rehearse it, in staging, on a schedule, because <b>if you have never rolled back, you do not have rollback</b>, you have a runbook nobody has executed under load."],["Data migrations and the two-truths window","Index and embedding changes follow dual-write, backfill, verify, cutover, and the interesting question is how long two stores both claim to be true and who reads which one during it. Bound the window explicitly, make the backfill idempotent and resumable so a crash resumes, and verify on a sample before the alias moves rather than after the complaints."],["The deprecation clock","Providers publish end-of-life dates and they do not move for you. Schedule <b>backwards</b> from the kill date through the ladder, the ramp and a buffer, and the start date falls out. Then run one migration per quarter whether or not you need one, because the muscle is the capability, and a team whose last swap was fourteen months ago will discover their eval set is stale exactly when the clock is shortest."]],"mistakes":["Sizing a canary by percentage and duration that felt reasonable, rather than by the sample the detection target requires","Treating a clean shadow run as permission to go to 100%, when shadow is structurally blind to user-behaviour change","Writing rollback triggers after launch, so the first one is negotiated in a call while the metric is still moving","Letting the deprecation clock run for five weeks before starting, then buying the lost days back with resolution","Never rehearsing a rollback, so the first live execution is also the first time anyone has run the procedure"],"flags":["\"We''ll canary at 5% for a few days\", a few days sized against which detectable effect?","\"Shadow looked clean, ship it\", nobody clicked anything in shadow","\"We''ll roll back if it looks bad\", name the metric, the number, the window and the person","\"We have 60 days, plenty of time\", the schedule runs backwards from the kill date, so start now","\"Rollback is one flag flip\", when did anyone last flip it, and was there traffic?"],"ask":["What is the smallest quality regression this rollout is powered to detect, and what sample does that require?","How many exposures does the canary accumulate, and what failure rate does that make us blind to?","Which rung are we on, and what is that rung structurally unable to see?","What is the rollback number, what window is it measured over, and who pulls it without a meeting?","When did we last execute a rollback with real traffic, and what did it cost in minutes?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"Monday 2026-09-01: the provider announces your model reaches end of life on <b>2026-10-31</b>. Sixty days. Everyone agrees there is plenty of time, and work starts on <b>2026-10-05</b>, with 26 days left.<br><br>Schedule backwards from the kill date. Buffer for the unexpected, 7 days, so 100% by 10-24. Ramp 50 to 100, 3 days, so the 50% stage ends 10-24. Canary ladder: baseline task success is <b>92%</b> and you want to detect a <b>1.5-point</b> drop at 80% power, alpha 0.05. Two-proportion arithmetic gives <b>5,570 scored tasks per arm</b>. At 40,000 tasks/day, a 5% cohort fully scored yields 2,000/day, so <b>3 days</b>; 25% and 50% clear it in a day each. Ladder = 5 days, starting 10-16. Shadow, 5 days, starting 10-11. Offline eval and re-baseline, 3 days, starting <b>10-08</b>. Total <b>23 days</b> against 26 available. Three days of slack. Fine.<br><br>Then on 10-05 someone opens the golden set and finds it was last refreshed in <b>February</b>. Since then the product shipped bulk-export summarisation and multilingual intake, which together are <b>22% of live traffic and 0% of the eval set</b>. Offline eval covers 78% of what users actually do. Refreshing it: sample 400 production traces across the two new task types, two labellers for 4 days, then 3 days to write judges and baseline the current model, about 11 working days, <b>15 calendar days</b>.<br><br>New total: <b>38 days</b> against 26. You miss the kill date by <b>12 days</b>, and the kill date does not negotiate.<br><br>What is left to sell? Resolution. Raise the minimum detectable effect from 1.5 points to <b>3.0</b> and the sample falls from 5,570 to <b>1,499 per arm</b>, a <b>3.7x</b> reduction, because n scales as 1/delta squared. The canary ladder compresses from 5 days to 3. Drop the shadow rung, 5 days. Cut the buffer from 7 days to 2. Total: <b>exactly 26 days</b>, landing on 10-31 with zero spare.<br><br>Read the bill. The 5% stage now accumulates 2,000 exposures, and seeing a 1-in-2,000 failure with 95% confidence takes about <b>6,000</b>, so you are blind to rare failures at the only stage small enough to contain them. You have agreed not to detect a 2-point quality drop. You gave up the rung that would have caught a cost or schema surprise before users did. <b>The stale eval set did not cost you time. It cost you resolution, and the invoice arrived in production.</b>","good":["Shadow, canary percentage and instant revert are configuration; nobody deploys code to change or undo a rollout","Every rollout plan states the baseline, the minimum detectable effect, the sample per arm and the resulting days at each percentage","Rollback triggers are written before traffic moves: metric, threshold, window, and one named person who pulls without a meeting","Backfills are idempotent and resumable by content hash, and the two-truths window is bounded and stated in the migration doc","One migration is rehearsed per quarter regardless of need, and the golden set is refreshed against live traffic mix on the same cadence"],"build":"Write the migration runbook for the next model swap you will genuinely have to do, the one with a date attached or obviously coming. One page, and it must contain numbers.<br><br>(1) <b>Baseline</b>: your current measured quality metric, with the sample it was measured on. (2) <b>Minimum detectable effect</b>: the smallest regression that would matter, stated in points, and the sample per arm the Patterns tab calculator says that requires. (3) <b>The ladder</b>: which rungs, at what percentages, for how many days, with the exposure count at each stage and the failure rate that count makes you blind to. (4) <b>Rollback trigger</b>: one metric, one number, one observation window, one named human who pulls it without convening anyone. (5) <b>Backwards schedule</b>: from the deadline through buffer, ramp and ladder, so the start date is derived rather than chosen.<br><br>Then <b>rehearse the rollback in staging</b>. Time it with a stopwatch, from the decision to traffic actually served by the old version. Write the number in the runbook. Most teams discover the revert takes four times longer than they assumed, or that it reverts the model and leaves the new prompt in place. Both are much cheaper to learn on a Tuesday afternoon than at 02:00 with the trigger already breached.","res":[["Google SRE Workbook, Canarying Releases","https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/","Use when designing the ladder, its treatment of canary sizing and evaluation windows transfers directly to quality metrics."],["Rule of three (statistics)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_three_(statistics)","Use right now: it is the one-line answer to \"how many exposures before I can claim I would have seen it?\""],["Statistical power","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_of_a_test","Use when someone asks why a shorter canary cannot simply be believed a bit less confidently."],["Anthropic model deprecations","https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/about-claude/model-deprecations","Use when building the backwards schedule, a published end-of-life policy is the shape of clock every provider imposes."]]},"depth":{"mech":"The dual-run is the whole mechanism. You never switch; you run both and measure. Everything else is a question about what evidence the dual-run produces and how much it costs to obtain.<br><br><b>The ladder, and what each rung cannot see.</b> Offline eval runs the new version against cases you already wrote, so it is fast, cheap, repeatable, and blind to distribution shift by construction, it can only be wrong in the ways you anticipated. Shadow sends real production inputs to both versions and discards the new one''s output; it catches schema changes, latency regressions and cost surprises on real input distribution, and it is structurally blind to user behaviour because nobody ever saw the output and therefore nobody clicked, abandoned, retried or escalated. Canary exposes real users and catches both classes, and pays for it in exposure time, which makes it slow precisely where failures are rare. Choosing a rung is choosing an ignorance, so name the ignorance in the plan.<br><br><b>Sample size is the plan.</b> For a quality rate you can score, comparing new against control is a two-proportion test. Given baseline p, a minimum detectable effect delta, alpha and power, the required n per arm is fixed. The property that governs schedules is that n scales as 1/delta squared: at a 92% baseline, detecting a 1.5-point drop needs about 5,570 scored tasks per arm, and detecting a 3.0-point drop needs about 1,499, a 3.7x difference for a factor of two in resolution. Days at each percentage follow from that number divided by the scored volume that percentage yields, which is why the canary percentage and the scoring fraction are the same decision. During a rollout, score the entire canary cohort rather than your usual production sample, it is 5% of traffic and it is the only period where the sample rate is on the critical path.<br><br><b>Rare failures are a different calculation entirely.</b> Average quality can look untouched while a 1-in-2,000 catastrophic path is live. To be 95% confident of observing at least one instance you need roughly 3/rate exposures, about 6,000 for that rate, which a 5% canary on 40,000 daily tasks reaches in three days and misses in one. Report exposures alongside percentages in every rollout plan, because percentages create a feeling of coverage that exposure counts flatly contradict.<br><br><b>Rollback is a number and a person.</b> Metric, threshold, window, owner, all written before traffic moves, because a trigger negotiated while the metric is moving is not a trigger. Rehearsal is what converts the runbook into a capability: schedule it, run it with real traffic in staging, time it, and write the number down. If the last rehearsal was never, you have documentation, not rollback.<br><br><b>Data migrations</b> follow the same logic with a longer tail: dual-write to both stores, backfill history, verify on a sample, then move the alias. The design question is the two-truths window, how long both stores claim to be correct and who reads which. Idempotent, resumable backfills keyed by content hash are what keep that window bounded, because a job that restarts from zero after a crash extends it by its entire runtime. And underneath all of it is the deprecation clock: providers publish kill dates, the dates do not move, so schedule backwards from them and start on the derived date rather than when the calendar feels comfortable.","trade":[["Shadow before canary vs going straight to canary","Days of schedule vs catching cost and schema surprises off-users","Discovering a 3x cost regression from the invoice instead of from a shadow run"],["Tight minimum detectable effect vs a schedule that fits","Resolution vs days, at 1 over delta squared","Buying the calendar with resolution and never writing down what you agreed not to detect"],["Small canary for longer vs large canary briefly","Blast radius vs time to reach the exposure count","A 1% canary that would need three weeks to see the failure it was created to find"],["Automated rollback on threshold vs a human decision","Seconds to revert vs flapping on a noisy metric","Auto-rollback triggered by a scoring outage, or a human waiting for consensus at 02:00"],["One migration per quarter by policy vs migrating only when forced","Standing capability vs the work you skip","The first swap in fourteen months, run against an eval set that is fourteen months stale"]],"scale":["Higher traffic buys resolution for free, the same three-day canary detects a much smaller effect, so large systems should tighten the detectable effect rather than shorten the ladder","Low-traffic and long-tail surfaces cannot reach the sample at any percentage, so they migrate on offline eval plus a longer soak, and the plan should say that outright","Judge cost during rollout is bounded by cohort size, scoring 100% of a 5% canary costs 5% of traffic, which is why full canary scoring is affordable exactly when it matters","Backfill duration grows with corpus while the two-truths window should not, so resumability and per-tenant batching are what keep the window flat as you grow"],"sec":["Shadow traffic duplicates real inputs into a second system, so it inherits every data-residency, retention and subprocessor constraint the primary path has","A rollback that reverts the model but leaves a new prompt or tool schema live is a configuration split-brain, version the behaviour bundle and revert it as one unit","Canary cohorts assigned by user identity leak the experiment across tenants in a shared workspace, partition on the tenant boundary rather than the user","Dual-write windows double the write surface, an access-control change during the window must reach both stores or the older one serves a revoked permission"],"interview":"My starting position is that you never switch, you run both and let evidence decide, so the property I care about architecturally is whether shadow, canary and instant revert are configuration rather than code. If either half needs a deploy, every model change is a project and you''ll run one every quarter forever. Then the real question is what evidence a given change has to produce. I think of it as a ladder where each rung is blind to something specific: offline eval only knows the cases we wrote, shadow misses user-behaviour change because nobody clicked anything, and canary catches both but is slow on rare failures. Picking a rung means picking what we''ve agreed not to know, so I say that out loud. And I insist the sample size lives in the rollout plan, not the retro. At a 92% baseline, detecting a point and a half needs about 5,570 scored tasks per arm; three points needs about 1,500, because n scales as one over delta squared. That''s also the honest lever when a deprecation clock is short, you buy days with resolution, and you write down the drop you agreed not to detect and who signed for it. Rollback is a metric, a number, a window and a named person, rehearsed quarterly, because if you''ve never rolled back you don''t have rollback."},"patterns":{"intro":"A rollout planner. You give it the baseline, the regression you need to catch, the traffic and the ladder; it gives you the sample per arm, the days at each percentage, the exposure count, and the rollback threshold, plus a verdict against the deprecation clock. The normal CDF is implemented here so nothing is imported, and the three scenarios show what a stale eval set actually costs.","code":"\"\"\"Rollout planner: how long must each rung of the migration ladder run?\n\nSample size is the rollout plan. This computes it, then prints the schedule\nand the per-stage rollback thresholds. Stdlib only (math), deterministic,\nno network. The normal CDF is implemented here so nothing is imported.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- statistics\n\n\ndef norm_cdf(z):\n    return 0.5 * (1.0 + math.erf(z / math.sqrt(2.0)))\n\n\ndef z_for(p, lo=-12.0, hi=12.0, tol=1e-12):\n    \"\"\"Inverse normal CDF by bisection. Deterministic, no scipy.\"\"\"\n    for _ in range(200):\n        mid = (lo + hi) / 2.0\n        if norm_cdf(mid) < p:\n            lo = mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n        if hi - lo < tol:\n            break\n    return (lo + hi) / 2.0\n\n\ndef sample_size_per_arm(baseline, mde, alpha=0.05, power=0.80):\n    \"\"\"Two-proportion test. n per arm to detect a drop of `mde` from `baseline`.\"\"\"\n    p1, p2 = baseline, baseline - mde\n    if p2 <= 0 or mde <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"mde must be positive and smaller than baseline\")\n    pbar = (p1 + p2) / 2.0\n    za = z_for(1 - alpha / 2.0)\n    zb = z_for(power)\n    num = (za * math.sqrt(2 * pbar * (1 - pbar))\n           + zb * math.sqrt(p1 * (1 - p1) + p2 * (1 - p2))) ** 2\n    return math.ceil(num / (mde ** 2))\n\n\ndef exposures_for_rare_event(rate, confidence=0.95):\n    \"\"\"Rule of three, generalised: N to see >=1 event of probability `rate`.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(math.log(1 - confidence) / math.log(1 - rate))\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the ladder\n\nLADDER = [\n    (\"offline eval\", \"misses distribution shift -- it only knows the cases you wrote\"),\n    (\"shadow\", \"misses user-behaviour change -- nobody clicked anything\"),\n    (\"canary\", \"catches both, but is slow on rare failures\"),\n    (\"full\", \"catches everything, informs nothing\"),\n]\n\n\ndef plan(label, baseline, mde, daily_tasks, stages, scored_fraction, rare_rate,\n         eval_refresh_days=0, offline_days=3, shadow_days=5, ramp_days=3,\n         buffer_days=7, alpha=0.05, power=0.80):\n    n = sample_size_per_arm(baseline, mde, alpha, power)\n    rare_n = exposures_for_rare_event(rare_rate)\n    rows, ladder = [], 0\n    for pct in stages:\n        per_day = daily_tasks * pct * scored_fraction\n        days = math.ceil(n / per_day) if per_day else float(\"inf\")\n        exposures = daily_tasks * pct * days\n        rows.append({\"pct\": pct, \"scored_per_day\": per_day, \"days\": days,\n                     \"exposures\": exposures, \"rare_ok\": exposures >= rare_n,\n                     \"rollback_at\": baseline - mde})\n        ladder += days\n    fixed = eval_refresh_days + offline_days + shadow_days + ramp_days + buffer_days\n    return {\"label\": label, \"mde\": mde, \"n_per_arm\": n, \"rare_n\": rare_n,\n            \"stages\": rows, \"ladder_days\": ladder, \"total_days\": ladder + fixed,\n            \"parts\": [(\"eval refresh\", eval_refresh_days), (\"offline eval\", offline_days),\n                      (\"shadow\", shadow_days), (\"canary ladder\", ladder),\n                      (\"ramp to 100%\", ramp_days), (\"buffer\", buffer_days)]}\n\n\ndef show(p, baseline, rare_rate, days_to_kill):\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"ROLLOUT PLAN -- %s\" % p[\"label\"])\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"  baseline %.1f%% | MDE %.1f points | alpha 0.05 | power 0.80\"\n          % (baseline * 100, p[\"mde\"] * 100))\n    print(\"  sample size: %s scored tasks PER ARM\" % format(p[\"n_per_arm\"], \",\"))\n    print(\"  rare-failure floor: %s exposures for 95%% confidence of seeing a 1-in-%d event\"\n          % (format(p[\"rare_n\"], \",\"), round(1 / rare_rate)))\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  %-6s %11s %5s %11s %-8s %s\"\n          % (\"stage\", \"scored/day\", \"days\", \"exposures\", \"rare?\", \"roll back if\"))\n    for r in p[\"stages\"]:\n        print(\"  %-6s %11s %5d %11s %-8s quality < %.1f%%\"\n              % (\"%.0f%%\" % (r[\"pct\"] * 100), format(int(r[\"scored_per_day\"]), \",\"),\n                 r[\"days\"], format(int(r[\"exposures\"]), \",\"),\n                 \"ok\" if r[\"rare_ok\"] else \"BLIND\", r[\"rollback_at\"] * 100))\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  \" + \"  \".join(\"%s %d\" % (k, v) for k, v in p[\"parts\"] if v))\n    slack = days_to_kill - p[\"total_days\"]\n    verdict = \"FITS with %d days spare\" % slack if slack >= 0 else \"MISSES BY %d DAYS\" % -slack\n    print(\"  TOTAL %d days vs %d to the kill date -- %s\"\n          % (p[\"total_days\"], days_to_kill, verdict))\n    print(\"\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    BASELINE = 0.92          # measured task success today\n    DAILY = 40_000           # tasks/day\n    SCORED = 1.00            # fraction of each cohort your judge scores during rollout\n    STAGES = [0.05, 0.25, 0.50]\n    RARE_RATE = 1 / 2000.0\n    DAYS_TO_KILL = 26        # what the provider''s deprecation clock says\n\n    print(\"WHAT EACH RUNG CANNOT SEE\")\n    print(\"-\" * 72)\n    for rung, blind in LADDER:\n        print(\"  %-13s %s\" % (rung, blind))\n    print(\"\")\n\n    a = plan(\"the plan you wrote, evals fresh\", BASELINE, 0.015, DAILY, STAGES, SCORED, RARE_RATE)\n    b = plan(\"same plan, evals 6 months stale\", BASELINE, 0.015, DAILY, STAGES, SCORED, RARE_RATE,\n             eval_refresh_days=15)\n    c = plan(\"what the calendar forces\", BASELINE, 0.030, DAILY, STAGES, SCORED, RARE_RATE,\n             eval_refresh_days=15, shadow_days=0, buffer_days=2)\n    for p in (a, b, c):\n        show(p, BASELINE, RARE_RATE, DAYS_TO_KILL)\n\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"THE TRADE YOU ARE ACTUALLY MAKING\")\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"  n falls %.1fx when the MDE doubles -- n scales as 1/delta^2.\"\n          % (a[\"n_per_arm\"] / c[\"n_per_arm\"]))\n    print(\"  A stale eval set cost %d days, and you paid for them in resolution:\"\n          % (b[\"total_days\"] - a[\"total_days\"]))\n    print(\"  the agreed blind spot went from %.1f to %.1f points, the shadow rung was\"\n          % (a[\"mde\"] * 100, c[\"mde\"] * 100))\n    print(\"  dropped, and the buffer went from 7 days to 2.\")\n    print(\"\")\n    print(\"  You do not buy schedule with hope. You buy it with resolution.\")\n    print(\"  Write down the quality drop you agreed not to detect, and who signed for it.\")\n","notes":["<code>z_for</code> is an inverse normal CDF by bisection. It is deterministic and needs no third-party package, which matters because this has to run in whatever environment your planning doc lives in.","The <code>rare?</code> column compares exposures against the rule-of-three floor. It is the column that fires on a plan that otherwise looks statistically respectable.","Scoring fraction defaults to 1.0 on purpose. During a rollout you score the whole canary cohort, not your usual production sample, because sample rate is on the critical path for exactly these few days.","The three scenarios differ only in <code>eval_refresh_days</code>, MDE, shadow and buffer, which is precisely the set of things people trade away under deadline, in that order.","Rollback threshold is printed per stage as baseline minus MDE, so the trigger you paste into the runbook is the same number the plan was powered to detect. Anything tighter is noise."],"debug":[["Canary passed, production quality dropped after full rollout","Canary ended before reaching the required sample per arm","Recompute n from the baseline and MDE, then set days from n divided by scored volume, not from a calendar preference"],["A rare catastrophic failure appeared only at 100%","Exposures never reached the rule-of-three floor for that rate","Print exposures per stage and lengthen the smallest stage until it clears 3/rate for the worst failure you fear"],["Shadow was clean, users complained immediately","Shadow cannot observe behaviour, nobody acted on its output","Move the change to a canary rung; keep shadow for schema, latency and cost, which is what it is actually good at"],["Rollback executed but the regression persisted","Model reverted, prompt or tool schema did not","Version the behaviour bundle as one artifact and revert it atomically; rehearse quarterly and time it"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:migrate', 'paid', '[{"q":"Baseline task success is 92% and you need to detect a 1.5-point drop at 80% power. You now decide 0.75 points matters. The sample per arm:","o":["Doubles, since required sample scales linearly with the effect size you want to detect","Roughly quadruples, because required sample scales as one over the effect size squared","Stays flat, as sample size is driven by the baseline rate rather than the effect size","Rises about 40%, following the square root relationship between sample and precision"],"a":1,"exp":"n scales as 1/delta squared, so halving the detectable effect is a 4x cost in traffic and days. This is the single fact that decides whether a rollout schedule is honest, and the reason resolution is the lever people reach for when the clock is short."},{"q":"A shadow deployment ran five days on live inputs with no anomalies. What is it structurally unable to tell you?","o":["Whether the new version''s latency profile holds up under real production input distribution","Whether output token growth has pushed cost per task above the budgeted figure per route","Whether users behave differently, since nobody ever saw or acted on the shadow output","Whether the response schema still validates against every downstream consumer''s expectations"],"a":2,"exp":"Shadow is excellent at latency, cost and schema because those are properties of the call itself. It is blind to abandonment, retries, escalation and satisfaction because no human was ever exposed. Clearing shadow is permission to canary, never permission to ship."},{"q":"You fear a 1-in-2,000 catastrophic failure. A 5% canary on 40,000 tasks/day. How long before you can claim you would have seen it?","o":["One day, since 2,000 exposures already equals the reciprocal of the failure rate itself","Three days, about 6,000 exposures, roughly three times the inverse of the rate","Seven days, since a full week is required to cover weekday and weekend traffic patterns","It cannot be established at 5%, because rare failures require a full production rollout"],"a":1,"exp":"Rule of three: about 3/rate exposures gives 95% confidence of seeing at least one. 2,000 exposures leaves a roughly 37% chance of seeing nothing even though the failure is live. Report exposure counts, not percentages, or the plan will feel safer than it is."},{"q":"A provider announces a 60-day deprecation. The correct first move is:","o":["Book the migration into the next planning cycle, which comfortably precedes the kill date","Start the offline eval immediately, since the ladder can be compressed later if needed","Schedule backwards from the kill date through buffer, ramp and ladder to derive a start date","Ask the provider for an extension, as published end-of-life dates are frequently negotiable"],"a":2,"exp":"The start date is derived, not chosen. Working forwards is how a team spends five of eight weeks feeling comfortable, then buys the lost days back with resolution. Working backwards also exposes prerequisites, like a stale eval set, while there is still slack to absorb them."},{"q":"Deadline pressure means the ladder must shorten by five days. The most honest lever is:","o":["Raise the minimum detectable effect and write down the drop you have agreed not to detect","Increase the canary percentage so the required sample accumulates over noticeably fewer days","Reduce the scoring fraction, which cuts judge cost and lets more stages run in parallel","Skip the offline eval rung, since the canary supersedes everything offline eval could show"],"a":0,"exp":"Raising the MDE is a real trade made explicit. Raising canary percentage does shorten days but widens blast radius, and is a fine second lever if stated. Cutting the scoring fraction lengthens the schedule. Offline eval is the cheapest rung and the wrong one to drop."},{"q":"Your rollback runbook exists, is reviewed annually, and has never been executed with real traffic. You have:","o":["Rollback capability, provided the runbook has been reviewed by more than one engineer","Documentation, since an unrehearsed procedure has no measured duration and no proof","Partial capability, sufficient for low-severity regressions but not for a full outage","Adequate coverage, as long as the feature flag it depends on is exercised in other flows"],"a":1,"exp":"An unrehearsed rollback has no measured time-to-revert, and rehearsals routinely reveal that the model reverts while the prompt or tool schema does not. Rehearse quarterly, time it with a stopwatch, and put the number in the runbook."},{"q":"During an index migration you dual-write to old and new stores while a backfill runs. The design question that matters most is:","o":["Which store receives writes first, so that ordering guarantees hold across both destinations","How the backfill is parallelised, since throughput sets the total duration of the migration","How long both stores claim to be true, and which of them reads serve during that window","Whether the new store''s schema is a strict superset of the old, to keep rollback available"],"a":2,"exp":"The two-truths window is the risk. Bound it explicitly, state who reads which store during it, and make the backfill idempotent and resumable so a crash at 80% does not extend the window by a whole runtime. The others are real concerns, one rung down."},{"q":"Traffic grows 10x. Your standing three-day canary at 5% now yields ten times the sample. The right response is:","o":["Shorten the canary proportionally, since the required sample now accumulates in far less time","Reduce the canary to 0.5%, keeping sample constant while shrinking the blast radius sharply","Tighten the detectable effect, converting the surplus sample into resolution you did not have","Leave the plan unchanged, as the extra sample simply raises confidence in the same conclusion"],"a":2,"exp":"Extra traffic is resolution you can now afford, and the smaller regressions it exposes are the ones that used to reach production unnoticed. Shrinking the cohort or the window is defensible for blast radius, but it spends the gain rather than banking it."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:quality', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"quality","block":"b4","title":"Quality as an Architectural Property","tag":"Where the gates sit, what they''re allowed to block, and who owns the number","why":"\"We need better evals\" is what every team says after being burned once, and the sentence contains no architecture: it doesn''t say where a check sits, what it''s permitted to stop, which budget line pays for it, or who gets woken when it fires. Quality in a running system isn''t a score you possess, it''s a set of places you built where something is authorized to say no, and every one of them is a line item you can price. This module turns that sentence into a diagram with numbers on it, which is the only form in which it survives a review.","mental":"Quality isn''t a score your system has. It''s <b>a set of places in the system where something is authorized to say no</b>, and architecture is choosing how many of those places exist, where they sit, and what each one is allowed to stop. Add a place and you spend milliseconds, dollars, and good answers wrongly blocked. Remove one and you buy the budget back and accept the escapes. The single most common defect in a quality design is a place that observes but cannot stop anything: <b>a control point without an authority is a dashboard nobody reads.</b>","concepts":[["Control point","A place, on the request path or beside it, where something is authorized to stop something else. You have five to choose from and a fixed budget to buy them with. The design question is never \"do we have evals\", it''s \"how many control points, sitting where, paid for out of which line?\""],["Authority","What a control point is permitted to do the moment it fires: block the release, block the request, block the response, open a ticket, or record only. Assign it explicitly, per control point, in writing. Record-only is a legitimate choice. It is not a legitimate accident."],["Placement cost","Every control point is a line item in the latency and cost tables you built in Block 1. A synchronous check that adds 700ms against 400ms of headroom does not fit, and enthusiasm doesn''t change that. Price it before you argue about it, because the argument ends the moment the arithmetic appears."],["False-block rate","The share of good traffic a blocking control point wrongly stops. Rails have a cost curve in both directions: a 2% false block on a support flow is itself a support incident, paid in tickets rather than in tokens, and it''s usually the larger of the two bills."],["Detection lag","How long a new regression runs before anyone is paged. It''s a division, not a vibe: alarm threshold over (daily volume x incidence x sampling rate x recognition rate). Choose the sampling rate by the lag it produces, never by whether the number is round."],["Escaped-defect rate","The share of tasks that reach a user wrong after every blocking control point has had its chance. This is the number your spec card''s quality bar is actually about. Detecting-only control points never lower it; they shorten how long it stays high, which is a different purchase."],["Control-point ownership","Each control point needs a named owner, a page target, and a growth process, because checks decay in both directions. The golden set is a product artifact whose relevance expires as traffic moves, and the judge is a component that drifts, so the judge itself needs a control point watching it."]],"mistakes":["Adding a control point without deciding its authority, so it ships as a score on a dashboard and has never stopped anything","Pricing a synchronous check in tokens when its dominant cost is false blocks, paid in support handling time","Choosing a sampling rate because the number is round (1%) instead of dividing it into the incidence rate you''re trying to catch","Putting human review on the highest-volume flow, where it can''t keep up, instead of the highest-blast-radius flow, where it''s the only thing that works","Treating the judge and the golden set as infrastructure that stays correct on its own, with no owner, no growth process, and no control point watching them drift"],"flags":["\"We''ll add an eval for that\" - with no answer to where it sits, what it''s allowed to block, or which budget line pays for it","\"The gate is in CI\" - and CI has been green on every release this year because the gate''s authority is record-only","\"We sample one percent\" - a number chosen for its roundness and never once divided into an incidence rate","\"The rail is only two percent false positives\" - quoted as a small ratio, never multiplied by monthly volume or a ticket cost","\"Quality is the AI team''s problem\" - the number has no owner on the product side, so nobody defends it when a launch date is at stake"],"ask":["For each control point on this diagram: what is it allowed to block, and who gets paged when it fires?","How many milliseconds and how many dollars per task do our checks consume, and against how much headroom?","If quality regressed on half a percent of traffic this morning, how many days until someone is paged? Show me the division.","What is our false-block rate, and what does one false block cost us downstream?","Which failure mode on the FMEA has no control point, and which control point has no owner?"],"estMin":45},"deep":{"worked":"One feature, two placements, priced against the same spec card. The feature: a billing support assistant. Spec card says p95 3,000ms, base pipeline p95 2,400ms (so 600ms of headroom), cost ceiling $0.0120/task against a base of $0.0081, 400,000 tasks/month, quality bar under 0.5% wrong answers, blast radius \"user-visible guidance, no automatic money movement\".<br><br><b>Placement A - a synchronous grounding gate on every response.</b> Measured p95 of the gate: 700ms. 2,400 + 700 = 3,100ms, against a 3,000ms budget. It fails on latency before you discuss anything else, and it fails by 100ms, which is the worst kind of failure because it invites people to argue about it. Cost: the gate re-reads the retrieved context, adding $0.0026/task, so $0.0107/task total, which does fit under the $0.0120 ceiling. Monthly compute: 400,000 x $0.0026 = $1,040. Now the part nobody prices: on a 3,000-response audit the gate''s false-block rate came out at 2.1%. 2.1% of 400,000 is <b>8,400 good answers per month refused</b>. If one in six of those users contacts support and a contact costs $0.68 in expected handling, that''s 8,400 x $0.68 = <b>$5,712/month</b>, five and a half times the gate''s compute bill. What it buys: escaped defects drop from roughly 0.25% to 0.06% of traffic, about 1,010 wrong answers a month down to 236. At $6.40 of damage per wrong answer served, that''s $6,464 saved against $6,752 spent.<br><br><b>Placement B - a deterministic rail inline, plus 3% async sampling.</b> The rail checks two things that need no model call: does the response match the required envelope, and does every cited document id appear in the set actually retrieved. Measured p95: 30ms. 2,400 + 30 = 2,430ms, leaving 570ms of headroom intact for the next thing that needs it. False-block rate 0.23%, or 920 answers a month, and every one of those was genuinely malformed. Async sampling at 3%: 12,000 scored tasks/month at $0.0026 = $31/month, and zero milliseconds on the request path because nothing waits for it.<br><br><b>The number that decides it is detection lag.</b> Daily volume 13,333. A regression touching 0.5% of traffic produces 13,333 x 0.005 = 67 bad answers/day. At 3% sampling with a 74% recognition rate you see 67 x 0.03 x 0.74 = 1.5 of them per day, so an 8-event alarm threshold fires in <b>5.4 days</b>, during which about 360 wrong answers reach users. At 1% sampling that same alarm takes 16 days. At 0.1% it takes 162 days, which is not a detection plan, it''s a quarterly surprise with a spreadsheet attached.<br><br><b>Which spec card selects which.</b> Placement B wins here: it fits the latency budget, its total monthly cost of being wrong is roughly $8,300 against Placement A''s $10,600, and its escape rate of 0.25% still clears the 0.5% quality bar. Now change one line on the spec card, not the design: make the blast radius \"applies an account credit automatically, irreversible\" and the quality bar 0.05%. Placement B''s 0.25% now misses the bar by 5x, and the 5.4-day blind window is 360 irreversible credits. Placement A''s 700ms is also no longer a problem, because an irreversible money-moving flow gets a p95 of 8,000ms and a confirmation screen. Same two checks, same arithmetic, opposite answer. <b>The check didn''t change. The spec card did.</b>","good":["Every control point on the request diagram is labelled with its authority, its p95 and cost contribution, its false-block rate, and the rotation that gets paged","The sampling rate is derived from a target detection lag and written down as a division, so raising or lowering it is an argument about days rather than about round numbers","The false-block rate is tracked and priced in downstream cost, and reviewed with the same seriousness as the escape rate","The golden set has a named product owner and a growth rule (every escaped defect becomes a case within one week), so its relevance doesn''t expire quietly","The judge has its own control point: a periodic agreement check against fresh human labels, with a threshold that pages someone when it drops"],"build":"Take the request diagram for a system you own and mark every control point on it. For each one write five things, and refuse to move on until all five have values: <b>(1) what it checks</b>, in one clause. <b>(2) Its authority</b>, exactly one of: block the release, block the request, block the response, open a ticket, record only. <b>(3) Its cost</b>, as added p95 in milliseconds and dollars per task, quoted against the headroom in your latency and cost tables. <b>(4) Its false-block rate</b>, measured if you have the data, estimated with a stated method if you don''t, multiplied by monthly volume so it''s a count and not a ratio. <b>(5) Who gets paged</b> when it fires, and who gets paged when it stops firing.<br><br>Then do the two searches that make the hour worth it. <b>Find the check with no owner</b>: ask who would notice if it silently started passing everything. If the answer is nobody, give it an owner or delete it, because a check nobody maintains is worse than no check - it''s a check people believe in. <b>Find the failure mode with no check</b>: take your FMEA table from Block 2 and, for every row with detectability 7 or higher, name the control point that would catch it. The rows where you can''t are your quality roadmap, already sorted by severity. Bring both lists to your next review.","res":[["Google SRE Workbook - Alerting on SLOs","https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/","Use when you''re choosing a sampling rate or an alarm threshold - the burn-rate arithmetic here is exactly the detection-lag math, written for availability."],["Sensitivity and specificity (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sensitivity_and_specificity","Use right now: before any argument about a rail''s threshold, so both sides are talking about the same two error types instead of one each."],["Nancy Leveson, Engineering a Safer World (MIT Press)","https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262533690/engineering-a-safer-world/","Use when you want the intellectual foundation for control points - safety as a control structure rather than a component property, from the field that got there first."],["Sample size determination (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sample_size_determination","Use when someone asks how many samples you need before an alarm is trustworthy, and you''d rather answer with a formula than a shrug."]]},"depth":{"mech":"You have five control points, each with fixed properties you cannot design around. <b>The offline CI gate</b> is cheap and blocks releases, the strongest authority here because it stops the change rather than the response - and it is structurally blind to production distribution, because it scores a fixed set that stops resembling live traffic the day after you freeze it. <b>The input rail</b> is cheap, sits before the expensive work, and blocks requests, so its false positives reach the user as a refusal to try. <b>The output rail</b> blocks responses and taxes the latency budget of <b>every single response</b>, including the 99.7% that were fine, which is why a deterministic output rail is worth a great deal and a model-call one usually isn''t. <b>Async online sampling</b> costs zero milliseconds and sees real traffic, but detects hours or days late and prevents nothing. <b>Human review</b> has the highest fidelity on the list and does not scale, which isn''t a flaw but a placement instruction: maximum blast radius, minimum volume.<br><br>The composition rules follow from that. Only blocking control points change the escaped-defect rate, and they compose multiplicatively on the residual: three blocking checks catching 22%, 18% and 10% of the defect stream leave 0.78 x 0.82 x 0.90 = 57.6% of it, not 50%. Detecting-only control points leave the escape rate exactly where it was and buy you a shorter blind window instead, which is a real purchase but a different one, and conflating the two is the most common modelling error in these designs. Deterministic rails and semantic checks also don''t catch the same things: a well-formed, correctly-cited, confidently wrong answer passes a schema rail and a citation-existence rail at 100%, every time, by construction. That''s why a design made entirely of cheap rails has a great p95 and no ability to see the failure mode that actually damages trust.<br><br><b>The budget arithmetic is the whole conversation.</b> Every control point contributes added p95 (zero for anything off the request path), dollars per task equal to its per-invocation cost times the share of traffic it inspects, and a false-block count equal to volume times coverage times false-positive rate. Sum the first against your latency headroom, the second against your cost ceiling, and price the third in whatever a blocked good answer costs you downstream. Combinations that break either envelope are not options, no matter how much anyone likes them, and saying so with a number ends the debate in about forty seconds.<br><br><b>Detection lag is a division you should do out loud.</b> Events per day equals daily volume times the regression''s incidence times your sampling rate times the recognition rate of whatever is doing the scoring. Days to alarm is your alarm threshold divided by that. Run it once for your real numbers and the sampling-rate argument resolves itself, because most teams discover their round 1% produces a two-to-three week lag on the regressions they''re most afraid of.<br><br><b>And the control points themselves decay.</b> The judge drifts when the provider ships a new version or when traffic moves somewhere the rubric never anticipated, so it needs a control point of its own: a periodic agreement check against fresh human labels, with a threshold and a page target. The golden set decays the same way, which makes it a product artifact with an owner and a growth rule rather than a file in a repo. A quality system with no control point on its own control points will pass every check on the day it stops working.","trade":[["Synchronous blocking gate vs async sampling","Escapes prevented vs latency headroom and false blocks","A p95 you can''t meet plus a false-block bill larger than the damage you prevented"],["Deterministic rail vs model-based check","Cost and speed vs the class of defect it can see at all","A fast, cheap wall of rails that is structurally blind to confidently wrong answers"],["Offline CI gate vs online sampling","Blocks the change vs sees real traffic","Green releases all quarter while production quality slides, or detection with nothing to block"],["Higher sampling rate vs lower scoring spend","Detection lag vs cost per task","A round sampling number that produces a three-week blind window on your worst regression"],["Human review on volume vs on blast radius","Examples seen vs damage prevented","Reviewers drowning in low-stakes traffic while the irreversible flow goes unwatched"]],"scale":["Rails scale with traffic, human review does not: past roughly a few hundred reviews a day you are choosing what humans see, so choose by blast radius and write the rule down","At 3x peak an output rail''s p95 contribution doesn''t change, it''s just charged 3x as often - what breaks first is the scoring queue behind async sampling, so give it its own degradation rung","Tier the sampling rate by route rather than globally - 100% on the money-moving route, 1% on the browse route - which buys detection lag where it matters at a fraction of the cost","Escape count and false-block cost both grow linearly with volume, so the crossover between two placements is itself a volume number - compute it once and record it as a revisit trigger"],"sec":["An input rail is a security control and a quality control at once with a single authority, so decide whether a suspected-malicious request is blocked or logged before an incident forces it","False blocks are a denial-of-service surface: if a rail can be reliably triggered by user-supplied content, someone can lock a competitor''s tenant out of your product","Control-point logs hold the raw inputs and outputs that failed, making them the highest-sensitivity store in the system and the one most often exempted from retention policy","A record-only control point on a compliance-relevant behaviour is a documented, dated record that you knew and chose not to stop - assign real authority or don''t build the check"],"interview":"I don''t think about quality as a score, I think about it as a set of places in the system where something is authorized to say no. There are five to choose from: a CI gate that can block a release, an input rail that can block a request, an output rail that can block a response, async sampling that can open a ticket, and human review that can stop something outright but doesn''t scale. Architecture is picking how many of those you have, where they sit, and what each one is allowed to stop, and then paying for every one of them out of the latency and cost budgets. So when someone says we need better evals, my first two questions are what is it allowed to block and who gets paged, and my third is what it costs in milliseconds and dollars against the headroom we actually have. If the answer to the first question is nothing, it''s a dashboard, not a control point. And I always do the detection-lag division out loud, because a one percent sample against a half-percent regression means you find out next quarter, and nobody realises that until you divide it in front of them."},"patterns":{"intro":"Placement is a search problem with a budget constraint, which means you can enumerate it instead of arguing about it. This script takes your candidate checks and your spec card, tries every combination, throws out the ones that don''t fit the latency and cost envelope, and ranks the survivors by the total monthly cost of being wrong: compute plus false blocks plus escaped defects plus the damage done during the detection lag. The output is a table you paste into the design doc.","code":"\"\"\"Control-point placement optimizer.\n\nYou do not \"add evals\". You place control points, give each one an authority,\nand pay for every one of them out of the latency and cost budgets on your spec\ncard. This script enumerates every placement combination of a candidate check\nset, discards the ones that do not fit the envelope, and ranks the survivors by\nthe total monthly cost of being wrong: compute + false blocks + escaped defects\n+ the defects that reach users during the detection lag.\n\nStdlib only, deterministic, no network. Prices, latencies and rates are INPUTS.\nEdit SPEC and CHECKS to match your own design doc, then paste the table.\n\n    python control_points.py\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\n# --- the spec card (the Block 1 artifact this whole file is priced against) ---\nSPEC = {\n    \"name\":                 \"billing support assistant\",\n    \"p95_budget_ms\":        3000,\n    \"base_p95_ms\":          2400,     # pipeline p95 before any control point\n    \"cost_ceiling_task\":    0.0120,   # $/task\n    \"base_cost_task\":       0.0081,\n    \"tasks_per_month\":      400_000,\n    \"defect_rate\":          0.0060,   # wrong answers per task with zero controls\n    \"release_defect_share\": 0.55,     # share of defects introduced by a release\n    \"cost_per_escape\":      6.40,     # $ damage per wrong answer that reaches a user\n    \"cost_per_false_block\": 0.68,     # $ expected support cost per good answer blocked\n    \"regression_incidence\": 0.0050,   # a new regression touches 0.5% of traffic\n    \"regressions_per_month\": 0.50,    # how often that happens\n    \"alarm_events\":         8,        # sampled failures needed before anyone is paged\n}\n\n# --- the candidate control points --------------------------------------------\n# blocks   : does it stop something, or only observe?\n# cov_rel  : share of RELEASES it inspects (pre-production reach)\n# cov_prod : share of PRODUCTION traffic it inspects\n# cost_cov : share of tasks it is billed against\n# catch    : share of the standing defect stream it correctly identifies\n# regr     : share of a NEW quality regression it can recognise. A deterministic\n#            rail scores 0 here on purpose: a well-formed, correctly-cited,\n#            semantically wrong answer is invisible to it. That is the whole\n#            reason async sampling exists.\n# fp       : share of GOOD traffic it wrongly stops (0 if it cannot stop anything)\nCHECKS = [\n    dict(id=\"ci_eval_gate\",   authority=\"block_release\",  blocks=True,  p95_ms=0,\n         cov_rel=1.00, cov_prod=0.000, cost_cov=0.006, cost_call=0.0022,\n         catch=0.62, regr=0.62, fp=0.000, owner=\"platform\"),\n    dict(id=\"input_rail\",     authority=\"block_request\",  blocks=True,  p95_ms=14,\n         cov_rel=0.00, cov_prod=1.000, cost_cov=1.000, cost_call=0.00004,\n         catch=0.10, regr=0.00, fp=0.0060, owner=\"platform\"),\n    dict(id=\"schema_rail\",    authority=\"block_response\", blocks=True,  p95_ms=9,\n         cov_rel=0.00, cov_prod=1.000, cost_cov=1.000, cost_call=0.0,\n         catch=0.22, regr=0.00, fp=0.0003, owner=\"product\"),\n    dict(id=\"citation_rail\",  authority=\"block_response\", blocks=True,  p95_ms=21,\n         cov_rel=0.00, cov_prod=1.000, cost_cov=1.000, cost_call=0.0,\n         catch=0.18, regr=0.00, fp=0.0020, owner=\"product\"),\n    dict(id=\"sync_gate\",      authority=\"block_response\", blocks=True,  p95_ms=700,\n         cov_rel=0.00, cov_prod=1.000, cost_cov=1.000, cost_call=0.0026,\n         catch=0.74, regr=0.74, fp=0.0210, owner=\"platform\"),\n    dict(id=\"async_sample_3\", authority=\"open_ticket\",    blocks=False, p95_ms=0,\n         cov_rel=0.00, cov_prod=0.030, cost_cov=0.030, cost_call=0.0026,\n         catch=0.74, regr=0.74, fp=0.000, owner=\"product\"),\n    dict(id=\"human_review\",   authority=\"block_response\", blocks=True,  p95_ms=0,\n         cov_rel=0.00, cov_prod=0.002, cost_cov=0.002, cost_call=0.42,\n         catch=0.93, regr=0.93, fp=0.000, owner=\"ops\"),\n]\n\nALLOWED_AUTHORITY = {\"block_release\", \"block_request\", \"block_response\",\n                     \"open_ticket\", \"record_only\"}\n\n\ndef audit_authorities(checks):\n    \"\"\"A control point with no authority and no owner is a dashboard.\"\"\"\n    problems = []\n    for c in checks:\n        if c[\"authority\"] not in ALLOWED_AUTHORITY:\n            problems.append(\"%s: unknown authority %r\" % (c[\"id\"], c[\"authority\"]))\n        if c[\"authority\"] == \"record_only\":\n            problems.append(\"%s: authority is record_only - it cannot stop anything\"\n                            % c[\"id\"])\n        if not c.get(\"owner\"):\n            problems.append(\"%s: no owner - nobody is paged when it misfires\" % c[\"id\"])\n    return problems\n\n\ndef evaluate(combo):\n    \"\"\"Price one placement combination against the spec card.\"\"\"\n    daily = SPEC[\"tasks_per_month\"] / 30.0\n\n    added_p95 = sum(c[\"p95_ms\"] for c in combo)\n    added_cost = sum(c[\"cost_call\"] * c[\"cost_cov\"] for c in combo)\n    cost_task = SPEC[\"base_cost_task\"] + added_cost\n\n    # --- escaped defects: only BLOCKING checks prevent an escape --------------\n    resid_prod = 1.0\n    resid_rel = 1.0\n    for c in combo:\n        if not c[\"blocks\"]:\n            continue\n        resid_prod *= (1.0 - c[\"catch\"] * c[\"cov_prod\"])\n        resid_rel *= (1.0 - c[\"catch\"] * c[\"cov_rel\"])\n    rs = SPEC[\"release_defect_share\"]\n    escaped_rate = SPEC[\"defect_rate\"] * (rs * resid_rel * resid_prod\n                                          + (1 - rs) * resid_prod)\n    escapes = escaped_rate * SPEC[\"tasks_per_month\"]\n\n    # --- false blocks: only BLOCKING checks can wrongly stop a good answer ----\n    false_blocks = sum(SPEC[\"tasks_per_month\"] * c[\"cov_prod\"] * c[\"fp\"]\n                       for c in combo if c[\"blocks\"])\n\n    # --- detection lag on a NEW regression ------------------------------------\n    # Any check that inspects production traffic is also a detector.\n    lag_days = float(\"inf\")\n    for c in combo:\n        if c[\"cov_prod\"] <= 0:\n            continue\n        per_day = daily * SPEC[\"regression_incidence\"] * c[\"cov_prod\"] * c[\"regr\"]\n        if per_day > 0:\n            lag_days = min(lag_days, SPEC[\"alarm_events\"] / per_day)\n    blind_escapes = (0.0 if lag_days == float(\"inf\")\n                     else daily * SPEC[\"regression_incidence\"] * lag_days)\n    if lag_days == float(\"inf\"):\n        blind_escapes = daily * SPEC[\"regression_incidence\"] * 90  # a quarter, unseen\n\n    monthly_compute = added_cost * SPEC[\"tasks_per_month\"]\n    monthly_fb = false_blocks * SPEC[\"cost_per_false_block\"]\n    monthly_esc = escapes * SPEC[\"cost_per_escape\"]\n    monthly_lag = (blind_escapes * SPEC[\"cost_per_escape\"]\n                   * SPEC[\"regressions_per_month\"])\n\n    fits_latency = added_p95 <= SPEC[\"p95_budget_ms\"] - SPEC[\"base_p95_ms\"]\n    fits_cost = cost_task <= SPEC[\"cost_ceiling_task\"]\n\n    return dict(\n        ids=[c[\"id\"] for c in combo],\n        added_p95=added_p95, cost_task=cost_task,\n        escaped_rate=escaped_rate, escapes=escapes,\n        false_blocks=false_blocks, lag_days=lag_days,\n        compute=monthly_compute, fb=monthly_fb, esc=monthly_esc, lagcost=monthly_lag,\n        regret=monthly_compute + monthly_fb + monthly_esc + monthly_lag,\n        fits=fits_latency and fits_cost,\n        why=(\"\" if fits_latency and fits_cost else\n             (\"p95 +%dms vs %dms headroom\" % (added_p95, SPEC[\"p95_budget_ms\"] - SPEC[\"base_p95_ms\"])\n              if not fits_latency else\n              \"$%.5f/task vs $%.4f ceiling\" % (cost_task, SPEC[\"cost_ceiling_task\"]))),\n    )\n\n\ndef fmt_lag(d):\n    return \"never\" if d == float(\"inf\") else (\"%.1fd\" % d)\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"CONTROL-POINT PLACEMENT  |  %s\" % SPEC[\"name\"])\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    head = SPEC[\"p95_budget_ms\"] - SPEC[\"base_p95_ms\"]\n    print(\"spec card: p95 %dms (base %dms -> %dms of headroom), \"\n          \"cost ceiling $%.4f/task\"\n          % (SPEC[\"p95_budget_ms\"], SPEC[\"base_p95_ms\"], head, SPEC[\"cost_ceiling_task\"]))\n    print(\"           %s tasks/month, %.2f%% raw defect rate, $%.2f per escape, \"\n          \"$%.2f per false block\"\n          % (format(SPEC[\"tasks_per_month\"], \",\"), SPEC[\"defect_rate\"] * 100,\n             SPEC[\"cost_per_escape\"], SPEC[\"cost_per_false_block\"]))\n\n    print(\"\\n--- authority audit ---\")\n    problems = audit_authorities(CHECKS)\n    if problems:\n        for p in problems:\n            print(\"  ! \" + p)\n    else:\n        print(\"  ok: every candidate check has a real authority and a named owner\")\n\n    print(\"\\n--- line items (what each control point costs to run) ---\")\n    print(\"  %-15s %-15s %7s %11s %7s %7s %8s\" %\n          (\"check\", \"authority\", \"p95ms\", \"$/task\", \"catch\", \"regr\", \"false+\"))\n    for c in CHECKS:\n        print(\"  %-15s %-15s %7d %11.6f %6.0f%% %6.0f%% %7.2f%%\" %\n              (c[\"id\"], c[\"authority\"], c[\"p95_ms\"], c[\"cost_call\"] * c[\"cost_cov\"],\n               c[\"catch\"] * 100, c[\"regr\"] * 100, c[\"fp\"] * 100))\n    print(\"  (regr 0% = structurally blind to a well-formed wrong answer)\")\n\n    results = []\n    for n in range(len(CHECKS) + 1):\n        for combo in combinations(CHECKS, n):\n            if not combo:\n                continue\n            results.append(evaluate(list(combo)))\n\n    feasible = sorted([r for r in results if r[\"fits\"]], key=lambda r: r[\"regret\"])\n    blocked = [r for r in results if not r[\"fits\"]]\n\n    print(\"\\n--- %d combinations enumerated: %d fit the envelope, %d do not ---\"\n          % (len(results), len(feasible), len(blocked)))\n\n    print(\"\\nRANKED FEASIBLE PLACEMENTS (by total monthly cost of being wrong)\")\n    print(\"  %-4s %9s %9s %8s %10s %10s %10s %10s\" %\n          (\"#\", \"+p95\", \"$/task\", \"lag\", \"escapes\", \"falseblk\", \"regret\", \"esc rate\"))\n    for i, r in enumerate(feasible[:6], 1):\n        print(\"  %-4d %8dms %9.5f %8s %10.0f %10.0f %10s %9.3f%%\" %\n              (i, r[\"added_p95\"], r[\"cost_task\"], fmt_lag(r[\"lag_days\"]),\n               r[\"escapes\"], r[\"false_blocks\"],\n               \"$\" + format(int(round(r[\"regret\"])), \",\"), r[\"escaped_rate\"] * 100))\n        print(\"       %s\" % \" + \".join(r[\"ids\"]))\n\n    best = feasible[0]\n    print(\"\\n--- what the winner spends its money on ---\")\n    print(\"  compute      $%8s/mo\" % format(int(round(best[\"compute\"])), \",\"))\n    print(\"  false blocks $%8s/mo  (%.0f good answers stopped)\"\n          % (format(int(round(best[\"fb\"])), \",\"), best[\"false_blocks\"]))\n    print(\"  escapes      $%8s/mo  (%.0f wrong answers served)\"\n          % (format(int(round(best[\"esc\"])), \",\"), best[\"escapes\"]))\n    print(\"  detection lag$%8s/mo  (%s to alarm on a new regression)\"\n          % (format(int(round(best[\"lagcost\"])), \",\"), fmt_lag(best[\"lag_days\"])))\n\n    # The placement everyone proposes first, priced honestly.\n    naive = evaluate([c for c in CHECKS if c[\"id\"] in\n                      (\"ci_eval_gate\", \"input_rail\", \"schema_rail\",\n                       \"citation_rail\", \"sync_gate\")])\n    print(\"\\n--- the placement everyone proposes first (rails + a sync gate) ---\")\n    print(\"  fits envelope : %s%s\" % (naive[\"fits\"], \"\" if naive[\"fits\"]\n                                      else \"  <-- \" + naive[\"why\"]))\n    print(\"  escape rate   : %.3f%% (vs %.3f%% for the ranked winner)\"\n          % (naive[\"escaped_rate\"] * 100, best[\"escaped_rate\"] * 100))\n    print(\"  false blocks  : %.0f/mo (vs %.0f) -- $%s/mo of self-inflicted tickets\"\n          % (naive[\"false_blocks\"], best[\"false_blocks\"],\n             format(int(round(naive[\"fb\"])), \",\")))\n    print(\"  total regret  : $%s/mo (vs $%s/mo)\"\n          % (format(int(round(naive[\"regret\"])), \",\"),\n             format(int(round(best[\"regret\"])), \",\")))\n    print(\"\\n  It buys a 4x lower escape rate and pays for it with a broken p95 and\")\n    print(\"  more support cost than the escapes it prevents. That is an arithmetic\")\n    print(\"  problem, not a philosophy problem.\")\n\n    # Detection lag is a sampling decision, so show the curve.\n    print(\"\\n--- detection lag vs sampling rate (regression touching %.1f%% of traffic) ---\"\n          % (SPEC[\"regression_incidence\"] * 100))\n    daily = SPEC[\"tasks_per_month\"] / 30.0\n    for rate in (0.001, 0.005, 0.01, 0.03, 0.10, 1.00):\n        per_day = daily * SPEC[\"regression_incidence\"] * rate * 0.74\n        days = SPEC[\"alarm_events\"] / per_day if per_day else float(\"inf\")\n        verdict = \"ok\" if days <= 7 else (\"slow\" if days <= 30 else \"not a plan\")\n        print(\"  %5.1f%% sampling -> %6.1f events/day -> %7.1f days to alarm   [%s]\"\n              % (rate * 100, per_day, days, verdict))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","notes":["The ''regr'' column is the important one, and it''s deliberately zero for the deterministic rails: a well-formed, correctly-cited, confidently wrong answer is invisible to a schema check by construction, which is why no wall of cheap rails detects a quality regression.","Blocking checks compose multiplicatively on the residual defect stream, not additively on the catch rate. Three checks at 22%, 18% and 10% leave 57.6% of defects, and the script does that multiplication so nobody has to do it in their head during a review.","Watch what the ranking does with the input rail: it catches 10% of defects and false-blocks 0.6% of traffic, and at this blast radius the false-block bill exceeds the damage prevented, so it drops out of the top combinations. That flips the moment cost_per_escape rises. Change one input, rerun, watch the answer move.","Every price, latency and rate is an input, and no provider price is hardcoded anywhere - the only reason this file will still be correct next year. Put your own measurements in and the conclusions are yours.","The ''placement everyone proposes first'' block exists to be pasted into a review. Saying ''that design costs $10,580 a month and misses p95 by 744ms, here''s the alternative at $8,289'' beats any argument about eval philosophy."],"debug":[["Every combination comes back infeasible","Base p95 already consumes the whole spec-card budget before any check","The problem isn''t the checks, it''s the pipeline - fix the base p95 or renegotiate the spec card, then rerun"],["The ranking always picks the empty-ish combination","cost_per_escape is set too low relative to cost_per_false_block","Re-derive cost_per_escape from the blast-radius line on your spec card; guessing it low is how systems end up with no controls at all"],["Detection lag shows ''never'' for a combination you liked","No selected check has a non-zero regr against production traffic","Add a sampling check or accept the printed 90-day blind window explicitly in the doc - the point is that it becomes a decision instead of an oversight"],["Two combinations rank within a few dollars of each other","The model is at the limit of its input precision, which is honest","Stop optimising and pick on a second axis: the one with the lower detection lag, or the one with fewer moving parts to own"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:quality', 'paid', '[{"q":"Your p95 budget leaves 600ms of headroom. The proposed grounding check measures 700ms at p95. What is the correct move?","o":["Ship it now and renegotiate the p95 target with product afterwards","Keep it inline but run it on the slowest five percent of requests","Move it off the request path, or swap it for a cheap inline rail","Keep it inline and buy the time back by trimming the retrieval stage"],"a":2,"exp":"700ms against 600ms of headroom is an arithmetic failure, not a philosophical one. Running it on the slowest requests inverts the logic - those are the ones with the least room. Trimming retrieval to fund a check trades a known quality contribution for an unknown one, and you''d have to prove that trade with numbers you don''t have yet."},{"q":"13,000 requests a day, a regression touching 0.5% of traffic, 1% async sampling, and you need 8 flagged samples before anyone is paged. When do you find out?","o":["Within a day, since one percent of thirteen thousand is ample signal","In about twelve days, most of an incident''s useful lifetime","Within an hour, because the sampler runs continuously on traffic","In about three days, once the daily rollup crosses the alert line"],"a":1,"exp":"13,000 x 0.005 x 0.01 = 0.65 flagged samples per day, and 8 / 0.65 is about 12 days. The sampler does run continuously, which is exactly why people assume it''s fast - continuity isn''t the same as volume. Do this division before choosing a sampling rate, not after the incident review."},{"q":"A rail wrongly blocks 2% of good answers on a flow doing 400,000 tasks a month. Which number belongs in the review?","o":["The two percent figure, which sits inside the normal band for rails","The rail''s added compute per task, the marginal spend it introduces","The share of blocked users who retried and succeeded on their own","Eight thousand blocked good answers a month, priced in support cost"],"a":3,"exp":"Ratios hide magnitude, counts don''t. 2% of 400,000 is 8,000 refusals a month, and at a modest expected support cost per refusal that bill routinely exceeds the rail''s entire compute cost. Retry-success data is genuinely useful, but it adjusts the price of a false block rather than replacing the count."},{"q":"Your eval suite runs on every release, posts a score to the pull request, and has never failed a build. What authority does it have?","o":["Record only, which means it has stopped precisely nothing all year","Block release, because a poor score is visible to every reviewer","Open ticket, since reviewers file follow-ups whenever scores drop","Block response, as the score governs what the release may do live"],"a":0,"exp":"Visibility is not authority. If no configuration in the system causes the check''s verdict to stop anything, its authority is record-only, whatever the team believes. That''s a legitimate choice when made deliberately - and a very expensive accident when it isn''t."},{"q":"One flow can move money irreversibly and carries 0.4% of your volume. Where does human review belong?","o":["On a random three percent sample across all flows, for coverage","On the flow with the most traffic, where reviewers see most cases","On every request that already tripped a rail, since those look bad","On that flow alone, because review buys fidelity and not coverage"],"a":3,"exp":"Human review is the highest-fidelity control point you have and it does not scale, so its placement rule is maximum blast radius, minimum volume. Sampling for coverage is what async scoring is for. Reviewing only rail-tripped requests reviews the cases a machine already caught."},{"q":"Why can an offline CI gate stay green all quarter while production quality falls?","o":["Because CI runs a cheaper model than production for cost reasons","Because thresholds in CI are usually set below production targets","Because it scores a fixed set that no longer matches live traffic","Because the gate runs after the deploy, so its verdict lands late"],"a":2,"exp":"A CI gate is structurally blind to production distribution: it scores what you froze, and traffic keeps moving. That''s not a flaw to fix, it''s a property to plan around - which is precisely why online sampling exists alongside it rather than instead of it."},{"q":"You add async sampling at 5%. Which number on the spec card does that change?","o":["The escaped-defect rate, since sampled failures are never served","None of them; it shortens how long a bad rate lasts, not the rate","The p95 latency, because scoring runs alongside each live response","The quality bar, which the new coverage lets you tighten right away"],"a":1,"exp":"Only blocking control points change the escape rate. A sampler observes what already went out, so it buys a shorter blind window, not fewer wrong answers. Conflating detection with prevention is the single most common modelling error in these designs."},{"q":"Your provider silently updated the judge model and scores shifted by four points. What was missing from the architecture?","o":["A contract clause barring vendor version changes without any notice","A larger golden set, so the shift would have surfaced in the scores","A second judge from a different family, averaged to damp variance","A control point on the judge itself, with an owner and a page target"],"a":3,"exp":"The judge is a component, and components drift. A periodic agreement check against fresh human labels, with a threshold and a rotation attached, is the control point that catches this. Bigger sets and ensembles change the variance, not your ability to notice a shift."},{"q":"Where does a new output rail''s cost belong in the design document?","o":["In the latency and cost tables, as a line item against the headroom","In the eval plan, beside the other quality work planned this quarter","In the risk register, since rails exist to reduce residual risk","In the platform backlog, because rails are shared infrastructure"],"a":0,"exp":"A rail taxes every response''s latency and every task''s cost, so it''s a budget line item first and everything else second. Putting it in a plan or a register is how checks get approved that the budget was never going to accommodate."},{"q":"Two designs: a synchronous gate on everything, or a cheap rail plus 3% sampling. What decides between them?","o":["Team maturity, since sampling demands more operational discipline","Your provider, because gate cost varies sharply between vendors","The spec card: blast radius, latency headroom, and the quality bar","The volume, because sampling only pays off beyond a certain scale"],"a":2,"exp":"The same two checks flip which one wins the moment the blast radius becomes irreversible or the quality bar tightens by 10x. Volume and vendor move the numbers, but the selection is made by the requirement, which is why the spec card sits at the front of the document."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:orgarch', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"orgarch","block":"b4","title":"Conway''s Law for AI Platforms","tag":"You will ship the boundaries your org already has, so draw them on purpose","why":"You can design a beautiful set of seams and watch the org dissolve every one of them inside two quarters, because interfaces become handoffs between people and handoffs are where latency, drift and unowned artifacts live. The AI version is sharper than usual: the artifacts that decide how the system behaves - prompts, tool definitions, golden sets, judges, rails - are small, editable by anyone, and almost never appear in an ownership document. This module produces the two artifacts that fix that: a matrix naming who owns and who is paged for each, and a queue calculation giving you the quarter your central team stops being leverage and becomes the roadmap.","mental":"<b>The seam is a team.</b> Every interface you draw in the architecture becomes a handoff between people, so when you draw the system you have also drawn the org chart it will grow into, or the one that will quietly deform it back. The corollary is the useful part: if you don''t like the org you''d get, redraw the seam, because the seam is the thing you actually control.","concepts":[["Drawing the seam on purpose","Conway''s law is usually quoted as a warning. Treat it as a design input instead: pick the seams you want people organised around, then staff to them. The failure isn''t that architecture mirrors the org, it''s letting that happen by default and calling the result a strategy."],["The platform/product split","Platform owns the choke points where consistency is worth a bottleneck: the model traffic choke point, key custody, the trace schema, eval infrastructure, safety rails. Product owns whatever must move at feature speed: prompts, tool definitions, retrieval tuning, golden sets, UX. Invert this and you get shadow spend on one side or a ticket queue on the other."],["The ownership matrix","Ten artifacts against three columns: owner, approver, who gets paged. It fits on one page and every empty or doubled cell has a matching incident signature you can go and find in last quarter''s incidents. It''s the cheapest architecture document you will ever write."],["Choke point","An artifact where consistency across teams is worth the cost of a bottleneck, because divergence is expensive or unrecoverable. Choke points take exactly one owner. Everything else can take several. Getting that rule backwards is the single most common org-design error in AI platforms."],["Interface contract","What the platform guarantees, stated as an engineering artifact, and what a product team must supply to get it: required trace fields, a declared budget, an eval coverage threshold. Not a policy document, a schema plus a set of thresholds that a CI job can check."],["Paved road","The version of the platform where the safe path is also the easy path, so nobody has to be persuaded. You measure a paved road by adoption, not compliance. 30% adoption after two quarters means the road is slower than the ditch, and that''s a road problem."],["The on-call split","A quality regression and an availability regression are different pages, need different skills, and usually belong to different rotations. Which rotation currently receives \"the answers got worse\" tells you what your ownership matrix really says, regardless of what''s written in it."]],"mistakes":["Putting the choke points in product teams (six sets of provider keys) and the fast-moving artifacts in the platform team (a prompt-change ticket queue), which is the split inverted on both axes","Writing an ownership matrix with an owner column and no paged column, so ownership means credit rather than a phone ringing","Starting a platform migration with the approval gate instead of the trace schema, which makes the platform a tax before it''s ever a service","Measuring paved-road success as compliance (percentage of teams mandated) rather than adoption (percentage that would choose it)","Sending quality regressions to the availability rotation, where they arrive as a page nobody has a runbook for and everyone treats as noise"],"flags":["\"Everyone owns quality\" - a sentence that has never once resulted in someone being paged","\"We''ll standardise later, teams need to move fast right now\" - later means after six sets of keys, eleven prompt copies and no shared trace fields","\"The platform team reviews every prompt change\" - ask for reviews per quarter and reviewer capacity, then compute the utilisation before agreeing","\"Adoption is low because teams don''t understand the benefits\" - adoption is low because the paved road is slower than the ditch, which is a measurable claim","\"That artifact is shared\" - shared means two owners, and two owners on a choke point is the shadow-spend signature"],"ask":["For each of these ten artifacts: who owns it, who approves a change, and who gets paged when it breaks?","Which of our artifacts are choke points, and does each of those have exactly one owner?","How many reviews per quarter does the platform team absorb, against how much reviewer capacity? What utilisation is that?","When the answers get worse rather than slower, whose phone rings, and do they have a runbook?","What does a product engineer get for free by coming through the platform, stated in things they''d otherwise have to build?"],"estMin":40},"deep":{"worked":"Six product teams, 34 engineers, all shipping AI features. Each team has its own provider account, prompts live inline in application code, and there is no shared trace schema. Monthly spend is $18,400 across six invoices, so nobody can attribute cost to a feature. The same support-summarisation prompt exists in 11 copies across 3 repositories. An incident in March that spanned two teams took <b>9 days to attribute</b>, because neither team''s logs contained a field the other could join on.<br><br><b>The redesign, by the choke-point rule.</b> Platform takes what''s expensive to have diverge: the model traffic choke point (one set of keys, spend attributed by team and feature tag, rate limits enforced in one place), the trace schema (seven required fields, enforced at the gateway), eval infrastructure (the runner and the storage, deliberately <b>not</b> the golden sets), and the rails service. Product keeps what must move at feature speed: prompts, tool definitions, retrieval tuning, their own golden sets, and UX. Note where golden sets landed. They''re the thing that most encodes domain judgement, so putting them in platform is how you get an eval suite that nobody in the product team believes.<br><br><b>The migration order, which is the part people get wrong.</b> Step 1, trace schema first, four weeks, additive and read-only: teams add seven fields, nothing is blocked, nothing needs approval. You cannot route or attribute what you cannot see, and this is the only step that makes every subsequent step measurable. Step 2, the traffic choke point in shadow for six weeks: teams keep their keys and mirror a copy of each call through it, so the platform proves its latency and reliability before it can hurt anyone. Step 3, key cutover team by team, highest spend first, one to two days each. Step 4, rails last, opt-in, default-on for new features only. The order that fails is the reverse: leading with \"all model changes now require platform approval\" makes the platform a tax before it has ever been a service, and the teams route around it inside a month.<br><br><b>The review-queue arithmetic, because this is where good intentions turn into a bottleneck.</b> Suppose the platform gates every prompt change. Six teams x 9 prompt changes/month x 3 months = <b>162 reviews per quarter</b>. At 45 minutes each that''s 7,290 minutes of demand. Capacity: 3 platform engineers, each able to give 270 minutes a week to review before their own work suffers, over a 13-week quarter = 3 x 270 x 13 = <b>10,530 minutes</b>. Utilisation today is 7,290 / 10,530 = <b>0.69</b>, which feels completely fine, and that feeling is the trap. Queue wait is non-linear: at a one-day service time, the M/M/1 approximation gives 0.69 / 0.31 = <b>2.2 days</b> of waiting at six teams, <b>4.2 days</b> at seven, <b>12 days</b> at eight, and unbounded at nine. The org adds roughly a team a quarter. So the platform team becomes the roadmap in <b>three quarters</b>, and product engineers start complaining in <b>one</b>.<br><br><b>The fix is not a fourth reviewer.</b> A fourth reviewer moves break-even from 8.7 teams to 11.6, buying about three quarters. Converting 70% of those reviews into an interface contract a CI job can check moves break-even to 28.9 teams. Reviewers buy linear headroom; contracts change the slope. That single comparison is the entire argument for the paved road, and it''s worth more in a staffing conversation than any diagram.","good":["A one-page ownership matrix, ten artifacts by owner / approver / paged, reviewed whenever a team is added or split","Exactly one owner on every choke point, and the choke points explicitly listed rather than inferred","Owner and pager are the same team for every artifact, so mean time to repair never includes a handoff","The interface contract is a schema plus thresholds that CI checks, not a policy page in a wiki, so conformance is a build status rather than a conversation","Paved-road adoption tracked as a percentage of new features choosing the platform voluntarily, reviewed monthly against the reasons the rest didn''t"],"build":"<b>Part one, the matrix.</b> Ten artifacts down the side: prompts, model choice, tool definitions, golden sets, judges, budgets, safety rails, traces, retrieval indexes, runbooks. Three columns across: owner, approver, who gets paged. Fill in all thirty cells with team names, never with \"shared\" and never with a person''s name (people leave, rotations don''t). Then mark which artifacts are choke points. Now audit: every empty cell, every cell containing two teams on a choke point, and every row where owner and pager differ. For each finding, go and look for its incident signature in your last two quarters of incidents. You will find most of them, and that discovery is what makes the matrix credible to the people who have to sign it.<br><br><b>Part two, the paved-road pitch.</b> Write one paragraph, addressed to a product engineer, that says what they get <b>for free</b> by coming through the platform - stated as things they would otherwise have to build themselves. Not \"governance\" and not \"consistency\"; those are your benefits, not theirs. Something closer to: spend attribution per feature without instrumenting anything, a trace you can replay, rate limits and failover already handled, and an eval runner you don''t have to host. Then read it back and ask the honest question: if this were optional, would they choose it? If the answer is no, you don''t have a paved road, you have a checkpoint, and adoption will only ever be as high as your ability to enforce it. Fix the paragraph by fixing the road.","res":[["Melvin Conway, Conway''s Law","https://www.melconway.com/Home/Conways_Law.html","Use when someone treats the law as a folk saying - the original framing is sharper and it''s a two-minute read before a reorg conversation."],["Team Topologies - key concepts","https://teamtopologies.com/key-concepts","Use when you''re naming your topology out loud: platform, stream-aligned, enabling, complicated-subsystem. Shared vocabulary makes the split argument much shorter."],["Google SRE Book - Being On-Call","https://sre.google/sre-book/being-on-call/","Use right now if you''re proposing a separate quality rotation - this is the load, escalation and handoff arithmetic you''ll be asked about."],["M/M/1 queue (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M/M/1_queue","Use when you need to show that review wait time explodes non-linearly near capacity, rather than asserting it and hoping."]]},"depth":{"mech":"<b>The split, as a rule you can apply rather than a philosophy.</b> An artifact belongs to platform when divergence across teams is expensive or unrecoverable, and to product when speed of change is worth more than consistency. That one test resolves nearly every case. Model traffic: divergence means six sets of keys, no aggregate rate limiting, and unattributable spend, so platform. Key custody: divergence means a credential in a repository somewhere, so platform. Trace schema: divergence means you cannot join two teams'' logs during an incident, so platform - and it is the one genuinely irreversible artifact, because every trace already written stays in its old shape. Eval runner and storage: commodity, so platform. Golden sets and judges encode domain judgement, and a suite the product team doesn''t believe is one they route around, so product. Prompts, tool definitions, retrieval tuning and UX are product, always.<br><br><b>The matrix is three columns because ownership without paging is a fiction.</b> Owner means the team whose backlog the change lands in. Approver means the team that must agree before an irreversible change happens. Paged means whose phone rings at 2am. Each degenerate cell has a signature you can go and find. No owner: the artifact decays silently, you hear about it from a user, and the fix lands in whichever team happened to notice, which is how golden sets rot. Two owners on a choke point: divergent configuration, shadow spend, and an incident nobody can roll back in one place. Owner and pager differ: mean time to repair permanently includes a handoff, because the paged team can only mitigate and the owning team has to fix it the next morning. Approver equals owner on an irreversible artifact: the change that cannot be undone receives the least scrutiny in the whole system.<br><br><b>Four topologies, each with a team count where it stops working.</b> One central team owning everything is right at one to three product teams and becomes a queue at roughly five. Fully embedded engineers with no platform work up to four or five teams, then produce duplicate infrastructure faster than anyone consolidates it, and you end up paying for three vector stores and two gateways. Platform plus product is stable from about four teams to about twenty, which is where most organisations should land. A guild - a cross-team forum with no dedicated staff - works below three teams and fails the moment anything needs a single owner, because a guild is structurally incapable of owning a choke point.<br><br><b>The interface contract is what stops the platform becoming a review queue.</b> State what the platform guarantees numerically: a p99 gateway overhead ceiling, an availability target, a spend report by feature tag, failover behaviour. Then state what a product team must supply to get it: the required trace fields, a declared latency and cost budget per route, and an eval coverage threshold before a route may take production traffic. All three are machine-checkable, which is the entire point - a contract a CI job enforces scales with team count, and a contract a human enforces scales with reviewer count. That difference is the slope change in the bottleneck estimator, and it''s why the paved road is an engineering artifact rather than an advocacy campaign.<br><br><b>Finally, the rotation split, which is the highest-signal question in this module.</b> \"The API is slow\" and \"the answers got worse\" are different pages: different symptoms, different diagnostics, different fixes, and usually different people. Availability pages go to the rotation that owns the infrastructure. Quality pages should go to a rotation that owns the model behaviour and has runbooks for it - roll back the prompt version, check judge agreement, compare canary quality scores against control. Send quality pages to the availability rotation and they arrive as an alert nobody can act on, get acknowledged and ignored twice, and then get tuned into silence. Ask an organisation which rotation receives \"the answers got worse\" and you learn what their ownership matrix actually says, whatever the document claims.","trade":[["Choke point in platform vs in each product team","Consistency and attribution vs feature velocity","A ticket queue in front of every prompt change, or six sets of keys and unattributable spend"],["Golden sets owned by platform vs by product","Standardisation vs domain credibility","A standardised eval suite the product team doesn''t believe, and quietly routes around"],["Human review gate vs machine-checkable contract","Time to set up vs how it scales with team count","Linear headroom bought with reviewers, against a bottleneck that arrives on a fixed date"],["Separate quality rotation vs one combined rotation","Right skills on the page vs on-call load per person","Quality alerts tuned into silence because they page people with no runbook for them"],["Mandating the platform vs making it the easy path","Adoption speed vs durability of adoption","High compliance, low adoption, and a shadow stack that appears the quarter after the mandate lapses"]],"scale":["The bottleneck quarter is computable: reviews per team per quarter times team count against reviewer capacity, with the queue biting at about 80% utilisation rather than at 100%","Team growth is the input people forget - run the estimator forward four to eight quarters at your real hiring rate, because the answer arrives sooner than anyone''s intuition","Platform headcount scales sub-linearly with team count only if the contract is machine-checked; if humans check it, platform headcount scales linearly and you have built a services org","Past roughly twenty product teams the platform itself needs an internal split - traffic, data, quality - and the same choke-point rule applies one level down"],"sec":["Key custody is the clearest choke point in the whole matrix: one owner, one rotation, one revocation path, and no product team holding a raw provider credential","An unowned artifact is an unpatched artifact - the row with an empty owner cell is where a disclosed vulnerability sits for a quarter because nobody''s backlog it lands in","Approver equal to owner on an irreversible artifact defeats separation of duties, which is both a security control and the reason your auditor asks for this matrix","Paved-road adoption is a security metric, not just an engineering one: every team outside the road is a set of credentials, rails and logs outside your controls"],"interview":"The way I think about it is that the seam is a team: every interface I draw becomes a handoff between people, so when I draw the system I''ve drawn the org chart it''s going to grow into, and I''d rather do that on purpose. The rule I use for splitting is that platform owns the places where divergence is expensive or unrecoverable - model traffic, key custody, the trace schema, eval infrastructure, the rails - and product owns whatever has to move at feature speed, which is prompts, tools, retrieval tuning and the golden sets, because a suite the product team doesn''t believe is a suite they route around. Then I write a one-page matrix, ten artifacts against owner, approver and who gets paged, and I audit it: unowned cells, two owners on a choke point, and any row where the owner isn''t the one being paged. Each of those has an incident signature I can usually find in the last two quarters. And I always compute the bottleneck quarter, because reviews per team times team count against reviewer capacity tells you the date the platform stops being leverage, and hiring another reviewer just moves that date, while turning the review into a contract CI can check changes the slope."},"patterns":{"intro":"Two calculations that turn org design into something you can put a number on. Part one audits an ownership matrix and prints, for each finding, the incident signature it produces, so you can go and check it against your own incident history. Part two answers the only question that really matters about a central platform team: which quarter does it become the constraint, and does hiring fix it.","code":"\"\"\"Ownership-matrix checker + central-team bottleneck estimator.\n\nPart one reads an artifact-ownership matrix and flags the three cells that\nreliably become incidents: artifacts nobody owns, artifacts owned by a team\nthat is not paged for them, and choke points with more than one owner. Each\nfinding prints the incident signature it produces, so you can check the finding\nagainst your own last two quarters of incidents.\n\nPart two answers the only question that matters about a central platform team:\nwhich quarter does it become the constraint? Reviews per quarter against review\ncapacity, with an M/M/1 queue approximation for the wait a product engineer\nactually experiences.\n\nStdlib only, deterministic, no network. Everything is an input.\n\n    python ownership.py\n\"\"\"\n\n# --- the matrix: 10 artifacts x owner / approver / paged ----------------------\n# owners        : list, because dual ownership is a finding, not a typo\n# choke         : consistency here is worth a bottleneck (one owner, always)\n# irreversible  : changing it is expensive or impossible to undo\nMATRIX = [\n    dict(artifact=\"prompts\",          owners=[\"product-billing\", \"product-claims\"],\n         approver=\"product-billing\", paged=\"product-billing\",\n         choke=False, irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"model choice\",     owners=[\"platform\"],\n         approver=\"platform\",        paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=True,  irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"tool definitions\", owners=[\"product-claims\"],\n         approver=\"product-claims\",  paged=\"product-claims\",\n         choke=False, irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"golden sets\",      owners=[],\n         approver=\"\",                paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=False, irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"judges\",           owners=[\"platform\"],\n         approver=\"platform\",        paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=False, irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"budgets\",          owners=[\"platform\", \"finance\"],\n         approver=\"finance\",         paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=True,  irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"safety rails\",     owners=[\"platform\"],\n         approver=\"platform\",        paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=True,  irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"trace schema\",     owners=[\"platform\"],\n         approver=\"platform\",        paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=True,  irreversible=True),\n    dict(artifact=\"retrieval indexes\", owners=[\"product-claims\"],\n         approver=\"product-claims\",  paged=\"platform\",\n         choke=False, irreversible=False),\n    dict(artifact=\"runbooks\",         owners=[\"ops\"],\n         approver=\"ops\",             paged=\"ops\",\n         choke=False, irreversible=False),\n]\n\n# --- the bottleneck inputs ----------------------------------------------------\nORG = {\n    \"teams_now\":              6,\n    \"team_growth_per_quarter\": 1.0,\n    \"reviews_per_team_month\": 9,      # changes that today require platform sign-off\n    \"review_minutes\":         45,\n    \"reviewers\":              3,\n    \"review_minutes_per_reviewer_week\": 270,   # 4.5h/week before platform work suffers\n    \"weeks_per_quarter\":      13,\n    \"service_days\":           1.0,    # queue-to-done for one review when the queue is empty\n    \"quarters\":               8,\n}\n\nSIGNATURES = {\n    \"unowned\": \"no owner -> it decays silently; you find out from a user, \"\n               \"and the fix lands in whichever team noticed\",\n    \"dual_choke\": \"two owners on a choke point -> divergent configs, shadow spend, \"\n                  \"and an incident nobody can roll back in one place\",\n    \"owner_not_paged\": \"owner is not paged -> MTTR includes a handoff; the paged team \"\n                       \"mitigates, the owning team fixes, a day later\",\n    \"self_approve\": \"approver is the owner on an irreversible artifact -> the change \"\n                    \"that cannot be undone gets the least scrutiny\",\n    \"no_approver\": \"no approver -> the artifact changes at whatever speed the loudest \"\n                   \"deadline demands\",\n}\n\n\ndef check_matrix(matrix):\n    findings = []\n    for row in matrix:\n        a, owners = row[\"artifact\"], row[\"owners\"]\n        if not owners:\n            findings.append((\"FAIL\", a, \"unowned\", \"owner cell is empty\"))\n        if row[\"choke\"] and len(owners) > 1:\n            findings.append((\"FAIL\", a, \"dual_choke\",\n                             \"choke point owned by %s\" % \" + \".join(owners)))\n        if owners and row[\"paged\"] and row[\"paged\"] not in owners:\n            findings.append((\"FAIL\", a, \"owner_not_paged\",\n                             \"owned by %s, paged to %s\"\n                             % (\"/\".join(owners), row[\"paged\"])))\n        if row[\"irreversible\"] and row[\"approver\"] in owners:\n            findings.append((\"WARN\", a, \"self_approve\",\n                             \"%s approves its own irreversible change\" % row[\"approver\"]))\n        if not row[\"approver\"]:\n            findings.append((\"WARN\", a, \"no_approver\", \"approver cell is empty\"))\n    return findings\n\n\ndef capacity_minutes(org):\n    return (org[\"reviewers\"] * org[\"review_minutes_per_reviewer_week\"]\n            * org[\"weeks_per_quarter\"])\n\n\ndef demand_minutes(org, teams):\n    return teams * org[\"reviews_per_team_month\"] * 3 * org[\"review_minutes\"]\n\n\ndef queue_wait_days(rho, service_days):\n    \"\"\"M/M/1 waiting time. Non-linear on purpose: the cliff is the lesson.\"\"\"\n    if rho >= 1.0:\n        return float(\"inf\")\n    return rho / (1.0 - rho) * service_days\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"=\" * 76)\n    print(\"PART 1 - OWNERSHIP MATRIX CHECK  (%d artifacts)\" % len(MATRIX))\n    print(\"=\" * 76)\n    print(\"  %-19s %-32s %-16s %s\"\n          % (\"artifact\", \"owner(s)\", \"approver\", \"paged\"))\n    for r in MATRIX:\n        print(\"  %-19s %-32s %-16s %s\"\n              % (r[\"artifact\"] + (\" *\" if r[\"choke\"] else \"\"),\n                 \" + \".join(r[\"owners\"]) or \"-- NOBODY --\",\n                 r[\"approver\"] or \"--\", r[\"paged\"] or \"--\"))\n    print(\"  (* = choke point: consistency here is worth a bottleneck)\")\n\n    findings = check_matrix(MATRIX)\n    fails = [f for f in findings if f[0] == \"FAIL\"]\n    print(\"\\n  %d finding(s): %d FAIL, %d WARN\\n\"\n          % (len(findings), len(fails), len(findings) - len(fails)))\n    for level, artifact, kind, detail in findings:\n        print(\"  [%s] %-18s %s\" % (level, artifact, detail))\n        print(\"         signature: %s\" % SIGNATURES[kind])\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 76)\n    print(\"PART 2 - WHEN DOES THE PLATFORM TEAM BECOME THE CONSTRAINT?\")\n    print(\"=\" * 76)\n    cap = capacity_minutes(ORG)\n    per_team = ORG[\"reviews_per_team_month\"] * 3 * ORG[\"review_minutes\"]\n    print(\"  capacity : %d reviewers x %d min/week x %d weeks = %s min/quarter\"\n          % (ORG[\"reviewers\"], ORG[\"review_minutes_per_reviewer_week\"],\n             ORG[\"weeks_per_quarter\"], format(cap, \",\")))\n    print(\"  demand   : %d reviews/team/month x 3 months x %d min = %s min/team/quarter\"\n          % (ORG[\"reviews_per_team_month\"], ORG[\"review_minutes\"], format(per_team, \",\")))\n    print(\"  break-even at %.1f teams; the queue starts biting well before that.\\n\"\n          % (cap / float(per_team)))\n\n    print(\"  %-8s %7s %9s %7s %11s  %s\"\n          % (\"quarter\", \"teams\", \"reviews/q\", \"util\", \"mean wait\", \"state\"))\n    first_pain = first_break = None\n    teams = float(ORG[\"teams_now\"])\n    for q in range(ORG[\"quarters\"]):\n        dem = demand_minutes(ORG, teams)\n        rho = dem / cap\n        wait = queue_wait_days(rho, ORG[\"service_days\"])\n        reviews = teams * ORG[\"reviews_per_team_month\"] * 3\n        if rho >= 0.8 and first_pain is None:\n            first_pain = q\n        if rho >= 1.0 and first_break is None:\n            first_break = q\n        state = (\"healthy\" if rho < 0.8 else\n                 \"QUEUE BITING\" if rho < 1.0 else \"UNBOUNDED - it is a ticket queue now\")\n        print(\"  Q+%-6d %7.1f %9.0f %6.0f%% %10s  %s\"\n              % (q, teams, reviews, rho * 100,\n                 \"unbounded\" if wait == float(\"inf\") else \"%.1f days\" % wait, state))\n        teams += ORG[\"team_growth_per_quarter\"]\n\n    print()\n    if first_pain is not None:\n        print(\"  Q+%d is when a product engineer starts noticing. The wait roughly\"\n              % first_pain)\n        print(\"  quadruples between 65% and 85% utilisation - a cliff, not a slope.\")\n    if first_break is not None:\n        print(\"  Q+%d is when the central team IS the roadmap. No amount of\"\n              % first_break)\n        print(\"  prioritising fixes it; only removing reviews from the human path does.\")\n\n    # The fix is not more reviewers. Show why.\n    print(\"\\n  --- two ways out, priced ---\")\n    for label, delta in ((\"hire a 4th reviewer\", dict(reviewers=4)),\n                         (\"automate 70% of reviews into a CI contract\",\n                          dict(reviews_per_team_month=2.7))):\n        org2 = dict(ORG)\n        org2.update(delta)\n        cap2 = capacity_minutes(org2)\n        break_even = cap2 / float(org2[\"reviews_per_team_month\"] * 3\n                                  * org2[\"review_minutes\"])\n        print(\"  %-42s break-even at %5.1f teams (%+.1f)\"\n              % (label, break_even, break_even - cap / float(per_team)))\n    print(\"  Reviewers buy you linear headroom. Contracts change the slope.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","notes":["The owners field is a list on purpose. Dual ownership is a finding, not a data-entry mistake, and modelling it as a list is what lets the checker distinguish ''two teams collaborate here'' from ''this choke point has no single owner''.","Every finding prints its incident signature. Read it aloud in the room and ask whether anyone recognises it from the last two quarters - matrices get signed much faster when three findings are incidents people remember.","The M/M/1 approximation is deliberately crude and deliberately non-linear. The exact wait doesn''t matter; the shape does, and the shape is that going from 69% to 92% utilisation takes the wait from about two days to about twelve while headcount looks unchanged.","The estimator''s most valuable output is the last block: a fourth reviewer moves break-even by about three teams, automating 70% of reviews moves it by twenty. That comparison is the paved-road business case, ready to paste.","Nothing here is AI-specific except the artifact list, which is the point: prompts, judges, golden sets and rails are small, editable by anyone, and almost never appear in an ownership document."],"debug":[["The matrix passes cleanly on the first run","Cells were filled in with the team that should own it rather than the team that does","Re-fill the paged column from the actual on-call schedule, not from intent, and rerun - that column is the honest one"],["Utilisation looks fine but engineers complain constantly","You measured capacity as total headcount hours rather than hours actually available for review","Use hours per reviewer per week that survive their own roadmap commitments; 4 to 6 is realistic, 20 is fiction"],["Break-even is far away but the queue is already painful","Service time, not capacity, is what people experience - a review that waits three days for a context switch has a long service time even at low utilisation","Measure service_days from PR-open to merge on real reviews and re-run; batching policy is often the actual fix"],["Every artifact shows owner and pager mismatched","One rotation is paged for everything, which means paging is centralised while ownership is not","That is the finding. Either distribute the rotation or accept that MTTR includes a handoff on every row, and write which one you chose"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:orgarch', 'paid', '[{"q":"Six product teams each hold their own provider keys, and the platform team reviews every prompt change. What is wrong with this split?","o":["Nothing structural; it needs clearer documentation and better tooling","It is inverted on both axes: choke point devolved, fast artifact gated","The platform team is understaffed for the review volume it has taken on","Prompt review should be automated before any key consolidation happens"],"a":1,"exp":"Keys are a choke point, where divergence costs you attribution, rate limiting and revocation, so they belong to platform. Prompts must move at feature speed, so they belong to product. This org gated the fast thing and devolved the expensive-to-diverge thing, which is both errors at once."},{"q":"Your ownership matrix lists an owner for every artifact but has no ''who gets paged'' column. What does that cost you?","o":["Very little, since ownership already implies operational responsibility","Slower onboarding, because new engineers can''t tell who to ask first","Ownership becomes credit rather than a phone ringing at three in the morning","Audit friction, because separation of duties can''t be demonstrated cleanly"],"a":2,"exp":"Owner and pager are different claims. Without the paging column, ownership degrades into attribution, and mean time to repair quietly includes a handoff on every row where the two differ. The column is one word wide and it''s the honest one."},{"q":"6 teams x 9 reviews/month, 45 minutes each, against 3 reviewers giving 270 minutes a week over 13 weeks. What is utilisation?","o":["About 0.69, which is comfortable and needs no action this quarter","About 0.69, and the queue is one added team away from getting painful","About 0.42, since reviewers also absorb work outside the review queue","About 1.15, which is why the current backlog keeps growing every week"],"a":1,"exp":"7,290 minutes of demand against 10,530 of capacity is 0.69. The trap is that 0.69 feels fine: wait time is non-linear, so one more team pushes it to 0.81 and roughly doubles the wait, and the team after that takes it to 0.92 and roughly triples it again."},{"q":"Adoption of your paved road is 30% after two quarters. What does that number mean?","o":["Teams don''t yet understand the benefits, so the rollout needs advocacy","The mandate isn''t being enforced, so compliance reporting needs teeth","The road is slower or harder than the ditch, which is a road problem","The rollout is on track, since platform adoption is slow by its nature"],"a":2,"exp":"Adoption is the measurement precisely because it''s a judgement made by people with alternatives. Low adoption is feedback about the road: onboarding friction, latency overhead, or missing capability. Reaching for enforcement converts an engineering problem into a political one and buries the signal."},{"q":"You''re migrating six teams onto a shared gateway. What ships first?","o":["An approval gate on model changes, to stop divergence getting worse","The trace schema, additive and read-only, blocking nothing for anyone","Key cutover for the highest-spend team, to prove the value fastest","The rails service, since safety is the strongest justification you have"],"a":1,"exp":"You can''t route, attribute or measure what you can''t see, and the trace schema is the only step that blocks nobody while making every later step measurable. Leading with an approval gate makes the platform a tax before it''s ever been a service, and teams route around it inside a month."},{"q":"Who should own the golden sets in a platform/product split?","o":["Platform, so that scoring methodology stays consistent across teams","A central quality guild, so no single team can lower its own bar","Whichever team has the most eval experience, regardless of the surface","Product, because a suite the product team doesn''t believe gets routed around"],"a":3,"exp":"Platform owns the runner and the storage, which are commodity. The set encodes domain judgement, and judgement doesn''t survive being centralised: a standardised suite the feature team doesn''t trust becomes a formality they satisfy rather than a control they use."},{"q":"Your platform team is at 92% review utilisation. What actually changes the trajectory?","o":["Hiring a fourth reviewer, which raises capacity by a third immediately","Prioritising the queue better so the important reviews clear faster","Turning most reviews into a contract that a build job can check","Batching reviews into a twice-weekly slot to cut context switching"],"a":2,"exp":"A reviewer buys linear headroom and moves break-even by about three teams. A machine-checked contract changes the slope, moving break-even by twenty. Prioritising and batching change who waits and for how long, not whether the queue exists."},{"q":"\"The answers got worse\" pages the infrastructure on-call rotation. What happens next?","o":["It gets acknowledged, has no runbook, and is eventually tuned to silence","It gets escalated correctly, because on-call escalation paths exist for this","It gets fixed slowly, since infra engineers can read model traces too","It gets triaged into the backlog and reviewed at the next sprint planning"],"a":0,"exp":"A page nobody can act on is a page that gets ignored, then adjusted until it stops firing. Quality and availability regressions need different diagnostics and different runbooks, which usually means different rotations. Which rotation currently receives this page tells you what your matrix really says."},{"q":"A guild - a cross-team forum with no dedicated platform staff - stops working at roughly what point?","o":["Around twelve teams, when meeting size makes decisions impractical","When the first artifact appears that needs exactly one accountable owner","When the organisation adopts more than one model provider concurrently","Around six teams, when review volume exceeds volunteer time available"],"a":1,"exp":"A guild is structurally incapable of owning a choke point, because its whole design is shared consent rather than single accountability. The failure isn''t about size; it arrives the day something has to have one owner, which is usually key custody or the trace schema."},{"q":"Which artifact in an AI platform is genuinely irreversible, and therefore needs an approver who isn''t the owner?","o":["The model choice, given how much prompt behaviour is tuned to one family","The rails service, since removing a rail exposes traffic that was screened","The trace schema, because every trace already written stays in its old shape","The budget tables, since committed spend is hard to claw back mid-year"],"a":2,"exp":"Model choice sits behind a choke point and is config. Rails and budgets are reversible by editing them. Historical traces cannot be rewritten into a new shape, so a schema change silently splits your incident history - which is exactly the kind of change that shouldn''t be self-approved."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'module:review', 'paid', '{"mod":{"id":"review","block":"b4","title":"The Architecture Review","tag":"One page, six numbers, three diagrams, and the dissent written by you","why":"Eleven artifacts in eleven places are not an architecture. They become one when they''re assembled into a single document that can be defended, attacked and revisited - and the assembling is where you find out which of them contradict each other, because almost every real review finding is two artifacts disagreeing while each looks fine alone. This is the capstone: the document, the review that stress-tests it, the ten questions that find most of the problems in someone else''s system, and the discipline that keeps the document alive afterwards.","mental":"<b>The artifact isn''t the diagram, it''s the argument.</b> Given these constraints, these alternatives were considered, this one was chosen, and here is exactly what would change our mind. A review is a defense: you''re not presenting a design for admiration, you''re exposing it to the strongest available attack while it''s still cheap to lose. If nobody in the room can articulate why you might be wrong, the review hasn''t happened yet, no matter how long the meeting ran.","concepts":[["The eleven artifacts","Spec card, three budget tables, chosen shape with rejected alternatives, FMEA with detectability, action inventory with autonomy tiers, degradation ladder with pre-authorizers, exit memo in engineer-weeks, derived-state register, migration runbook, control points with authorities, ownership matrix. That is the document''s table of contents; the check is that you can point at each one."],["Constraints, not conclusions","Record the number that forced the decision and where it came from, not just the decision. \"We chose the two-stage shape\" is unmaintainable in twelve months because nobody can tell whether the reason expired. \"Planner p95 measured 7,400ms against a 3,000ms requirement, benchmark dated March\" is maintainable forever."],["Cross-artifact contradiction","The characteristic finding of a real review, and the reason a linter beats a reader: budgets that don''t sum to the spec card, a staleness bound exceeding the freshness tolerance three pages earlier, an action inventory granting auto-execute on the thing the blast-radius line ruled out."],["Red-team role","Someone in the room is assigned, by name and in advance, the job of arguing for the rejected shape as though they believed it. Not devil''s advocacy as a mood - an actual assignment, with prep time, because unassigned dissent reliably fails to appear in a room containing the author''s manager."],["Pre-mortem","\"It''s twelve months later and this failed. Why?\" Asked as a fact rather than a risk, which measurably loosens tongues. Run it for ten minutes, write every answer down, and check each one against a control point in the doc. The ones with no control point are your roadmap."],["Revisit trigger","A numeric condition that makes the document stale, tied to spec-card values: volume x3, a change to the cost ceiling, a provider deprecation notice on a seam. Never a calendar date, because calendar reviews get skipped by exactly the busy quarters in which the numbers actually moved."],["The respected no","\"This should not be built with AI\" is a legitimate architectural output and needs to be made cheap to say. That means the standard is the same as for any other rejected shape - name the binding constraint and the number behind it - so declining reads as engineering rather than as a lack of ambition."]],"mistakes":["Presenting conclusions without the numbers that forced them, which makes the document unmaintainable the moment its author changes teams","Running the review as a walkthrough where the author narrates and the room nods, instead of assigning someone the rejected shape to argue for","Reviewing the diagram rather than the arithmetic - the diagram is almost always fine, and the budgets almost never sum","Setting a calendar reminder to revisit the design instead of numeric triggers, so the review gets skipped in precisely the quarter the volume tripled","Treating \"don''t build it with AI\" as a career-limiting conclusion, so nobody ever reaches it and the roadmap fills with features that were never going to close"],"flags":["\"We chose X\" - with no number and no source anywhere near the sentence","\"The eval gate covers that\" - ask what the gate is allowed to block, then check whether it ever has","\"We''ll review it next quarter\" - a calendar trigger, which is a reminder people delete during the busy quarter","\"Everyone agreed in the review\" - unanimity in a room containing the author''s manager is a measurement of the room, not the design","\"That''s out of scope for this document\" - said about the artifact that would have contradicted another one"],"ask":["Which number on the spec card forced this shape, and where did that number come from?","Do the stage budgets add up to the spec card? Let''s add them now, out loud.","For each control point: what is it allowed to block, and who gets paged when it fires?","For each derived store: what''s the staleness bound, and what''s the invalidation path within it?","What would have to become true for us to reverse this decision, and who is watching for it?"],"estMin":50},"deep":{"worked":"A review of someone else''s system: a mid-size claims triage assistant, 400,000 tasks a month, five engineers, eighteen months old. Sixty minutes, ten questions asked in yield order. Three findings, each surfaced by a number rather than by an opinion.<br><br><b>Question 2 - \"do the stage budgets add up?\"</b> Latency table: retrieval 300ms, generation 2,100ms, faithfulness gate 700ms, serialisation and egress 190ms. Spec card p95: 3,000ms. Sum: <b>3,290ms</b>, over by 290ms, and that''s before any queueing at the 3x peak the same spec card declares. Nobody had added the column, because each row had been added in a different week by a different person, and every individual row was justified. Ninety seconds of arithmetic, and it reframed the rest of the hour: the design as documented cannot meet its own latency requirement, so at least one control point is coming out or the requirement is wrong. That''s a decision the room can make; it was not a decision anyone knew they were owed.<br><br><b>Question 3 - \"what is each control point allowed to block?\"</b> The eval gate runs on every release and posts a score to the pull request. Asked what happens when the score drops below the stated bar, the answer was \"we''d look into it\". So its authority is record-only. We pulled the last 22 releases: <b>14 of them shipped with a score below the documented bar</b>. Not one had been questioned, because a number appearing in a pull request comment is not a gate, it''s decoration, and everyone had been reading it as a gate for four months. The fix is one line of CI config and one uncomfortable conversation about which of those 14 releases to re-examine.<br><br><b>Question 4 - \"for each derived store, what''s the staleness bound and the invalidation path?\"</b> The claim summary cache is rebuilt nightly, so its staleness bound is <b>24 hours</b>. The spec card''s freshness tolerance, four pages earlier, is <b>15 minutes</b>. That''s <b>96x over</b>, and the rebuild takes 41 minutes, so a full rebuild can never satisfy the tolerance no matter how often it runs. What''s missing isn''t compute, it''s an invalidation path: an event on claim update that invalidates one key. Both pages had been reviewed and approved separately, each by someone who never held the other one open at the same time. That is the shape of nearly every real finding.<br><br><b>What the yield order bought.</b> All three findings came from questions 2, 3 and 4. Questions 5 through 10 produced one minor note between them, because this team happened to be strong there. That''s the argument for a fixed order: you don''t know which questions will pay, but you do know the distribution, so an hour that runs out has run out in the right place.<br><br><b>Then the pre-mortem, ten minutes.</b> \"It''s twelve months on and this failed - why?\" Five answers, three of which already had control points. Two didn''t: the summarisation model gets deprecated and its replacement cites differently, and claims volume triples after the acquisition. Both became revisit triggers on the front page - deprecation notice on any seam, and volume x3 - written as numbers rather than dates, because a calendar reminder gets deleted in exactly the quarter volume actually triples.","good":["One document, eleven named sections, each traceable to the module that produced it and each pointed at by name in the table of contents","Every decision states the constraint and the source, dated, so a reader in twelve months can check whether the reason expired rather than guessing","A named red-team reviewer with prep time, assigned to argue the strongest rejected shape as though they believed it","Revisit triggers stated as numeric conditions on spec-card values, with an owner watching each one, and no calendar reminders anywhere","The linter runs in CI against the document as structured data, so a contradiction introduced by an edit fails a build instead of surviving to the next review"],"build":"<b>Assemble the document.</b> Eleven sections, in the order the course produced them: spec card, budget tables, shape decision with rejections and binding constraints, FMEA with detectability, action inventory with autonomy tiers, degradation ladder with pre-authorizers, exit memo in engineer-weeks, derived-state register, migration runbook with its numeric rollback trigger, control points with authorities and owners, ownership matrix. Where a section is thin, write \"we have not decided this\" rather than leaving it out, because an absent section reads as a decided one.<br><br><b>Run the review, sixty minutes, two colleagues.</b> Assign one of them, in the invitation and not in the room, to argue for the strongest rejected shape - document a day early, prepared as though they held it. Walk the ten questions in yield order, not the document in page order; page order is the author''s order and it protects the author''s assumptions. Spend the last ten minutes on the pre-mortem: it is twelve months later and this failed, why? Write every answer down, then check each against a control point in the document.<br><br><b>Record what you couldn''t answer.</b> Every question you couldn''t answer with a number goes on one list, and that list is your roadmap, already prioritised - the questions a skeptical peer asks in an hour are close to the ones production asks in a quarter. Finally, write the dissent yourself, and write it in numbers: name the rejected shape you would switch to, the spec-card value that would have to move for it to win, and by how much. A dissent stated that way isn''t a paragraph of fairness, it''s a revisit trigger - it goes straight onto the front page with an owner attached.","res":[["Architectural Decision Records","https://adr.github.io/","Use when you want a lightweight, durable format for the decision-plus-constraint records this module keeps insisting on - conventions, templates and tooling in one place."],["Gary Klein, Performing a Project Premortem (HBR)","https://hbr.org/2007/09/performing-a-project-premortem","Use right now, before your next review: three pages on why asking ''it failed, why?'' surfaces objections that asking ''what are the risks?'' never will."],["AWS Well-Architected Framework","https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html","Use when you''re building your own review question list - a mature, ordered set of review questions to steal structure from, even though its content predates AI systems."],["Google SRE Book - Postmortem Culture","https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/","Use when ''we shouldn''t build this with AI'' needs to be a survivable conclusion in your org - the blamelessness machinery is the same machinery."]]},"depth":{"mech":"<b>The document is eleven artifacts and a contradiction check.</b> Assembly isn''t clerical work: it''s the first time the artifacts sit where they can be compared, and comparison is where the findings are. Nearly every real finding is two artifacts disagreeing while each is individually defensible - budgets that don''t sum to the spec card, a staleness bound four pages from the tolerance it violates, an action inventory granting auto-execute on the thing the blast-radius line ruled out. Humans miss these because the two halves are read on different days by different people: a mechanical problem with a mechanical fix, which is why the Patterns tab ships a linter rather than a template.<br><br><b>Record constraints and their sources, not conclusions.</b> \"We chose the two-stage shape\" is dead prose within a year: nobody can tell whether the reason still holds, so the design becomes load-bearing by inertia. \"Rejected the agentic planner: measured p95 7,400ms against a 3,000ms requirement, benchmark dated March, rerun when the requirement or the model changes\" stays alive indefinitely, because a reader can check every clause. The test for your own document: could a competent stranger determine whether each reason has expired, using only what''s on the page? If not, you recorded an outcome and threw away the reasoning.<br><br><b>Running the review.</b> Assign the red-team role by name, in the invitation, with the document a day early - unassigned dissent does not appear in a room containing the author''s manager, and pretending otherwise is how designs get approved unanimously and fail predictably. Ask the ten questions in yield order rather than walking the document in page order, because page order is the author''s order and defends the author''s assumptions by construction. Close with a pre-mortem: \"it is twelve months later and this failed, why?\" The past-tense framing produces more and more specific answers than \"what are the risks?\", because it asks for explanation rather than speculation.<br><br><b>The ten questions, ordered by yield.</b> <b>1.</b> Which number on the spec card forced this shape, and where did it come from? <b>2.</b> Do the stage budgets sum to within the spec card - latency, cost per task, cost per month at peak? Add them out loud. <b>3.</b> For each control point, what is it allowed to block, and who is paged when it fires? <b>4.</b> For each derived store, what is the staleness bound and the invalidation path inside it? <b>5.</b> Which actions are irreversible, and who approves each one? <b>6.</b> Show me the degradation ladder and the name that pre-authorised each rung. <b>7.</b> Which failure modes have low detectability, and is the control for each one structural rather than an alert? <b>8.</b> What''s the exit cost in engineer-weeks for each vendor seam? <b>9.</b> What''s the migration''s rollback trigger as a number, and who owns pulling it? <b>10.</b> Who owns each artifact and who gets paged for it? Questions 2 through 4 produce most findings in most reviews, which is exactly why they''re not at the end, where an hour that overruns would drop them.<br><br><b>Saying it shouldn''t be built with AI.</b> This is a legitimate architectural output and it needs to be cheap to reach, which means holding it to the same standard as any other rejected shape: name the binding constraint and the number behind it. \"The bar implied by this blast radius is 99.95% task success; our best measured shape reaches 96.5%; closing that requires human review on 100% of traffic at $0.42 per task against a $0.03 ceiling\" is an engineering conclusion. \"It feels risky\" is a career risk. Building the number is what makes declining respectable in a room that''s excited.<br><br><b>Keeping it alive.</b> Revisit triggers are numeric conditions on spec-card values, each with an owner watching it: volume triples, the cost ceiling moves, the p95 target moves, the freshness tolerance moves, or a provider posts a deprecation notice on a seam you priced an exit for. Never a calendar date - calendar reviews get skipped in busy quarters, and busy quarters are precisely when the numbers move.","trade":[["Document as prose vs as structured data","Readability vs mechanically checkable consistency","A beautiful document that contradicts itself in three places nobody has held open at once"],["Assigned red-team role vs open discussion","Genuine dissent vs meeting comfort","Unanimous approval that measured the room''s hierarchy rather than the design"],["Yield-ordered questions vs walking the document","Findings per hour vs the author''s narrative flow","An hour that overruns having skipped the budget arithmetic, which was the finding"],["Numeric revisit triggers vs a calendar review","Fires when reality changes vs fires when you remembered","A design revisited on a quiet quarter and skipped on the quarter volume tripled"],["Recording constraints vs recording conclusions","Maintainability in a year vs pages written today","A decision nobody can evaluate, so it stays load-bearing purely through inertia"]],"scale":["One document per system, not per team: if two teams need two documents for one system you have found a seam, and it belongs in the ownership matrix rather than a second file","Run the linter in CI on the structured version so a contradiction introduced by an edit fails a build, which is the only mechanism that survives the document''s third author","Past about eight systems, review quality degrades unless the question list is fixed and ordered - free-form reviews scale with the reviewer''s memory, which does not","Revisit triggers should be instrumented where possible: an alert on volume crossing 3x is worth more than a line in a document that says volume x3"],"sec":["The document names your choke points, your keys'' custody path and your degradation behaviour, which makes it an attractive internal artifact - classify and access-control it accordingly","The action inventory is the authoritative statement of what the system may do unattended, so it is the artifact an auditor will ask for first and the one most likely to be out of date","Recording the pre-authorizer for each degradation rung is a security control: it prevents an on-call engineer from unilaterally lowering a safety posture during an incident","A revisit trigger on provider deprecation is also a supply-chain control, because a deprecated model version is an unmaintained dependency with your traffic running through it"],"interview":"The way I think about an architecture review is that the artifact isn''t the diagram, it''s the argument: given these constraints, these alternatives were considered, this one was chosen, and here''s exactly what would change our mind. So the document records the constraint and its source rather than the conclusion, because a doc that says we chose X without the number that forced it is unmaintainable in twelve months - nobody can tell whether the reason expired. When I review someone else''s system I ask ten questions in yield order, and the first four find most of it: which number forced this shape, do the stage budgets actually sum to the spec card, what is each control point allowed to block and who''s paged, and for each derived store what''s the staleness bound and the invalidation path. Almost every real finding is two artifacts contradicting each other while each looks fine alone, so I''d rather run a linter over the document as structured data than trust a room to hold eleven pages open at once. Then I assign someone to argue the rejected shape, run a ten-minute pre-mortem, and set revisit triggers on numbers rather than on a calendar - because the calendar review is the one that gets skipped in the quarter the volume tripled."},"patterns":{"intro":"This is the capstone artifact, and it is deliberately not a template. Templates tell you what to write; this reads what you wrote and tells you where it disagrees with itself. Express the eleven artifacts as one Python dict, run the file, and get a PASS/FAIL checklist naming each exact contradiction - composed budgets against the spec card, low-detectability failures without structural controls, derived stores that can''t invalidate inside the freshness bound, control points with no authority or owner, irreversible actions with no approver, degradation rungs nobody authorised. The example document ships with real contradictions planted in it.","code":"\"\"\"The architecture review linter.\n\nAn architecture document is not prose with a diagram in it. It is eleven\nartifacts that have to agree with each other. Almost every finding a good\nreviewer produces is a CONTRADICTION BETWEEN TWO ARTIFACTS that both look fine\nin isolation: stage budgets that do not sum to the spec card, a derived store\nwhose staleness bound exceeds the freshness tolerance three pages earlier, an\naction inventory that quietly grants auto-execute on the thing the blast-radius\nline said would never be automatic.\n\nHumans miss those in a 60-minute meeting. A linter does not.\n\nDOC below is a real (fictional) architecture document expressed as data, with\nthree deliberate contradictions and one warning planted in it. Replace it with\nyours, run the file, and paste the checklist into the review invite.\n\nStdlib only, deterministic, no network, no prices baked in - every threshold\ncomes from the doc''s own spec card.\n\n    python review_linter.py\n\"\"\"\n\n# =============================================================================\n# THE DOCUMENT (eleven artifacts, one dict)\n# =============================================================================\nDOC = {\n    \"system\": \"claims triage assistant\",\n    \"owner\": \"product-claims\",\n    \"revision\": \"2026-08-11\",\n\n    # 1. spec card ------------------------------------------------------------\n    \"spec_card\": {\n        \"quality_bar_wrong_answer_rate\": 0.005,\n        \"p95_ms\": 3000,\n        \"cost_per_task_usd\": 0.0120,\n        \"cost_per_month_usd\": 5000.0,\n        \"volume_per_month\": 400_000,\n        \"peak_multiplier\": 3.0,\n        \"blast_radius\": \"user-visible claim guidance; no automatic payout\",\n        \"freshness_tolerance_s\": 900,          # 15 minutes\n        \"auto_execute_allowed\": False,\n    },\n\n    # 2. budgets --------------------------------------------------------------\n    \"latency_budget\": [\n        (\"retrieval\",           300),\n        (\"generation\",         2100),\n        (\"faithfulness_gate\",   700),\n        (\"serialise + egress\",  190),\n    ],\n    \"cost_budget\": [\n        (\"retrieval\",          0.0006),\n        (\"generation\",         0.0081),\n        (\"faithfulness_gate\",  0.0026),\n        (\"rails\",              0.0001),\n    ],\n    \"reliability_budget\": [\n        (\"retrieval\",   0.9990),\n        (\"generation\",  0.9950),\n        (\"rails\",       0.9999),\n    ],\n\n    # 3. shapes ---------------------------------------------------------------\n    \"shapes\": {\n        \"chosen\": \"retrieve-then-generate, single pass, sync gate\",\n        \"feasible\": [\n            \"retrieve-then-generate, single pass, sync gate\",\n            \"retrieve-then-generate, single pass, async sampling\",\n            \"classify-then-route, two-model\",\n        ],\n        \"rejected\": [\n            (\"agentic multi-step planner\", \"p95 3000ms; measured planner p95 7400ms\"),\n            (\"fine-tuned single model\", \"freshness 900s; retrain cycle is 14 days\"),\n            (\"human-in-the-loop on every claim\", \"cost/task $0.0120; review costs $0.42\"),\n        ],\n    },\n\n    # 4. FMEA -----------------------------------------------------------------\n    # detectability: 1 = obvious in seconds, 10 = you find out from a customer\n    \"fmea\": [\n        dict(mode=\"retrieval returns stale policy doc\", severity=8, occurrence=4,\n             detectability=9, control=\"alert on index age\",\n             control_class=\"mitigation\"),\n        dict(mode=\"generation cites a document it was not given\", severity=7,\n             occurrence=3, detectability=3, control=\"citation-id rail, block_response\",\n             control_class=\"structural\"),\n        dict(mode=\"provider 429 during peak\", severity=5, occurrence=6,\n             detectability=2, control=\"degradation ladder rung 2\",\n             control_class=\"structural\"),\n        dict(mode=\"tenant data crosses in the derived store\", severity=10,\n             occurrence=1, detectability=8,\n             control=\"tenant key in the partition key, enforced at write\",\n             control_class=\"structural\"),\n    ],\n\n    # 5. action inventory -----------------------------------------------------\n    \"actions\": [\n        dict(name=\"draft claim summary\", reversible=True, tier=\"auto\", approver=None),\n        dict(name=\"request document from claimant\", reversible=True, tier=\"auto\",\n             approver=None),\n        dict(name=\"issue goodwill credit\", reversible=False, tier=\"auto\",\n             approver=None),\n        dict(name=\"close claim as denied\", reversible=False, tier=\"approve\",\n             approver=\"claims-adjuster\"),\n    ],\n\n    # 6. degradation ladder ---------------------------------------------------\n    \"degradation\": [\n        dict(rung=1, action=\"drop reranking\", pre_authorizer=\"platform on-call\"),\n        dict(rung=2, action=\"serve cached answer for repeat questions\",\n             pre_authorizer=\"platform on-call\"),\n        dict(rung=3, action=\"drop citations, serve ungrounded summary\",\n             pre_authorizer=\"claims-director\"),\n        dict(rung=4, action=\"queue and answer asynchronously\",\n             pre_authorizer=\"claims-director\"),\n        dict(rung=5, action=\"turn the assistant off, route to humans\",\n             pre_authorizer=\"claims-director\"),\n    ],\n\n    # 7. seams / exit memo ----------------------------------------------------\n    \"seams\": [\n        dict(seam=\"model provider\", exit_engineer_weeks=2),\n        dict(seam=\"vector store\", exit_engineer_weeks=6),\n        dict(seam=\"trace backend\", exit_engineer_weeks=3),\n    ],\n\n    # 8. derived-state register -----------------------------------------------\n    \"derived_state\": [\n        dict(store=\"claim summary cache\", staleness_bound_s=86400,\n             invalidation=\"nightly full rebuild\", rebuild_s=2460,\n             tenant_key=True, deletion_path=\"cascade on tenant delete\"),\n        dict(store=\"policy embedding index\", staleness_bound_s=600,\n             invalidation=\"event: policy.updated -> targeted upsert\", rebuild_s=5400,\n             tenant_key=False, deletion_path=\"n/a - shared corpus\"),\n    ],\n\n    # 9. migration runbook ----------------------------------------------------\n    \"migration\": dict(name=\"cutover to shared gateway\",\n                      rollback_trigger=\"error rate > 1.5% over 10 min\",\n                      owner=\"platform\"),\n\n    # 10. control points ------------------------------------------------------\n    \"control_points\": [\n        dict(name=\"ci_eval_gate\", authority=\"block_release\", owner=\"product-claims\"),\n        dict(name=\"input_rail\", authority=\"block_request\", owner=\"platform\"),\n        dict(name=\"citation_rail\", authority=\"block_response\", owner=\"product-claims\"),\n        dict(name=\"async_sample_3pct\", authority=\"open_ticket\", owner=\"product-claims\"),\n    ],\n\n    # 11. ownership matrix ----------------------------------------------------\n    \"ownership\": [\n        dict(artifact=\"prompts\", owner=\"product-claims\", paged=\"product-claims\"),\n        dict(artifact=\"rails\", owner=\"platform\", paged=\"platform\"),\n        dict(artifact=\"golden sets\", owner=\"product-claims\", paged=\"product-claims\"),\n        dict(artifact=\"indexes\", owner=\"platform\", paged=\"platform\"),\n    ],\n}\n\nALLOWED_AUTHORITY = {\"block_release\", \"block_request\", \"block_response\",\n                     \"open_ticket\", \"record_only\"}\nLOW_DETECTABILITY = 7          # >= this and you cannot see it happening\nSTRUCTURAL = \"structural\"\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# RULES  -  each returns (rule_name, [contradiction strings], [warning strings])\n# =============================================================================\ndef r1_budgets_compose(doc):\n    \"\"\"Composed stage budgets must fit the spec card. Add them up. Every time.\"\"\"\n    bad, warn = [], []\n    spec = doc[\"spec_card\"]\n\n    lat = sum(ms for _, ms in doc[\"latency_budget\"])\n    if lat > spec[\"p95_ms\"]:\n        bad.append(\"latency: stages sum to %dms against a %dms p95 spec \"\n                   \"(over by %dms, before any queueing) -- %s\"\n                   % (lat, spec[\"p95_ms\"], lat - spec[\"p95_ms\"],\n                      \", \".join(\"%s %d\" % (n, v) for n, v in doc[\"latency_budget\"])))\n\n    cost = sum(c for _, c in doc[\"cost_budget\"])\n    if cost > spec[\"cost_per_task_usd\"]:\n        bad.append(\"cost: stages sum to $%.5f/task against a $%.4f ceiling\"\n                   % (cost, spec[\"cost_per_task_usd\"]))\n    monthly = cost * spec[\"volume_per_month\"]\n    if monthly > spec[\"cost_per_month_usd\"]:\n        bad.append(\"cost: $%.5f/task x %s tasks = $%s/month against a $%s ceiling\"\n                   % (cost, format(spec[\"volume_per_month\"], \",\"),\n                      format(int(monthly), \",\"),\n                      format(int(spec[\"cost_per_month_usd\"]), \",\")))\n    else:\n        warn.append(\"cost headroom at peak: $%s/month at %.1fx peak vs a $%s ceiling\"\n                    % (format(int(monthly * spec[\"peak_multiplier\"]), \",\"),\n                       spec[\"peak_multiplier\"],\n                       format(int(spec[\"cost_per_month_usd\"]), \",\"))\n                    if monthly * spec[\"peak_multiplier\"] > spec[\"cost_per_month_usd\"]\n                    else \"cost fits at %.1fx peak ($%s/month)\"\n                    % (spec[\"peak_multiplier\"],\n                       format(int(monthly * spec[\"peak_multiplier\"]), \",\")))\n\n    rel = 1.0\n    for _, r in doc[\"reliability_budget\"]:\n        rel *= r\n    warn.append(\"composed availability %.4f (%.2f%%) -- reliability multiplies, \"\n                \"quote this number, never the per-stage one\" % (rel, rel * 100))\n    return (\"composed budgets fit the spec card\", bad, warn)\n\n\ndef r2_fmea_structural(doc):\n    \"\"\"Low detectability means you cannot watch for it. Watching is not a control.\"\"\"\n    bad = []\n    for row in doc[\"fmea\"]:\n        if row[\"detectability\"] >= LOW_DETECTABILITY and row[\"control_class\"] != STRUCTURAL:\n            rpn = row[\"severity\"] * row[\"occurrence\"] * row[\"detectability\"]\n            bad.append(\"''%s'': detectability %d/10, severity %d, RPN %d, but the \"\n                       \"control is a %s (''%s''). You cannot alert on what you cannot \"\n                       \"observe -- this needs a structural fix\"\n                       % (row[\"mode\"], row[\"detectability\"], row[\"severity\"], rpn,\n                          row[\"control_class\"], row[\"control\"]))\n    return (\"low-detectability failures have structural controls\", bad, [])\n\n\ndef r3_derived_state(doc):\n    \"\"\"Every derived store invalidates inside the freshness tolerance, or it lies.\"\"\"\n    bad, warn = [], []\n    tol = doc[\"spec_card\"][\"freshness_tolerance_s\"]\n    for st in doc[\"derived_state\"]:\n        if st[\"staleness_bound_s\"] > tol:\n            bad.append(\"''%s'': staleness bound %s vs a %s freshness tolerance \"\n                       \"(%.0fx over) and invalidation is ''%s'' -- a scheduled rebuild \"\n                       \"is not an invalidation path\"\n                       % (st[\"store\"], _dur(st[\"staleness_bound_s\"]), _dur(tol),\n                          st[\"staleness_bound_s\"] / float(tol), st[\"invalidation\"]))\n        elif st[\"rebuild_s\"] > tol and \"event:\" not in st[\"invalidation\"]:\n            bad.append(\"''%s'': rebuild takes %s, longer than the %s tolerance, with no \"\n                       \"targeted invalidation path\"\n                       % (st[\"store\"], _dur(st[\"rebuild_s\"]), _dur(tol)))\n        if not st[\"tenant_key\"]:\n            warn.append(\"''%s'' has no tenant key -- fine only if the corpus is genuinely \"\n                        \"shared; say so in the doc\" % st[\"store\"])\n        if not st[\"deletion_path\"]:\n            warn.append(\"''%s'' has no deletion path -- a deletion request will miss it\"\n                        % st[\"store\"])\n    return (\"derived stores invalidate inside the freshness bound\", bad, warn)\n\n\ndef r4_shape_feasible(doc):\n    \"\"\"The chosen shape must be in the feasible set, and rejections must name a number.\"\"\"\n    bad = []\n    sh = doc[\"shapes\"]\n    if sh[\"chosen\"] not in sh[\"feasible\"]:\n        bad.append(\"chosen shape ''%s'' is not in the feasible set\" % sh[\"chosen\"])\n    for name, constraint in sh[\"rejected\"]:\n        if not constraint or not any(ch.isdigit() for ch in constraint):\n            bad.append(\"rejected shape ''%s'' names no numeric binding constraint -- \"\n                       \"in 12 months nobody can tell whether the reason expired\" % name)\n    return (\"chosen shape is feasible; rejections name their binding constraint\",\n            bad, [])\n\n\ndef r5_control_points(doc):\n    \"\"\"A control point without an authority is a dashboard. Without an owner, decor.\"\"\"\n    bad, warn = [], []\n    for cp in doc[\"control_points\"]:\n        if cp.get(\"authority\") not in ALLOWED_AUTHORITY:\n            bad.append(\"''%s'' has authority %r, which is not one of %s\"\n                       % (cp[\"name\"], cp.get(\"authority\"),\n                          \"/\".join(sorted(ALLOWED_AUTHORITY))))\n        elif cp[\"authority\"] == \"record_only\":\n            warn.append(\"''%s'' can only record -- confirm nothing in this doc relies on \"\n                        \"it to enforce the quality bar\" % cp[\"name\"])\n        if not cp.get(\"owner\"):\n            bad.append(\"''%s'' has no owner -- nobody is paged when it fires or when it \"\n                       \"stops firing\" % cp[\"name\"])\n    blocking = [c for c in doc[\"control_points\"]\n                if c.get(\"authority\", \"\").startswith(\"block\")]\n    if not blocking:\n        bad.append(\"no control point in this system is authorised to block anything\")\n    return (\"every control point has an authority and an owner\", bad, warn)\n\n\ndef r6_irreversible_actions(doc):\n    \"\"\"Irreversible + automatic is the trifecta. It needs an approver, or a tier change.\"\"\"\n    bad, warn = [], []\n    spec = doc[\"spec_card\"]\n    for a in doc[\"actions\"]:\n        if not a[\"reversible\"] and a[\"tier\"] == \"auto\" and not a[\"approver\"]:\n            bad.append(\"''%s'' is irreversible, runs at tier ''auto'', and has no approver\"\n                       % a[\"name\"])\n            if not spec[\"auto_execute_allowed\"]:\n                bad.append(\"   ...and the spec card blast radius says %r -- the action \"\n                           \"inventory contradicts the requirement it was derived from\"\n                           % spec[\"blast_radius\"])\n        elif not a[\"reversible\"] and not a[\"approver\"]:\n            warn.append(\"''%s'' is irreversible with no named approver\" % a[\"name\"])\n    return (\"irreversible actions have an approver\", bad, warn)\n\n\ndef r7_degradation_authorised(doc):\n    \"\"\"Somebody agreed to each rung in daylight, or the on-call decides at 2am.\"\"\"\n    bad = []\n    for rung in doc[\"degradation\"]:\n        if not rung.get(\"pre_authorizer\"):\n            bad.append(\"rung %d (''%s'') has no pre-authorizer -- at 2am the on-call \"\n                       \"engineer decides this alone, unprepared, on your behalf\"\n                       % (rung[\"rung\"], rung[\"action\"]))\n    return (\"every degradation rung has a named pre-authorizer\", bad, [])\n\n\ndef r8_ownership_paging(doc):\n    \"\"\"The team that owns it is the team that gets paged, or MTTR includes a handoff.\"\"\"\n    bad = []\n    for row in doc[\"ownership\"]:\n        if not row.get(\"owner\"):\n            bad.append(\"artifact ''%s'' has no owner\" % row[\"artifact\"])\n        elif row.get(\"paged\") and row[\"paged\"] != row[\"owner\"]:\n            bad.append(\"artifact ''%s'' is owned by %s but paged to %s\"\n                       % (row[\"artifact\"], row[\"owner\"], row[\"paged\"]))\n    return (\"owner and pager are the same team\", bad, [])\n\n\nRULES = [r1_budgets_compose, r2_fmea_structural, r3_derived_state, r4_shape_feasible,\n         r5_control_points, r6_irreversible_actions, r7_degradation_authorised,\n         r8_ownership_paging]\n\nARTIFACTS = [\"spec_card\", \"latency_budget\", \"cost_budget\", \"reliability_budget\",\n             \"shapes\", \"fmea\", \"actions\", \"degradation\", \"seams\", \"derived_state\",\n             \"migration\", \"control_points\", \"ownership\"]\n\n\ndef _dur(seconds):\n    if seconds < 60:\n        return \"%ds\" % seconds\n    if seconds < 3600:\n        return \"%dmin\" % round(seconds / 60.0)\n    if seconds < 86400:\n        return \"%.1fh\" % (seconds / 3600.0)\n    return \"%.0fh\" % (seconds / 3600.0)\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"ARCHITECTURE REVIEW CHECKLIST\")\n    print(\"system   : %s\" % DOC[\"system\"])\n    print(\"owner    : %s     revision: %s\" % (DOC[\"owner\"], DOC[\"revision\"]))\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n\n    missing = [a for a in ARTIFACTS if not DOC.get(a)]\n    print(\"\\nARTIFACT COMPLETENESS  %d/%d present\"\n          % (len(ARTIFACTS) - len(missing), len(ARTIFACTS)))\n    if missing:\n        for m in missing:\n            print(\"  MISSING  %s\" % m)\n    else:\n        print(\"  all artifacts present -- completeness is not correctness, keep reading\")\n\n    total_bad = total_warn = 0\n    print()\n    for i, rule in enumerate(RULES, 1):\n        name, bad, warn = rule(DOC)\n        verdict = \"FAIL\" if bad else \"PASS\"\n        print(\"[%s] R%d  %s\" % (verdict, i, name))\n        for b in bad:\n            print(\"       X %s\" % b)\n        for w in warn:\n            print(\"       ~ %s\" % w)\n        total_bad += len(bad)\n        total_warn += len(warn)\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"-\" * 78)\n    if total_bad:\n        print(\"VERDICT: REVIEW BLOCKED -- %d contradiction(s), %d note(s).\"\n              % (total_bad, total_warn))\n        print(\"Every X above is two artifacts in this document disagreeing with each\")\n        print(\"other. None of them is a matter of taste, so none of them is a matter\")\n        print(\"for debate: fix the doc or fix the system, then re-run.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"VERDICT: NO CONTRADICTIONS -- %d note(s).\" % total_warn)\n        print(\"The document is internally consistent. That is the floor, not the\")\n        print(\"ceiling: now run the pre-mortem and let someone argue the rejected shape.\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    print(\"\\nREVISIT TRIGGERS (numeric, not calendar)\")\n    spec = DOC[\"spec_card\"]\n    print(\"  volume x3          : %s -> %s tasks/month\"\n          % (format(spec[\"volume_per_month\"], \",\"),\n             format(spec[\"volume_per_month\"] * 3, \",\")))\n    print(\"  cost ceiling moves : any change to $%.4f/task or $%s/month\"\n          % (spec[\"cost_per_task_usd\"], format(int(spec[\"cost_per_month_usd\"]), \",\")))\n    print(\"  p95 target moves   : any change to %dms\" % spec[\"p95_ms\"])\n    print(\"  freshness moves    : any change to %s\" % _dur(spec[\"freshness_tolerance_s\"]))\n    print(\"  provider deprecation notice on any seam: %s\"\n          % \", \".join(\"%s (%d eng-weeks to exit)\" % (s[\"seam\"], s[\"exit_engineer_weeks\"])\n                      for s in DOC[\"seams\"]))\n    print(\"\\nIf none of those numbers has moved, this document is still current, and\")\n    print(\"no calendar reminder needed to tell you so.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()","notes":["This is a linter and not a prose template because prose hides cross-artifact contradictions by construction: the two halves live on different pages and are read on different days. As data, they''re one comparison apart.","Every threshold comes from the document''s own spec_card - freshness tolerance, p95, cost ceiling, blast radius. There are no constants baked in and no prices, which is what lets the same file lint a document written next year.","Read R6 carefully. It fails when an action is irreversible, runs at tier auto and has no approver, then prints a second line comparing that against the spec card''s blast-radius text. That second line is the finding a human reviewer almost never makes, because those two facts are eight pages apart.","Put the structured document in the repository next to the code and run this file in CI, so a contradiction introduced by an edit fails a build instead of surviving to the next review. That is the only mechanism that outlives the document''s original author.","The revisit-trigger block is generated from the spec card rather than typed, so it can''t drift from it. Paste it onto the front page and give each line an owner."],"debug":[["Everything passes on the first run","The document was written to satisfy the linter rather than to describe the system","Re-derive three fields from production measurements rather than from the design intent - base p95, false-block rate, and the derived store''s real staleness - and rerun"],["R1 fails by a small margin and people want to ignore it","Stage budgets were each estimated optimistically and the error accumulated","That is the finding, not a rounding issue: composed budgets have no slack for queueing at peak, so either a stage comes out or the spec card changes"],["R3 fires on a store you believe is fine","The invalidation path is scheduled rather than targeted, so it cannot beat the tolerance regardless of frequency","Add an event-driven invalidation for the key, or raise the freshness tolerance on the spec card and say why in the document"],["The linter passes but the review still finds problems","It checks consistency, which is the floor, not correctness, which is the ceiling","Consistency is what a machine can do; the pre-mortem and the assigned red-team role are what the hour with humans is for"]]}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'quiz:review', 'paid', '[{"q":"A design document states \"we chose the two-stage retrieval shape\" with no number attached. What is the cost of that omission?","o":["Very little, since the choice is recorded and the team can explain it","Reviewers can''t verify the choice, so approval takes an extra round trip","In twelve months nobody can tell whether the reason for it has expired","New engineers onboard slowly, because the rationale isn''t written down"],"a":2,"exp":"A conclusion without its constraint is unmaintainable. The design becomes load-bearing through inertia rather than through a reason anyone can check. Record the number and its source, dated, so a stranger can evaluate whether it still holds."},{"q":"Latency stages read 300 + 2,100 + 700 + 190 ms and the spec card says p95 3,000ms. What is the finding?","o":["3,290ms: it misses by 290ms before any queueing at the declared peak","It fits, since stage p95 values don''t compose by simple addition anyway","It''s within tolerance, given that 290ms is under ten percent of budget","It depends on the shape of the distribution across the four stages"],"a":0,"exp":"Composed p95 is at least the sum of stage p95s and usually worse, so addition is the optimistic case and it already fails. It''s true that percentiles don''t add cleanly - that argument makes the finding stronger, not weaker. Ninety seconds of arithmetic, and it''s the finding most reviews skip."},{"q":"You are reviewing someone else''s AI architecture with sixty minutes. What do you ask after \"which number forced this shape?\"","o":["Which model and provider they selected, and what the fallback path is","How the retrieval layer is chunked and what the reranking strategy is","Whether they''ve load-tested the system at the declared peak multiplier","Whether the stage budgets actually sum to within the spec card''s numbers"],"a":3,"exp":"The budget question takes ninety seconds and fails startlingly often, because each row was added in a different week by a different person and nobody added the column. Model choice, chunking and load tests all matter, but none of them yields findings at that rate per minute."},{"q":"A derived store rebuilds nightly. The spec card''s freshness tolerance is 15 minutes and a rebuild takes 41 minutes. What''s the fix?","o":["Rebuild hourly instead, cutting the worst-case staleness by a factor of 24","An event-driven invalidation path, since no rebuild cadence can beat 41 min","Raise the freshness tolerance to 24 hours to match what''s actually delivered","Cache with a 15-minute expiry so entries can never exceed the tolerance"],"a":1,"exp":"A rebuild taking 41 minutes cannot satisfy a 15-minute bound at any cadence, so frequency is the wrong lever. You need targeted invalidation on the event that makes a key stale. Raising the tolerance is legitimate only if you can defend the new number, and a blanket short expiry just trades staleness for a rebuild storm."},{"q":"Your review ends with unanimous approval and no objections raised. What has that measured?","o":["The design''s quality, since experienced reviewers found nothing to object to","The room, not the design - dissent doesn''t appear unless it''s assigned","Review fatigue, meaning the document was too long to engage with properly","That the pre-read worked, since objections were resolved before the meeting"],"a":1,"exp":"Unassigned dissent reliably fails to appear in a room containing the author''s manager. That''s why the red-team role is assigned by name, in the invitation, with the document a day early - so someone arrives prepared to make the strongest case for the shape you rejected."},{"q":"Why is a pre-mortem phrased as \"it''s twelve months later and this failed, why?\" rather than \"what are the risks?\"","o":["It''s shorter, so more objections fit into the time the meeting has left","It assigns blame in advance, which raises the stakes and sharpens focus","It asks for explanation rather than speculation, which people do far better","It moves the discussion to concrete dates, making mitigations schedulable"],"a":2,"exp":"Stating the failure as a fact and asking why converts the task from forecasting into explaining, and explaining is something people are markedly better at. The output is more answers and more specific ones. It is emphatically not about blame - that framing shuts the room down."},{"q":"Which revisit trigger keeps an architecture document alive?","o":["A quarterly calendar reminder assigned to the document''s original author","A standing agenda item at the monthly architecture review for the group","Volume x3, a cost-ceiling change, or a deprecation notice on a priced seam","A review whenever the team changes ownership or the on-call rotation shifts"],"a":2,"exp":"Calendar triggers get skipped in busy quarters, and busy quarters are exactly when the numbers moved. Numeric triggers tied to spec-card values fire when reality changes, which is the only time the document was worth reopening anyway."},{"q":"You conclude a feature shouldn''t be built with AI. How do you make that a respected outcome?","o":["Frame it as a delay, so the option stays open when models improve later","Hold it to the same standard as a rejected shape: the number that forced it","Escalate the decision so the accountability sits above the design team","Present it alongside a non-AI design so there''s still something to approve"],"a":1,"exp":"\"96.5% measured task success against a 99.95% bar, closable only with human review at $0.42 a task against a $0.03 ceiling\" is an engineering conclusion. \"It feels risky\" is a career risk. Building the number is what makes declining read as rigour rather than as timidity."},{"q":"Why express the architecture document as structured data and lint it, rather than as prose?","o":["Prose is slower to write and harder to keep consistent across many authors","Structured data renders into diagrams automatically, which reviewers prefer","Linting removes the need for a human review, freeing the hour for building","Cross-artifact contradictions sit pages apart, so a reader can''t hold both"],"a":3,"exp":"The characteristic real finding is two artifacts disagreeing while each looks fine alone, and prose guarantees they''re read on different days by different people. As data, they''re one comparison apart. The linter checks consistency, which is the floor - the human hour is still where correctness gets argued."},{"q":"During a review you can''t answer four of the ten questions with a number. What is that list?","o":["A sign the review was scheduled too early in the design process","Follow-up work for the reviewers, who raised the questions in the first place","Your actual roadmap, already prioritised by a skeptical peer in one hour","Evidence the question list needs tailoring to your particular system"],"a":2,"exp":"The questions a skeptical peer thinks to ask in an hour are close to the ones production will ask within a quarter, and they arrive already ordered by yield. Write them down as they occur, don''t answer them in the room, and take the list away as the plan."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'cards', 'paid', '[{"m":"reqs","f":"Requirement reads \"it should be accurate.\" What''s missing?","b":"A measurement, a set, a threshold, and an abstention allowance. \"92% on a 300-case golden set, abstaining on 8% or less\" eliminates three architectures. \"Accurate\" eliminates none, which is why it isn''t a requirement."},{"m":"reqs","f":"$0.08/task, 4,000 tasks/day, $12,000/month ceiling. Consistent?","b":"Yes — 0.08 × 4,000 × 30 = $9,600, so 20% headroom. Always do this multiplication out loud: the two ceilings usually come from two people who never spoke to each other."},{"m":"reqs","f":"Identical ask, two teams, two architectures. Which field diverged?","b":"Blast radius. A wrong FAQ answer is an annoyance → retrieval read path. A wrong answer that auto-closes a billing dispute is churn → verified chain plus a human queue. Field 5 picks the shape."},{"m":"reqs","f":"Quality bar 0.92, abstention allowance left blank.","b":"You just required the system to answer every single time. \"Right when it answers\" and \"must always answer\" are different price tags, and the second one climbs a rung to buy back the same quality."},{"m":"budgets","f":"5-stage pipeline, 99.5% per stage. End-to-end?","b":"97.5%. Reliability composes multiplicatively — quote the product, never the per-stage number. This is the arithmetic that makes box count a reliability decision instead of an aesthetic one."},{"m":"budgets","f":"Stage p95s: 260, 300, 2,100, 200ms. End-to-end p95?","b":"About 2,520ms, not 2,860ms. Compose the spreads by root-sum-square or simulate. Summing p95s over-states by ~14% — conservative, but it deletes designs you could have afforded."},{"m":"budgets","f":"\"Just one more verification call\": +1,600ms p95, +$0.040. Budget 3,500ms / $0.08.","b":"3,704ms and $0.097 — over by 204ms and $0.017, which is $4,800/month. Now name what gets cut to fund it. Dropping rerank (300ms, $0.004) nearly does. That''s the real conversation."},{"m":"budgets","f":"99.9% available, 92% correct. What share of requests actually worked?","b":"91.9% — the product. Answering and being right are two independent ways to fail, so you need two error budgets. Availability has a pager by default; correctness has to be given one."},{"m":"shapes","f":"The corpus is unbounded. Which rungs die, and on what grounds?","b":"Rungs 1 and 2, on capability — no chain of calls makes a corpus fit that doesn''t fit. Note what didn''t kill them: cost and latency were both comfortable. Check capability first; budget never rescues it."},{"m":"shapes","f":"Volume ×20. Read path at $0.0228/task against a $12,000/month ceiling.","b":"$54,720/month — and every rung fails. So move a card field: async mode opens the batch rung at $0.0060/task = $14,400/month. One number moved the answer from rung 3 to rung 6."},{"m":"shapes","f":"Composite: 55% batch $0.0060, 42% read path $0.0228, 3% loop $0.0780.","b":"$0.0152 blended. At 25% hatch instead of 3% it''s $0.0274 — 1.8×. Alert on hatch share, not total spend: the blend is linear in the share, and spend is the lagging indicator."},{"m":"shapes","f":"A box in the diagram with no recorded forcing constraint.","b":"A deletion candidate. Take it to the team — either someone supplies the constraint and it finally goes in the doc, or nobody can and you''ve found real complexity to remove. Both outcomes are wins."},{"m":"failure","f":"Severity 9, likelihood 3, detectability 9. Disposition?","b":"Structural. RPN 243, but the number is not what decides it — detectability above 7 removes mitigation from the menu. Constrain the output, remove the capability, or gate it. Never plan a mitigation for a failure you cannot observe."},{"m":"failure","f":"Row A is 8/4/2 with a 97% catch-rate rail. Residual RPN?","b":"16, down from 64. Escaping likelihood 4 x 0.03 = 0.12, rounded up to 1 (never to zero), so 8 x 1 x 2 = 16. A 75% reduction from forty lines of deterministic arithmetic."},{"m":"failure","f":"4% per-step error, 35% growth per step, 8 steps. Chance of at least one bad step?","b":"72%, against 28% if the steps were independent — a 2.6x inflation. Compounding modes get a step ceiling; only linear modes can be budgeted for with a per-step control."},{"m":"failure","f":"Someone proposes a scorer to check whether a cited clause is from the current contract version.","b":"Notice that the scorer needs supplier_id + effective_date resolved against the contract table — which is the answer. If you can build the detector, build the answer instead."},{"m":"blast","f":"20,000 actions/mo, tolerance 4 bad. Required accuracy at act-irreversibly?","b":"99.98%. Required = 1 - T/(V x E), and E is 1.0 when nothing catches it. Behind an approval gate (E about 0.05) the same tolerance needs only 99.60%."},{"m":"blast","f":"Model is 94.0%. Act-with-undo needs 99.90%, suggest needs 93.3%.","b":"Demote the tier. Suggest multiplies your error budget by 300x against the same tolerance — a five-point accuracy gain you can deploy on Thursday, not a model you can buy."},{"m":"blast","f":"Agent reads CRM, reads customer tickets, sends email. Rails are in place. Verdict?","b":"Fail. Private + untrusted + egress in one turn is an escalation path regardless of rail quality. Break a leg: resolve recipients from pre-turn data, split the turns, or remove the egress."},{"m":"blast","f":"An allowlist the model populates at call time. Bulkhead or theatre?","b":"Theatre. A boundary the compromised turn can influence is a suggestion with extra steps. Resolve the allowlist from data that predates the untrusted content and it becomes structural."},{"m":"capacity","f":"40k tasks/day, 4,400 effective tokens each, 8x Monday peak. Quota to request?","b":"122,222 TPM steady, 977,778 at peak, about 1.22M with 25% headroom. Size against the peak minute, never the daily average — and start the conversation weeks early, because quota has a lead time."},{"m":"capacity","f":"42 workers gives 0.11s wait. 34 gives 11.73s. Same load. What moved?","b":"Utilisation, from 79% to 98%. The queueing curve is asymptotic near saturation, so an 18% fleet cut bought a hundredfold wait. Above ~80%, utilisation is a latency decision wearing a cost costume."},{"m":"capacity","f":"333 arrivals/min, 182 capacity, uncapped queue added to absorb the burst.","b":"Backlog grows 151/min — 9,060/hour — and everyone inherits it. The queue converted a fast refusal for 45% into a slow one for 100%. A queue is a lever only with a depth cap and admission control."},{"m":"capacity","f":"Your rung says: trigger, what''s disabled, customer effect, quality delta. Ship it?","b":"No — the fifth field is missing. Without a named pre-authorizer, the 3am engineer has a runbook they are not empowered to execute. Authority is what makes a rung a rung."},{"m":"seams","f":"31 raw call sites, 11 distinct behaviours. Which number goes in the exit memo?","b":"11. Exit cost scales with behaviours you depend on, not call sites, mechanical replacement is cheap and semantic differences are not. Sizing by call sites is how a fourteen-behaviour seam gets estimated as a three-day job."},{"m":"seams","f":"Wrapper saves 3.5 engineer-weeks at exit, costs 3.6 engineer-weeks a year to carry. Contract runs 3 years.","b":"Don''t build it. Break-even is 11.7 months against a 36-month commitment. Saving divided by annual carry, times twelve, is the only number this argument needs."},{"m":"seams","f":"The vendor hosts your golden set. What is the real exit-cost line?","b":"Not a data export. An eval capability you don''t have, roughly six engineer-weeks, sitting on the critical path of every vendor decision you make afterwards. You cannot swap what you cannot compare."},{"m":"seams","f":"P(leave within 18 months) = 35%. Wrapper carry 5.4 weeks. Exit saving 3.5 weeks.","b":"Expected saving 1.2 weeks against 5.4 certain, about 4 to 1 against building. Weight the saving by probability; never weight the carry, because you pay carry whether you leave or not."},{"m":"statearch","f":"Nine locations, four can receive a deletion. Two survivors never expire.","b":"That fraction is a compliance finding and an architecture diagram at once. Start with the two that never expire, not the index everyone is watching, because bounded exposure is a schedule and unbounded is an obligation."},{"m":"statearch","f":"Spec card freshness tolerance 15 minutes. Cache TTL 24 hours, no tombstone wiring.","b":"96x over contract, and two findings not one: the staleness breach, and an empty invalidation column. A TTL bounds how long you stay wrong, it never detects that you are wrong."},{"m":"statearch","f":"Re-index run: $34 embedding, $29 compute, 60 engineer-hours.","b":"People are 99% of it. Optimising the embedder buys you nothing, iteration rate is set by verification and re-baselining effort. Budget divided by run cost is how many times a year you may change your mind, and one is already spent."},{"m":"statearch","f":"\"The AI started getting things wrong on Tuesday.\" First check?","b":"Mirror lag, not the model. Compare each serving store''s last successful write to its source''s. Thirty seconds, and it resolves a surprising share of regressions that otherwise eat three weeks of prompt archaeology."},{"m":"migrate","f":"Baseline 92%, detect a 1.5-point drop, 80% power, alpha 0.05. Sample per arm?","b":"About 5,570 scored tasks. Halve the detectable effect and it quadruples, n scales as one over delta squared. The rollout schedule is a power calculation, not a calendar preference."},{"m":"migrate","f":"5% canary, 40,000 tasks/day, 3 days. Can it see a 1-in-2,000 failure?","b":"6,000 exposures, just clearing the 3/rate floor for 95% confidence. Run it for one day instead and you have 2,000 exposures and roughly a 37% chance of seeing nothing while the failure is live."},{"m":"migrate","f":"26 days to the kill date, 38 days of plan. What do you sell?","b":"Resolution, not hope. MDE 1.5 to 3.0 points cuts the sample 3.7x and compresses the ladder. Then write the sentence: we agreed not to detect a 2-point drop, and this person signed for it."},{"m":"migrate","f":"Shadow ran five days on live inputs, completely clean. Ship to 100%?","b":"No. Shadow is blind to user behaviour because nobody saw the output, so nobody abandoned, retried or escalated. It clears schema, latency and cost. Quality on real users needs a canary sized by sample."},{"m":"quality","f":"A synchronous check adds 700ms. Your headroom is 600ms.","b":"It doesn''t fit. Move it off the request path or make it deterministic. A check you can''t pay for isn''t a plan, it''s a preference."},{"m":"quality","f":"1% sampling, a regression on 0.5% of traffic, 13,000 requests/day, 8 events to alarm.","b":"0.65 events/day, so about 12 days to page. Choose the sampling rate from the lag you''re willing to accept, not from the roundness of the number."},{"m":"quality","f":"2% false-block rate on 400,000 tasks/month.","b":"8,000 good answers refused. Priced at even a modest support cost per refusal, that exceeds the check''s entire compute bill. Quote the count, never the ratio."},{"m":"quality","f":"Your eval runs in CI and posts a score to the PR.","b":"That''s authority record-only. It has stopped nothing. Assign a real authority or delete the check, because a check people believe in is worse than no check."},{"m":"orgarch","f":"6 teams x 9 reviews/month at 45 min, against 3 reviewers x 270 min/week x 13 weeks.","b":"0.69 utilisation, about 2 days of wait. At 7 teams it''s 4 days, at 8 it''s 12, at 9 it''s unbounded. Compute the bottleneck quarter before you agree to the gate."},{"m":"orgarch","f":"A choke point with two owners.","b":"That''s the shadow-spend signature: divergent config and an incident nobody can roll back in one place. Choke points take exactly one owner; features take several."},{"m":"orgarch","f":"Artifact owned by the product team, paged to platform.","b":"MTTR now permanently includes a handoff - platform mitigates, product fixes tomorrow. Owner and pager should be the same team, or you''ve priced a delay into every incident."},{"m":"orgarch","f":"Paved-road adoption sits at 30% after two quarters.","b":"The road is slower than the ditch. Adoption is the metric, not compliance - reaching for enforcement converts an engineering problem into a political one and buries the signal."},{"m":"review","f":"The document says \"we chose the two-stage shape.\"","b":"Missing the number that forced it. Record the constraint and its source, dated - otherwise nobody in twelve months can tell whether the reason expired."},{"m":"review","f":"Stage p95s: 300 + 2,100 + 700 + 190. Spec card says 3,000ms.","b":"3,290ms, over by 290 before any queueing at peak. Adding the column takes ninety seconds and is the finding most reviews never get to."},{"m":"review","f":"Revisit trigger: \"review this quarterly.\"","b":"Calendar triggers get skipped in busy quarters, which are the quarters the numbers moved. Tie it to spec-card values: volume x3, cost ceiling change, deprecation notice on a seam."},{"m":"review","f":"The review ended with unanimous approval.","b":"That measured the room, not the design. Assign the red-team role by name in the invitation, with the doc a day early, or dissent will not show up."}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.content (course_id, id, tier, data) values ('ai-architect', 'scenarios', 'paid', '[{"id":"s1","block":"b1","title":"The two-week estimate","sit":"Your engineering manager has told the VP that the internal engineering-docs assistant is \"about two weeks of work.\" The estimate came from a prototype one engineer built over a weekend: a single model call with a chunk of the handbook pasted into the prompt, and it demoed beautifully. The real corpus is roughly 40,000 documents across six systems, updated continuously. Expected traffic is 2,500 queries/day. A baseline model call measures $0.010 and 1,300ms p95 on your infrastructure. The commitment meeting is Thursday and nothing has been signed yet.","task":"What do you tell the VP on Thursday, and what number do you put next to it?","model":"The two-week estimate is real, and it prices the wrong thing. Start with the ladder. Rung 1, single call: $0.0100/task, 1,330ms — comfortably inside any sane budget, and rejected anyway, because 40,000 continuously-updated documents is an unbounded corpus and no prompt makes it fit. That is a capability fact, not a budget fact, so no amount of money rescues rung 1. The prototype demoed well because it was asked questions the pasted chunk happened to cover.<br><br>Rung 3, retrieval read path: 1.9× baseline = $0.0190/task, 1.5 × 1,300 + 220 = 2,170ms p95. Monthly inference: 0.019 × 2,500 × 30 = $1,425. That number is small enough to be a rounding error, and it is not the number that matters.<br><br>The number that matters is operate cost. A read path is roughly 0.8 FTE steady state — index freshness across six systems, permission drift, re-embedding on document change, and the on-call that comes with a second subsystem. At a loaded $200K/year that''s about $13,300/month, which is <b>9.3× the inference spend</b>. The two-week estimate has no operate line in it at all, and the operate line is where 90% of the cost lives at this volume.<br><br>So: tell the VP two weeks is a fair build estimate for rung 1, which we cannot ship, and that the shape the corpus forces is five to six weeks plus roughly 0.8 FTE ongoing. Then offer the cheap alternative honestly — if we scope the corpus to the 400 documents that cover 80% of queries, rung 1 becomes available again and the two-week estimate is real. That''s a scope decision for the VP, made with both numbers visible, on Thursday rather than in month four.","pts":["Identifies that the unbounded corpus is a capability constraint that kills rung 1 regardless of budget","Prices the read path with actual arithmetic ($0.0190/task, $1,425/month) rather than asserting it''s more expensive","Surfaces operate cost as the dominant term and quantifies it against inference spend (~9×)","Names that the estimate covered build only, and separates build estimate from ongoing ownership","Offers a scope alternative that would make the cheap rung genuinely available, and leaves the choice with the VP"]},{"id":"s2","block":"b1","title":"The \"just make it accurate\" ticket","sit":"The ticket says: \"Add AI answering to the customer help widget. It should be accurate and fast, and handle anything customers ask.\" The PM who wrote it is capable and busy, and genuinely believes she has given you a requirement. Support currently handles 1,200 contacts/day at a fully-loaded $4.20 per contact. Nobody has discussed a budget, a latency target, or what happens when the assistant is wrong. You have a thirty-minute meeting with her tomorrow and you would like to leave it with something you can design against.","task":"Run the elicitation and produce the six-field spec card. Show your arithmetic.","model":"Three questions, asked in this order, produce five of the six fields.<br><br><b>Who is harmed by a wrong answer?</b> Her answer — \"the customer gets bad advice about their plan and might act on it\" — gives blast radius: recoverable but visible, not costly. That sets a defensible quality bar of about 0.92 on a golden set of 300 real contacts, with abstention allowed on 8%. Note that abstention has to be explicit, or you''ve required the widget to answer every question ever asked, which is the \"handle anything\" clause and is unaffordable at any rung.<br><br><b>What does the user do in the next ten seconds?</b> They''re staring at a widget. Interactive, so p95 in the low seconds — 3,000ms, sourced from the widget''s existing abandonment curve rather than invented.<br><br><b>What does the status quo cost?</b> $4.20 per contact, 1,200/day. This is the field that turns your constraint into her business case. If the assistant deflects 60% of contacts, that''s 720/day × $4.20 = $3,024/day, about $90,720/month of avoided cost. Against that, a ceiling of $0.30/task is 0.30 × 1,200 × 30 = $10,800/month — you''re proposing to spend 12% of the value to capture it. That is an easy conversation, and it''s only available because you asked the third question.<br><br>Fields five and six: volume 1,200/day with a peak multiplier pulled from traffic history, not guessed; freshness — plan and pricing docs change weekly, so 24 hours is tolerable and she should confirm that with legal, because it''s also a statement about deleted records.<br><br>Then close the triangle: with 0.92 quality and a 3,000ms budget, tell her plainly which one you''re refusing to optimise. Get her name against it.","pts":["Uses the three elicitation questions rather than asking the PM to specify a technical requirement","Converts \"accurate\" into a threshold on a named set, and adds an explicit abstention allowance","Identifies \"handle anything\" as the unbounded-scope clause and closes it via abstention","Uses status-quo cost ($4.20 × 1,200) to derive a ceiling in comparative form, with the arithmetic shown","Names the impossible-triangle loser explicitly and gets it signed rather than deciding it silently"]},{"id":"s3","block":"b1","title":"The feature that can''t afford itself","sit":"A strong team has proposed an orchestrator-worker architecture for a consumer-facing content assistant: fan out to several specialist workers, merge, return. The design doc is genuinely good — clear seams, sensible failure handling, tested prototype. The surface serves 200,000 sessions/day and is ad-supported at roughly $0.004 of attributable revenue per session. A baseline call on your stack is $0.011. The team has asked for a decision this week and is expecting a yes.","task":"Decide, and show the team the arithmetic that makes the decision for you.","model":"The design is fine. The unit economics are not, and no amount of design quality fixes them.<br><br>Orchestrator-worker runs about 14× a baseline call: 14 × $0.011 = <b>$0.154/task</b>. At full scope that''s 0.154 × 200,000 × 30 = <b>$924,000/month</b> against attributable revenue of 0.004 × 200,000 × 30 = <b>$24,000/month</b>. The feature is 38× underwater. That is not a number you optimise your way out of.<br><br>Walk down the ladder before concluding anything. Single call at 1×: $0.011/task = $66,000/month — still 2.75× underwater. Batch precompute at ~0.5×: $0.0055/task = $33,000/month — <b>still</b> underwater, and that''s the cheapest rung that exists. So the honest finding is that <b>no shape on the ladder closes at full scope</b>. The binding constraint isn''t the architecture, it''s that $0.004/session cannot fund an AI call of any kind at 200,000 sessions/day.<br><br>Which means the decision is about scope, not shape. Suppose 5% of sessions are the high-intent ones where the assistant plausibly changes behaviour, and those sessions carry roughly $0.06 of attributable value. Serve only those: 10,000/day on a retrieval read path at 1.9× = $0.0209/task, or 0.0209 × 10,000 × 30 = <b>$6,270/month</b>, against 0.06 × 10,000 × 30 = <b>$18,000/month</b> of value. That closes at about 2.9× coverage, and it''s a real feature.<br><br>So: no to the orchestrator, not because it''s badly designed but because it''s 38× underwater and the ladder proves nothing cheaper closes either. Yes to a scoped read path on high-intent sessions, with the trigger rate instrumented from day one — because the whole business case rests on that 5% staying near 5%, and if it drifts to 20% the coverage ratio collapses.","pts":["Prices the proposed shape per task and per month, and compares it directly against attributable revenue","Walks down the ladder to establish that no shape closes at full scope, rather than just rejecting the expensive one","Reframes the decision as scope rather than architecture once the ladder is exhausted","Shows the scoped alternative closing with explicit arithmetic and a stated coverage ratio","Identifies the trigger-rate assumption as the thing to instrument, since the case depends on it holding"]},{"id":"s4","block":"b1","title":"The demo that set the SLO","sit":"DESIGN REVIEW. Six weeks ago a team demoed a prototype that answered 12 curated questions in about 1.2 seconds each. Leadership was delighted and announced a \"under 2 seconds, always\" commitment externally. You''ve now been handed the resulting architecture doc to review before build. It specifies six serial stages; the latency section sums the stage p95s to 1.9s and claims 100ms of headroom. Availability is listed as \"99.9% per stage.\" Cost is quoted as \"$0.009 per call.\" There''s an escape hatch to a tool loop \"for hard cases.\" Expected volume is 50,000 tasks/day. There is no abstention path and no quality section.","task":"Find the flaws and rank them by which one invalidates the most other decisions.","model":"<b>1. The SLO''s provenance.</b> Everything below inherits this. \"Under 2 seconds\" was derived from twelve curated questions measured at what was effectively a p50 on a happy path. That is not a distribution, and a p95 over real production traffic on 50,000 tasks/day will not resemble it. Every latency decision in the doc is being made against a number nobody sourced. Fix this first or the rest is arithmetic on sand.<br><br><b>2. Cost is understated 6×.</b> \"$0.009 per call\" across six stages is <b>$0.054 per task</b>. At volume: 0.054 × 50,000 × 30 = <b>$81,000/month</b>, not the $13,500/month the per-call figure implies. This is second because it doesn''t invalidate the design, it invalidates the business case — and it''s the number that will be quoted back at the team in six months.<br><br><b>3. Composed availability.</b> Six stages at 99.9% is 0.999^6 = <b>99.4%</b>, not 99.9%. At 50,000/day that''s about <b>300 failed tasks per day</b> against an implied 50. Per-stage nines are not a commitment anyone experiences.<br><br><b>4. The escape hatch has no measured share and no cap.</b> A tool loop runs ~6.5× baseline. The demo set contained zero hard cases, so the trigger rate is not merely unmeasured, it''s unmeasurable from the evidence available. At 3% it''s a rounding error; at 20% it dominates the blend. This needs a rate cap and an alert before build, not after.<br><br><b>5. No abstention path and no quality section.</b> The system is therefore required to answer every question, which is the most expensive requirement in the document and the only one that isn''t written down anywhere. There''s also no correctness budget, so quality regressions will page nobody.<br><br><b>6. Summed p95s.</b> Ranked last deliberately: composing properly would give roughly 1.6s rather than 1.9s, so this error is conservative and in the team''s favour. It''s still wrong and still worth fixing, but it is the only flaw here that isn''t costing anyone anything.<br><br>The one-line recommendation: don''t review the design, re-source the SLO. Five of these six flaws are downstream of a number that came from a demo.","pts":["Ranks the demo-sourced SLO first and explains that the other findings are downstream of it","Catches per-call versus per-task cost and computes the 6× gap with monthly figures","Composes availability multiplicatively (0.999^6 = 99.4%) and converts it to failed tasks/day","Flags the uncapped escape hatch and notes the demo set could not have measured its share","Correctly ranks the summed-p95 error last, recognising it errs conservatively, rather than leading with the most technical finding"]},{"id":"s5","block":"b2","title":"The tool nobody audited","sit":"You are reviewing a sales-research agent two weeks before its launch. It has grown from three tools to nine over the quarter, one at a time, each addition reviewed on its own merits and each perfectly sensible. It reads account records from the CRM, reads prospect replies pulled from a shared inbox, drafts outreach, and — added last month so reps would stop copy-pasting — posts summaries to a team chat channel (Slack) via an incoming webhook. The team is proud of it. Nobody has ever looked at the nine tools on one page at the same time, and the design doc still describes the original three.","task":"Do the blast-radius pass this system never got: inventory the action surface, run the trifecta check, and say what has to change before launch.","model":"Put all nine on one page first, because the problem is composition and no single-tool review could have caught it. Three legs are present. <code>read_account</code> puts private customer data in the turn. <code>read_inbox</code> puts prospect-authored text in the turn — a stranger typed that, which is the entire premise. The Slack webhook moves bytes out of the boundary. Private plus untrusted plus egress inside one turn is an escalation path, and the fact that each tool was individually reasonable is exactly how it got built.<br><br>Name the specific path in the doc rather than summarising it: read_account to read_inbox to slack_post. \"We have an injection risk\" gets deferred; a named path gets fixed.<br><br>Then price the fixes rather than asserting one. Removing the webhook costs the reps the copy-pasting it was added to eliminate — call it 40 posts a day at 30 seconds, about 5 hours a month, which is real but small. Narrowing the destination to a single channel id resolved from config, decided before any untrusted text arrived, costs nothing at all and breaks the egress leg outright. That is the move: the channel is not chosen inside the turn, so a compromised turn cannot redirect it, and the content is still constrained to the account already in scope.<br><br>Then do the tier arithmetic on the drafting path. At 6,000 drafts a month, a tolerance of two bad sends and an 88% measured accuracy, acting directly needs 99.97% and delivers 720 bad actions a month — 360x over. Approval-gated at a 0.05 escape needs 99.6% and yields 36, still over. Suggest-only yields 2.2 and passes. Ship at suggest, and put the requirement table in the doc.","pts":["Inventories all nine tools on one page and identifies that the vulnerability is compositional, not present in any single tool review","Runs the trifecta check explicitly and names the specific path (private read, untrusted read, egress) rather than describing a general injection risk","Proposes breaking a leg structurally — resolving the destination from pre-turn config — rather than adding a rail or prompt instruction","Prices at least two options against lost product value with actual numbers, instead of asserting the safest one","Does the tier arithmetic on the write path and lands on a demotion justified by the required-accuracy figures"]},{"id":"s6","block":"b2","title":"The Monday-morning peak","sit":"You are two months out from launching a claims-triage assistant for an insurer. The spec card says 40,000 claims a day, 3,200 tokens per claim, and a Monday-morning peak of 8x — the industry''s traffic is famously lumpy, since nobody files a claim on a Saturday. The platform team has sized a worker fleet from the latency target and is happy. Procurement has \"an account with the provider.\" Nobody has written a number next to the words tokens per minute, and the launch checklist has a line item called \"load testing\" scheduled for the final week.","task":"Produce the capacity plan: the quota to request, the pool to run, and what the system does when the peak arrives larger than predicted.","model":"Start with effective tokens, because getting this wrong understates everything downstream. 3,200 splits roughly 2,800 in and 400 out, and output usually counts for more against a throughput meter — at a weight of 4.0 that is 2,800 + 1,600 = 4,400 effective tokens per claim.<br><br>Throughput: 40,000/86,400 = 0.463 claims a second, times 4,400 times 60 = 122,222 TPM steady. At the spec card''s 8x peak that is 977,778 TPM and 222 RPM. Add 25% headroom and request roughly 1.22M TPM. Say the important part out loud in the meeting: quota has a procurement lead time measured in weeks, so this is a schedule dependency for January, not a checklist item for the final week.<br><br>Pool: Little''s Law, L = lambda x W. At peak, 3.70 claims a second and 9.0 seconds each gives 33.3 in flight — a floor, not a target. At 34 workers you are at 98% utilisation and 11.73 seconds of mean wait; at 42 you are at 79% and 0.11 seconds. Six workers, a hundredfold difference in wait, with nothing else changed. Take 42 and stop treating utilisation as an efficiency metric.<br><br>Then the part that is actually the deliverable. If procurement returns the 800k tier rather than 1.22M, real capacity is min(280, 182) = 182 claims a minute and the fleet was never the constraint. At a 12x Monday, 333 arrive against 182: an uncapped queue grows 151 a minute and the 09:20 claim waits fifty minutes. So write five rungs — cache-only, smaller model, template deflection, admission-controlled queue with notification, honest reject with a retry-after — each with a trigger, the quality delta, and the name of the claims director who signed it. Then game-day rung 3 in staging before launch, not during it.","pts":["Computes effective tokens with an output weighting and shows the steady, peak, and headroom TPM figures as arithmetic","Frames quota as a schedule dependency with procurement lead time, rather than a launch-week checklist item","Applies Little''s Law to get the concurrency floor and explicitly rejects sizing at that floor because of the queueing cliff","States capacity as min(fleet, quota) and works through what happens when the quota is the binding constraint","Delivers a five-rung ladder with triggers, quality deltas and a named pre-authorizer, plus a game day before launch"]},{"id":"s7","block":"b2","title":"The undetectable failure","sit":"A clinical-operations team wants an assistant that answers staff questions about internal care protocols by retrieving from a document library. It is read-only, it takes no actions, it touches no money, and for those reasons it has sailed through two review gates already. In the third review you ask what happens when the library holds both the current protocol and the one it replaced in March, and the room goes quiet. Someone offers to add an LLM-as-judge scorer over a sample of answers. Everyone likes this idea, and the meeting starts to wrap up.","task":"Score this failure mode properly and say what has to change, including why the proposed scorer is the wrong answer.","model":"Score it before anyone proposes anything. Severity 9: a nurse acting on a superseded protocol is patient harm and a reportable event, and read-only systems can absolutely cause it — no action surface is required to be dangerous. Likelihood 6: the library holds every version, superseded documents read almost identically to current ones, and retrieval has no opinion about time. Detectability 9, which is the number that ends the discussion. The answer is fluent, cites a real document, quotes it accurately, and the document genuinely exists in the library. No reviewer scanning outputs flags it. It surfaces when a patient outcome triggers a chart review, months later. RPN 9 x 6 x 9 = 486.<br><br>Now the scorer. Follow it one step: to judge staleness, the scorer must know which protocol version is effective for that unit on that date. That means resolving protocol_id plus effective_date against a registry — a deterministic lookup. Which is the whole point: <b>if you can build the detector, build the answer instead.</b> The moment that lookup exists and is reliable, you do not need the model for the question and you certainly do not need it to be graded by a second model on 3% of traffic days later.<br><br>So the change is structural. Retrieval is filtered at query time to documents whose effective range covers today, superseded versions are excluded from the index the assistant reads rather than ranked below current ones, and every answer carries the protocol version and effective date on the face of it so a human can see the freshness without asking. Post-change, the mode''s likelihood is not reduced, it is zero — the surface is gone. RPN 486 to 0.<br><br>Keep the sampler if you like, but for over-abstention, not for this. This row was never a monitoring row.","pts":["Scores all three dimensions with justification, and identifies detectability around 9 as the score that removes mitigation from the menu","Rejects the sampler with the specific reason: its oracle, once built, is the correct answer","Proposes a structural fix (effective-date filtering at retrieval, superseded versions excluded) rather than a detection mechanism","Notes that a read-only system with no action surface can still carry severity 9, so tier and severity are independent axes","States the post-change likelihood as zero because the surface is removed, not merely reduced by a control"]},{"id":"s8","block":"b2","title":"The queue that made everything slower","sit":"You have been handed another team''s design doc to review before it goes to the architecture board. It is a document-summarisation service, 15,000 documents a day, tail-heavy Monday traffic. The design reads: \"All requests enter a durable queue to absorb bursts. Workers pull from the queue; the pool autoscales on queue depth up to 60 workers, targeting 92% utilisation for cost efficiency. If the primary provider returns errors, traffic fails over automatically to a secondary provider. Under sustained load we will degrade gracefully.\" The p95 latency target is 8 seconds. Every sentence sounds reasonable. The team has asked for a rubber stamp.","task":"Review this design: find the flaws, rank them by how much damage they do, and say what you would change.","model":"Four flaws, ranked by damage.<br><br><b>One, the uncapped queue is the worst thing here and it is presented as the safety feature.</b> A queue creates no capacity; it converts refusal into waiting. With no depth cap and no admission rule, when arrivals exceed service rate the backlog grows without bound and every request inherits the accumulated delay — so the median user gets slower specifically so that nobody is refused. Against an 8-second p95 target this is fatal: a 60-second backlog means every request misses, not the marginal ones. Fix: a depth cap derived from the p95 target and service time (at 3 seconds a task and 60 workers, a queue deeper than about 160 already guarantees a miss), plus admission control that refuses immediately with a retry-after at the boundary.<br><br><b>Two, targeting 92% utilisation is a latency decision described as a cost decision.</b> The queueing curve is asymptotic near saturation — the difference between 79% and 93% is roughly a twentyfold change in mean wait at fixed load. Targeting 92% means the autoscaler is aiming at the cliff. Fix: target 70-75% and state the p95 cost of the alternative in the doc.<br><br><b>Three, autoscaling on queue depth is a lagging signal that will oscillate.</b> Depth rises only after the wait has already been incurred, so the pool arrives late and then overshoots. Scale on arrival rate with depth as a secondary, and add hysteresis.<br><br><b>Four, automatic failover to an unevaluated secondary.</b> Failover preserves availability and says nothing about quality — a silent 200 at fleet scale. Run the secondary against the golden set, record the delta, and make failover a rung with a known quality cost.<br><br>And \"degrade gracefully\" is not a design. Five rungs, five fields each, with a named pre-authorizer.","pts":["Identifies the uncapped queue as the primary flaw and explains that a queue redistributes delay rather than creating capacity","Connects the 92% utilisation target to the asymptotic queueing curve and quantifies the latency cost against the stated p95 target","Flags queue depth as a lagging autoscaling signal that produces oscillation, and proposes a leading signal with hysteresis","Catches the unevaluated failover target as a quality incident hiding inside a capacity save","Ranks the findings by damage rather than listing them, and replaces \"degrade gracefully\" with five specified rungs including the pre-authorizer"]},{"id":"s9","block":"b3","title":"The 60-day deprecation notice","sit":"You lead platform for a claims-triage assistant. On 2026-09-01 your provider announces the model you launched on reaches end of life in 45 days. Traffic is 12,000 triage tasks a day, measured task success is 88%, and a 2-point drop would push you below the accuracy commitment in three enterprise contracts. The team''s instinct is to swap the model, run the golden set, and ship the following week. The golden set was last refreshed nine months ago and covers the English intake flow; a bulk-upload workflow shipped in April now carries about 30% of daily volume and appears in none of the eval cases.","task":"Produce the rollout plan: which rungs, what sample, what schedule, and what you have agreed not to detect.","model":"Schedule backwards from the kill date. At an 88% baseline, detecting a 2-point drop at 80% power and alpha 0.05 needs about <b>4,438 scored tasks per arm</b>. Score the whole canary cohort during rollout: a 10% cohort yields 1,200/day, so the 10% stage runs <b>4 days</b>, and 50% clears in one. Ladder 5 days, ramp 3, shadow 4, offline eval 3, buffer 5, total <b>20 days</b> against 45 available. Comfortable, which is the trap, because the eval set is the binding constraint, not the calendar.<br><br>The golden set covers 70% of traffic. Offline eval on it cannot see the bulk workflow at all, so a rung that is supposed to be your cheapest filter is silently scoped to the minority of your users. Refresh it first: sample 300 bulk-workflow traces, label, write a judge, baseline the current model, about 10 working days. New total <b>30 days</b>, still inside 45, with 15 days of slack. Start on 2026-09-08 and the plan lands on 2026-10-08, eight days before the kill date.<br><br>Rollback trigger, written now: scored task success below <b>86%</b> over any rolling 1,000 scored tasks, pulled by the assistant on-call without convening anyone. Blind spots to state in the doc: shadow cannot see user behaviour, and 4,800 exposures at the 10% stage leaves you unable to claim you would have seen a failure rarer than about 1 in 1,600.","pts":["Schedules backwards from the kill date so the start date is derived, not chosen","Computes sample per arm from baseline and minimum detectable effect, then derives days at each percentage","Identifies the stale golden set as the binding constraint and refreshes it before the ladder, while slack still exists","Writes the rollback trigger as a metric, a threshold, a window and a named puller","States the blind spots explicitly: what shadow cannot see, and the rare-failure rate the exposure count cannot cover"]},{"id":"s10","block":"b3","title":"The customer who exercised deletion","sit":"An enterprise customer of your document-assistant product submits an erasure request covering one of their departed employees. You have 30 days. The architecture diagram on the wall shows three boxes. The actual system has eight places the person''s data reached: the source document store, a change stream, a chunk staging bucket, the vector index, a summary table built nightly, a semantic cache with a 6-hour TTL, per-user agent memory files written by an extraction job, and a trace store with 180-day retention. The spec card sets freshness tolerance at 30 minutes. Legal has asked for written confirmation, and nobody can currently produce it.","task":"Design the deletion architecture and give legal a number they can sign.","model":"Build the register before touching code: eight rows, seven columns each, source, staleness, invalidation, rebuild time, rebuild cost, tenant key, deletion path. Then trace one record. Source store deletes immediately. The change stream emits a tombstone but retains the pre-image for its window. The index accepts delete-by-filter, reclaimed at the next merge, measured lag 90 minutes, which is <b>3x the 30-minute freshness contract</b> and worth recording. Everything else fails: staging bucket has a lifecycle rule and no path, the summary table has no tenant key so a scoped delete cannot be expressed, the cache serves for up to <b>360 minutes, 12x over contract</b>, memory files carry no identifier at all, and traces persist 180 days. <b>Three of eight can receive a deletion; two of the survivors never expire.</b><br><br>Fix order is by unboundedness, not by visibility. Memory files: add owner front matter plus a delete-by-owner sweep, 3 engineer-days. Summary table: add the tenant key and a scoped delete, 2 days. Cache: tombstone-driven invalidation replacing the TTL, 1 day, dropping serving-path exposure from 360 minutes to under a minute. Staging lifecycle from 90 days to 7, one hour. Traces: shorten retention to 30 days and add a subject-scoped purge, 1 day. About <b>7 engineer-days, roughly 1.5 engineer-weeks</b>, plus a quarterly synthetic-record test that proves the sweep still works.<br><br>What legal signs is the register plus the test result, not an assurance. The number is: all eight reached, worst residual exposure 7 days, serving path under 60 seconds.","pts":["Builds the register first and traces one real record through every store rather than reasoning from the diagram","Counts survivors explicitly and treats that fraction as the finding","Distinguishes TTL as a damage bound from a real invalidation path, and quantifies the breach against the spec card","Prioritises unbounded stores over visible ones, and names the store with no tenant key as unable to express a scoped delete","Prices the fix in engineer-days and proposes a recurring proof, so legal signs evidence rather than an assurance"]},{"id":"s11","block":"b3","title":"The abstraction that leaked both vendors","sit":"Two years ago your team wrapped its retrieval vendor behind an internal interface so a swap would be easy. The interface now has 19 methods, six of which exist only because vendor B supports something vendor A does not. A grep finds the vendor''s SDK imported in six files outside the adapter. Maintaining the wrapper, tracking vendor features, and keeping its conformance tests green costs roughly 5 engineer-weeks a year. Nine eval suites exist and re-baselining them is manual, about 20 hours each. A staff engineer has proposed a rewrite of the wrapper. Renewal is in four months and the contract runs three years.","task":"Decide what to do with the wrapper, with the arithmetic that supports it.","model":"Compute exit both ways before arguing design. With the wrapper: coupling is small on paper but six files import the SDK directly, so real coupling is about 2.2 engineer-weeks, re-derivation 0.6, revalidation <b>9 suites x 20h = 180h = 4.8 weeks</b>. Exit <b>7.6 weeks</b>. Delete the wrapper and go raw: coupling rises to about 3.0, everything else identical. Exit <b>8.4 weeks</b>.<br><br>So the wrapper saves <b>0.8 engineer-weeks at exit and costs 5 a year</b>. Break-even is 0.8/5 x 12 = <b>1.9 months</b>, against a 36-month commitment. It is not a close call, and rewriting it makes the arithmetic worse by adding cost to an asset that already loses. It is a union abstraction: 19 methods spanning both vendors'' feature sets, forbidding nothing, and leaking through six import sites. If you cannot name what an abstraction forbids, it is a second SDK.<br><br>Now read where the number actually lives. Revalidation is <b>4.8 of 7.6 weeks, 63%</b>, and it is invariant to seam placement because the suites are yours either way. Build a re-baseline harness instead: roughly 3 engineer-weeks once, cutting 20 hours per suite to about 7, which drops revalidation to 1.7 weeks and total exit to <b>5.3 weeks</b>, for every vendor, permanently.<br><br>Recommendation: delete the wrapper, ban the SDK outside a four-method boundary, spend the rewrite budget on the re-baseline harness, and stop paying 5 weeks a year for 0.8 weeks of optionality.","pts":["Computes exit cost with and without the wrapper before discussing design, and quotes the difference in engineer-weeks","Derives break-even from saving over annual carry and compares it against the contract term","Names the union-abstraction failure using the test of what the interface forbids, not its size","Identifies revalidation as the dominant, seam-invariant term and redirects investment there","Rejects the rewrite explicitly and proposes a smaller boundary plus an enforcement rule instead"]},{"id":"s12","block":"b3","title":"The re-index nobody budgeted","sit":"You are reviewing another team''s architecture document for a knowledge assistant before it goes to a design review. It promises quarterly chunking experiments and monthly embedding evaluation. The corpus is 3.4M documents averaging 1,100 tokens. The doc states that index rebuilds run in place during a 2-hour maintenance window, that the cache''s 12-hour TTL is the invalidation strategy, and that deletion is handled by delete-by-filter on the index. The nightly summary table has no tenant key. The golden set is described as refreshed as needed. Spec card freshness tolerance is 20 minutes. Measured full rebuild time is 14 hours and last year''s re-derivation budget was spent on one run.","task":"Find the flaws, rank them, and say which single change you would require before this document is approved.","model":"Five flaws, ranked by what you cannot recover from.<br><br><b>1. Deletion fan-out is one store deep.</b> Delete-by-filter reaches the index; the summary table has no tenant key, so a scoped erasure cannot even be expressed, and the cache and any memory or trace stores are unaddressed. This is the only flaw with a statutory deadline attached, and it is unbounded, so it ranks first regardless of engineering elegance.<br><br><b>2. The rebuild is physically impossible as described.</b> 14 hours measured against a 2-hour window. In-place rebuild is not an option, so the document needs dual-index cutover with a bounded two-truths window and a resumable, content-hash-keyed backfill. Without it, no experiment in the document can actually be executed.<br><br><b>3. The experiment cadence is fiction.</b> 3.4M x 1,100 tokens is <b>3.74 billion tokens</b>, but embedding is rounding error. Verification and eval re-baselining dominate a run, and last year''s budget covered <b>one</b>. Subtract the mandatory model upgrade and the discretionary allowance is <b>zero</b>, against four quarterly experiments promised. Say the number in the review before the roadmap does.<br><br><b>4. TTL recorded as invalidation.</b> 720 minutes against a 20-minute tolerance is <b>36x over contract</b>, and a deleted record stays servable for the rest of the window.<br><br><b>5. Golden set refreshed as needed</b> means every migration rung is unmeasurable, and it will surface when a deprecation clock is shortest.<br><br>The single required change before approval: a derived-state register with a measured staleness and a deletion path per store. Everything above is a row in it.","pts":["Ranks by unbounded and statutory exposure first, not by engineering interest or by order in the document","Catches that measured rebuild time exceeds the stated window, making the in-place plan unexecutable","Computes the re-derivation budget and contrasts the affordable cadence with the promised one","Identifies TTL-as-invalidation and quantifies the breach against the spec card''s freshness tolerance","Names one required artifact, the derived-state register, rather than a list of fixes"]},{"id":"s13","block":"b4","title":"The gate with no authority","sit":"Priya is staff engineer on a contract-review assistant: 220,000 tasks a month, p95 budget 2,500ms against a measured base of 2,150ms, cost ceiling $0.018 per task against a base of $0.0134, quality bar under 1% wrong clause summaries. Her team''s quality plan for the quarter is one line: \"add faithfulness evals.\" Pressed for detail, they mean a model-based grounding check on every response, measured at 620ms p95, $0.0031 per task, with a 1.8% false-block rate on their audit sample. The team also has an eval suite that runs on every release and posts a score to the pull request. It has never failed a build. Nothing is on fire; she has to commit to a quality design this week.","task":"Design the control-point placement that fits her envelope, assign each one an authority - and say what happens to the CI suite.","model":"Headroom first: 2,500 - 2,150 = 350ms. The proposed gate measures 620ms, so the inline version is dead in one line. Note that cost was never the binding constraint: $0.0134 + $0.0031 = $0.0165 against an $0.018 ceiling, so it would have fit on money. Latency is what rules it out, and saying which constraint binds is half the value of the analysis.<br><br>Then price the false blocks, because nobody has. 1.8% of 220,000 is 3,960 good answers refused per month. Against the current ~1% escape rate the gate would cut wrong summaries from about 2,200 to about 570, preventing roughly 1,630 bad answers while creating 3,960 refusals. It generates more incidents than it prevents, before you count the p95 breach.<br><br>The placement that fits: (1) a deterministic output rail - required envelope plus every cited clause id present in the retrieved set - in tens of milliseconds, authority block_response, owner product. (2) Async sampling, with the rate chosen by detection lag rather than by roundness: 7,333 tasks a day, a regression on 0.5% of traffic is 37 a day, and at 5% sampling with 0.75 recognition you see 1.37 a day, so an 8-event alarm fires in about 5.8 days. At 1% it takes 29 days. Take 5%: 11,000 scored tasks a month at $0.0031 is $34, and zero milliseconds on the request path. Authority open_ticket, pages the quality rotation.<br><br>(3) The CI suite: give it authority block_release with a stated threshold, or delete it. Today it is record-only while the team believes it is a gate, which is the most expensive state a check can be in.","pts":["Computes headroom (350ms) against the gate''s measured 620ms and names latency, not cost, as the binding constraint","Prices the false blocks as a count (3,960/month) and compares it to the ~1,630 wrong answers prevented, rather than quoting 1.8%","Places a deterministic rail inline and moves semantic checking off the request path, preserving headroom for future needs","Derives the sampling rate from a detection-lag division (5% gives ~5.8 days, 1% gives ~29) instead of picking a round number","Names the CI suite''s real authority as record-only and forces the choice: give it block_release with a threshold, or delete it"]},{"id":"s14","block":"b4","title":"The eleven-day review queue","sit":"Marcus is principal engineer on a three-person AI platform team serving eight product teams. Every prompt change routes through platform review: 9 changes per team per month, about 45 minutes of reviewer attention each. Each reviewer can give roughly 270 minutes a week to review before their own roadmap slips, across a 13-week quarter. Median time from review request to merge is now eleven days, and two product leads have escalated. The org is adding roughly one product team per quarter. Marcus''s director has offered to fund two more platform engineers to clear the queue, and wants an answer by Friday.","task":"Decide what to change, and show with numbers why hiring reviewers is the wrong lever.","model":"Capacity: 3 x 270 x 13 = 10,530 reviewer-minutes per quarter. Demand: 8 teams x 9 changes x 3 months x 45 minutes = 9,720. Utilisation 0.92, and at a one-day service time the M/M/1 approximation gives 0.92 / 0.08 = about 12 days of waiting, which matches the observed eleven. Break-even is 10,530 / 1,215 = 8.7 teams, so they crossed into the painful zone a quarter ago and nobody noticed because headcount hadn''t changed.<br><br>Two reviewers raise capacity to 17,550, moving break-even to 14.4 teams. At one team per quarter that buys about six quarters and costs two engineers permanently, and the queue returns on a known date. Reviewers buy linear headroom; they don''t change the slope.<br><br>The lever that changes the slope: classify the 9 monthly changes by reversibility. A prompt change behind the traffic choke point, with eval coverage above threshold and no new tool grant, is reversible in one revert and should never have required a human. A change that grants a new irreversible action, or moves a route''s budget, is the kind that genuinely deserves an approver. In their data that split is roughly 7 to 2. Convert the seven into an interface contract a CI job checks: required trace fields present, budget declared for the route, eval coverage above the threshold, no new irreversible action in the tool manifest. Remaining human demand: 8 x 2 x 3 x 45 = 2,160 minutes, utilisation 0.21, wait about a quarter of a day, break-even near 39 teams.<br><br>Same three people, four times the runway, and the human review that remains is the approver role on irreversible changes rather than a style review of prompts.","pts":["Computes utilisation (9,720 / 10,530 = 0.92) and connects it to the observed eleven-day wait through queueing, not through effort","Shows hiring moves break-even from 8.7 to 14.4 teams - roughly six quarters at the current growth rate - and names it as linear headroom","Splits the review population by reversibility, and argues that a change revertible in one commit behind the choke point never needed a human","Specifies the interface contract as machine-checkable items (trace fields, declared budget, eval coverage, no new irreversible action)","Recomputes after automation (utilisation 0.21, break-even near 39 teams) and states that contracts change the slope while reviewers move the date"]},{"id":"s15","block":"b4","title":"The 2am quality page","sit":"Dana is architect at a healthcare scheduling company, six weeks from launching an AI intake assistant at roughly 9,000 tasks a day. Today a single rotation - four infrastructure engineers - receives every page for the service. The quality design is already agreed: an input rail (block_request), an output schema rail (block_response), async sampling at 4% (open_ticket), and a canary quality comparison on every deploy. The degradation ladder has five rungs; rungs 3 through 5 visibly change what patients see. The alert definition currently reads \"faithfulness_rate below 0.94 for 30 minutes\", and the runbook attached to it reads, in full, \"investigate\". Nothing is broken yet.","task":"Design the paging split: which alert goes to which rotation, on what window, with what runbook.","model":"Start with whether the alert can even fire honestly. 4% of 9,000 is 360 scored tasks a day, or about 7.5 in a 30-minute window. The standard deviation of a proportion at p=0.94 with n=7.5 is sqrt(0.94 x 0.06 / 7.5) = 0.087, so a single 30-minute window cannot distinguish 0.94 from 0.85. That alert will fire on noise, get acknowledged twice, and be tuned into silence within a month. Widen the window to 6 hours (n = 90, sd = 0.025) or raise sampling on the intake route specifically. Choose the window from the arithmetic, then write it down.<br><br>Then split the rotations, because \"the API is slow\" and \"the answers got worse\" need different skills and different runbooks. Availability rotation (infra) keeps: gateway error rate, latency SLO burn, rail service unavailable, provider 429 rate. Quality rotation (two product engineers plus one platform engineer) takes: faithfulness_rate below bar on the 6-hour window, canary quality delta versus control, judge agreement below its threshold, and - this is the one people misfile - a false-block-rate spike, which looks like an availability symptom and is a quality failure.<br><br>Runbooks, written now: roll back to the previous prompt version; compare canary against control on the same route; check judge agreement against the last human-labelled batch; pull degradation rungs 1 and 2, which the on-call is pre-authorised for. Rungs 3 through 5 change what patients see, so they require the named pre-authorizer from the ladder - the clinical director - which means the 2am action is a phone call to a specific person whose number is in the runbook. That decision is made now, in daylight, by Dana, and not at 2am by whoever happens to be holding the pager.","pts":["Checks whether the alert can fire honestly first: 7.5 samples per 30-minute window, sd 0.087, so the threshold is inside the noise","Fixes it by widening the window (n=90, sd 0.025) or raising sampling on the specific route, and derives the window from the arithmetic","Splits availability and quality into separate rotations on the grounds of different diagnostics and different runbooks, not different people","Routes the false-block-rate spike to the quality rotation despite it presenting as an availability symptom","Pre-authorises rungs 1-2 to on-call and binds rungs 3-5 to the named pre-authorizer, so the 2am decision is a phone call rather than a judgement call"]},{"id":"s16","block":"b4","title":"The doc that outlived its reason","sit":"You''ve been handed another group''s architecture document to review before their 14-month-old support assistant is extended to a second product line. It is thorough and well written. Spec card: p95 3,000ms, freshness tolerance 15 minutes, $0.012 per task, 400,000 tasks a month, peak multiplier 3x, blast radius \"user-visible guidance, no automatic payout\". Latency stages: retrieval 300, generation 2,100, faithfulness gate 700, serialise and egress 190. A derived claim-summary cache, rebuilt nightly, rebuild time 41 minutes. One FMEA row reads: stale policy document, severity 8, occurrence 4, detectability 9, control \"alert on index age\", class mitigation. The action inventory lists \"issue goodwill credit\", reversible: no, tier: auto, approver: none. Every rejected shape names a numeric binding constraint, and every degradation rung has a pre-authorizer.","task":"Find the contradictions, rank them, and say which one you would fix before the extension ships.","model":"Four findings, and the ranking rule is irreversibility times whether it is happening right now - not the RPN score.<br><br><b>First: the action inventory contradicts the spec card.</b> \"Issue goodwill credit\" is irreversible, runs at tier auto, and has no approver, while the blast-radius line four pages earlier says no automatic payout. This is the only finding where the system is currently doing something the requirements forbid, and because it is irreversible the damage does not wait for a threshold to be crossed. Fix today: tier moves to approve, with a named approver.<br><br><b>Second: the derived store cannot meet the freshness tolerance at any cadence.</b> Nightly rebuild is a 24-hour staleness bound against a 15-minute tolerance, 96x over, and since the rebuild itself takes 41 minutes, no rebuild frequency fixes it. The fix is architectural - an event-driven invalidation on claim update - so it needs planning, and it is silently producing answers grounded in stale policy right now.<br><br><b>Third: the FMEA row is the same defect wearing a different hat.</b> Severity 8 x occurrence 4 x detectability 9 gives an RPN of 288, and the control is \"alert on index age\", which is a mitigation on a proxy rather than an observation of the failure. Detectability 9 requires a structural fix, and the structural fix is finding two''s invalidation path. Merging them makes the roadmap shorter, which is worth saying out loud.<br><br><b>Fourth: the budgets don''t sum.</b> 300 + 2,100 + 700 + 190 = 3,290ms against a 3,000ms p95, over by 290 before any queueing at the declared 3x peak. It ranks last only because it is bounded, user-visible and self-announcing: the SLO will tell them.<br><br>And say what''s good, or the review gets discounted: every rejection names a numeric constraint, and every degradation rung has a pre-authorizer. Both are rarer than they should be.","pts":["Finds all four contradictions, each as a disagreement between two artifacts rather than as a flaw in one","Ranks by irreversibility and whether it is currently happening, and states that ranking rule explicitly rather than sorting by RPN","Puts the auto-tier irreversible action first because the system is doing what the spec card forbids, and it cannot be undone","Recognises that the FMEA row and the derived-store finding share one structural fix, shortening the roadmap","Names what the document does well (numeric binding constraints on rejections, pre-authorised degradation rungs) so the review lands as a review rather than as a verdict"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (course_id, id) do update set tier = excluded.tier, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();

